-- Physics part 2 of 2: longer explanations for PHY-0621 onwards (rest of Physics 11, the gap and hard sets and past-paper questions).
-- Longer explanations: each question's "why" now says in two or three sentences why the key is right, and each wrong
-- option gets a sentence or two on the mistake behind it. Facts checked against the source textbook named in each
-- question's source. Stems, options and answer keys are unchanged: every UPDATE also matches on the current key, so a
-- question whose key was edited since is left alone. Safe to run twice.

UPDATE questions SET why = 'When polar ice melts and the water flows towards the equator, mass moves further from the Earth''s axis, so the Earth''s moment of inertia I increases. No outside torque acts, so angular momentum L = Iω stays constant and ω must fall slightly. A slower spin means each day lasts a little longer.', wrong = '{"0": "This would need the moment of inertia to fall. Mass moving away from the axis increases I, so ω decreases and the Earth spins more slowly, not faster.", "2": "Moment of inertia depends on how the mass is spread about the axis, not just on the total mass. Moving water outward raises I even though the mass is unchanged.", "3": "With no external torque the angular momentum stays constant; it cannot double. The change in the day is only slight."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0621' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A flywheel is a heavy wheel mounted on an axle. Once spinning it holds a large amount of rotational kinetic energy, so it stores energy and smooths out ups and downs in a machine''s output. Its large angular momentum also gives stability in vehicles, machinery and spacecraft.', wrong = '{"0": "A flywheel is not a brake. It keeps rotation going steadily rather than adding friction to slow the machine.", "1": "A spinning flywheel resists changes in its rotation because of its large angular momentum. It keeps the motion steady instead of reversing it.", "3": "A flywheel is deliberately heavy, since a large moment of inertia lets it store more rotational energy. It does not reduce a vehicle''s mass."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0622' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A gyroscope is a fast-spinning flywheel mounted in supporting rings. Spinning at high speed gives it a large angular momentum, and changing the direction of that angular momentum needs a correspondingly large torque. Without such a torque its axis keeps the same direction, which is why gyroscopes help navigate aeroplanes, ships and spacecraft.', wrong = '{"0": "A gyroscope has a large moment of inertia, not zero. Its large I and high spin rate together give it large angular momentum.", "1": "Gravity acts on every object with mass, spinning or not. The gyroscope keeps its orientation because of its angular momentum, not because gravity is absent.", "2": "The gyroscope is mounted in rings so that it can turn freely. Friction does not lock its axis; its angular momentum keeps the axis steady."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0623' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In physics, work is done only when a force moves its point of application: W = Fd cos θ. The wall does not move, so d = 0 and W = 0, however large the force or however long it is applied. The student''s muscles get tired from internal effort, but no work is done on the wall.', wrong = '{"1": "A large force alone does not give work. Work also needs a displacement, and here the wall''s displacement is zero.", "2": "Force × time is impulse, not work. Work is force × displacement, and the displacement is zero.", "3": "Negative work needs a displacement opposite to the force. The wall does not move at all, so the work is zero, not negative."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0624' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the force makes an angle θ with the displacement, only its component F cos θ along the motion does work, so W = Fd cos θ. Here W = 50 N × 4 m × cos 60° = 200 × 0.5 = 100 J.', wrong = '{"0": "12.5 comes from dividing the force by the distance (50 ÷ 4). Work is force multiplied by displacement, not divided.", "2": "173 J uses sin 60° (0.866) instead of cos 60°. The sine gives the component perpendicular to the motion, which does no work.", "3": "200 J is Fd with no angle factor. It would be correct only if the force acted along the direction of motion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0625' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Work is W = Fd cos θ, where θ is the angle between force and displacement. For angles between 90° and 180°, cos θ is negative, so the work is negative. This means the force acts partly against the motion, as friction does on a sliding block.', wrong = '{"0": "Work is positive only when θ is less than 90°, so that the force has a component along the motion.", "1": "Work is zero only when θ is exactly 90°, because cos 90° = 0. Above 90°, cos θ is negative.", "2": "Work is greatest when θ = 0°, since cos 0° = 1. Beyond 90° the work is negative, not maximum."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0626' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a circular orbit the Earth''s gravitational pull acts along the radius, towards the centre, while the satellite''s displacement at each moment is along the tangent. The angle between them is 90°, so W = Fd cos 90° = 0. This is why the satellite''s speed stays constant.', wrong = '{"0": "Gravity does pull on the satellite, but the pull is at right angles to its motion. A force perpendicular to the displacement does no work.", "1": "In a circular orbit the satellite''s distance from the Earth''s centre stays the same, so it is not moving away and the work is not negative.", "2": "mgh applies to a vertical change in height near the Earth''s surface. In a circular orbit the height does not change at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0627' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Work is defined as the scalar (dot) product of force and displacement, W = F·d = Fd cos θ. The dot product of two vectors gives a number with no direction, so work is a scalar quantity.', wrong = '{"1": "The vector (cross) product of two vectors gives another vector, as in torque. Work uses the dot product, so it is a scalar.", "2": "A unit does not decide whether a quantity is a scalar or a vector. For example, the newton is the unit of force, which is a vector.", "3": "Work is not always positive; it is negative when θ is more than 90°. A negative sign does not make it a vector, so this is not the reason."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0628' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a constant force the force–displacement graph is a horizontal line, and the rectangle under it has area F × d, which equals the work. For a varying force the same idea holds: the area between the F cos θ–d curve and the displacement axis gives the work done.', wrong = '{"1": "The slope of a force–displacement graph is force per unit displacement, with units N m⁻¹. For a spring this is the spring constant, not work.", "2": "Force × time is impulse, which changes momentum. Work is force × displacement.", "3": "The intercept on the force axis is just the force at zero displacement. It is a force, not a work."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0629' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Work is the area under the force–displacement graph. From 0 to 3 m the area is a rectangle, 8 N × 3 m = 24 J. From 3 m to 5 m the force falls to zero, giving a triangle, ½ × 2 m × 8 N = 8 J. The total work is 24 J + 8 J = 32 J.', wrong = '{"0": "16 J is 8 N × 2 m, only the last 2 m drawn as a full rectangle. It misses the 24 J of the first 3 m, and the last section is a triangle.", "1": "24 J is only the rectangle from 0 to 3 m. The triangle from 3 m to 5 m adds another 8 J.", "3": "40 J treats the force as 8 N over the whole 5 m. The force falls to zero in the last 2 m, so that part is a triangle worth 8 J, not 16 J."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0630' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The path is divided into many small displacements Δd. Over each one the force is nearly constant, so the work there is F cos θ Δd. Adding all these pieces and letting each Δd approach zero gives the exact total work, which equals the area under the F cos θ–d curve.', wrong = '{"0": "The maximum force acts only at some points. Using it for the whole path overestimates the work when the force varies.", "1": "Average speed × time gives a distance, not work. Work involves the force along the path.", "2": "The force changes along the path, so its starting value does not represent the rest of the motion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0631' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A force is conservative if the work it does in moving an object between two points does not depend on the path taken. An equivalent test is that the work done around any closed path is zero. Gravitational, electrostatic and elastic spring forces are examples.', wrong = '{"0": "A conservative force can do negative work. Gravity does negative work on a body being lifted, yet it is conservative.", "1": "The kinetic energy can change under a conservative force; a falling stone speeds up under gravity. What is conserved is the total mechanical energy.", "2": "Conservative forces act on moving bodies too. The definition is about the work done as a body moves between two points."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0632' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kinetic friction always acts opposite to the motion, so it does negative work equal to the friction force times the length of the path. A longer path between the same two points means more work against friction, so the work depends on the path and friction is non-conservative.', wrong = '{"1": "The work done by gravity depends only on the change in height, not on the path. Gravity is the standard example of a conservative force.", "2": "The electrostatic force between charges, like gravity, does path-independent work. It is a conservative force.", "3": "The elastic spring force does work that depends only on the start and end stretch of the spring. It is conservative."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0633' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gravity is a conservative force, so its work depends only on the change in height and not on the path. The book moves upward, against gravity, so the work done by gravity is negative: W = −mgh = −2 kg × 10 m s⁻² × 1.5 m = −30 J for every path.', wrong = '{"0": "The size is right but the sign is wrong. The book moves up while gravity pulls down, so gravity does negative work.", "2": "Every path that starts and ends at the same heights gives the same work by gravity, −30 J. The vertical path is not special.", "3": "Different paths give different work only for non-conservative forces such as friction. Gravity''s work is path independent."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0634' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'On the way up gravity opposes the motion and does −mgh of work. On the way down it acts along the motion and does +mgh. The total is −mgh + mgh = 0, as expected for a conservative force taken round a closed path.', wrong = '{"0": "2mgh adds the sizes of the two halves and ignores the signs. The upward half is negative and cancels the downward half.", "2": "−mgh is only the work done by gravity during the upward journey. The downward half adds +mgh.", "3": "mgh is only the work done by gravity during the fall. The upward half adds −mgh, so the total is zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0635' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Power measures how quickly work is done. Average power is P = ΔW/Δt, the work done divided by the time taken. Its SI unit is the watt, where 1 W = 1 J s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "Total work is measured in joules. Power tells how fast that work is done, not how much is done.", "1": "Force × displacement is the definition of work, not power. Power divides the work by the time taken.", "2": "The capacity to do work is energy. Power is the rate at which work is done or energy is transferred."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0636' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Power is P = ΔW/Δt. Since ΔW = F·Δd, dividing by Δt gives P = F·(Δd/Δt) = F·v. So power is the scalar (dot) product of force and velocity. For example, a 100 N force moving its point of application at 2 m s⁻¹ delivers 200 W.', wrong = '{"1": "The cross product of two vectors gives a vector, but power is a scalar. The scalar (dot) product F·v is needed.", "2": "For a given force, power increases with velocity, so it cannot be F divided by v. The units of F/v are not watts either.", "3": "½Fv² has units N m² s⁻², which are not the units of power. Power is simply F·v."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0637' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a force F moves its point of application at a steady speed v in its own direction, the power is P = Fv. Here P = 2000 N × 25 m s⁻¹ = 50 000 W = 50 kW.', wrong = '{"0": "2.5 kW is twenty times too small. Multiplying 2000 by 25 gives 50 000 W, not 2500 W.", "1": "5 kW is ten times too small, a misplaced zero. 2000 × 25 = 50 000 W, which is 50 kW.", "3": "80 W comes from dividing the force by the speed (2000 ÷ 25). Power is force multiplied by speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0638' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Climbing the stairs she does work against gravity equal to her gain in potential energy: W = mgh = 50 kg × 9.8 m s⁻² × 6.0 m = 2940 J. Power is work divided by time, so P = 2940 J ÷ 10 s = 294 W.', wrong = '{"0": "30 W comes from 50 × 6 ÷ 10, which uses the mass instead of the weight. The factor g = 9.8 m s⁻² is missing.", "1": "49 W is 2940 ÷ 60, which treats the time as 60 s. The climb takes 10 s.", "3": "2940 is the work done in joules. It must be divided by the 10 s to get the power."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0639' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A kilowatt-hour is the energy used when a power of one kilowatt runs for one hour. So 1 kW h = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 × 10⁶ J, or 3.6 MJ. It is the commercial unit of electrical energy on electricity bills.', wrong = '{"1": "3.6 × 10³ J (3600 J) is one watt for one hour, a watt-hour. A kilowatt is 1000 times bigger.", "2": "6.0 × 10⁴ J is 1000 W for 60 s, one kilowatt for one minute. An hour has 3600 s.", "3": "1000 J is one kilowatt for only one second. An hour is 3600 s, so the energy is 3600 times larger."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0640' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Energy = power × time. The heater runs 4 h × 30 = 120 h in the month. Energy = 1.5 kW × 120 h = 180 kW h, which is 180 units on an electricity bill. Working in kW and hours keeps the numbers simple.', wrong = '{"0": "6 kW h is 1.5 kW × 4 h, the energy for one day only. It must be multiplied by 30 days.", "1": "45 kW h is 1.5 × 30, which counts only one hour per day. The heater runs 4 hours each day.", "3": "The number is right but the unit is wrong. 180 kW h is about 6.5 × 10⁸ J, far more than 180 J."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0641' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Energy is the capacity of a body to do work, and it is measured in joules like work. Mechanical energy has two basic forms: kinetic energy, due to motion, and potential energy, due to position or a constrained state.', wrong = '{"1": "The rate of doing work is power, measured in watts. Energy is the ability to do the work in the first place.", "2": "Force per unit area is pressure, measured in pascals (N m⁻²).", "3": "Mass × velocity is linear momentum, a vector measured in kg m s⁻¹. Energy is a scalar measured in joules."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0642' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kinetic energy is KE = ½mv². With m = 4 kg and v = 5 m s⁻¹, KE = ½ × 4 × 5² = ½ × 4 × 25 = 50 J. Notice that the speed is squared, so it affects the kinetic energy more strongly than the mass does.', wrong = '{"0": "10 J is ½ × 4 × 5, which uses v instead of v². The speed must be squared.", "1": "20 is m × v = 4 × 5, the ball''s momentum in kg m s⁻¹, not its kinetic energy.", "3": "100 J is mv² = 4 × 25, which leaves out the factor ½."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0643' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kinetic energy is KE = ½mv², so it is proportional to the square of the speed. If v is tripled, v² becomes 3² = 9 times larger, and the kinetic energy becomes 9 times as large.', wrong = '{"0": "This treats KE as proportional to v. Because KE depends on v², tripling the speed multiplies it by 9.", "1": "6 comes from 2 × 3, which doubles the factor instead of squaring it. Squaring 3 gives 9.", "3": "27 is 3³, which would be true only if KE depended on v³. It depends on v²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0644' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Writing v = p/m in KE = ½mv² gives KE = p²/2m. For the same momentum p, kinetic energy is inversely proportional to mass, so the lighter body, which must be moving faster, has more kinetic energy.', wrong = '{"0": "With equal momentum, KE = p²/2m is smaller for the larger mass. The heavy body moves slowly and has less kinetic energy.", "2": "Equal momentum does not mean equal kinetic energy. KE = p²/2m also depends on the mass.", "3": "The speeds are not needed. Since p is the same, KE = p²/2m can be compared using mass alone."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0645' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kinetic energy can be written in terms of momentum as KE = p²/2m. With the mass fixed, KE is proportional to p². Doubling p multiplies p² by 2² = 4, so the kinetic energy becomes four times as large.', wrong = '{"0": "This treats KE as proportional to p. KE = p²/2m depends on the square of momentum, so doubling p gives four times the KE.", "1": "If the momentum doubles at fixed mass, the speed doubles, so the kinetic energy must change.", "2": "A larger momentum at the same mass means a larger speed, so the kinetic energy increases, not decreases."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0646' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The car''s kinetic energy is used up as work against the braking force: ½mv² = fd. KE = ½ × 1000 kg × (20 m s⁻¹)² = 2.0 × 10⁵ J. So f = 2.0 × 10⁵ J ÷ 50 m = 4000 N.', wrong = '{"0": "400 N comes from dividing the momentum, 1000 × 20 = 20 000, by 50 m. The kinetic energy, 200 000 J, must be used.", "1": "2000 N is half the correct value. It would be the answer if the stopping distance were 100 m.", "3": "8000 N comes from mv²/d = 400 000 ÷ 50, which leaves out the ½ in the kinetic energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0647' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Potential energy is stored energy due to a body''s position or its constrained (deformed) state. A compressed or stretched spring stores energy because of its deformation, and this form is called elastic potential energy. It is released when the spring returns to its natural length.', wrong = '{"0": "Kinetic energy is energy of motion. A spring held compressed is not moving, so its stored energy is not kinetic.", "1": "Gravitational potential energy depends on height in a gravitational field. The spring''s energy comes from its compression, not its height.", "2": "Chemical energy is stored in the bonds of substances, as in fuels and food. A spring stores mechanical energy through its deformation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0648' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Absolute gravitational potential energy at a point is defined as the work done by the gravitational force in moving the object from that point to infinity. Infinity is chosen as the reference because there the gravitational force becomes zero, so the potential energy there is taken as zero.', wrong = '{"1": "The Earth''s surface is the usual reference for mgh, which is a relative potential energy. Absolute potential energy uses infinity as the reference.", "2": "The reference point for absolute potential energy is infinity, where gravity vanishes, not the Earth''s centre.", "3": "The highest point a body reaches depends on how it is thrown. Absolute potential energy needs a fixed reference, which is infinity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0649' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gravity weakens with distance, so the work is found by adding small steps from r out to infinity. The result is U = −GMm/r, where M is the Earth''s mass and m is the object''s mass. The minus sign shows that the field is attractive: work must be done on the body to take it to infinity, where U = 0.', wrong = '{"0": "The magnitude is right but the sign is wrong. Gravity is attractive, so the potential energy below the zero at infinity is negative.", "2": "GMm/r² is the size of the gravitational force from Newton''s law of gravitation. Potential energy has 1/r, not 1/r².", "3": "mgr extends the near-surface mgh to large distances. g is not constant far from the Earth, so this is not valid."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0650' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'U = −GMm/r is always negative. As r increases, the fraction GMm/r gets smaller, so U becomes less negative, which means it increases. At an infinite distance U reaches zero. This matches the idea that lifting a body increases its potential energy.', wrong = '{"0": "As r grows, GMm/r gets smaller, so −GMm/r moves towards zero. U becomes less negative, not more negative.", "1": "U = −GMm/r depends on r, so it changes as the body moves further from the Earth''s centre.", "3": "U only approaches zero as r approaches infinity. At any finite distance it stays negative."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0651' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The formula PE = mgh treats the gravitational force mg as constant over the height h. This is a good approximation only near the Earth''s surface. Over large distances the force falls as 1/r², so the absolute formula U = −GMm/r must be used instead.', wrong = '{"0": "At large heights g decreases as 1/r², so mgh gives wrong answers. It is not valid for any height.", "1": "At the centre of the Earth gravity behaves very differently. The formula is meant for heights just above the surface.", "2": "Orbiting satellites are far enough away that g changes noticeably. Their energy is found using −GMm/r."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0652' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Escape velocity is the minimum initial velocity with which an object must be launched so that it goes out of the Earth''s gravitational field and never returns. Its initial kinetic energy just equals the increase in potential energy needed to reach infinity. For the Earth it is about 11 km s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "A satellite in a low orbit moves at the orbital (critical) velocity, about 7.9 km s⁻¹. It stays bound to the Earth and does not escape.", "2": "The maximum speed of a body falling through air is its terminal velocity. It depends on air resistance, not on escaping gravity.", "3": "The Earth''s speed around the Sun, about 30 km s⁻¹, is an orbital speed and has nothing to do with escaping the Earth''s field."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0653' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To escape, the initial kinetic energy must equal the increase in potential energy from the surface to infinity: ½mv² = GMm/R. The mass m cancels, so v² = 2GM/R and v_esc = √(2GM/R). Using GM = gR², this is also √(2gR).', wrong = '{"0": "√(GM/R) is the orbital speed of a satellite just above the surface. Escape speed is √2 times larger.", "2": "2GM/R is v², not v. The square root must be taken to get a speed.", "3": "The factor 2 belongs in the numerator. √(GM/2R) would be smaller than even the orbital speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0654' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Using v_esc = √(2gR) with g = 9.8 m s⁻² and R = 6.4 × 10⁶ m gives √(1.25 × 10⁸) ≈ 1.1 × 10⁴ m s⁻¹. That is about 11 km s⁻¹, the Earth''s escape velocity.', wrong = '{"0": "1.1 km s⁻¹ is ten times too small, a slip in the power of ten. The correct value is about 11 km s⁻¹.", "1": "3 × 10⁵ km s⁻¹ is the speed of light in vacuum. Nothing with mass can reach it.", "2": "7.9 km s⁻¹ is the speed for a satellite in a low circular orbit. Escape needs about √2 times more, about 11 km s⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0655' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The escape velocity is v_esc = √(2GM/R), while a satellite skimming the surface needs v_orbit = √(GM/R). Dividing gives v_esc/v_orbit = √2. Check: 7.9 km s⁻¹ × 1.41 ≈ 11.2 km s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "The ratio is √2, about 1.41, not 2. It comes from the factor 2 inside the square root.", "1": "Neither speed depends on the mass of the moving body. In both formulas the body''s mass cancels.", "3": "The formulas v_esc = √(2GM/R) and v_orbit = √(GM/R) contain no term for the Earth''s rotation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0656' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For escape, ½mv² = GMm/R. The rocket''s mass m appears on both sides and cancels, leaving v_esc = √(2GM/R). So escape velocity depends only on the planet''s mass M and radius R. A heavier rocket needs more energy to escape, but not more speed.', wrong = '{"0": "A heavier rocket needs more kinetic energy, but its required speed is the same. The mass cancels from ½mv² = GMm/R.", "1": "The rocket''s mass does not appear in v_esc = √(2GM/R), so a lighter rocket needs the same speed.", "2": "Escape velocity is defined from energy, which does not depend on direction. The launch angle does not bring the rocket''s mass into it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0657' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The work–energy theorem says the work done on a body by the net force equals the change in its kinetic energy: W = ½mv_f² − ½mv_i². It follows from F = ma combined with the equation 2ad = v_f² − v_i².', wrong = '{"1": "Potential energy changes are linked to work done against conservative forces such as gravity. The theorem links net work to kinetic energy.", "2": "The theorem gives the change in kinetic energy, not the total energy the body has.", "3": "Momentum is changed by impulse (force × time). Work, which is force × displacement, changes kinetic energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0658' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By the work–energy theorem, the net work equals the gain in kinetic energy. W = Fd = 10 N × 5 m = 50 J. Starting from rest, ½mv² = 50, so ½ × 2 × v² = 50, giving v² = 50 and v ≈ 7.1 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "5 m s⁻¹ comes from 2v² = 50, which leaves out the ½ in ½mv².", "2": "10 m s⁻¹ comes from ½v² = 50, which treats the mass as 1 kg. The mass is 2 kg.", "3": "50 is the work done in joules. It must be set equal to ½mv² and solved for v."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0659' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By the work–energy theorem, the work done on the ball equals its change in kinetic energy. The speed drops from 14 m s⁻¹ to 6 m s⁻¹, so the kinetic energy falls and the work is negative. The force must act against the ball''s motion.', wrong = '{"0": "A force acting does not make the work positive. Positive work would increase the kinetic energy, but here it decreases.", "2": "Work is zero only if the kinetic energy does not change. Here the speed, and so the KE, has dropped.", "3": "The mass is needed for the size of the work, but not its sign. A fall in speed always means negative work."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0660' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With no air resistance, mechanical energy is conserved, so PE + KE stays equal to the starting value mgh. Halfway down the height is h/2, so PE = mg(h/2) = ½mgh. The rest has become kinetic energy: KE = mgh − ½mgh = ½mgh.', wrong = '{"0": "The kinetic energy reaches the full mgh only just before the body hits the ground, when all the PE has been converted.", "1": "KE is zero only at the start. By halfway the body has gained speed, so its KE is no longer zero.", "3": "The total mechanical energy is only mgh, so the kinetic energy can never exceed it. Energy is not created during the fall."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0661' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The loss of potential energy becomes kinetic energy: mgh = ½mv². The mass cancels, so v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 10 m s⁻² × 20 m) = √400 = 20 m s⁻¹. The 0.5 kg mass is not needed.', wrong = '{"0": "10 m s⁻¹ is half the correct speed. It is the speed after falling only 5 m, since √(2 × 10 × 5) = 10.", "1": "14 m s⁻¹ is √200 = √(gh), which leaves out the factor 2 in v = √(2gh).", "3": "200 is 2gh, which equals v² in m² s⁻². The square root must be taken to get the speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0662' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When air friction f acts over the fall height h, part of the lost potential energy is used to do work against friction and only the rest becomes kinetic energy. So mgh = ½mv² + fh: the loss in PE equals the gain in KE plus the work done against friction.', wrong = '{"0": "Loss in PE equals gain in KE only when friction is ignored. With air resistance some energy goes into work against friction.", "1": "The body still speeds up as it falls, so part of the PE becomes kinetic energy. Friction takes only a share.", "2": "The work against friction is an extra share of the lost PE, so it must be added to the KE gain, not subtracted."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0663' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Loss in PE = gain in KE + work against air. Loss in PE = mgh = 2 × 10 × 20 = 400 J. Gain in KE = ½ × 2 × 18² = 324 J. So work against air = 400 − 324 = 76 J = f × 20 m, giving f = 3.8 N.', wrong = '{"0": "In free fall from 20 m the speed would be 20 m s⁻¹. The object lands at only 18 m s⁻¹, so air resistance must have acted.", "2": "20 N is the object''s weight, mg = 2 × 10. Air resistance is much smaller than that.", "3": "76 J is the energy used against air resistance, which is work in joules. Dividing by the 20 m distance gives the force."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0664' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Without friction, loss of PE equals gain of KE. The car drops from 5.0 m to 1.8 m, a fall of 3.2 m. So ½mv² = mgΔh and v = √(2gΔh) = √(2 × 10 × 3.2) = √64 = 8 m s⁻¹. The mass cancels.', wrong = '{"0": "6 m s⁻¹ is √(2 × 10 × 1.8), which uses the final height as the drop. The car actually falls 5.0 − 1.8 = 3.2 m.", "2": "10 m s⁻¹ is √(2 × 10 × 5.0), the speed after the full 5.0 m drop to ground level.", "3": "64 is v² in m² s⁻². Its square root, 8 m s⁻¹, is the speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0665' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After falling a distance x, the body has PE = mg(h − x) and, from v² = 2gx, KE = mgx. Their sum is mg(h − x) + mgx = mgh. So without friction the total mechanical energy is mgh at every point; the loss in PE equals the gain in KE.', wrong = '{"1": "At the top the body is at rest but has PE = mgh, so its total energy there is mgh, not zero.", "2": "Energy is not created as the body falls. Just before landing all of it is kinetic, still mgh in total.", "3": "The total stays mgh throughout the fall. Only its split between PE and KE changes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0666' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kinetic friction always acts against the motion, so the work done against it equals the friction force multiplied by the length of the path. On the same rough surface the friction force is the same, so a path twice as long needs twice as much work. This path dependence is what makes friction non-conservative.', wrong = '{"0": "Equal work along different paths is true for conservative forces like gravity. Friction''s work grows with the length of the path.", "2": "A longer path means more distance against friction, so more work, not less.", "3": "For friction, the work round a closed path is not zero, because friction opposes the motion on every part of the trip. The question also compares two open paths."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0667' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In crystalline solids, the atoms, molecules or ions are arranged in a regular pattern that repeats throughout the material, called long-range order. Most metals and ceramics are crystalline, as are quartz, diamond and common salt.', wrong = '{"0": "Amorphous means without form or structure. Their particles have no regular repeating arrangement, like a liquid frozen in place.", "1": "Glassy solids is another name for amorphous solids, such as ordinary glass, which have a disordered structure.", "3": "Polymeric solids have a structure between order and disorder. They are only partially or poorly crystalline."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0668' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A crystalline solid melts at a definite temperature because its ordered structure breaks down suddenly once the atomic vibrations become large enough. Quartz is crystalline, like calcite, sugar, mica and diamond, so it has a definite melting point.', wrong = '{"0": "Ordinary glass is amorphous. On heating it softens gradually into a paste and then a viscous liquid, with no sharp melting point.", "1": "Plastic is amorphous in its structure, so it softens over a range of temperatures instead of melting at one point.", "2": "Fused silica is a glassy, amorphous form of silicon dioxide. Like other glasses it has no definite melting point."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0669' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Glass is an amorphous solid, which means its molecules have no regular arrangement, like a liquid whose disorder has been frozen in. On heating there is no ordered structure to collapse at one temperature, so it softens gradually into a paste and becomes a very viscous liquid at around 800 °C.', wrong = '{"0": "The atoms in every solid, including glass, vibrate about their positions. The missing sharp melting point is due to the disordered structure.", "1": "Glass is not crystalline. A crystalline solid would have a sharp melting point.", "2": "Glass does not melt at one fixed temperature. It softens gradually and is already a very viscous liquid at about 800 °C."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0670' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Atoms in a crystal are not fixed. Each atom vibrates about a fixed mean position, and the amplitude of this vibration increases as temperature rises. Only the average positions are perfectly ordered. At the melting point the vibrations become so large that the structure suddenly breaks up.', wrong = '{"1": "Atoms moving freely through the whole material describes a liquid or gas. In a crystal each atom stays near its own fixed point.", "2": "Atoms in a crystal vibrate at all ordinary temperatures, not only at the melting point.", "3": "Heating gives the atoms more energy, so their vibrations get larger, not smaller."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0671' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Polymers have a structure partway between the full order of crystals and the disorder of amorphous solids. For this reason they are described as partially or poorly crystalline solids. Examples are polythene, polystyrene and nylon.', wrong = '{"0": "Perfect long-range order describes crystalline solids such as quartz, diamond and metals.", "1": "A completely disordered structure, like a frozen liquid, describes amorphous solids such as glass.", "3": "Polymers are mainly chains of carbon combined with hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen and other elements, not metal atoms."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0672' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Polymers are made by polymerisation, in which many small molecules join chemically into very long chain molecules. Nylon is a synthetic polymer, along with polythene and polystyrene.', wrong = '{"0": "Diamond is a crystalline form of carbon, with atoms in a regular three-dimensional network, not long chains.", "1": "Common salt, NaCl, is a crystalline solid made of Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions in a regular lattice.", "3": "Copper is a metal with a crystalline structure. It is not made of long chain molecules."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0673' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A deforming force pushes the atoms of a solid away from their equilibrium positions. When the force is removed, the atoms return and the body regains its original shape, as long as the force was not too large. This ability is called elasticity.', wrong = '{"0": "Plasticity is the opposite behaviour: a permanent change in shape that remains after the force is removed.", "1": "Viscosity is the internal friction between layers of a moving fluid. It has nothing to do with a solid regaining its shape.", "2": "Ductility is the ability of a material to be stretched or drawn into wires through plastic deformation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0674' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Stress is the force applied per unit area to produce a change in the shape, volume or length of a body: σ = F/A. Its SI unit is N m⁻², which is called the pascal (Pa).', wrong = '{"1": "Change in length per unit original length is tensile strain, which measures the deformation that stress produces.", "2": "Force per unit extension is the spring constant k from Hooke''s law, F = kx.", "3": "Change in volume per unit volume is volumetric strain, again a measure of deformation, not of stress."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0675' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Stresses are named after the change they cause. A stress that changes length is tensile stress, one that changes volume is volume stress, and one that changes shape is shear stress. Shear stress acts parallel to a face, making one face slide relative to the opposite face.', wrong = '{"0": "Tensile stress changes the length of a body, as when a wire is stretched.", "2": "Volume stress changes the volume of a body, as when an object is squeezed equally from all sides.", "3": "Strain is the deformation produced, not the stress that causes it. Compressive strain is a fractional decrease in length."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0676' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Strain is the fractional change in size, for example ΔL/L₀ for a change in length. It is a ratio of two lengths, so the units cancel. Strain is therefore dimensionless and has no unit.', wrong = '{"0": "N m⁻² is the unit of stress (the pascal), not strain.", "1": "Strain is the change in length divided by the original length, so the metres cancel. It is a fraction, not a length.", "3": "N is the unit of force. Strain is a ratio and has no unit."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0677' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Tensile strain is ΔL/L₀, with both lengths in the same unit. Here ΔL = 1.0 mm = 1.0 × 10⁻³ m and L₀ = 2.0 m. So strain = (1.0 × 10⁻³)/(2.0) = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴, with no unit.', wrong = '{"0": "0.5 is 1.0 ÷ 2.0 with the millimetres not converted to metres. Both lengths must be in the same unit.", "1": "2.0 × 10⁻³ multiplies the extension by the length (1.0 × 10⁻³ × 2.0) instead of dividing.", "2": "2.0 × 10³ is L₀/ΔL, the ratio turned upside down. Strain is ΔL/L₀."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0678' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When one face of a block slides a distance Δx relative to the opposite face a distance y away, the shear strain is γ = Δx/y = tan θ. For small angles measured in radians, tan θ ≈ θ, so the shear strain is approximately the shear angle θ in radians.', wrong = '{"0": "The approximation tan θ ≈ θ works only when θ is in radians. In degrees the number would be about 57 times too large.", "1": "Change in volume per unit volume is volumetric strain. Shear strain measures a change of shape at constant volume.", "2": "A change of shape is a real deformation and is measured by shear strain, so it is not zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0679' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Young''s modulus is the ratio of tensile stress to tensile strain: Y = stress ÷ strain = FL₀/(AΔL). Since strain has no unit, Y has the same unit as stress, N m⁻². A large Y means the material is stiff and stretches little.', wrong = '{"1": "Strain divided by stress is the inverse of Young''s modulus. Y is stress over strain.", "2": "Force divided by extension is the spring constant k. It depends on the wire''s length and area, while Y depends only on the material.", "3": "Young''s modulus is a ratio of stress to strain, not their product."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0680' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Stress = F/A = 100 N ÷ 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ m² = 5.0 × 10⁷ N m⁻². Strain = ΔL/L₀ = 1.5 × 10⁻³ m ÷ 3.0 m = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴. Young''s modulus Y = stress/strain = 5.0 × 10⁷ ÷ 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 1.0 × 10¹¹ N m⁻².', wrong = '{"1": "1.0 × 10⁸ N m⁻² uses a strain of 0.5, from 1.5 ÷ 3.0 with the millimetres not converted to metres.", "2": "2.5 × 10¹⁰ N m⁻² is four times too small. It would need a strain of 2.0 × 10⁻³, four times the real strain of 5.0 × 10⁻⁴.", "3": "5.0 × 10⁷ N m⁻² is the stress. It must still be divided by the strain to get Young''s modulus."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0681' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Young''s modulus measures stiffness: how much stress is needed for a given strain. In the table of elastic constants, diamond has by far the largest value, about 1120 × 10⁹ N m⁻², because its carbon atoms are held by very strong bonds in a rigid network.', wrong = '{"0": "Steel is stiff, with Y about 200 × 10⁹ N m⁻², but diamond''s value is more than five times greater.", "2": "Aluminium has Y of about 70 × 10⁹ N m⁻², far below diamond.", "3": "Lead is a soft metal with one of the smallest Young''s moduli among common metals, about 16 × 10⁹ N m⁻²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0682' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Searle''s apparatus uses a reference wire and a test wire of the same material, length and diameter hung from the same support. A spirit level joins them, so anything that affects both wires equally, such as a temperature change or the support sagging, cancels out. Only the extra extension of the loaded test wire is measured.', wrong = '{"0": "Only the test wire receives the extra load. The reference wire carries a fixed weight that just keeps it straight.", "1": "A single wire can carry the load easily. The second wire is a reference for comparison, not for extra strength.", "3": "Both wires are of the same material, so the experiment gives Young''s modulus of one material only."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0683' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In Searle''s experiment, the stress F/A and the strain ΔL/L₀ are found for several loads and plotted against each other. Within the proportional region the graph is a straight line, and its slope, stress/strain, is the Young''s modulus of the wire.', wrong = '{"0": "The extension is measured with the micrometer screw and used to calculate strain. It is not the slope of the graph.", "1": "Elastic energy is given by the area under a force–extension graph, not by the slope of a stress–strain graph.", "2": "The breaking stress lies far beyond the straight-line region used in this experiment."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0684' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hooke''s law says that, up to the proportional limit, strain (the deformation) is directly proportional to stress (the load). On a stress–strain curve this is the straight-line part from the origin. Beyond the proportional limit the line starts to curve.', wrong = '{"1": "A larger stress produces a larger strain, not a smaller one, so the relation is direct, not inverse.", "2": "Stress and strain are linked: increasing the stress increases the strain in proportion.", "3": "Within the proportional limit the relation is linear. A square law would give a curved graph."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0685' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Below the yield point (elastic limit) a wire returns to its original length when the load is removed. Beyond it the material becomes plastic: its structure is permanently changed, so it stays longer than before and does not recover its original length.', wrong = '{"0": "A full return to the original length happens only when the stress stays below the elastic limit.", "2": "Going past the elastic limit does not break the wire at once. Fracture comes later, after the ultimate tensile strength is passed.", "3": "A wire stretched beyond its elastic limit is left permanently longer, not shorter."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0686' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The ultimate tensile strength is the maximum stress a material can withstand. It is the highest point of the stress–strain curve and is taken as the material''s nominal strength. After this point the material soon reaches its fracture stress and breaks.', wrong = '{"0": "The stress at which Hooke''s law stops working is the proportional limit, at the end of the straight-line part.", "1": "The stress at the yield point is the elastic limit, where plastic deformation starts. The UTS is higher.", "3": "The stress when loading first begins is near zero. The UTS is the peak stress."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0687' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A brittle substance breaks just after its elastic limit is reached, with little or no plastic stretching. Glass is the common example; high-carbon steel and metals such as beryllium, bismuth and chromium are also brittle.', wrong = '{"0": "Copper is ductile. It stretches plastically a long way before breaking, which is why it can be drawn into wires.", "1": "Lead is ductile and soft. It deforms plastically a great deal before it breaks.", "2": "Wrought iron is ductile. It undergoes plastic deformation until it breaks."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0688' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A ductile substance continues to deform plastically after its elastic limit, stretching a lot until it finally breaks. That is why ductile metals such as lead, copper and wrought iron can be drawn into wires.', wrong = '{"1": "Breaking just after the elastic limit is the mark of a brittle substance, such as glass.", "2": "Ductile materials do have an elastic region first. Their plastic region follows the elastic limit.", "3": "Ductile substances can be stretched a great deal. That is the meaning of ductility."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0689' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a spring obeying Hooke''s law, the stretching force rises steadily from 0 to F = kx, so the average force is ½F. Work = average force × extension = ½F × x = ½kx². This is also the area of the triangle under the force–extension graph.', wrong = '{"0": "kx² uses the final force kx for the whole stretch. The force starts at zero, so the average is half of it.", "2": "kx is the final stretching force from Hooke''s law. Work needs force multiplied by extension.", "3": "½kx has the units of force, not energy. It is the average force, not the work."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0690' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Elastic potential energy stored is U = ½kx², with x in metres. Here x = 5 cm = 0.05 m. U = ½ × 400 N m⁻¹ × (0.05 m)² = 200 × 0.0025 = 0.5 J. This equals the work done in stretching the spring, the area under its force–extension graph.', wrong = '{"1": "1.0 J is kx² = 400 × 0.0025, which leaves out the factor ½.", "2": "20 is kx = 400 × 0.05, the stretching force in newtons, not the energy.", "3": "5000 J is ½ × 400 × 5², which uses x = 5 instead of converting 5 cm to 0.05 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0691' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Work done = average force × distance moved, so the area under a force–extension graph equals the work done in stretching the material. Within the limit of proportionality this area is the triangle ½Fx, and the work is stored in the material as elastic potential energy.', wrong = '{"0": "Young''s modulus is the slope of a stress–strain graph, not an area under a force–extension graph.", "1": "The spring constant k is the slope (force ÷ extension) of this graph, not the area under it.", "3": "An area under force against extension has units of N × m = J, an energy. It is not a force."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0692' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Archimedes'' principle states that a body totally or partly immersed in a fluid feels an upward force, the upthrust, equal to the weight of the fluid it displaces. In symbols, upthrust = ρgV, where ρ is the fluid''s density and V is the volume of fluid displaced.', wrong = '{"0": "Upthrust equals the body''s own weight only when it floats. A sinking body''s weight is greater than its upthrust.", "1": "Upthrust is a force measured in newtons. The volume displaced helps decide it, but it is not a volume.", "2": "Upthrust comes from the difference between the pressures on the bottom and top faces. The pressure at the bottom alone is not the upthrust."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0693' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Liquid pressure P = ρgh increases with depth. The bottom face is deeper, so the upward force on it, ρgh₂A, is larger than the downward force on the top face, ρgh₁A. The difference ρgA(h₂ − h₁) = ρgV is the upthrust, the weight of liquid displaced.', wrong = '{"1": "The top face is shallower, so the pressure there is smaller. If it were larger, the net force would push the cylinder down.", "2": "Forces on the sides are horizontal and cancel each other in pairs. They give no upward push.", "3": "The cylinder''s mass does not change in the liquid. Only its apparent weight falls because of the upthrust."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0694' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Archimedes'' principle, upthrust = weight of water displaced = ρgV. A fully immersed block displaces its own volume, so upthrust = 1000 kg m⁻³ × 10 m s⁻² × 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ m³ = 2.0 N. The material of the block does not matter.', wrong = '{"0": "0.2 N is 1000 × 2.0 × 10⁻⁴, the mass of displaced water in kg, with g left out.", "2": "20 N is ten times too large, a slip in the power of ten in the volume.", "3": "2000 N would need a volume of 0.2 m³, a thousand times the block''s real volume."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0695' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An object floats when its weight is balanced by the upthrust. Upthrust equals the weight of fluid displaced, so a floating object displaces fluid whose weight equals its own weight. This is the principle of floatation.', wrong = '{"0": "A floating object is usually only partly submerged, so it displaces less than its own volume of fluid.", "2": "An upthrust does act on a floating object. It is what holds the object up against its weight.", "3": "If the weight were greater than the upthrust, the object would sink, not float."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0696' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A submarine has tanks that can be filled with or emptied of seawater. Filling them makes the submarine heavier while the upthrust on its hull stays the same, so once its weight is greater than the upthrust it dives. Emptying the tanks makes it lighter so it rises.', wrong = '{"0": "Emptying the tanks reduces the weight below the upthrust, which makes the submarine rise, not dive.", "1": "The hull''s volume is fixed. A bigger volume would increase the upthrust and make it harder to sink.", "3": "Switching off the engines stops forward motion but does not change weight or upthrust, so it does not make the submarine dive."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0697' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Upthrust = weight of air displaced = ρ_air gV = 1.25 × 10 × 8 = 100 N. The balloon itself, fabric and helium together, weighs 50 N. The largest extra load is the upthrust minus the balloon''s weight: 100 N − 50 N = 50 N.', wrong = '{"0": "10 is 1.25 × 8, the mass of displaced air in kilograms, with g left out and the balloon''s weight not subtracted.", "2": "100 N is the total upthrust. Part of it is needed to hold up the balloon''s own 50 N weight.", "3": "150 N adds the balloon''s weight to the upthrust. The balloon''s weight uses up upthrust, so it must be subtracted."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0698' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In streamline or laminar flow the motion is smooth and steady. Every particle that passes a given point follows exactly the same path as the particles that passed that point earlier. These paths are called streamlines, and two streamlines can never cross.', wrong = '{"0": "Eddies and whirlpools are features of turbulent flow, where the particles'' paths keep changing.", "1": "If streamlines crossed, a particle at the crossing would have two directions at once. In streamline flow they never cross.", "2": "Abrupt changes of velocity at a point happen in turbulent flow. In streamline flow the velocity at each point stays steady."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0699' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A fluid flows smoothly as long as its speed is low. Above a certain speed the motion becomes irregular and unsteady, with eddies and whirlpools, and the velocity at a point changes abruptly. This is turbulent flow.', wrong = '{"1": "A fluid at rest is not flowing at all, so it cannot have turbulent flow.", "2": "Very slow flow tends to be smooth and streamlined. Turbulence appears at higher speeds.", "3": "Zero viscosity describes an ideal fluid, which is assumed to flow smoothly. Turbulence is linked to high speed, not to the absence of viscosity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0700' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An ideal fluid is a simplified model with three conditions: it is non-viscous (no friction between its layers), incompressible (constant density), and its flow is steady. A high viscosity means a lot of internal friction, which is the opposite of the first condition, so it is not a property of an ideal fluid.', wrong = '{"0": "Being incompressible, so that its density stays constant, is one of the conditions for an ideal fluid.", "2": "Steady flow, where the velocity at each point does not change with time, is one of the conditions for an ideal fluid.", "3": "An ideal fluid is non-viscous, with no friction between adjacent layers. This is a property it does have."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0701' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rate of flow is the volume of fluid passing a section of the pipe each second, given by A × v. Here rate = 5 × 10⁻⁴ m² × 2 m s⁻¹ = 1 × 10⁻³ m³ s⁻¹. That is one litre every second.', wrong = '{"1": "1 × 10⁻⁴ m³ s⁻¹ is ten times too small, a slip in the power of ten when multiplying 5 × 10⁻⁴ by 2.", "2": "2.5 × 10⁻⁴ comes from dividing the area by the speed (5 × 10⁻⁴ ÷ 2). The rate of flow is area times speed.", "3": "4 × 10³ comes from dividing the speed by the area (2 ÷ 5 × 10⁻⁴). It does not have units of volume per second."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0702' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For an incompressible fluid in steady flow, the volume entering a pipe each second must equal the volume leaving it. Volume per second is area × speed, so A₁v₁ = A₂v₂. Where the pipe is narrow the fluid moves faster, and where it is wide the fluid moves slower.', wrong = '{"0": "A₁/v₁ = A₂/v₂ would mean speed rises with area. In reality the speed rises where the area falls, so it is the product Av that stays constant.", "1": "P × A is the force due to pressure on a cross-section. The continuity equation is about flow rate, not force.", "3": "The speeds are equal only if the areas are equal. In a pipe of changing width the speed changes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0703' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In time Δt, a mass ρA₁v₁Δt flows in through A₁ and a mass ρA₂v₂Δt flows out through A₂. In steady flow no fluid piles up or disappears inside the pipe, so these masses are equal. The continuity equation therefore expresses the conservation of mass.', wrong = '{"0": "Conservation of energy leads to Bernoulli''s equation, which links pressure, speed and height.", "1": "The continuity equation balances the mass of fluid flowing in and out, not momentum.", "2": "Conservation of charge applies to electric currents in circuits, not to the flow of a fluid."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0704' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By continuity, A₁v₁ = A₂v₂. The area of a circle is proportional to the square of its diameter, so A₁/A₂ = (4/2)² = 4. Then v₂ = v₁ × A₁/A₂ = 1.5 m s⁻¹ × 4 = 6 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "0.375 m s⁻¹ is 1.5 ÷ 4, which makes the water slower in the narrow part. In fact it speeds up where the pipe narrows.", "1": "3 m s⁻¹ uses the ratio of diameters (2) instead of areas. Halving the diameter makes the area four times smaller.", "3": "12 m s⁻¹ multiplies by 8, the cube of the diameter ratio. The area depends on the square of the diameter."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0705' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The continuity equation says Av stays constant along the flow. Squeezing the end of the hose reduces the outlet area A, so the speed v must increase to keep the same volume of water flowing each second. The water leaves faster and travels further.', wrong = '{"1": "The thumb only narrows the outlet. The faster jet comes from the smaller area, as the continuity equation requires.", "2": "Squeezing the hose does not change the supply pressure from the tap. The change happens at the outlet.", "3": "Water is almost incompressible, so its density stays the same when the hose is squeezed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0706' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Mass flowing per second = 12 kg ÷ 30 s = 0.4 kg s⁻¹. This equals ρAv, so v = 0.4 ÷ (1000 × 2.0 × 10⁻⁴) = 0.4 ÷ 0.2 = 2.0 m s⁻¹. Here ρAv is the mass of water leaving the outlet each second.', wrong = '{"0": "0.4 is the mass flow rate in kg s⁻¹. It must be divided by ρA to give the speed.", "2": "4.0 × 10⁻⁴ is the volume flow rate, 0.4 ÷ 1000 in m³ s⁻¹. It still has to be divided by the area.", "3": "20 m s⁻¹ is ten times too large, a slip in the power of ten when dividing by 0.2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0707' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Bernoulli''s equation says that for an ideal fluid in steady flow, the sum of the pressure, the kinetic energy per unit volume and the potential energy per unit volume stays constant along a streamline: P + ½ρv² + ρgh = constant.', wrong = '{"0": "The terms are energies per unit volume. The kinetic term is ½ρv², not ρv, and the height term needs g, giving ρgh.", "2": "Av = constant is the equation of continuity, which comes from conservation of mass.", "3": "Pressure is not the same everywhere. It changes as the speed and height of the fluid change."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0708' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In deriving Bernoulli''s equation, the net work done on a portion of fluid by the pressure forces is set equal to the change in its kinetic energy plus the change in its gravitational potential energy. This is the principle of conservation of energy applied to a moving fluid.', wrong = '{"0": "Conservation of mass gives the equation of continuity, A₁v₁ = A₂v₂, not Bernoulli''s equation.", "1": "The derivation uses work and energy changes, not changes in momentum.", "3": "Archimedes'' principle describes the upthrust on an immersed body. It is not used to derive Bernoulli''s equation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0709' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a horizontal pipe the height term ρgh is the same everywhere, so Bernoulli''s equation becomes P + ½ρv² = constant. Where the speed v is high, ½ρv² is large, so the pressure P must be low.', wrong = '{"0": "If pressure rose with speed, P + ½ρv² could not stay constant. Faster flow means lower pressure.", "1": "Bernoulli''s equation links speed and pressure, so a change in speed changes the pressure.", "2": "The pressure falls where the speed is high, but it does not drop to zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0710' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An aeroplane wing is shaped so that the streamlines crowd together above it, which means the air moves faster over the top than underneath. By Bernoulli''s principle, faster air has lower pressure. The higher pressure below pushes the wing upward, giving lift.', wrong = '{"1": "This has the flow reversed. The air moves faster over the top, so the pressure is lower there, not higher.", "2": "A wing is far denser than air, so it cannot float like a balloon. Lift comes from a pressure difference.", "3": "The engine provides forward thrust. The upward lift comes from the pressure difference across the wing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0711' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A spinning ball drags air round with it, so the air moves faster on one side than on the other. By Bernoulli''s principle the faster side has lower pressure, and the pressure difference gives a sideways force. This force curves the ball''s path and is called the Magnus effect.', wrong = '{"0": "The Doppler effect is the change in the observed frequency of a wave when the source or observer moves.", "2": "The Venturi effect is the drop in pressure when a fluid speeds up through a narrow part of a pipe.", "3": "Torricelli''s theorem gives the speed of a liquid flowing out of a hole in a tank, √(2gh)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0712' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Squeezing the bulb blows air quickly through a narrow opening across the top of a tube dipped in the liquid. By Bernoulli''s principle the fast air has low pressure, so the higher atmospheric pressure on the liquid surface pushes the liquid up the tube. The air stream then breaks it into a fine spray.', wrong = '{"0": "The bulb blows air across the top of the tube; it does not pump the liquid directly. The liquid rises because of the pressure difference.", "1": "The liquid rises up the tube as a liquid and is broken into droplets. It is not evaporating.", "3": "The liquid is pushed up the tube by atmospheric pressure, not pushed down and out by the air."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0713' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a horizontal pipe Bernoulli''s equation gives P₁ − P₂ = ½ρ(v₂² − v₁²). When the speed in the wide part is much smaller, it can be neglected, giving the Venturi relation P₁ − P₂ ≈ ½ρv₂². Here P₁ − P₂ = ½ × 1000 × 4² = ½ × 1000 × 16 = 8 × 10³ Pa.', wrong = '{"0": "1.6 × 10⁴ Pa is ρv₂² = 1000 × 16, which leaves out the ½.", "1": "2 × 10³ Pa is ½ × 1000 × 4, which uses v instead of v².", "2": "4 × 10³ Pa is ρv = 1000 × 4. The relation needs ½ρv², and v must be squared."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0714' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Torricelli''s theorem says the speed of efflux from a hole a depth h below the surface equals the speed of a body falling freely through h: v = √(2gh). Here v = √(2 × 10 m s⁻² × 5 m) = √100 = 10 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "5 m s⁻¹ is √(gh/2) = √25. The correct formula has 2gh under the square root.", "2": "50 is gh = 10 × 5, with no factor 2 and no square root.", "3": "100 is 2gh, which equals v² in m² s⁻². Its square root, 10 m s⁻¹, is the speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0715' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An object moving through a liquid or gas feels a retarding force, called drag, because of the fluid''s viscosity. The drag force increases as the speed of the object increases. That is why a hand held out of a fast car''s window is pushed back much more strongly than in a slow one.', wrong = '{"0": "Faster motion produces more drag, not less. That is why a fast car meets much more air resistance than a slow one.", "1": "Drag depends on speed. For a small sphere moving slowly, Stokes'' law even makes it directly proportional to v.", "2": "Drag is a retarding force, so it acts opposite to the direction of motion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0716' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Stokes'' law gives the drag force on a small sphere of radius r moving slowly at speed v through a fluid of viscosity η as F = 6πηrv. The force is directly proportional to η, r and v. At high speeds the drag is no longer simply proportional to v.', wrong = '{"1": "The drag in Stokes'' law is proportional to r, not r². It is the sphere''s weight that depends on r³.", "2": "Dividing by r would make bigger spheres feel less drag. In Stokes'' law a bigger sphere feels more.", "3": "½ρv²A is a form of drag used for fast motion, where force grows with v². Stokes'' law for slow motion is 6πηrv."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0717' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'As a raindrop falls faster, the upward drag force on it grows. Eventually the drag becomes equal to the drop''s weight, so the net force is zero. From then on the drop does not accelerate and falls at a constant speed, its terminal velocity.', wrong = '{"0": "The drop''s weight stays the same while it falls. It is the drag force that grows until it balances the weight.", "2": "The drag is largest at terminal velocity, where it balances the weight. Zero drag would mean free fall.", "3": "At terminal velocity the net force is zero, so the acceleration is zero, not g. The acceleration is g only at the very start."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0718' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The terminal velocity formula v_t = 2gr²ρ/9η shows that v_t is proportional to r². Doubling the radius multiplies r² by 2² = 4, so the terminal velocity becomes four times as large. This is why big raindrops fall faster than fine drizzle.', wrong = '{"0": "This treats v_t as proportional to r. The formula has r², so doubling r gives four times the speed.", "1": "Larger drops fall faster, not slower. Their weight grows as r³ while the drag grows only as r.", "3": "Eight times would need v_t ∝ r³. The weight grows as r³, but the drag grows as r, so v_t goes as r²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0719' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'v_t = 2gr²ρ/9η. With r² = (2.0 × 10⁻⁵)² = 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m², the top line is 2 × 9.8 × 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ × 1000 = 7.84 × 10⁻⁶ and the bottom line is 9 × 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴. So v_t = 7.84 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ ≈ 0.044 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "0.0044 m s⁻¹ is ten times too small, a slip in the powers of ten.", "1": "0.022 m s⁻¹ is half the correct value. It drops the factor 2 in 2gr²ρ.", "3": "4.4 m s⁻¹ is 100 times too large. A droplet this tiny drifts down slowly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0720' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An ideal fluid would have no viscosity and could not be compressed. No such fluid exists in practice: every real fluid, such as water, petrol, kerosene and honey, has some viscosity. So ideal fluids are only a useful model, and real fluids are viscous.', wrong = '{"0": "Water has a small but real viscosity, so it is a real fluid, not an ideal one.", "1": "Honey flows much more slowly than water because it is far more viscous.", "2": "Real fluids all possess viscosity. Zero viscosity is the idealised case."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0721' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A superfluid has zero viscosity, so it flows without friction and without losing kinetic energy. Superfluid helium-4, formed a little below 4 K, can pass through extremely narrow gaps and can even creep up the walls of its container and over the edge.', wrong = '{"1": "A superfluid has zero viscosity, not infinite viscosity. It stays a liquid and flows freely.", "2": "Superfluids flow through incredibly narrow spaces where ordinary liquids cannot pass.", "3": "With no viscosity to slow it, a superfluid keeps flowing once it starts moving. Stirring does not stop it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0722' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Superfluids have few practical uses so far. Superfluid helium-4 is used as a coolant for high-field magnets, which must be kept extremely cold. Helium-3 and helium-4 are also used in advanced particle detectors.', wrong = '{"0": "Helium is chemically inert and does not burn, so it cannot be a rocket fuel.", "2": "Glass is made mainly from silica (sand). Helium plays no part in making it.", "3": "Superfluid helium exists only a few kelvin above absolute zero. A hot car engine would boil it away at once."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0723' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The kinetic theory assumes that gas molecules collide with each other and with the container walls perfectly elastically, so no kinetic energy is lost overall. It also assumes constant random motion, negligible molecular volume and no forces between molecules except during collisions.', wrong = '{"0": "The theory assumes there are no attractive or repulsive forces between molecules, except during collisions.", "1": "The volume of the molecules is assumed to be negligible compared with the volume of the gas, so they act like points.", "2": "Gas molecules are always in constant, random motion. They are not at rest when the gas is cool."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0724' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gas molecules constantly strike the walls of their container and bounce back. Each collision changes a molecule''s momentum, so it exerts a small force on the wall. The total force per unit area from all these collisions is the gas pressure.', wrong = '{"1": "Gas pressure acts equally on all the walls, including the top and sides. The weight of the gas would only press on the bottom.", "2": "The kinetic theory assumes no attraction between molecules and the walls. Pressure comes from collisions.", "3": "The volume of the molecules is assumed to be negligible, so it cannot be the cause of pressure."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0725' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'According to the kinetic theory, the average kinetic energy of gas molecules is directly proportional to the absolute (kelvin) temperature: ⟨KE⟩ = (3/2)k_BT. Doubling the kelvin temperature doubles the average kinetic energy.', wrong = '{"1": "Volume does not set the average molecular kinetic energy. Gases at the same temperature have the same average KE per molecule whatever their volume.", "2": "The proportionality is with kelvin temperature. On the Celsius scale, 0 °C does not mean zero kinetic energy.", "3": "The average KE per molecule does not depend on how many molecules there are. The total KE does."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0726' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A real gas behaves like an ideal gas when its density is low. At low pressure and high temperature, the molecules are far apart, so the forces between them and their own volume become negligible. Real gases deviate most at high pressure and low temperature.', wrong = '{"0": "High pressure and low temperature are the conditions where real gases deviate most, because intermolecular forces and molecular volume become important.", "1": "High pressure squeezes the molecules close together, so their volume and attractions matter. A high temperature alone does not fix this.", "2": "At low temperature molecules move slowly, and their mutual attraction becomes important, making the gas less ideal."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0727' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In PV = nRT, n is the number of moles of the gas, R is the universal gas constant (about 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹), and T is the absolute temperature. The number of moles can be found from n = m/M, the mass divided by the molar mass.', wrong = '{"0": "The number of molecules is N, used in the form PV = Nk_BT. In PV = nRT the quantity n counts moles.", "1": "Density is mass per unit volume, ρ = PM/RT. It is not a symbol in PV = nRT.", "2": "Molar mass is M, which links mass to moles through n = m/M. It is not n itself."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0728' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Boltzmann constant is the gas constant for a single molecule. Dividing the gas constant R (per mole) by Avogadro''s number N_A (molecules per mole) gives k_B = R/N_A = 8.31 ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ ≈ 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "R × N_A would be about 5 × 10²⁴, a huge number. k_B is a tiny constant because it applies to a single molecule.", "2": "N_A/R is the inverse of the correct relation and has the wrong units.", "3": "R/T changes with temperature, so it cannot be a constant like k_B."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0729' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The number of moles is n = N/N_A, and the gas constant is R = N_A k_B. Substituting into PV = nRT gives PV = (N/N_A)(N_A k_B)T = Nk_BT. This is the ideal gas equation written for N molecules.', wrong = '{"0": "R goes with the number of moles. When N counts molecules, the matching constant is k_B.", "2": "When n counts moles, the matching constant is R. Mixing n with k_B gives a value N_A times too small.", "3": "Volume multiplies the pressure on the left side. P = Nk_BTV would make pressure grow with volume, which is wrong."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0730' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From PV = nRT, P = nRT/V. With n = 2 mol, R = 8.3 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, T = 300 K and V = 0.050 m³: nRT = 2 × 8.3 × 300 = 4980 J, so P = 4980 ÷ 0.050 ≈ 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa, close to atmospheric pressure.', wrong = '{"1": "2.0 × 10⁵ Pa is double the correct value, as if there were four moles of gas.", "2": "5.0 × 10⁴ Pa is half the correct value. It uses one mole instead of two.", "3": "5.0 × 10³ Pa is nRT = 4980 divided by 1 m³ instead of by 0.050 m³."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0731' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Boyle''s law states that for a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature, the pressure is inversely proportional to the volume: P ∝ 1/V. So the product PV is constant, and P₁V₁ = P₂V₂.', wrong = '{"0": "V/T = constant is Charles'' law, which holds at constant pressure.", "2": "P/T = constant is the pressure law (Gay-Lussac''s law), which holds at constant volume.", "3": "Pressure and volume are inversely proportional, so their product is constant, not their sum."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0732' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At constant temperature, Boyle''s law gives P₁V₁ = P₂V₂. So 100 kPa × 6 L = P₂ × 2 L, which gives P₂ = 600 ÷ 2 = 300 kPa. Squeezing the gas to a third of its volume triples its pressure.', wrong = '{"0": "33 kPa is 100 × 2 ÷ 6, which makes the pressure fall. Compressing a gas at constant temperature raises its pressure.", "1": "200 kPa is 100 × 2, which multiplies by the new volume. The correct step is P₂ = P₁V₁/V₂ = 600 ÷ 2.", "3": "600 is P₁V₁ in kPa L. It still has to be divided by the new volume of 2 L."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0733' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Charles'' law says V ∝ T at constant pressure, with T in kelvin. T₁ = 27 + 273 = 300 K and T₂ = 127 + 273 = 400 K. So V₂/V₁ = 400/300 = 4/3, and the volume increases by a factor of 4/3.', wrong = '{"0": "Doubling the volume would need the kelvin temperature to double, from 300 K to 600 K.", "1": "3/4 is the ratio turned upside down. Heating at constant pressure makes the gas expand, so the factor is more than 1.", "3": "127/27 uses Celsius temperatures. Charles'' law works only with absolute (kelvin) temperatures."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0734' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a sealed rigid container the volume is constant, so the pressure law gives P ∝ T (in kelvin). P₂ = P₁ × T₂/T₁ = 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa × 450/300 = 2.0 × 10⁵ × 1.5 = 3.0 × 10⁵ Pa.', wrong = '{"0": "1.3 × 10⁵ Pa uses 300/450, the ratio upside down. Heating a gas at constant volume raises its pressure.", "1": "At constant volume the pressure rises with temperature, so it cannot stay at 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa.", "3": "4.5 × 10⁵ Pa is a rise by a factor of 2.25. The temperature ratio 450/300 allows a factor of only 1.5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0735' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Charles'' law says V is directly proportional to T at constant pressure, when T is in kelvin. A direct proportion gives a straight line through the origin, so on a V–T graph the line passes through 0 K. On a Celsius scale the same line meets the axis at −273 °C.', wrong = '{"0": "A hyperbola is the shape of a P–V graph at constant temperature (Boyle''s law), not a V–T graph.", "1": "A horizontal line would mean the volume does not change. At constant pressure, volume rises with temperature.", "3": "The line meets zero volume at 0 K, which is −273 °C. 273 K is 0 °C."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0736' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Heating the gas raises the average kinetic energy of its molecules, so they move faster. Faster molecules hit the walls more often, and each collision exerts a greater force. With the volume fixed, this gives a greater force per unit area, so the pressure rises.', wrong = '{"0": "Molecules do not grow on heating. They only move faster.", "1": "In a sealed container the number of molecules stays fixed. Heating does not create new ones.", "2": "The kinetic theory assumes no attraction between molecules and the walls. Pressure comes from collisions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0737' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Heat flows from a hotter body to a colder one. When two bodies in contact reach the same temperature, there is no longer any driving force for heat transfer, so the net heat flow is zero. They are then in thermal equilibrium, like a spoon left in coffee once both reach the same temperature.', wrong = '{"1": "Bodies do not contain heat; heat is energy in transfer. Equilibrium depends on equal temperature, not on equal energy content.", "2": "Mass does not decide the direction of heat flow. A small hot body can heat a large cold one.", "3": "If one body is hotter, heat flows from it to the colder one, so they are not yet in equilibrium."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0738' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Internal energy is the total of all the molecular energies in a substance. It includes the kinetic energy of the molecules'' random motion and the potential energy due to the forces between them.', wrong = '{"0": "The kinetic energy of the whole body moving is ordinary mechanical energy. Internal energy is about the random energies of the molecules inside.", "2": "The kinetic energy of the molecules is also included. For an ideal gas it is in fact the whole of the internal energy.", "3": "Heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference. Internal energy is the energy stored within the substance."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0739' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Molecules of an ideal gas exert no forces on one another, so they have no intermolecular potential energy. Their internal energy is just the kinetic energy of their random motion, and the average kinetic energy is proportional to the absolute temperature. So U depends only on temperature.', wrong = '{"0": "With no forces between the molecules, spreading them into a larger volume does not change their energy.", "1": "Pressure can change while the temperature stays fixed, as in Boyle''s law, without any change in U.", "3": "The shape of the container does not affect the molecules'' kinetic energy, so it does not affect U."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0740' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A function of state depends only on the condition of the system (its pressure, volume and temperature), not on how it got there. So the change in internal energy ΔU between two states is the same whatever path is taken. In this way it is like gravitational potential energy, where only the change matters.', wrong = '{"0": "Heat and work depend on how a change is carried out. Internal energy does not, which is why it is a function of state.", "1": "A gas always has internal energy from the random motion of its molecules. It is not zero.", "2": "Internal energy changes in many processes, for example when a gas is heated. Being a state function only means the change does not depend on the path."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0741' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The kinetic theory gives the average translational kinetic energy of one molecule as ⟨KE⟩ = (3/2)k_BT. At 300 K: ⟨KE⟩ = 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹ × 300 K ≈ 6.2 × 10⁻²¹ J.', wrong = '{"0": "1.2 × 10⁻²⁰ J is 3k_BT, twice the correct value. The factor is 3/2, not 3.", "1": "3.7 × 10³ J is (3/2)RT, which uses R. That is the energy of a whole mole, not of one molecule.", "2": "4.1 × 10⁻²¹ J is k_BT alone. The factor 3/2 is missing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0742' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The sign convention comes from the steam engine, where heat is put in and work is taken out. So heat added to the system and work done by the system on its surroundings are both counted as positive. Work done on the system by the surroundings is negative.', wrong = '{"1": "Negative work is the work done on the system by its surroundings, for example when a gas is compressed.", "2": "A system that expands against its surroundings does real work, so it is not zero.", "3": "The work equals the heat supplied only in special cases, such as an isothermal change of an ideal gas. It is not a sign rule."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0743' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At constant pressure, the work done by an expanding gas is W = PΔV. The change in volume is ΔV = 3.0 × 10⁻³ − 1.0 × 10⁻³ = 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³. So W = 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa × 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³ = 400 J.', wrong = '{"0": "200 J uses ΔV = 1.0 × 10⁻³ m³, the starting volume. The change in volume is 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³.", "2": "600 J uses the final volume, 3.0 × 10⁻³ m³, instead of the change in volume.", "3": "800 J adds the two volumes, (1.0 + 3.0) × 10⁻³, instead of subtracting them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0744' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a small expansion, the work done by a gas is PΔV, which is the area of a thin strip under the P–V curve. Adding all such strips gives the total work as the area under the curve between the initial and final volumes.', wrong = '{"0": "The slope of a P–V graph is ΔP/ΔV, which has units of Pa m⁻³, not joules. It is not the work.", "2": "PV at one point does not measure the work. Work depends on how the pressure varied while the volume changed.", "3": "The length of the curve has no physical meaning here. Work is given by the area under the curve."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0745' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first law of thermodynamics is conservation of energy. Heat Q added to a system is used partly to raise its internal energy by ΔU and partly to do work W on its surroundings. So Q = ΔU + W, which can also be written ΔU = Q − W.', wrong = '{"0": "Q = ΔU − W would mean doing work gives the system extra heat. With W as work done by the system, the correct form is Q = ΔU + W.", "1": "W = Q + ΔU would let the system do more work than the heat supplied while also gaining internal energy. The heat is shared between ΔU and W.", "3": "Energies add and subtract. Multiplying heat by work gives a quantity with the wrong units."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0746' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By the first law, ΔU = Q − W, with Q the heat added to the gas and W the work done by the gas. Here ΔU = 500 J − 200 J = +300 J, so the internal energy increases by 300 J.', wrong = '{"0": "+700 J adds the work instead of subtracting it. Work done by the gas uses up part of the heat supplied.", "1": "−300 J has the wrong sign. More heat is added (500 J) than the work done (200 J), so the internal energy rises.", "2": "200 J is the work done by the gas, not the change in its internal energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0747' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Pushing the piston in quickly does work on the trapped air. The process is so fast that hardly any heat escapes, so by the first law the work done on the air goes into its internal energy. A rise in internal energy shows up as a rise in temperature.', wrong = '{"1": "The push is quick and the air ends up hotter than its surroundings, so heat does not flow in from outside. The energy comes from the work done.", "2": "The outlet is blocked, so the mass of trapped air stays the same.", "3": "Friction produces heat; it does not cool. In any case the main cause of heating is the work done on the gas."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0748' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Applying the first law, ΔU = Q − W, to the human body: when the body does work, such as walking or lifting, its internal energy decreases. This energy is replaced by the food we eat, through the energy-releasing processes of metabolism. The body never creates energy.', wrong = '{"0": "Energy cannot be created. The body only converts the chemical energy of food into work and heat.", "2": "Doing work uses up internal energy, so it decreases. Eating food is what restores it.", "3": "Metabolism is an example of the first law in action. Energy taken in as food balances the work done and heat lost."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0749' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an isothermal process the temperature stays constant. The internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on temperature, so ΔU = 0. The first law, Q = ΔU + W, then gives Q = W: all the heat absorbed is used to do work during the expansion.', wrong = '{"0": "Q = 0 describes an adiabatic process. In an isothermal expansion heat must flow in to keep the temperature steady.", "1": "An expanding gas pushes on its surroundings and does work, so W is not zero. The temperature stays the same.", "3": "Isothermal means at constant temperature, so the temperature cannot rise."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0750' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an isothermal expansion the gas does work and needs heat to flow in to replace the energy; in a compression heat must flow out. Heat transfer takes time, so the change has to be carried out slowly to keep the temperature constant.', wrong = '{"0": "Ordinary compressions or expansions do not break gas molecules. Speed matters only because heat needs time to flow.", "1": "In an isothermal process the internal energy of an ideal gas stays constant. The slow pace is to keep the temperature fixed.", "2": "Boyle''s law holds for any gas kept at constant temperature. It is not about sound waves; in fact sound waves cause rapid, adiabatic changes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0751' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an adiabatic process no heat enters or leaves, so Q = 0 and the first law gives W = −ΔU. When the gas expands it does work on its surroundings, and the energy for this comes from its own internal energy. So the internal energy decreases and the temperature falls.', wrong = '{"1": "Constant temperature describes an isothermal process. In an adiabatic expansion the gas cools.", "2": "No heat is exchanged in an adiabatic process. That is what adiabatic means.", "3": "The gas uses its internal energy to do work, so the internal energy goes down, not up."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0752' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An adiabatic change happens when a gas expands or is compressed so quickly that there is no time for heat to flow in or out. Air rushing out of a burst tyre expands suddenly and cools. Other examples are the rapid compressions and expansions of air as a sound wave passes, and cloud formation.', wrong = '{"0": "Ice melting slowly in a glass takes in heat steadily from its surroundings, so it is not adiabatic.", "2": "A slow compression in a water bath lets heat flow out to the water, keeping the temperature steady. That is close to isothermal.", "3": "Water boiling in an open pan is heated continuously by the stove, so heat is being transferred all the time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0753' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an adiabatic change the temperature does not stay constant, so Boyle''s law does not apply. Instead an ideal gas follows PVᵞ = constant, where γ = C_p/C_v is the ratio of the molar specific heat at constant pressure to that at constant volume.', wrong = '{"0": "PV = constant is Boyle''s law, which holds only when the temperature is constant (isothermal).", "1": "P/V = constant would mean pressure rises as volume rises. It does not describe an adiabatic change.", "3": "V/T = constant is Charles'' law, which holds at constant pressure."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0754' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an isothermal compression only the decrease in volume raises the pressure. In an adiabatic compression the work done also heats the gas, and the higher temperature pushes the pressure up further. So pressure changes faster with volume, and the adiabat (PVᵞ = constant with γ > 1) is steeper than the isotherm.', wrong = '{"0": "PV is constant on an isotherm. On an adiabat PVᵞ is constant.", "1": "No heat is exchanged in an adiabatic change. The temperature rises because of the work done on the gas.", "2": "γ = C_p/C_v is always greater than 1, since C_p is larger than C_v. That is why the adiabat is steeper."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0755' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A reversible process can be run backwards through exactly the same stages, with every heat and work effect reversed, so that the system and its surroundings return to their original state with no change left behind. Slow evaporation and condensation are practically reversible.', wrong = '{"1": "Sudden changes, such as explosions, are irreversible. A reversible process must be carried out slowly.", "2": "Friction dissipates energy as heat, which cannot be fully recovered. Processes with friction are irreversible.", "3": "A process that can only go one way is irreversible, the opposite of the definition."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0756' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Any change that happens suddenly, or involves friction or loss of energy by conduction, convection or radiation, cannot be retraced and is irreversible. An explosion is a highly irreversible process: the burnt gases and released heat cannot be turned back into the original explosive.', wrong = '{"0": "Evaporation and condensation carried out slowly can be run backwards through the same stages, so they are practically reversible.", "2": "A gas compressed very slowly can be expanded again by slowly reducing the pressure, so this is nearly reversible.", "3": "Slow liquefaction can be reversed by slow evaporation, so it is practically reversible."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0757' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A heat engine works in a cycle. It absorbs heat Q₁ from a high-temperature reservoir, converts part of it into mechanical work W, and rejects the remaining heat Q₂ to a low-temperature reservoir. Since the working substance returns to its starting state each cycle, W = Q₁ − Q₂.', wrong = '{"0": "Converting all the heat into work in a cycle is forbidden by the second law. Some heat must always be rejected to a colder reservoir.", "1": "Moving heat from a cold body to a hot body, using work, is what a refrigerator does.", "3": "A heat engine turns heat into work, not work into heat. Energy also cannot be created."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0758' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kelvin''s statement of the second law says no process can take heat from a single reservoir and convert it entirely into work in a cycle without leaving some other change. To get work from heat, an engine needs two reservoirs at different temperatures, a hot source and a colder sink.', wrong = '{"0": "Work can be turned completely into heat, for example when friction stops a sliding block.", "1": "Heat flows naturally from a hot body to a cold one. The second law does not forbid this.", "2": "Heat engines are possible and common. They work because they use both a hot source and a cold sink."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0759' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The ocean holds a huge amount of heat, but by Kelvin''s statement of the second law, heat from a single reservoir cannot be turned entirely into work. An engine also needs a colder reservoir to reject heat into. Nothing on board is colder than the ocean and the air, so the ship cannot run on that heat alone.', wrong = '{"1": "The ocean contains an enormous amount of heat energy. The problem is that there is no colder sink.", "2": "The weight of seawater has nothing to do with converting heat into work.", "3": "The first law only requires energy to be conserved, which this would not break. It is the second law that forbids it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0760' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Real engines convert only part of the heat from burning fuel into work, because the second law requires some heat to be rejected. A petrol engine converts roughly 25% of the available heat into work, and a diesel engine about 35 to 40%. The rest is lost to the atmosphere.', wrong = '{"0": "About 75% is much higher than any real petrol engine achieves. Roughly 75% of the heat is in fact wasted.", "2": "The second law forbids any engine from converting 100% of its heat into work.", "3": "About 5% is far too low. Petrol engines convert roughly a quarter of the heat into work."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0761' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Carnot cycle has four reversible steps: an isothermal expansion at the hot temperature T₁, taking in heat Q₁; an adiabatic expansion that cools the gas to T₂; an isothermal compression at T₂, rejecting heat Q₂; and an adiabatic compression back to T₁. So it has two isothermal and two adiabatic steps.', wrong = '{"0": "With only isothermal steps the gas could never move between the two temperatures. Adiabatic steps are needed for that.", "1": "With only adiabatic steps no heat would be taken in or given out. The isothermal steps exchange heat with the reservoirs.", "3": "Constant-pressure (isobaric) and constant-volume (isochoric) steps are not part of the Carnot cycle."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0762' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Efficiency is η = W/Q₁ = (Q₁ − Q₂)/Q₁ = 1 − Q₂/Q₁. For a Carnot engine the heats are proportional to the kelvin temperatures, Q₂/Q₁ = T₂/T₁. So η = 1 − T₂/T₁.', wrong = '{"0": "Since T₁ is greater than T₂, T₁/T₂ is more than 1, so 1 − T₁/T₂ would be negative. The ratio must be T₂/T₁.", "1": "T₂/T₁ = Q₂/Q₁ is the fraction of heat rejected to the cold reservoir, not the fraction turned into work.", "2": "(T₁ + T₂)/T₁ is always more than 1, which would mean an efficiency above 100%."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0763' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Carnot formula needs kelvin temperatures: T₁ = 527 + 273 = 800 K and T₂ = 127 + 273 = 400 K. So η = 1 − T₂/T₁ = 1 − 400/800 = 0.5, an efficiency of 50%.', wrong = '{"0": "33% divides the temperature difference, 400 K, by T₁ + T₂ = 1200 K. It should be divided by T₁ = 800 K.", "2": "76% uses Celsius temperatures, 1 − 127/527. The formula only works with kelvin temperatures.", "3": "100% efficiency would need the cold reservoir to be at absolute zero, 0 K."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0764' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Carnot efficiency is η = 1 − T₂/T₁. It equals 1, or 100%, only if T₂/T₁ = 0, that is, if the cold reservoir is at absolute zero (0 K). Such a reservoir is not available, so every real engine''s efficiency is less than 100%.', wrong = '{"1": "The hot reservoir must be hotter than the cold one. If it were at absolute zero, no heat could flow from it to run the engine.", "2": "Carnot efficiency depends only on the two reservoir temperatures, not on the working substance.", "3": "Running fast does not change the temperatures, so it cannot raise the Carnot limit. Real fast engines also lose more to friction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0765' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Carnot''s theorem states that no heat engine can be more efficient than a reversible Carnot engine working between the same two temperatures. It also follows that all Carnot engines between the same temperatures have the same efficiency, whatever their working substance.', wrong = '{"0": "Real engines lose energy to friction and heat leaks, so they are always less efficient than a Carnot engine.", "2": "Carnot efficiency, 1 − T₂/T₁, depends only on the temperatures. The working substance does not matter.", "3": "A Carnot engine''s efficiency is 1 − T₂/T₁, which is always less than 100% because T₂ cannot be 0 K."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0766' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Efficiency is η = 1 − T₂/T₁. When the cold reservoir T₂ is fixed near room temperature, the only way to make T₂/T₁ smaller is to raise the hot reservoir temperature T₁. A larger temperature difference gives a higher efficiency.', wrong = '{"0": "Lowering T₁ makes T₂/T₁ larger and the temperature difference smaller, so the efficiency falls.", "1": "The ideal efficiency depends only on the two temperatures, not on the working substance.", "3": "The engine''s mass does not appear in η = 1 − T₂/T₁, so it does not raise the efficiency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0767' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A refrigerator is a heat engine run in reverse. Work W is done on it, usually by an electric compressor, to draw heat Q₂ out of a cold reservoir and reject a larger amount Q₁ = Q₂ + W to a hot reservoir, such as the kitchen air.', wrong = '{"0": "Energy cannot be destroyed. A refrigerator moves heat from inside to outside; it does not make it vanish.", "1": "A refrigerator uses work to move heat. It does not produce work from heat as an ordinary heat engine does.", "2": "Heat does not flow from cold to hot by itself. The refrigerator needs work done on it to make this happen."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0768' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The coefficient of performance of a refrigerator is the heat removed from the cold space divided by the work done on it: COP = Q₂/W. Here COP = 600 J ÷ 150 J = 4. A larger COP means more cooling for the same work.', wrong = '{"0": "0.25 is W/Q₂ = 150/600, the ratio turned upside down.", "2": "5 uses Q₁ = 600 + 150 = 750 J, the heat rejected to the room. The COP uses the heat removed, Q₂.", "3": "450 J is Q₂ − W, a difference of energies. The COP is a ratio with no unit."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0769' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For an ideal refrigerator, COP = Q₂/W = T₂/(T₁ − T₂), with temperatures in kelvin, where T₂ is the cold space and T₁ the room. Here COP = 260/(299 − 260) = 260/39 ≈ 6.7.', wrong = '{"0": "0.13 is (T₁ − T₂)/T₁ = 39/299, the efficiency of a Carnot engine between these temperatures, not the refrigerator''s COP.", "1": "0.87 is T₂/T₁ = 260/299. The COP divides T₂ by the temperature difference, not by T₁.", "3": "7.7 is T₁/(T₁ − T₂) = 299/39, which uses the hot temperature on top instead of the cold one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0770' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a system absorbs heat ΔQ reversibly at absolute temperature T, its entropy increases by ΔS = ΔQ/T. The unit of entropy is J K⁻¹. The concept was introduced by Rudolf Clausius to give the second law a quantitative form.', wrong = '{"1": "Entropy change is heat divided by temperature. ΔQ × T would have units J K, not J K⁻¹.", "2": "T/ΔQ is the ratio turned upside down and has the wrong units.", "3": "Heat (J) and temperature (K) are different quantities, so they cannot be subtracted."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0771' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The hot reservoir loses heat Q, so its entropy falls by Q/T₁. The cold reservoir gains Q, so its entropy rises by Q/T₂. Since T₂ < T₁, Q/T₂ is larger than Q/T₁, so the gain is bigger than the loss and the total entropy increases.', wrong = '{"0": "The hot reservoir does lose entropy, but the cold reservoir gains more, since the same Q is divided by a smaller temperature.", "2": "The total entropy stays the same only in a reversible process. Heat flowing from hot to cold is irreversible.", "3": "Entropy changes here are Q/T₁ and Q/T₂. The total does not become zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0772' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Natural processes move towards greater disorder, and entropy measures this disorder. Each time entropy increases, some chance of turning heat into work is lost, as when hot and cold water mix. The energy is not lost but is degraded into a less orderly form, from which less useful work can be extracted.', wrong = '{"0": "An increase in entropy means less energy is available for useful work, not more.", "1": "Entropy rises when molecular randomness increases, such as when a solid melts or a gas expands.", "3": "Energy is always conserved. Rising entropy means energy is degraded into a less useful form, not destroyed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0773' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Natural processes can only keep the total entropy of a system and its surroundings constant or make it increase. In a reversible process the total entropy stays constant, while every irreversible process increases it.', wrong = '{"0": "The total entropy increases in irreversible processes. In a reversible one it stays constant.", "1": "The total entropy of a system plus its surroundings never decreases in any process.", "2": "Entropy changes are finite amounts, ΔQ/T. They do not become infinite."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0774' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Melting at constant temperature absorbs heat ΔQ = mL = 2.0 kg × 3.36 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹ = 6.72 × 10⁵ J. The temperature stays at 0 °C = 273 K, so ΔS = ΔQ/T = 6.72 × 10⁵ ÷ 273 ≈ +2.46 × 10³ J K⁻¹. Melting increases disorder, so the change is positive.', wrong = '{"1": "1.23 × 10³ J K⁻¹ is the entropy change for 1.0 kg of ice. Here the mass is 2.0 kg.", "2": "Melting absorbs heat and makes the molecules more disordered, so the entropy increases. The sign must be positive.", "3": "6.72 × 10⁵ is the heat absorbed in joules. It must be divided by the temperature, 273 K."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0775' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In every natural process the total entropy of a system and its surroundings increases. A local decrease, such as cooling something in a refrigerator, is always paid for by a larger increase somewhere else. Taking all systems together as the universe, the entropy always increases.', wrong = '{"0": "Entropy can fall in one part of the universe, but only with a larger rise elsewhere. The total never decreases.", "2": "The universe''s entropy is not zero. Every natural process adds to it.", "3": "There is no point at which the total entropy turns round and falls. It keeps increasing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0776' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A wave is a regular disturbance that carries energy outward from its source. As a wave passes through a medium, each particle only vibrates up and down or to and fro about its mean (equilibrium) position. The energy travels onward, but the medium itself is not carried along.', wrong = '{"0": "The particles vibrate about fixed mean positions and stay where they are. There is no overall transport of matter.", "1": "It is the other way round. A wave carries energy but does not carry matter along.", "2": "Mechanical waves travel through solids, liquids and gases, and electromagnetic waves can even cross a vacuum."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0777' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Wavelength λ is the distance between two consecutive points on a wave that are in phase, meaning they are at the same stage of their vibration. For example, it is the distance from one crest to the next crest, or one trough to the next trough.', wrong = '{"1": "A crest and the next trough are out of phase. The distance between them is half a wavelength.", "2": "The distance from the equilibrium position up to a crest is the amplitude, not the wavelength.", "3": "How far a wave travels before dying out depends on energy losses. It is not the wavelength."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0778' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The speed of a wave equals its frequency times its wavelength, v = fλ, because in each second f waves, each λ long, pass a point. Here v = 250 Hz × 1.36 m = 340 m s⁻¹, about the speed of sound in air.', wrong = '{"0": "184 m s⁻¹ is 250 ÷ 1.36, dividing the frequency by the wavelength instead of multiplying.", "1": "251 comes from adding 250 and 1.36. Frequency and wavelength are multiplied.", "3": "3400 m s⁻¹ is ten times too large, a slip with the decimal point."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0779' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The frequency is the number of oscillations per second. In 2 minutes = 120 s there are 600 oscillations, so f = 600 ÷ 120 = 5 Hz. The period is the time for one oscillation: T = 1/f = 1/5 = 0.2 s.', wrong = '{"0": "0.0033 s is 2 ÷ 600, which treats the time as 2 s. Two minutes is 120 s.", "2": "5 is the frequency in hertz. The period is its reciprocal, 1/5 = 0.2 s.", "3": "300 is 600 ÷ 2, dividing by minutes instead of seconds. It is neither the frequency nor the period."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0780' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electromagnetic waves do not need a medium, so they can travel through a vacuum; this is how sunlight reaches the Earth. X-rays are electromagnetic waves. Seismic waves, sound and water ripples are all mechanical waves, which need a material medium to travel.', wrong = '{"0": "Seismic waves are mechanical waves that travel through the rock of the Earth. They cannot cross empty space.", "1": "Sound is a mechanical wave and needs a solid, liquid or gas. It cannot travel through a vacuum.", "2": "Ripples on a pond are mechanical waves on the surface of water. They need the water to travel."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0781' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Waves associated with particles such as electrons and photons are called quantum waves. The matter waves of moving electrons, also called de Broglie waves, are an example. They are not vibrations of a material medium.', wrong = '{"0": "Mechanical waves are vibrations passed through a material medium, such as sound or water waves. Electron waves are not of this kind.", "1": "Surface waves travel along the boundary between two media, like ocean waves on the water surface.", "2": "Sound is a longitudinal mechanical wave in a material medium. It is not linked to moving electrons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0782' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A transverse wave is one in which the particles of the medium vibrate at right angles to the direction in which the wave''s energy travels, as in a wave on a string. In a longitudinal wave, such as sound, the particles vibrate along the direction of travel.', wrong = '{"1": "Particles vibrating parallel to the direction of energy travel describes a longitudinal wave, such as sound in air.", "2": "Particles in a wave move back and forth about their mean positions. They are not carried steadily away from the source.", "3": "The particles do vibrate. It is only their average position that stays the same while energy moves on."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0783' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sound needs a material medium to travel, so it is a mechanical wave. As it passes through air, the air particles vibrate back and forth along the direction of travel, forming compressions and rarefactions. So sound in air is a longitudinal mechanical wave.', wrong = '{"1": "In air the particles vibrate along the direction of travel, not across it, so sound in air is not transverse.", "2": "Sound needs a medium and cannot cross a vacuum, so it is not electromagnetic.", "3": "Electromagnetic waves are transverse and need no medium. Sound is mechanical, not electromagnetic."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0784' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By the principle of superposition, the resultant displacement is the algebraic sum of the individual displacements. When the waves are exactly out of phase, one displacement is positive while the other is negative, so the resultant amplitude is 5 mm − 3 mm = 2 mm.', wrong = '{"0": "Complete cancellation to zero happens only when two out-of-phase waves have equal amplitudes. Here 5 mm and 3 mm leave 2 mm.", "2": "5.8 mm is √(5² + 3²), which adds them like perpendicular vectors. Displacements along the same line add algebraically.", "3": "8 mm is 5 + 3, the result when the waves meet in phase. Out of phase, they subtract."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0785' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The principle of superposition says that the resultant displacement is the algebraic sum of the individual displacements. It holds for linear, small-amplitude waves. Within that limit it works whatever the type of wave, its frequency or its direction of travel.', wrong = '{"0": "Superposition applies to mechanical waves too, such as sound and waves on a string, not only to electromagnetic waves.", "1": "Waves of different frequencies also superpose. Two slightly different frequencies superpose to give beats.", "3": "Waves travelling in opposite directions also superpose. That is how stationary waves form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0786' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When two waves of slightly different frequencies travel in the same direction and superpose, they drift in and out of step. The loudness at a point rises and falls periodically. This pulsing effect is called beats.', wrong = '{"0": "Stationary waves form when two waves of the same frequency travel in opposite directions.", "1": "A steady interference pattern needs waves of the same frequency with a constant phase difference.", "2": "Diffraction is the bending and spreading of waves around obstacles or through gaps. It does not need two waves."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0787' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Noise-cancelling headphones produce an anti-noise signal that is the exact opposite of the ambient noise: the same amplitude but opposite phase. Where a crest of the noise arrives, a trough of the anti-noise arrives, so by superposition the two cancel and the listener hears much less noise.', wrong = '{"0": "Same amplitude and same phase would add the two waves together, doubling the noise instead of cancelling it.", "1": "A wave of a different frequency cannot stay exactly opposite to the noise, so it cannot cancel it steadily.", "2": "A larger signal in the same phase would add to the noise and make it louder."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0788' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Noise-cancelling headphones contain one or more microphones that pick up the ambient noise, such as engine rumble or background chatter. The microphone sends this signal to an amplifier and processing unit, which then creates the anti-noise signal.', wrong = '{"1": "The processing unit receives the noise signal from the microphone and generates the anti-noise. It does not detect the sound itself.", "2": "The ear cushion blocks some sound passively, but it cannot capture noise as a signal.", "3": "The music source supplies the desired audio, which is played along with the anti-noise. It does not detect background noise."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0789' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Coherent sources emit waves of the same frequency that keep a constant phase difference. Two loudspeakers fed by the same audio generator vibrate together at exactly the same frequency and in phase, so they are coherent. This lets them produce a steady interference pattern.', wrong = '{"0": "Placing sources close together does not make them coherent. What matters is the same frequency and a fixed phase difference.", "1": "Coherence has nothing to do with loudness. Two very loud independent sources are still not coherent.", "3": "Waves of different frequencies drift in and out of step, so their phase difference keeps changing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0790' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A steady interference pattern needs coherent sources, which give the same frequency with a constant phase difference. Two speakers connected to the same audio generator are driven by one signal, so they are coherent and produce fixed points of loud and soft sound.', wrong = '{"0": "Forks of 256 Hz and 260 Hz have different frequencies. Together they produce 4 beats per second, not a steady pattern.", "1": "Two different songs contain changing frequencies with no fixed phase relation, so no steady pattern forms.", "3": "A speaker and a person humming are independent sources. Their frequencies and phases are not locked together."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0791' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Destructive interference occurs where the path difference is an odd number of half-wavelengths: ΔS = (2n + 1)λ/2. A path difference of 2.5λ equals 5 × λ/2, which is (2 × 2 + 1)λ/2 with n = 2. The waves arrive exactly out of phase, so the interference is destructive.', wrong = '{"0": "Constructive interference needs a path difference of a whole number of wavelengths, such as 2λ or 3λ, not 2.5λ.", "1": "The resultant amplitude does not grow with the path difference. At 2.5λ the waves are out of phase.", "2": "Interference happens wherever the waves overlap. A path difference only decides whether it is constructive or destructive."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0792' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First find the wavelength: λ = v/f = 340 m s⁻¹ ÷ 680 Hz = 0.5 m. Destructive interference needs a path difference of (2n + 1)λ/2, and the smallest value (n = 0) is λ/2 = 0.25 m.', wrong = '{"1": "0.5 m is one whole wavelength. A path difference of λ gives constructive interference.", "2": "1.0 m is two wavelengths, which also gives constructive interference.", "3": "2.0 m comes from f/v = 680/340, the ratio turned upside down. The wavelength is v/f = 0.5 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0793' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first minimum occurs where the path difference is half a wavelength: ΔS = λ/2. With ΔS = 1.0 m, λ = 2.0 m. The frequency is f = v/λ = 340 m s⁻¹ ÷ 2.0 m = 170 Hz. Any lower frequency would have a longer wavelength and could not give a minimum.', wrong = '{"0": "85 Hz gives λ = 4.0 m, so the 1.0 m path difference is only λ/4. That is neither a maximum nor a minimum.", "2": "At 340 Hz, λ = 1.0 m, so the path difference is one whole wavelength. That gives a maximum.", "3": "At 680 Hz, λ = 0.5 m, so the path difference is 2λ. That is also a maximum."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0794' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a stationary wave, neighbouring nodes are λ/2 apart, and each antinode lies midway between two nodes. So the distance from a node to the next antinode is half of λ/2, which is λ/4.', wrong = '{"1": "λ/2 is the distance from one node to the next node, or from one antinode to the next antinode.", "2": "A full wavelength covers two loops of the stationary wave, from a node to the node two places along.", "3": "2λ covers four loops. It is much longer than the gap between a node and its neighbouring antinode."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0795' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a stationary wave the nodes are always at rest. Since energy is carried by the motion of the particles, it cannot flow past a node. The energy stays between neighbouring nodes, changing back and forth between kinetic and potential energy.', wrong = '{"0": "Energy moving along the medium at the wave speed describes a progressive (travelling) wave, not a stationary one.", "1": "Nodes are permanently at rest, so they have no vibrational energy. The energy is concentrated around the antinodes.", "3": "A stationary wave does hold energy, which alternates between kinetic and potential forms. It just does not carry it along."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0796' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a stationary wave, neighbouring antinodes are half a wavelength apart, so λ/2 = 0.25 m and λ = 0.50 m. The wave speed is v = fλ = 400 Hz × 0.50 m = 200 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "50 m s⁻¹ uses λ = 0.125 m, which halves the antinode spacing instead of doubling it.", "1": "100 m s⁻¹ takes the antinode spacing, 0.25 m, as the full wavelength. It is only half a wavelength.", "3": "400 m s⁻¹ uses λ = 1.0 m, four times the antinode spacing. The wavelength is twice the spacing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0797' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A string fixed at both ends has nodes at both ends. In the fundamental mode it vibrates in one loop, so its length is half a wavelength: λ = 2l = 1.6 m. The fundamental frequency is f₁ = v/2l = 240 m s⁻¹ ÷ 1.6 m = 150 Hz.', wrong = '{"0": "75 Hz is v/4l = 240/3.2, the formula for a pipe closed at one end. A string fixed at both ends uses v/2l.", "2": "192 comes from multiplying v by l (240 × 0.8). Frequency is speed divided by wavelength.", "3": "300 Hz is v/l, which takes the wavelength as l. In the fundamental mode the wavelength is 2l."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0798' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The speed of transverse waves on a string is v = √(F/m), where F is the tension and m is the mass per unit length. Making F four times larger makes v √4 = 2 times larger. Since f₁ = v/2l and the length is unchanged, the fundamental frequency also doubles.', wrong = '{"0": "More tension makes the waves faster, which raises the frequency. It does not halve it.", "1": "The wave speed depends on tension, so changing the tension changes the frequency. This is how instruments are tuned.", "2": "The frequency depends on the square root of the tension. Four times the tension gives only twice the frequency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0799' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The speed of a transverse wave on a stretched string is v = √(F/m). Here F/m = 64 N ÷ 0.01 kg m⁻¹ = 6400 m² s⁻², so v = √6400 = 80 m s⁻¹. A tighter or lighter string carries waves faster.', wrong = '{"0": "8 m s⁻¹ is √64, which leaves out the mass per unit length. F must first be divided by 0.01.", "2": "640 is 64 ÷ 0.1, a slip in the decimal, and the square root is not taken either.", "3": "6400 is F/m, which equals v² in m² s⁻². The square root must be taken."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0800' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a string vibrates in n loops, its frequency is n times the fundamental: fₙ = nf₁. Here 3 loops at 150 Hz is the third harmonic, so f₁ = 150 Hz ÷ 3 = 50 Hz.', wrong = '{"0": "25 Hz would be the fundamental if 150 Hz were the 6th harmonic, with 6 loops. Here there are 3 loops.", "2": "150 Hz is the given frequency for 3 loops, the third harmonic. The fundamental has one loop.", "3": "450 Hz multiplies by the number of loops instead of dividing. The fundamental is the lowest frequency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0801' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A string fixed at both ends can vibrate only at whole-number multiples of its fundamental: fₙ = nf₁ with n = 1, 2, 3, and so on. This is called quantisation of frequencies. 150 Hz is 2.5 × 60 Hz, not a whole-number multiple, so it cannot be a natural frequency.', wrong = '{"0": "120 Hz is 2 × 60 Hz, the second harmonic, so it is a natural frequency.", "2": "180 Hz is 3 × 60 Hz, the third harmonic, so it is a natural frequency.", "3": "240 Hz is 4 × 60 Hz, the fourth harmonic, so it is a natural frequency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0802' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A pipe closed at one end has a node at the closed end and an antinode at the open end. The pipe length must then be an odd number of quarter-wavelengths, so fₙ = nv/4l with n = 1, 3, 5 only. A closed pipe produces only the odd harmonics.', wrong = '{"1": "Even harmonics are exactly the ones a closed pipe cannot produce, because a node and an antinode must sit at its two ends.", "2": "All harmonics are produced by a pipe open at both ends, not by one closed at one end.", "3": "A closed pipe can also produce higher odd harmonics, such as 3f₁ and 5f₁, not just the fundamental."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0803' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A pipe open at both ends has antinodes at both ends. In the fundamental mode its length is half a wavelength, so λ = 2l and f₁ = v/2l = 340 m s⁻¹ ÷ (2 × 0.85 m) = 340 ÷ 1.7 = 200 Hz.', wrong = '{"0": "100 Hz is v/4l, the fundamental for a pipe of the same length closed at one end.", "2": "400 Hz takes λ = l = 0.85 m. In the fundamental mode of an open pipe, λ = 2l.", "3": "800 Hz takes λ = l/2. That is far shorter than the fundamental wavelength."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0804' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A pipe closed at one end has fundamental f₁ = v/4l = 340 ÷ (4 × 0.50) = 340 ÷ 2.0 = 170 Hz. It produces only odd harmonics, so the next resonance above the fundamental is the third harmonic, 3f₁ = 3 × 170 = 510 Hz.', wrong = '{"0": "170 Hz is the fundamental itself. The question asks for the next frequency above it.", "1": "340 Hz is 2f₁, an even harmonic. A pipe closed at one end cannot produce even harmonics.", "3": "680 Hz is 4f₁, also an even harmonic, so a closed pipe cannot produce it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0805' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a pipe open at both ends, the fundamental is f₁ = v/2l. For a pipe closed at one end it is f₁ = v/4l. With the same length, (v/2l) ÷ (v/4l) = 2, so the open pipe''s fundamental is twice that of the closed pipe.', wrong = '{"1": "The open pipe''s fundamental wavelength is 2l, shorter than the closed pipe''s 4l, so its frequency is higher, not lower.", "2": "The ends behave differently: an open end is an antinode and a closed end is a node. This changes the fundamental.", "3": "Four times is wrong. The ratio of the two fundamentals is (v/2l) ÷ (v/4l) = 2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0806' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A pipe open at both ends can produce all harmonics, f₁, 2f₁, 3f₁ and so on, while a pipe closed at one end produces only the odd ones. Having both odd and even harmonics means the open pipe is richer in harmonics, which gives its sound a fuller quality.', wrong = '{"0": "Producing only odd harmonics is what a pipe closed at one end does.", "1": "For the same length, an open pipe''s fundamental is higher, not lower. Being rich in harmonics is about which harmonics are present.", "3": "Stationary waves in an open pipe do have nodes; the fundamental has one node in the middle."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0807' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At a closed end the air molecules cannot move, so there is always a displacement node there. At an open end the air is free to move, so there is always an antinode. These end conditions decide which harmonics a pipe can produce.', wrong = '{"0": "This reverses the rule. Air cannot move at a closed end, so that is a node, while an open end is an antinode.", "1": "An open end lets the air move freely, so it is an antinode, not a node.", "2": "A closed end stops the air moving, so it is always a node, not an antinode."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0808' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Microwaves reflected from the metal plate superpose with the incoming waves to form a stationary wave. The probe finds maxima at the antinodes, which are half a wavelength apart. So λ/2 = 1.5 cm and λ = 3.0 cm.', wrong = '{"0": "0.75 cm halves the spacing. The spacing between antinodes must be doubled to give the wavelength.", "1": "1.5 cm is the gap between neighbouring antinodes, which is only half a wavelength.", "3": "6.0 cm treats the spacing as λ/4, which is the distance from a node to an antinode. Two maxima are λ/2 apart."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0809' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The microwaves travel from the transmitter to the metal plate and are reflected back. The incident and reflected waves have the same frequency and travel in opposite directions, so they superpose to form a stationary wave with fixed nodes and antinodes, which the probe detects.', wrong = '{"0": "Only one transmitter is used. The second wave is the reflection from the metal plate.", "1": "The probe only detects the pattern. The pattern itself comes from reflection and superposition, not diffraction.", "2": "Metal reflects microwaves; they do not pass into it and refract."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0810' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Diffraction is the spreading of waves as they pass through a gap or around an obstacle. It is most noticeable when the gap width is comparable to or smaller than the wavelength. A ripple tank shows this: a narrow gap spreads the waves into a near-semicircle, while a wide gap lets them pass with little bending.', wrong = '{"1": "A gap many times wider than the wavelength lets most of the wave pass straight through, with very little spreading.", "2": "The amount of diffraction depends on how the gap width compares with the wavelength, so gaps do not all diffract equally.", "3": "Amplitude is the size of the vibration and does not decide diffraction. The wavelength is what the gap is compared with."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0811' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Frequency is the number of ripples per second: f = 300 ÷ 15 s = 20 Hz. The ripples are 1.2 cm apart, so λ = 1.2 cm. The speed is v = fλ = 20 Hz × 1.2 cm = 24 cm s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "0.06 is 1.2 ÷ 20, which divides the wavelength by the frequency instead of multiplying.", "1": "12.5 is 15 ÷ 1.2, dividing the time by the ripple spacing. It is not a speed.", "3": "250 is 300 ÷ 1.2, dividing the number of ripples by the spacing. It ignores the 15 s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0812' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Diffraction is strong when the gap size is comparable to the wavelength. In a crystal the atoms form regular rows about 10⁻¹⁰ m apart, which is similar to the wavelength of X-rays. So the rows of atoms act like a diffraction grating for X-rays.', wrong = '{"0": "X-rays have a very short wavelength, around 10⁻¹⁰ m. It matches the tiny spacing between atoms.", "1": "If the crystal absorbed all the X-rays, there would be no pattern at all. Diffraction needs the waves to be scattered.", "3": "X-rays are electromagnetic waves, not mechanical waves."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0813' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Diffraction is strong only when the gap is comparable to the wavelength. Sound waves have wavelengths of around a metre, similar to a doorway, so they spread round it. Light waves are about 5 × 10⁻⁷ m long, far smaller than the doorway, so light hardly bends round the edge.', wrong = '{"0": "Light is actually about a million times faster than sound. Speed is not the reason.", "1": "Light passes straight through an open doorway. It just does not spread round the corner.", "3": "Sound in air is longitudinal, not transverse. In any case, the type of wave is not the reason."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0814' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When two notes of slightly different frequencies sound together, the number of beats per second equals the difference between their frequencies. Here the beat frequency is 512 Hz − 508 Hz = 4 beats per second.', wrong = '{"0": "2 is half the difference. The beat frequency is the full difference between the two frequencies.", "2": "8 is twice the difference. Each second the waves fall in and out of step only 4 times.", "3": "510 Hz is the average of the two frequencies, close to the pitch heard. It is not the beat rate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0815' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each beat is one rise and fall of loudness, from one maximum to the next. With 5 beats per second, the time between successive maxima is the beat period, 1 ÷ 5 = 0.2 s.', wrong = '{"0": "0.1 s would mean 10 maxima each second. Each beat contains only one maximum.", "2": "2.5 is half of the beat frequency, a number of beats rather than a time.", "3": "5 is the number of beats per second. The time between maxima is its reciprocal, 0.2 s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0816' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Six beats per second means B is either 384 + 6 = 390 Hz or 384 − 6 = 378 Hz. Wax makes B heavier, so its frequency falls. The beats drop to 2 per second, so B has moved closer to 384 Hz, which can only happen if it started above A. So B was 390 Hz and falls to 386 Hz.', wrong = '{"0": "372 Hz is 384 − 12, which would give 12 beats per second with A, not 6.", "1": "If B were 378 Hz, the wax would lower it further from 384 Hz and the beats would increase, not fall.", "2": "If B were 384 Hz, the two forks would be in unison and give no beats at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0817' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Beats disappear when two frequencies become equal. A musician sounds the instrument with a standard tuning fork and adjusts the string tension until the beats slow down and stop. The instrument is then tuned to the fork''s frequency.', wrong = '{"0": "Measuring the speed of a car uses the Doppler effect, as in radar speed guns.", "1": "X-ray wavelengths are measured using diffraction by the regular rows of atoms in a crystal.", "2": "Noise-cancelling headphones use destructive superposition with an anti-noise signal of opposite phase."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0818' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Beats come from two waves of slightly different frequencies. At some moments they are in phase and reinforce each other, giving a loud sound; a little later they are out of phase and nearly cancel, giving a soft sound. So the loudness at a fixed point rises and falls periodically.', wrong = '{"1": "A steady, louder sound comes from constant constructive interference of waves with the same frequency, not from beats.", "2": "Permanent silence would need complete, steady cancellation. With beats the sound keeps returning.", "3": "The loudness swings up and down in a cycle. It does not keep growing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0819' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Intensity is the power carried by a wave per unit area at right angles to its direction of travel: I = P/A. Power is measured in watts and area in square metres, so the SI unit of intensity is W m⁻².', wrong = '{"0": "W m⁻¹ would be power per unit length. Intensity is power spread over an area.", "1": "J m⁻² is energy per unit area. Intensity involves power, the energy per second.", "3": "The watt alone is the unit of power. Intensity also divides by area."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0820' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A point source spreads its power evenly over a sphere of area 4πr², so I = P/4πr². At r = 2.0 m the area is 4π × (2.0)² = 16π ≈ 50.3 m². So I = 100 W ÷ 50.3 m² ≈ 1.99 W m⁻².', wrong = '{"1": "3.98 W m⁻² divides by 2πr², the area of a hemisphere. The sound spreads over a full sphere, 4πr².", "2": "7.96 W m⁻² divides by πr², the area of a circle, not the surface of a sphere.", "3": "25 W m⁻² divides by r² alone (100 ÷ 4) and leaves out the 4π."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0821' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a point source, the power spreads over a sphere of area 4πr², so intensity varies as 1/r². Doubling the distance from 4 m to 8 m multiplies r² by 4, so the intensity falls to a quarter: 0.8 ÷ 4 = 0.2 W m⁻².', wrong = '{"0": "0.1 W m⁻² divides by 8, which would be an inverse-cube law. Intensity follows 1/r².", "2": "0.4 W m⁻² only halves the intensity, an inverse (1/r) law. Doubling r reduces I to a quarter.", "3": "1.6 W m⁻² doubles the intensity. Moving further from the source spreads the energy out, so I falls."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0822' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'As the train approaches, it moves towards the waves it has already sent ahead, so the waves in front of it are squeezed closer together. The wavelength reaching the listener is shorter, so more waves arrive each second. The listener hears a higher frequency, a higher pitch, than the whistle actually emits. This is the Doppler effect.', wrong = '{"0": "A lower pitch is heard after the train passes and moves away, when its waves are stretched out.", "1": "Relative motion between the source and the listener changes the frequency received, so the pitch does not stay the same.", "2": "The speed of sound depends on the air, not on the motion of the source. What changes is the wavelength and so the pitch."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0823' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A moving source squeezes the waves in front of it, so the wavelength becomes (v − u_s)/f and the frequency heard rises. But once a sound wave leaves the source, its speed depends only on the air. In still air the speed of sound v stays the same.', wrong = '{"1": "The wavelength in front of an approaching source is shortened to (v − u_s)/f, so it does change.", "2": "The listener hears a higher apparent frequency as the source approaches, so the frequency changes.", "3": "Both the wavelength and the frequency change. Only the speed of sound in still air stays the same."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0824' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a source moving towards a stationary observer, f′ = vf/(v − u_s). Here f′ = 340 × 680 ÷ (340 − 20) = 231 200 ÷ 320 = 722.5 Hz, about 723 Hz. The pitch heard is higher because the siren is approaching.', wrong = '{"0": "642 Hz uses v + u_s in the bottom line, the formula for a source moving away. This siren is approaching.", "1": "680 Hz is the siren''s true frequency. A moving source changes the frequency heard.", "2": "700 Hz adds the source speed to the frequency (680 + 20). A speed cannot be added to a frequency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0825' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a source moving away from a stationary observer, the waves are stretched and f′ = vf/(v + u_s). Here f′ = 360 × 500 ÷ (360 + 40) = 180 000 ÷ 400 = 450 Hz, lower than the true 500 Hz.', wrong = '{"0": "444 Hz is f(1 − u_s/v), the formula for a moving observer, not a moving source. A moving source uses vf/(v + u_s).", "2": "500 Hz is the whistle''s true frequency. A receding source is heard at a lower frequency.", "3": "563 Hz uses v − u_s, the formula for an approaching source. This train is moving away."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0826' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For an approaching source, f′/f = v/(v − u_s). Setting this equal to 1.25 gives v − u_s = v ÷ 1.25 = 0.8v. So u_s = 0.2v = 0.2 × 340 = 68 m s⁻¹. Check: 340 ÷ (340 − 68) = 340 ÷ 272 = 1.25.', wrong = '{"1": "85 m s⁻¹ is 0.25 × 340, which assumes the frequency rises by the same fraction as the source speed. Solving v/(v − u_s) = 1.25 gives 0.2v.", "2": "272 m s⁻¹ is v − u_s = 0.8 × 340. The source speed is the difference, 340 − 272 = 68 m s⁻¹.", "3": "425 m s⁻¹ is 1.25 × 340, faster than sound. A source that fast would not fit this formula at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0827' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In one period, 1/f, the sound travels v/f, while the source moves u_s/f towards the listener. So each wave is squeezed into a shorter distance: λ′ = v/f − u_s/f = (v − u_s)/f.', wrong = '{"1": "(v + u_s)/f is the longer wavelength behind the source, heard by a listener the source is moving away from.", "2": "v/f is the wavelength when the source is at rest. Motion towards the listener shortens it.", "3": "vf/(v − u_s) is the apparent frequency heard by the listener, not the wavelength."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0828' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'SONAR stands for Sound Navigation And Ranging. It is an underwater echo-ranging system that sends out sound or ultrasound and listens for echoes to find objects. Using the Doppler shift of the echo, it can also find a target''s speed, for example to locate submarines.', wrong = '{"0": "This is not what the letters stand for. Sonar uses sound for navigation and ranging, not oscillation and reflection.", "2": "This is not what the letters stand for. Sonar is about navigation and ranging, not natural resonance.", "3": "This mixes sonar with radar. Radar uses radio waves, while sonar uses sound, and the letters stand for Sound Navigation And Ranging."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0829' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A radar speed trap sends out short bursts of microwaves, which reflect off the moving car. Because the car is moving, the reflected microwaves come back with a changed frequency, a Doppler shift. The size of this shift is used to calculate the car''s speed.', wrong = '{"0": "Radar speed traps use microwaves, which are electromagnetic, not sound echoes.", "1": "Diffraction of light around a car tells nothing about its speed. The method relies on the Doppler shift.", "3": "Beats between engines are not measured. The speed comes from the frequency shift of reflected microwaves."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0830' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The observed wavelength, 440 nm, is longer than the laboratory value, 400 nm. This red shift means the galaxy is moving away. From f′ = cf/(c + u), λ′/λ = (c + u)/c, so u = c(440/400 − 1) = 0.1c = 0.1 × 3 × 10⁸ = 3.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "A longer observed wavelength (red shift) means the galaxy is moving away, not towards the Earth. 2.7 × 10⁷ also divides the shift by 440 nm instead of 400 nm.", "2": "The speed is right, but a shift to a longer wavelength means the galaxy is receding, not approaching.", "3": "3.0 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ is the speed of light itself. A 10% change in wavelength gives a speed of 0.1c."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0831' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a star moves towards the Earth, the light waves it emits are squeezed, so they arrive with shorter wavelengths than if the star were at rest. Its spectrum is shifted towards the shorter-wavelength, blue end. So a blue shift means the star is approaching.', wrong = '{"0": "A star moving away from the Earth shows a red shift, with its spectral lines moved to longer wavelengths.", "1": "A star at rest relative to the Earth shows no Doppler shift at all.", "2": "The Doppler shift comes from motion along the line of sight, not from a change in the star''s temperature."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0832' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Doppler echocardiography sends ultrasound into the heart and measures the frequency shift of the echoes from moving blood. From this shift it finds the speed of blood flow and detects heart problems, such as narrowed valves (stenosis) or leaking valves (regurgitation).', wrong = '{"1": "X-ray images of bones are made by passing X-rays through the body. They do not use the Doppler shift.", "2": "Body temperature is measured with a thermometer or an infrared sensor, not with Doppler ultrasound.", "3": "The heart''s electrical activity is recorded by an ECG (electrocardiogram), which uses electrodes, not sound waves."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0833' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Humans can hear frequencies from about 20 Hz to 20 000 Hz (20 kHz). Dogs can hear much higher frequencies, up to roughly 45 to 50 kHz. A 40 kHz whistle is ultrasound for humans, so a dog can hear it but a person cannot.', wrong = '{"0": "40 kHz is about twice the upper limit of human hearing, so a human cannot hear it.", "2": "Humans hear only up to about 20 kHz, so they cannot hear a 40 kHz whistle, although a dog can.", "3": "A dog''s hearing extends well above 40 kHz, so a dog can hear this whistle."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0834' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Interference and diffraction prove that light is a wave, but they happen with both transverse and longitudinal waves. Polarization, restricting the vibrations to one plane, is possible only for transverse waves. Since light can be polarized, light waves must be transverse.', wrong = '{"0": "Reflection happens for both transverse and longitudinal waves; sound echoes are an example. It cannot show that light is transverse.", "1": "Refraction also happens for both kinds of wave, including sound, so it does not show the type of wave.", "2": "Diffraction shows that light is a wave, but longitudinal waves such as sound diffract too."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0835' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Polarization means restricting the vibrations to a single plane across the direction of travel. Only transverse waves have such sideways vibrations. Sound in air is a longitudinal wave, vibrating along its direction of travel, so it cannot be polarized.', wrong = '{"0": "Radio waves are transverse electromagnetic waves, so they can be polarized; aerials are often aligned to match.", "1": "Visible light is a transverse electromagnetic wave and is easily polarized, for example by a polaroid sheet.", "2": "Waves on a stretched string are transverse mechanical waves. Their vibrations can be confined to one plane, so they can be polarized."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0836' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an incandescent bulb, countless atoms emit light independently, so the electric field vibrations point in all directions across the beam at random. Such light is unpolarized. Sunlight, fluorescent lamps and candle flames are other examples.', wrong = '{"1": "A polaroid sheet lets through only the vibrations along its axis, so the light leaving it is plane polarized.", "2": "Light reflected at the polarizing angle is almost completely plane polarized. This is Brewster''s law.", "3": "An analyzer is just a second polarizer, so any light leaving it is plane polarized."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0837' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a polarized transverse wave, the vibrations are confined to one direction across the direction of travel. The plane of polarization is the plane that contains both this direction of vibration and the direction in which the wave travels.', wrong = '{"0": "A single direction is a line and cannot define a plane. The direction of vibration is also needed.", "1": "Vibrations in all possible directions describe unpolarized light, which has no single plane of polarization.", "2": "The plane of polarization must include the direction of propagation. It is defined by the vibration direction together with the direction of travel."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0838' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Unpolarized light can be polarized by passing it through a polarizing filter such as a polaroid sheet, by using a polarizing beam splitter, or by passing it through certain crystals such as calcite or quartz. Ordinary window glass at normal incidence lets vibrations in all planes through, so it does not polarize the light.', wrong = '{"1": "A polaroid sheet is the most common way to polarize light. It transmits only the vibrations along its axis.", "2": "A polarizing beam splitter separates light into polarized beams, so it is a method of polarizing light.", "3": "Certain crystals, such as calcite and quartz, polarize light passing through them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0839' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In circularly polarized light, the tip of the electric field vector E rotates in a circle as the wave travels, either clockwise (right-handed) or anticlockwise (left-handed). Light reflected from a CD or DVD is an example.', wrong = '{"0": "In linearly polarized light, E oscillates back and forth in one fixed plane. It does not rotate.", "1": "In elliptically polarized light, E traces an ellipse, not a circle, because its two components are unequal or differ in phase.", "3": "In unpolarized light the direction of E changes randomly. It does not trace a regular circle."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0840' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the two components of the electric field, Ex and Ey, are unequal, or differ in phase by an arbitrary angle, the tip of E traces an ellipse as the wave travels. This is elliptical polarization, a combination of linear and circular polarization.', wrong = '{"0": "In linear polarization E stays in a single plane and traces a straight line, not an ellipse.", "1": "Circular polarization needs equal components 90° out of phase, so E traces a circle.", "2": "Unpolarized light has E pointing in random directions, with no fixed pattern."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0841' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The light passing through the analyzer is brightest when its axis is parallel to the polarizer''s axis and disappears when the two axes are perpendicular. In one full turn the axes are perpendicular twice, at 90° and at 270°, so the light disappears two times.', wrong = '{"0": "In one full rotation the axes become perpendicular twice, not once.", "2": "There are only two positions, 90° and 270°, where the axes are perpendicular in a full turn.", "3": "At 0° and 180° the axes are parallel and the light is brightest. Only 90° and 270° give darkness."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0842' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A polaroid blocks vibrations across the beam that are not along its axis. A longitudinal wave vibrates along the direction of travel, so there would be nothing for crossed polaroids to block, and the light would still pass. In reality light is extinguished by crossed polaroids, which proves it is transverse.', wrong = '{"1": "Extinction is what actually happens, and it happens because light is transverse. A longitudinal wave could not be blocked this way.", "2": "Polaroids do not change the colour of light. They only affect which plane of vibration passes.", "3": "Crossed polaroids absorb the light; they do not reflect it back."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0843' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Brewster''s law says that when light is incident from air, the polarizing angle θp satisfies tan θp = n, the refractive index of the medium. Here tan θp = 1.6, so θp = tan⁻¹ 1.6 ≈ 58.0°.', wrong = '{"0": "32.0° is 90° − 58.0°, the angle of refraction when light enters at the polarizing angle.", "1": "38.7° is sin⁻¹(1/1.6), the critical angle for glass, used for total internal reflection.", "2": "51.3° is cos⁻¹(1/1.6). Brewster''s law uses the tangent, tan θp = n."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0844' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the polarizing angle, the reflected light is plane polarized, and the reflected and refracted rays are at exactly 90° to each other. So θp + θr = 90°. Putting this into Snell''s law gives Brewster''s law, tan θp = n.', wrong = '{"1": "The reflected ray goes back into air and the refracted ray enters the medium, so they cannot be parallel.", "2": "The angle between the reflected and refracted rays at the polarizing angle is 90°, not 45°.", "3": "Most of the light is still refracted into the medium. Only a small part is reflected."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0845' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Brewster''s law, tan θp = n = 1.33, so θp ≈ 53.1°. At the polarizing angle the reflected and refracted rays are at right angles, so θr = 90° − θp = 90° − 53.1° ≈ 36.9°.', wrong = '{"1": "45° would need n = 1, since tan 45° = 1. For water tan θp = 1.33.", "2": "48.8° is sin⁻¹(1/1.33), the critical angle for water, not the angle of refraction here.", "3": "53.1° is the polarizing angle itself, the angle of incidence. The question asks for the angle of refraction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0846' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Reflected light is plane polarized when the angle of incidence equals the polarizing angle. Brewster''s law then gives the refractive index: n = tan θp = tan 56° ≈ 1.48.', wrong = '{"0": "0.56 is cos 56°. A refractive index less than 1 is impossible for an ordinary medium.", "1": "0.83 is sin 56°. Brewster''s law uses the tangent of the polarizing angle.", "3": "1.79 is 1/cos 56°. The correct relation is n = tan θp."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0847' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When unpolarized light reflects from a smooth surface such as water, the reflected light is partially polarized, with its vibrations mostly parallel to the surface. This reflected glare can be cut down by polaroid sunglasses, whose axis is set to block these vibrations.', wrong = '{"0": "Reflection does polarize light partly; this is why polaroid sunglasses reduce glare from water.", "2": "The vibrations in light reflected from water are mostly parallel to the surface, not at right angles to it.", "3": "Reflection from water gives partial plane polarization, not circular polarization."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0848' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Malus''s law gives the intensity passing through the analyzer as I = I₀ cos²θ, where θ is the angle between the plane of polarization and the analyzer''s axis. With cos 60° = 0.5, cos² 60° = 0.25, so I = 48 × 0.25 = 12 W m⁻².', wrong = '{"0": "6 W m⁻² uses 0.125 for cos² 60°. The correct value is 0.5² = 0.25.", "2": "24 W m⁻² uses cos θ (0.5) instead of cos²θ. Intensity depends on the square of the cosine.", "3": "36 W m⁻² uses sin² 60° = 0.75, which is the fraction blocked, not the fraction transmitted."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0849' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A polarizer passes half of unpolarized light, so 60 ÷ 2 = 30 W m⁻² leaves it, now plane polarized. By Malus''s law the analyzer passes I = 30 cos² 30° = 30 × 0.75 = 22.5 W m⁻².', wrong = '{"0": "15 W m⁻² uses sin² 30° = 0.25 instead of cos² 30° = 0.75.", "2": "30 W m⁻² is the intensity after the first polarizer only. The analyzer at 30° reduces it further.", "3": "45 W m⁻² is 60 × 0.75, which forgets that the first polarizer halves unpolarized light."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0850' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The analyzer splits the incoming amplitude A₀ into a component A₀ cos θ along its axis, which passes, and A₀ sin θ across it, which is absorbed. So the transmitted amplitude is A = 12 × cos 60° = 12 × 0.5 = 6 units.', wrong = '{"0": "3 units uses cos²θ, which applies to intensity. Amplitude uses cos θ.", "2": "10.4 units is 12 × sin 60°, the component that is absorbed, not transmitted.", "3": "12 units would pass only if the axis were parallel to the plane of polarization. At 60° part is absorbed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0851' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Malus''s law, I/I₀ = cos²θ. For 75%, cos²θ = 0.75, so cos θ = √0.75 ≈ 0.866. The angle with this cosine is θ = 30°. Check: cos² 30° = (0.866)² = 0.75, so 75% of the light gets through.', wrong = '{"1": "41.4° is cos⁻¹ 0.75, which forgets to take the square root. Intensity depends on cos²θ.", "2": "At 45°, cos²θ = 0.5, so the intensity falls to 50%, not 75%.", "3": "At 60°, cos²θ = 0.25, so the intensity falls to 25%."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0852' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first polaroid passes half of unpolarized light: I₀/2. The middle polaroid at 45° passes (I₀/2) cos² 45° = (I₀/2)(½) = I₀/4. The last polaroid, also at 45° to the middle one, passes (I₀/4)(½) = I₀/8.', wrong = '{"0": "Without the middle polaroid no light passes. With it, the light gains a component along the last axis, so some gets through.", "1": "I₀/4 forgets that the first polaroid halves unpolarized light. It applies only the two cos² 45° factors.", "3": "I₀/2 is the intensity after the first polaroid only. Each later polaroid reduces it further."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0853' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Certain transparent crystals, such as tourmaline and calcite, have a strongly directional crystal structure. They let through light vibrating in only one plane, so they act as polaroids.', wrong = '{"0": "Rock salt (sodium chloride) has a symmetric structure and does not confine light to a single plane of vibration.", "1": "Ordinary window glass transmits vibrations in all planes equally, so it does not polarize light passing through it.", "2": "Copper is a metal and is opaque, so light does not pass through it at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0854' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An optically active substance rotates the plane of polarization of light passing through it. Sugar solution is a common example; others are quartz crystals, cinnabar, insulin and collagen.', wrong = '{"1": "Distilled water does not rotate the plane of polarization, so it is not optically active.", "2": "Air does not rotate the plane of polarization of light passing through it.", "3": "A flat plate of ordinary glass does not rotate the plane of polarization."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0855' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The amount of rotation produced by an optically active substance depends on the type of substance, its concentration, the distance the light travels through it, and the wavelength of the light. The intensity, or brightness, of the light does not affect the angle of rotation.', wrong = '{"0": "A more concentrated solution rotates the plane further, so the rotation does depend on concentration.", "2": "The longer the path through the substance, the more the plane is rotated.", "3": "Different wavelengths are rotated by different amounts, so the rotation depends on wavelength."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0856' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The angle through which an optically active solution rotates the plane of polarization depends on its concentration. So by measuring the rotation, the concentration of a solution such as sugar in water can be found.', wrong = '{"0": "The speed of light is measured by other methods. Optical activity does not give it.", "1": "The refractive index of glass can be found from Brewster''s law, tan θp = n, not from optical activity.", "3": "Sound waves in air are longitudinal and cannot be polarized, so optical activity cannot measure their frequency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0857' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Optical activity arises because the molecules of the substance are asymmetric, for example helical (spiral) in shape. Such molecules affect light in a twisting way, which rotates the plane of polarization as the light passes through.', wrong = '{"0": "Temperature is not the cause. Optical activity comes from the shape of the molecules.", "1": "Free electrons make a material a good electrical conductor, as in metals. They do not cause optical activity.", "2": "Colour is not the cause; a colourless sugar solution is optically active."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0858' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Light from the sky is scattered by tiny particles and molecules in the air, and this scattered light is partly polarized. A polarizing filter on the camera blocks much of it, reducing glare and haze and increasing contrast, so clouds and the sky appear sharper and more detailed.', wrong = '{"1": "A filter blocks some of the light, so it reduces the total amount reaching the camera, not increases it.", "2": "A filter cannot change how the clouds move. It only changes which light enters the camera.", "3": "A polarizing filter selects the plane of vibration but does not change the wavelength of light."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0859' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a transparent material such as plastic is under stress, its structure changes how it affects polarized light. Viewed between polarizers, it shows coloured fringe patterns that reveal where the stress is concentrated. This technique is called photoelasticity.', wrong = '{"0": "The photoelectric effect is the emission of electrons from a metal surface when light falls on it.", "2": "Holography records three-dimensional images using the interference of laser light.", "3": "Optical activity is the rotation of the plane of polarization by substances such as sugar solution, not stress analysis."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0860' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An LCD (liquid crystal display) screen places a layer of liquid crystal between polarizing filters. The liquid crystal rotates the plane of polarization by different amounts, controlling how much light passes the second filter at each pixel. So the screen depends on polarization, like polarized sunglasses do.', wrong = '{"0": "An incandescent bulb gives out unpolarized light from a hot filament. It does not use polarization.", "1": "A magnifying glass forms an enlarged image by refraction through a convex lens. Polarization plays no part.", "3": "A glass prism spectrometer separates colours by dispersion, since different wavelengths refract by different amounts."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0861' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gravitational waves are ripples in the fabric of space-time, produced by violent cosmic events such as colliding black holes. They travel outward at the speed of light, about 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹, carrying information about their source.', wrong = '{"0": "Gravitational waves are not mechanical waves and need no medium, so they do not travel at the speed of sound.", "1": "Gravitational waves travel at the speed of light, not at a lower speed.", "2": "Gravitational waves take time to arrive. The first ones detected had travelled for about 1.3 billion years."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0862' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In Einstein''s general theory of relativity, gravity is not treated as an ordinary force. Massive bodies curve the space-time around them, much as a heavy object makes a dent in a mattress, and other bodies move along this curved space-time. Gravitational waves are ripples in this curvature.', wrong = '{"1": "Gravity acts on mass, not on electric charge. An electric force is a different interaction.", "2": "Gravity acts everywhere in the universe, holding planets in orbit and galaxies together, not just on Earth.", "3": "Sound needs a material medium and cannot travel through empty space. Gravity is a curvature of space-time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0863' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gravitational waves were observed directly for the first time by LIGO on 14 September 2015. They came from the collision of two black holes about 1.3 billion light years away. The discovery was announced to the world in early 2016.', wrong = '{"0": "1916 is when Einstein predicted gravitational waves from his general theory of relativity, about 100 years before they were detected.", "2": "2016 is when the discovery was announced. The detection itself was made in September 2015.", "3": "2017 is the year of the Nobel Prize for this work and of the Virgo detector''s first detection."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0864' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A single massive object spinning at a steady rate, such as a neutron star, produces gravitational waves with a constant frequency and amplitude. These are called continuous gravitational waves.', wrong = '{"0": "Burst gravitational waves come from sudden violent events such as supernovas and gamma-ray bursts, not from a steady spin.", "2": "Stochastic gravitational waves are weak, random signals formed by many distant sources added together.", "3": "Compact binary inspiral waves come from two compact objects orbiting each other, with rising frequency and amplitude."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0865' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Stochastic gravitational waves are weak, random signals formed when the waves from many weak sources, such as distant binary systems, add together. With no clear pattern and very low strength, they are the most difficult type to detect.', wrong = '{"0": "Burst waves come from violent single events such as supernovas. They are stronger, short signals and easier to pick out.", "1": "Continuous waves have a steady frequency and amplitude, so their regular pattern helps in finding them.", "3": "Compact binary inspiral waves, from merging black holes, were in fact the first gravitational waves ever detected."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0866' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Burst gravitational waves are short, strong signals produced by sudden violent events, such as supernova explosions and gamma-ray bursts. Cosmic strings are another suggested source.', wrong = '{"0": "A neutron star spinning at a steady rate produces continuous gravitational waves, not bursts.", "1": "Many weak distant sources together produce stochastic gravitational waves, a random background signal.", "2": "Vehicles and aeroplanes do produce gravitational waves, but these are far too weak to detect, not bursts."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0867' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Every accelerating mass produces gravitational waves, including cars and aeroplanes. But the masses and accelerations of objects on Earth are tiny compared with those of colliding black holes or neutron stars, so their waves are far too weak to be detected.', wrong = '{"1": "Cars and aeroplanes do accelerate when they speed up, slow down or turn, and this does produce gravitational waves.", "2": "Any accelerating mass produces gravitational waves, not only stars. Earthly sources are just too weak.", "3": "Gravitational waves pass easily through the atmosphere and even through the whole Earth. They are not absorbed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0868' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A gravitational wave makes space-time itself oscillate. A body it passes through is periodically stretched in one direction and squeezed in another, at right angles to the wave''s direction of travel. This effect, called tidal forces, is extremely small.', wrong = '{"0": "A passing gravitational wave stretches and squeezes space; it is not a heating effect.", "2": "The effect is an oscillating stretch and squeeze across the direction of travel, not a steady push along it.", "3": "Gravitational waves act on mass and space-time. They do not add or remove electric charge."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0869' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gravitational waves travel at the speed of light, so f = c/λ. Converting the wavelength, 6000 km = 6 × 10⁶ m. Then f = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ ÷ 6 × 10⁶ m = 50 Hz.', wrong = '{"0": "0.02 is λ/c = 6 × 10⁶ ÷ 3 × 10⁸, the ratio upside down. Frequency is c divided by λ.", "1": "5 Hz is ten times too small. It would need a wavelength of 6 × 10⁷ m, ten times the real one.", "3": "500 Hz is ten times too large. It would need a wavelength of 600 km, not 6000 km."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0870' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gravitational waves travel at the speed of light, so λ = c/f. Here λ = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ ÷ 250 Hz = 1.2 × 10⁶ m. Since 1 km = 1000 m, this is 1200 km. Gravitational waves have very long wavelengths compared with light.', wrong = '{"0": "12 km is a hundred times too small, a slip in the powers of ten. 3 × 10⁸ ÷ 250 = 1.2 × 10⁶ m.", "1": "120 km is ten times too small. 1.2 × 10⁶ m converts to 1200 km.", "3": "12 000 km is ten times too large, a slip in the powers of ten."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0871' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'LIGO stands for Laser Interferometer Gravitational-wave Observatory. It is a pair of large laser interferometers in the USA built to detect gravitational waves, and it made the first detection in 2015.', wrong = '{"0": "This is not what the letters stand for. LIGO is a laser interferometer, not a light intensity oscillator.", "1": "This is not what the letters stand for. LIGO detects gravitational waves, not ionized gas.", "3": "This is not what the letters stand for. LIGO measures tiny length changes with laser interferometry, not images of galaxies."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0872' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each LIGO detector is an interferometer with two perpendicular arms, each about 4 km long. The Virgo detector in Italy, a similar instrument, has arms of 3 km. Long arms make the tiny length changes caused by a gravitational wave easier to measure.', wrong = '{"0": "0.4 km is ten times too short. Each LIGO arm is about 4 km long.", "1": "3 km is the arm length of the Virgo detector near Pisa, Italy, not LIGO.", "3": "40 km is ten times too long. LIGO''s arms are about 4 km."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0873' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a gravitational-wave interferometer, the beam splitter divides the laser beam into two beams sent at right angles down the two arms. After reflecting from mirrors at the ends, the beams come back and recombine, and their interference shows any change in the arm lengths.', wrong = '{"0": "The laser produces the stable, high-intensity light. The beam splitter only divides it.", "1": "The photodetector measures the returning beams and their interference pattern.", "2": "Sending used light back into the instrument is the job of the power recycling mirror."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0874' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A Fabry–Perot cavity is two mirrors facing each other in each arm. The laser light bounces back and forth between them many times before leaving, which greatly increases the effective path length. A longer path makes the tiny change caused by a gravitational wave easier to detect.', wrong = '{"1": "Splitting the beam into two is done by the beam splitter, before the light enters the arms.", "2": "A stable, high-intensity beam is produced by the laser itself.", "3": "The interference pattern is measured by the photodetector, not by the mirrors in the arms."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0875' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A passing gravitational wave changes the distance between the mirrors in the two arms by a tiny amount. The two beams then travel slightly different distances, so they return with a phase shift. When they recombine, the interference pattern at the photodetector changes, revealing the wave.', wrong = '{"0": "The gravitational wave changes the distances the light travels, not the colour (wavelength) of the laser.", "2": "The laser keeps running throughout. The signal is a change in the interference pattern.", "3": "The photodetector measures light, not sound. Gravitational waves are not sound waves."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0876' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The power recycling mirror reflects laser light that has already travelled through the interferometer back into it, instead of letting it escape. Reusing the light in this way builds up the power inside the instrument and improves its sensitivity, which is why it is called recycling.', wrong = '{"0": "Dividing the beam into two arms is the job of the beam splitter.", "1": "Measuring the phase shift of the returning beams is done by the photodetector.", "3": "The arms are stretched and squeezed by the passing gravitational wave itself, not by any mirror."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0877' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the study of gravitational waves, a binary system is two compact objects, such as black holes, neutron stars or white dwarfs, orbiting each other. As they orbit and accelerate, they emit gravitational waves, and when they finally merge they produce some of the strongest signals.', wrong = '{"0": "A single spinning star is one object. A binary system has two objects orbiting each other.", "1": "That is the computing meaning of binary. In astronomy, binary means a pair of objects in orbit.", "2": "The two objects in a binary system orbit each other because of their strong gravitational interaction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0878' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Eccentricity e measures how far an orbit differs from a perfect circle. A circle has e = 0, an ellipse has 0 < e < 1, a parabola has e = 1 and a hyperbola has e > 1. Since 0.4 lies between 0 and 1, the orbit is elliptical.', wrong = '{"1": "A circular orbit has an eccentricity of exactly 0.", "2": "A parabolic path has an eccentricity of exactly 1.", "3": "A hyperbolic path has an eccentricity greater than 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0879' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The 2017 Nobel Prize in Physics was awarded to Rainer Weiss, Barry C. Barish and Kip S. Thorne for their key contributions to LIGO and the observation of gravitational waves. The Pakistani-born physicist Nergis Mavalvala of MIT was a member of the LIGO team.', wrong = '{"0": "Einstein predicted gravitational waves in 1916 but died in 1955, and the Nobel Prize is not awarded after death.", "2": "Nergis Mavalvala was an important member of the LIGO team, but she was not one of the prize winners.", "3": "Stephen Hawking never won a Nobel Prize, and Roger Penrose''s 2020 prize was for black hole theory, not gravitational-wave detection."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0880' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A dielectric medium between two charges reduces the force between them by a factor equal to its relative permittivity εr: F_medium = F_air/εr. Air has εr very close to 1, so F_medium = 0.8 N ÷ 4 = 0.2 N.', wrong = '{"0": "0.05 N divides by εr² = 16. The force is divided by εr only once.", "2": "A dielectric medium always reduces the electrostatic force, so it cannot stay at 0.8 N.", "3": "3.2 N multiplies by εr. A dielectric reduces the force, it does not increase it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0881' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Coulomb''s law gives F = kq₁q₂/r². The product of the charges is (2 × 10⁻⁶)(5 × 10⁻⁶) = 1 × 10⁻¹¹ C², so k × q₁q₂ = 9 × 10⁹ × 10⁻¹¹ = 0.09. Dividing by r² = 0.09 m² gives F = 1.0 N.', wrong = '{"0": "0.3 N divides by r (0.3 m) instead of r² (0.09 m²).", "2": "10 N is ten times too large, a slip in the powers of ten when multiplying the microcoulombs.", "3": "100 N is a hundred times too large, a slip in the powers of ten."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0882' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Coulomb''s law in free space is F = q₁q₂/(4πε₀r²), so ε₀ = q₁q₂/(4πFr²). Its unit is C² ÷ (N × m²) = C² N⁻¹ m⁻². The value of ε₀ is 8.85 × 10⁻¹² C² N⁻¹ m⁻².', wrong = '{"0": "N m² C⁻² is the unit of the Coulomb constant k = 1/4πε₀, the reciprocal of the unit of ε₀.", "1": "N C⁻¹ is the unit of electric field strength, force per unit charge.", "2": "C m⁻² is the unit of surface charge density, charge per unit area."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0883' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Coulomb force between two charges is mutual: if one charge pushes the other, the other pushes back equally. Both forces have the size F = k(4q)(q)/r², since the product of the charges is the same for each. They act in opposite directions along the line joining the charges.', wrong = '{"1": "The force on each charge depends on the product of both charges, 4q × q, which is the same for both. So neither feels four times more.", "2": "The force on the +4q charge is not smaller. Both charges feel the same size of force.", "3": "Each charge exerts a force on the other. The smaller charge pushes on the larger one just as strongly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0884' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Relative permittivity εr tells how much a medium reduces the electrostatic force compared with a vacuum, where εr = 1. Air has εr ≈ 1.0006, so close to 1 that Coulomb''s law for a vacuum can be used in air with negligible error.', wrong = '{"0": "Distilled water has a very large relative permittivity, about 80, so it reduces the force between charges a great deal.", "1": "Glass has a relative permittivity of several units, roughly 5 to 10, far from 1.", "2": "Mica has a relative permittivity of several units, well above 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0885' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A volt is a joule per coulomb, and a joule is a newton metre. So 1 V m⁻¹ = 1 J C⁻¹ m⁻¹ = 1 N m C⁻¹ m⁻¹. The metres cancel, leaving 1 N C⁻¹. Both units describe electric field strength.', wrong = '{"1": "N m C⁻¹ is a joule per coulomb, which is the volt itself. Dividing by metres removes the m.", "2": "J C⁻¹ is the volt, the unit of potential difference, not of electric field.", "3": "C N⁻¹ is the unit turned upside down. Electric field is force per unit charge, N C⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0886' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The field of a point charge is E = kq/r². Here kq = 9 × 10⁹ × 4 × 10⁻⁹ = 36 N m² C⁻¹ and r² = (0.3)² = 0.09 m². So E = 36 ÷ 0.09 = 400 N C⁻¹. Remember to square the distance, since the field follows an inverse-square law.', wrong = '{"0": "40 N C⁻¹ is ten times too small, a slip in the powers of ten.", "1": "120 N C⁻¹ is 36 ÷ 0.3, dividing by r instead of r².", "3": "1200 N C⁻¹ is 36 ÷ 0.03, which mistakes r² for 0.03 instead of 0.09."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0887' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The electric field of a point charge is E = kq/r², an inverse-square law. Tripling the distance multiplies r² by 3² = 9, so the field becomes one-ninth of its original value.', wrong = '{"0": "One-third would be true for a 1/r law. The field of a point charge falls as 1/r².", "1": "The field weakens as the distance increases, so it cannot become three times larger.", "2": "The field gets weaker, not stronger, further from the charge. Nine times larger reverses the effect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0888' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Between two like charges the fields point in opposite directions, so E = 0 where their sizes are equal. Let the point be d from the +9 μC charge: 9/d² = 1/(4 − d)². Taking square roots, 3/d = 1/(4 − d), so 12 − 3d = d and d = 3 m.', wrong = '{"0": "1 m is the distance of the zero-field point from the +1 μC charge, not from the +9 μC charge.", "1": "At the midpoint the distances are equal, so the +9 μC charge''s field is nine times stronger. The fields do not cancel.", "3": "3.6 m uses a 9 : 1 ratio of distances. Because E ∝ 1/r², the distances must be in the ratio √9 : 1 = 3 : 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0889' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electric field lines are drawn so that the number of lines passing through a unit area at right angles to them is proportional to the field strength. So the field is strongest where the lines are closest together, as near a point charge.', wrong = '{"1": "Widely spaced lines show a weak field, as far from a charge where the lines spread out.", "2": "The curve of a field line shows how the direction of the field changes, not how strong it is.", "3": "Field lines never cross, because the field at any point has only one direction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0890' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the point midway between two identical positive charges, each charge produces a field of the same size, but pointing in opposite directions, away from each charge. The two fields cancel, so the resultant field is zero. This is a neutral point, or zero-field spot.', wrong = '{"0": "The two fields cancel at the midpoint, so the field there is zero, not strongest.", "1": "Field lines never cross, because the field at any point has only one direction.", "2": "Potential is a scalar, and both positive charges give a positive potential there. It is the field, a vector, that is zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0891' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A uniform electric field has the same size and direction at every point. Field lines show this by being parallel (same direction) and equally spaced (same strength). This is the pattern in the middle region between two oppositely charged parallel plates.', wrong = '{"1": "Radial lines spreading from a point show the field of a point charge, which weakens with distance and changes direction.", "2": "Electric field lines start on positive charges and end on negative charges, so they do not form closed loops.", "3": "Field lines never cross, because the field at any point has only one direction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0892' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electric flux through a flat area is Φ = EA cos θ, where θ is the angle between the field and the normal to the area. Here Φ = 200 N C⁻¹ × 0.5 m² × cos 60° = 100 × 0.5 = 50 N m² C⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "Zero flux needs the area to lie parallel to the field, with θ = 90° between E and the normal. Here θ = 60°.", "2": "86.6 N m² C⁻¹ uses sin 60° instead of cos 60°. The angle is measured from the normal, so cosine is used.", "3": "100 N m² C⁻¹ is EA, the maximum flux when the normal points along the field (θ = 0°)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0893' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Flux is Φ = EA cos θ, with θ measured between the field and the normal to the surface. When the surface lies parallel to the field lines, its normal is at 90° to the field, so cos 90° = 0 and no lines pass through it. The flux is zero.', wrong = '{"0": "When the surface is perpendicular to the field lines, its normal is along the field and the flux is maximum, EA.", "1": "A stronger field gives more flux through a surface at any angle other than parallel.", "3": "A larger area catches more field lines unless it lies parallel to them. Size alone does not make the flux zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0894' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gauss''s law says the total flux through a closed surface is Φ = Q/ε₀, where Q is the algebraic sum of the charges inside. Here Q = +3 μC − 1 μC = +2 μC. So Φ = 2 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 8.85 × 10⁻¹² ≈ 2.26 × 10⁵ N m² C⁻¹. The outside charge adds nothing.', wrong = '{"0": "1.13 × 10⁵ uses only 1 μC of charge. The enclosed charge is +3 − 1 = +2 μC.", "2": "4.52 × 10⁵ adds the sizes, 3 + 1 = 4 μC. The signs must be kept, giving +2 μC.", "3": "7.91 × 10⁵ also counts the +5 μC charge outside. Every line from an outside charge enters and leaves, so it adds no net flux."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0895' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Gauss''s law, the total flux through a closed surface is q/ε₀, which depends only on the charge enclosed and the medium, not on the size or shape of the surface. When the radius doubles, E falls to a quarter but the area becomes four times larger, so the flux stays the same.', wrong = '{"0": "The flux depends only on the charge enclosed, which has not changed, so it does not double.", "1": "The larger area makes up exactly for the weaker field, so the flux does not halve.", "2": "E does fall to a quarter, but the area grows four times, so their product, the flux, is unchanged."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0896' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Outside a charged conducting sphere, Gauss''s law shows the field is the same as if all the charge were at the centre: E = kq/r², with r measured from the centre. Here E = 9 × 10⁹ × 2 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ (0.3)² = 18 ÷ 0.09 = 200 N C⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "60 N C⁻¹ is 18 ÷ 0.3, dividing by r instead of r².", "2": "450 N C⁻¹ uses r = 0.2 m, the distance from the surface. The distance must be measured from the centre, 0.3 m.", "3": "1800 N C⁻¹ uses r = 0.1 m, the radius of the sphere, which gives the field at its surface, not at 30 cm."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0897' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The metal body of a car acts as a Faraday cage, a hollow conductor. If lightning strikes, the charge spreads over the outer metal surface and travels into the ground, while the electric field inside the hollow conductor stays zero. So the passengers inside are protected.', wrong = '{"1": "The protection comes from the metal body. Lightning can easily cross a small gap of rubber tyres, so they are not the reason.", "2": "Glass is an insulator, so it cannot carry the charge away. Only the metal shell does that.", "3": "Lightning can strike moving cars and aeroplanes. They are safe because of their metal shells."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0898' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A conductor has free electrons that move whenever there is a field along its surface. If the field just outside had a component parallel to the surface, these electrons would keep flowing. At electrostatic equilibrium no charge flows, so there is no parallel component, and the field must be perpendicular to the surface.', wrong = '{"1": "A field parallel to the surface would push the free electrons along it. At equilibrium there is no such flow.", "2": "The field is zero inside the conductor, not just outside a charged one. Outside there is a field at right angles to the surface.", "3": "A field at 45° would have a component along the surface, which would make charges move. At equilibrium the angle is 90°."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0899' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Excess charges on a conductor repel each other, and because they are free to move, they spread as far apart as possible. They end up on the outer surface of the conductor. At equilibrium the field inside the conductor is then zero.', wrong = '{"0": "Mutual repulsion drives the excess charges outward, so they do not stay spread through the volume.", "1": "Like charges repel, so they move away from each other, not together at the centre.", "3": "An isolated conductor keeps its excess charge. The charge only moves to the surface."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0900' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The potential difference between two points is the work done per unit charge in moving a positive charge from one point to the other, keeping it in equilibrium: ΔV = W/q. Here V_B − V_A = 6 J ÷ 2 C = 3 V, since 1 J C⁻¹ = 1 V.', wrong = '{"0": "0.33 V is 2 ÷ 6, which divides the charge by the work. Potential difference is work per unit charge.", "2": "8 comes from adding 6 J and 2 C. Quantities with different units cannot be added.", "3": "12 comes from multiplying the work by the charge. Potential difference divides the work by the charge."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0901' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Between parallel plates the field is uniform, and its size equals the potential gradient: E = ΔV/Δd. With the plate separation in metres, d = 2 cm = 0.02 m, so E = 300 V ÷ 0.02 m = 1.5 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹ = 15 000 V m⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "6 comes from multiplying the voltage by the distance (300 × 0.02). The field is voltage divided by distance.", "1": "150 is 300 ÷ 2, which leaves the distance in centimetres. It must be converted to 0.02 m.", "2": "1500 uses d = 0.2 m, a slip in converting 2 cm. 2 cm is 0.02 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0902' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The electric field equals the negative of the potential gradient, E = −ΔV/Δd. The minus sign means the field points in the direction in which the potential decreases, from high potential to low. A positive charge left free moves this way, losing potential energy.', wrong = '{"0": "The field points from higher to lower potential, so it is opposite to the direction in which the potential increases.", "1": "The field points along the steepest fall in potential, not at right angles to it. Equipotential surfaces are what lie at right angles to the field.", "2": "The minus sign is about direction compared with the potential change. It has nothing to do with where the potential is zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0903' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electric potential is the work done per unit charge. Work and charge are both scalars, so potential is a scalar: it has a size but no direction. The potential due to several charges is found by simple addition.', wrong = '{"0": "Electric field intensity is force per unit charge and has a direction, so it is a vector.", "1": "Electrostatic force acts along the line joining the charges in a definite direction, so it is a vector.", "2": "Vector area has a size and a direction, along the normal to the surface, so it is a vector."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0904' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A charge q accelerated through a potential difference ΔV gains kinetic energy qΔV. For an alpha particle, q = 2e, so it gains 2e × 500 V = 1000 eV. Since 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, this is 1000 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁶ J.', wrong = '{"0": "The energy is 1000 eV, not 1000 J. Each electron volt is only 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.", "2": "3.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ J uses a charge of 4e. An alpha particle has a charge of 2e.", "3": "8.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ J uses a charge of e. An alpha particle carries twice that, 2e."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0905' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The energy gained, eV, becomes kinetic energy: eV = ½mv². So v = √(2eV/m) = √(2 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 50 ÷ 9.1 × 10⁻³¹) = √(1.76 × 10¹³) ≈ 4.2 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹. This is fast, but still far below the speed of light.', wrong = '{"0": "1.8 × 10¹³ is v² in m² s⁻². The square root must still be taken.", "1": "3.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ is √(eV/m), which leaves out the factor 2 from ½mv².", "3": "4.2 × 10³ m s⁻¹ is a thousand times too small, a slip in the powers of ten."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0906' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The force on the electron is F = eE, and by Newton''s second law a = F/m = eE/m. Here F = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 2000 = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ N, so a = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ ÷ 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ ≈ 3.5 × 10¹⁴ m s⁻². The tiny mass gives a huge acceleration.', wrong = '{"0": "1.8 × 10¹¹ is e/m, the charge-to-mass ratio, without multiplying by the field E.", "1": "3.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ is the force eE in newtons. It must be divided by the mass.", "3": "5.7 × 10⁻¹⁵ m s⁻² is far too small, like a ratio with the mass on top. Acceleration is eE/m, a very large number for something as light as an electron."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0907' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Inside a uniform field the particle feels a constant force along the field lines. Its velocity component at right angles to the field stays constant, while the component along the field grows steadily. This is the same as a ball thrown horizontally under gravity, so the path is a parabola.', wrong = '{"1": "The constant force acts across the original motion and bends the path, so it cannot stay a straight line.", "2": "Circular motion needs a force that keeps changing direction to stay towards a centre. Here the force has a fixed direction.", "3": "A spiral would need a force that changes direction as the particle moves. The force in a uniform field is constant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0908' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The proton and electron have charges of the same size but opposite sign, so the field pushes them towards opposite plates with equal forces. Acceleration is a = eE/m, and the electron''s mass is about 1/1836 of the proton''s, so its acceleration, and hence its deflection, is much larger.', wrong = '{"0": "Opposite charges are pushed in opposite directions, and their very different masses give very different deflections.", "1": "The direction is right, but the electron''s much smaller mass gives it a much larger acceleration, so the deflections are not equal.", "3": "Opposite charges deflect towards opposite plates, and the lighter electron is deflected more, not less."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0909' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electric current is the rate of flow of charge: I = Q/t, with time in seconds. One minute is 60 s, so I = 90 C ÷ 60 s = 1.5 A. One ampere means one coulomb of charge passing each second.', wrong = '{"0": "0.67 is 60 ÷ 90, which divides the time by the charge. Current is charge divided by time.", "2": "90 A treats the time as 1 s. One minute is 60 s.", "3": "5400 multiplies the charge by the time. Current divides the charge by the time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0910' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A wire is already full of free electrons. When the switch is closed, an electric field is set up along the whole circuit almost at once, so all the free electrons start drifting together, including those inside the bulb. Each electron moves slowly, but the bulb lights almost immediately.', wrong = '{"0": "Individual electrons drift very slowly, around 10⁻⁴ m s⁻¹. They do not travel from the switch to the bulb at the speed of light.", "1": "A bulb does not store electrons. The electrons already in its filament start moving when the circuit is closed.", "3": "The drift velocity stays small at all times. The quick response comes from all the electrons moving together."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0911' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Conventional current is the imagined flow of positive charge. In the external circuit it flows from the positive terminal of the source to the negative terminal, that is, from higher potential to lower potential. This is opposite to the direction in which electrons actually drift.', wrong = '{"0": "Negative to positive is the direction in which electrons actually move, which is opposite to conventional current.", "1": "In the external circuit conventional current flows from higher to lower potential, as a positive charge would.", "2": "In a direct current circuit the current flows in one direction only, not both at once."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0912' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Current in a wire is I = nAve, where n is the number of free electrons per m³. So v = I/(nAe). Here nAe = 8.0 × 10²⁸ × 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 1.28 × 10⁴, so v = 1.28 ÷ 1.28 × 10⁴ = 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m s⁻¹ is a hundred times too small, a slip in the powers of ten in nAe.", "2": "1.0 × 10⁻² m s⁻¹ is a hundred times too large, again a slip in the powers of ten.", "3": "1.0 × 10² m s⁻¹ is far too fast. Drift velocities of electrons in wires are tiny, about 10⁻⁴ m s⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0913' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In series, the same current flows through both wires. Since I = nAve and n and e are the same for the same metal, the drift velocity v is inversely proportional to the area A. Halving the area doubles the drift velocity, so the electrons in the thin wire drift twice as fast.', wrong = '{"1": "For the same current, a smaller area needs the electrons to drift faster, not slower.", "2": "With n and e fixed, I = nAve makes v depend on 1/A, so the different areas give different drift velocities.", "3": "Four times would need v ∝ 1/A². The drift velocity is inversely proportional to the area itself."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0914' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ohm''s law states that the current through a conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across it, provided the physical state of the conductor, such as its temperature, does not change. If the conductor heats up, its resistance changes and V is no longer proportional to I.', wrong = '{"0": "Ohm''s law is stated here for steady direct current. Alternating current is not a condition for it.", "1": "Many semiconductor devices, such as diodes, do not obey Ohm''s law at all.", "3": "There is no such voltage limit. The condition is that the physical state, such as temperature, stays the same."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0915' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The resistance of a uniform wire is R = ρL/A. Here R = 1.69 × 10⁻⁸ Ω m × 10 m ÷ 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m² = 1.69 × 10⁻⁷ ÷ 10⁻⁶ = 0.169 Ω. This small value is why copper is used for connecting wires.', wrong = '{"0": "0.0169 Ω is ten times too small, a slip in the powers of ten.", "2": "1.69 Ω is ten times too large, a slip in the powers of ten.", "3": "16.9 Ω is a hundred times too large, a slip in the powers of ten."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0916' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'R = ρL/A, and the area of a round wire is proportional to the square of its diameter. Wire A has twice the length and, with twice the diameter, four times the area. So R_A/R_B = (2/4) ÷ 1 = 1/2, giving R_A : R_B = 1 : 2.', wrong = '{"1": "1 : 1 would follow if doubling the diameter only doubled the area. In fact it makes the area four times larger.", "2": "1 : 4 includes the larger area but forgets that wire A is also twice as long.", "3": "2 : 1 includes the extra length but forgets that wire A has four times the area."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0917' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Resistivity is a property of the material itself, not of the size or shape of a sample. Cutting the wire in half halves the resistance of each piece, because R = ρL/A, but the resistivity ρ of the metal stays exactly the same.', wrong = '{"0": "The resistance of each half is halved, but the resistivity, a property of the material, does not change.", "1": "Resistivity depends on the material and its temperature, not on the length of the piece.", "2": "Resistivity does not depend on the dimensions of the wire, so it does not change to a quarter."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0918' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Conductivity is the reciprocal of resistivity, σ = 1/ρ. Resistivity is measured in ohm metres (Ω m), so conductivity is measured in (Ω m)⁻¹ = Ω⁻¹ m⁻¹, also written mho m⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "Ω m is the unit of resistivity, ρ. Conductivity is its reciprocal.", "2": "Ω⁻¹ (mho or siemens) is the unit of conductance, the reciprocal of resistance, not of conductivity.", "3": "Ω m⁻¹ is not the reciprocal of Ω m. Inverting Ω m gives Ω⁻¹ m⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0919' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Resistance comes from free electrons colliding with the atoms of the metal lattice. As the temperature rises, the atoms vibrate with larger amplitude, so they present a bigger target and the electrons collide with them more often. More collisions mean a greater resistance.', wrong = '{"1": "In a metal the number of free electrons stays almost constant as temperature changes.", "2": "Heating makes a wire expand slightly, not shrink, and this effect is far too small to explain the rise in resistance.", "3": "If electrons got through more easily, the resistance would fall. In metals the extra collisions make it rise."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0920' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Resistance varies with temperature as R_t = R₀(1 + αt). Here R_t = 50 Ω × (1 + 0.004 × 100) = 50 × 1.4 = 70 Ω. The resistance rises by R₀αt = 50 × 0.4 = 20 Ω, a 40% increase over the 100 K rise.', wrong = '{"0": "20 Ω is only the increase in resistance, R₀αt = 50 × 0.4. It must be added to the original 50 Ω.", "1": "50.4 Ω adds αt = 0.4 directly to 50 Ω. The increase is R₀ × αt = 20 Ω.", "3": "120 Ω is far too large. A coefficient of 0.004 K⁻¹ over 100 K raises R by only 40%, to 70 Ω."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0921' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The temperature coefficient is the fractional change in resistance per kelvin: α = (R_t − R₀)/(R₀t). Here α = (46 − 40) ÷ (40 × 50) = 6 ÷ 2000 = 3.0 × 10⁻³ K⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "0.12 is 6 ÷ 50, which forgets to divide by the original resistance R₀ = 40 Ω.", "1": "0.15 is 6 ÷ 40, which forgets to divide by the temperature change of 50 K.", "2": "2.6 × 10⁻³ divides by 46 Ω, the resistance at 50 °C. The definition uses R₀, the resistance at 0 °C."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0922' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A negative temperature coefficient means the resistance falls as temperature rises. Semiconductors such as silicon and germanium behave this way, because heating frees more charge carriers. Metals have positive coefficients.', wrong = '{"0": "Copper is a metal, and its resistance rises with temperature, so its coefficient is positive.", "1": "Tungsten is a metal with a positive coefficient. A light bulb filament''s resistance rises a lot as it heats up.", "3": "Nichrome has a small positive coefficient, which is why it is used in heating elements."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0923' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The temperature coefficient α tells how much resistance changes per kelvin. Constantan has α of only about 0.00001 K⁻¹, hundreds of times smaller than for ordinary metals. So its resistance hardly changes with temperature, which makes it useful for standard resistors.', wrong = '{"0": "Tungsten has α of about 0.0046 K⁻¹, hundreds of times larger than constantan''s.", "1": "Iron has α of about 0.0052 K⁻¹, one of the larger values among the metals listed.", "2": "Copper has α of about 0.0039 K⁻¹, much larger than constantan''s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0924' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Power is P = V²/R, so R = V²/P. For this heater, R = (220 V)² ÷ 1100 W = 48 400 ÷ 1100 = 44 Ω. Check: the current is P/V = 5 A, and V/I = 220 ÷ 5 = 44 Ω.', wrong = '{"0": "0.2 is 220 ÷ 1100, which divides the voltage by the power. That is not a resistance.", "1": "5 is P/V = 1100 ÷ 220, the current in amperes, not the resistance.", "3": "4840 Ω is a slip in dividing 48 400 by 1100. The correct result is 44 Ω."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0925' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The power dissipated in a resistor is P = I²R. With R fixed, P is proportional to the square of the current. Doubling the current multiplies I² by 2² = 4, so the power becomes four times as large.', wrong = '{"0": "Twice as large treats P as proportional to I. Since P = I²R, doubling I gives four times the power.", "1": "More current through the same resistor produces more heat, not less.", "2": "P = I²R depends on the current, so changing the current changes the power."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0926' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The emf of a source is the energy it gives to each unit of charge passing through it: E = W/Q. Its unit is the joule per coulomb, which is the volt. So emf is measured in volts, even though its name contains the word force.', wrong = '{"1": "Despite its name, emf is not a force and is not measured in newtons. It is energy per unit charge.", "2": "The charge a cell can deliver is measured in coulombs or ampere-hours, not in volts.", "3": "Power is energy per second, measured in watts. Emf is energy per unit charge."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0927' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The current is I = E/(R + r) = 6 V ÷ (2.5 + 0.5) Ω = 2 A. The terminal potential difference is the voltage across the external resistor: V = IR = 2 A × 2.5 Ω = 5 V. Check: V = E − Ir = 6 − 2 × 0.5 = 5 V.', wrong = '{"0": "1 V is Ir = 2 × 0.5, the voltage lost inside the cell, not the terminal potential difference.", "2": "6 V is the emf. The terminal voltage equals it only when no current is drawn.", "3": "When a cell supplies current, some voltage is lost inside it, so the terminal voltage must be less than the 6 V emf."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0928' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'On open circuit the reading equals the emf, so E = 9.0 V. With the resistor connected, I = V/R = 8.0 V ÷ 4 Ω = 2 A. From E = V + Ir: 9.0 = 8.0 + 2r, so the internal resistance is r = 1.0 V ÷ 2 A = 0.5 Ω.', wrong = '{"0": "0.25 Ω divides the lost 1.0 V by 4 instead of by the 2 A current.", "2": "2 is the current in amperes, I = 8 V ÷ 4 Ω. It is not the internal resistance.", "3": "4.5 is 9 V ÷ 2 A, which gives the total resistance of the circuit, not the internal resistance alone."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0929' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The terminal potential difference is V = E − Ir, where r is the internal resistance. Some voltage is lost inside the cell whenever current flows. V equals the emf E only when I = 0, that is, when no current is drawn from the cell (open circuit).', wrong = '{"0": "A large current makes the lost volts, Ir, large, so the terminal voltage falls well below the emf.", "1": "With zero external resistance the cell is short-circuited, and the terminal voltage falls to zero.", "3": "A large internal resistance makes the lost volts larger whenever current flows, so the terminal voltage falls further below the emf."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0930' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kirchhoff''s first rule says the total current flowing into a junction equals the total current flowing out, because charge cannot pile up at a point. Here 3 + 5 = 2 + I, so 8 = 2 + I and I = 6 A.', wrong = '{"0": "With I = 4 A the outflow would be 2 + 4 = 6 A, which is less than the 8 A flowing in. Charge would build up at the junction.", "2": "8 A is the total current flowing in. It forgets that 2 A of this already leaves through the other wire.", "3": "10 A comes from adding all three known currents, 3 + 5 + 2. The 2 A is an outgoing current and must be subtracted."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0931' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kirchhoff''s first rule states that the sum of currents entering a junction equals the sum leaving it. Since a junction cannot create, store or destroy charge, whatever charge flows in each second must flow out, so the rule expresses conservation of charge.', wrong = '{"0": "Conservation of energy is the basis of Kirchhoff''s second (loop) rule: the sum of emfs around a loop equals the sum of potential drops.", "1": "Momentum plays no part in either of Kirchhoff''s rules. They deal only with charge and energy in circuits.", "3": "The rule is about the flow of charge, not mass. Current is the rate of flow of charge, measured in coulombs per second."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0932' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kirchhoff''s second rule says the sum of emfs around a closed loop equals the sum of IR drops. Since the cells oppose each other, the net emf is 12 − 4 = 8 V. Then I = net emf ÷ total resistance = 8 ÷ 4 = 2 A.', wrong = '{"0": "1 A comes from using only the 4 V cell: 4 ÷ 4 = 1 A. The 12 V cell is ignored.", "2": "3 A comes from using only the 12 V cell: 12 ÷ 4 = 3 A. The opposing 4 V cell is ignored.", "3": "4 A comes from adding the emfs, (12 + 4) ÷ 4. That would be right only if the cells helped each other, but here they oppose."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0933' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At balance no current flows through the galvanometer, so B and D are at the same potential. The balance condition is then AB/BC = AD/DC. Substituting, 10/20 = X/30, so X = 30 × 10/20 = 15 Ω.', wrong = '{"0": "6.7 Ω comes from inverting the ratio: X = 10 × 20/30 ≈ 6.7 Ω. The pairs of arms are matched the wrong way round.", "2": "20 Ω simply repeats the value of BC. With DA = 20 Ω, AD/DC = 20/30, which does not equal AB/BC = 1/2.", "3": "60 Ω comes from using BC/AB instead of AB/BC: X = 30 × 20/10 = 60 Ω. The ratio is upside down."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0934' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A Wheatstone bridge is balanced when the ratios of the resistances in its arms are equal. Then the two ends of the galvanometer are at the same potential, so no current flows through it and it shows zero deflection.', wrong = '{"0": "Balance is defined by zero galvanometer current, not maximum. A large galvanometer current means the bridge is far from balance.", "1": "The battery still drives current through the four arms. Only the galvanometer branch carries no current at balance.", "2": "The four resistances need not be equal. Only the ratios must match, for example 10/20 = 15/30."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0935' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With a steady current, the potential drop along a uniform potentiometer wire is proportional to length. So the emf balanced at length ℓ is E_x = E × ℓ/L = 2.0 × 75/100 = 1.5 V.', wrong = '{"0": "0.5 V is the drop across the remaining 25 cm of wire, 2.0 × 25/100. The balancing length is 75 cm, not 25 cm.", "2": "2.0 V is the voltage across the whole 100 cm wire. The cell balances at only 75 cm, so its emf is smaller.", "3": "2.7 V comes from dividing 2.0 by 0.75 instead of multiplying. An emf larger than the total wire voltage could never be balanced on it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0936' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'On the same potentiometer wire, emf is proportional to the balancing length, so E₁/E₂ = ℓ₁/ℓ₂. Rearranging, E₂ = E₁ × ℓ₂/ℓ₁ = 1.5 × 40/60 = 1.0 V.', wrong = '{"0": "0.6 V comes from 1.5 × 40/100, using a 100 cm length in place of the first cell''s balancing length of 60 cm.", "2": "Equal emfs would balance at equal lengths. Here the lengths are 60 cm and 40 cm, so the emfs must differ.", "3": "2.25 V comes from inverting the ratio, 1.5 × 60/40. A shorter balancing length means a smaller emf, not a larger one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0937' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the balance point the galvanometer shows no deflection, so no current is drawn from the cell being tested. With zero current there is no voltage drop across the cell''s internal resistance, so the full emf is measured. An ordinary voltmeter always draws some current.', wrong = '{"1": "Its accuracy comes from the null (zero current) condition, not from having a small resistance.", "2": "The driver cell only sets up a steady potential drop along the wire. A bigger driver cell does not improve accuracy.", "3": "A potentiometer compares potential differences. It is a voltage-measuring device, not an ammeter."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0938' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a null method the setup is adjusted until the galvanometer shows zero current. With no current through it, the galvanometer''s own resistance causes no voltage drop and has no effect on the result, which makes null methods very accurate.', wrong = '{"0": "In a null method the galvanometer is only used to detect zero current. It is a sensitive detector of small currents, not a meter for large ones.", "1": "A battery or other source is still needed to set up the currents and potential differences being compared.", "3": "The galvanometer''s resistance does not matter at the null point, so it does not need to be zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0939' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A thermistor is a heat-sensitive resistor. Most thermistors have a negative temperature coefficient: heating gives the charge carriers (electrons or holes) more energy so they move more freely, and the resistance decreases as temperature rises.', wrong = '{"0": "Resistance rising with temperature is true of metals and of positive temperature coefficient (PTC) thermistors, not of most thermistors.", "1": "Thermistors are designed to be strongly temperature-sensitive. A constant resistance would make them useless as sensors.", "2": "Thermistor resistance changes smoothly with temperature. It does not rise and then drop to zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0940' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Thermistors are semiconductor ceramics. They are made by heating, under high pressure, mixtures of metallic oxides such as those of manganese, nickel, cobalt, copper and iron. These are pressed into shapes like beads, rods or washers and baked at high temperature.', wrong = '{"1": "Copper and silver are good metallic conductors whose resistance rises slightly with temperature. They are not used to make thermistors.", "2": "Cadmium sulphide deposited in a pattern on an insulating plate is how a light dependent resistor (LDR) is made.", "3": "Carbon fibres in concrete form a conducting network used to monitor the condition of concrete bridges."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0941' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Thermistors act as temperature sensors: they turn a change in temperature into a change in voltage. One example is the coolant temperature sensor in a car engine, which helps prevent overheating. Others are digital thermometers and limiting the inrush current when a device is switched on.', wrong = '{"1": "A street light that switches on at dusk uses an LDR, whose resistance rises as light fades.", "2": "Camera exposure control uses an LDR to adjust exposure according to the amount of light available.", "3": "Finding an unknown emf by a null method is done with a potentiometer, not a thermistor."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0942' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An LDR is made of a semiconductor such as cadmium sulphide. In darkness it has few free electrons, so its resistance is high. Light photons give energy to electrons and free them to conduct, so the resistance decreases as light intensity increases.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the reverse. More light frees more electrons, so the resistance goes down, not up.", "1": "An LDR is defined by its sensitivity to light. If its resistance did not depend on light, it could not sense light levels.", "3": "An LDR is designed to respond to light intensity, not to temperature. Temperature sensing is the job of a thermistor."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0943' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a voltage divider the supply voltage is shared in proportion to resistance, so the larger resistor takes the larger share. In darkness the LDR''s resistance is much higher than 100 kΩ, but in bright light it becomes very low, so the voltage across the LDR falls.', wrong = '{"0": "Bright light lowers the LDR''s resistance, it does not raise it. So the LDR''s share of the voltage falls.", "1": "The supply is fixed, but how it is shared depends on the two resistances. When the LDR''s resistance changes, its voltage changes too.", "2": "The LDR would take nearly all the supply only if its resistance became very large compared with 100 kΩ, which happens in darkness, not bright light."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0944' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Carbon fibres embedded in the concrete slab form a conducting network. If its resistance stays steady over time, the bridge is keeping its structural strength. A rising resistance means the concrete is deteriorating or the fibres are being damaged, for example by cracks pulling them apart.', wrong = '{"0": "A stronger bridge would leave the fibre network intact, so its resistance would stay the same, not increase.", "1": "The rising resistance is used as a sign of damage to the structure, not as a measure of cooling.", "2": "If the fibres became better conductors, the network''s resistance would fall, not rise."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0945' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 1820 Hans Christian Oersted noticed that a current-carrying wire deflects a nearby compass needle. This showed that an electric current produces a magnetic field, the first link found between electricity and magnetism.', wrong = '{"0": "Michael Faraday discovered electromagnetic induction in 1831, the reverse effect, where a changing magnetic field produces a current.", "1": "Heinrich Lenz gave the rule (1834) for the direction of an induced current. He did not discover the magnetic effect of current.", "2": "Charles de Coulomb measured the force between electric charges. That is electrostatics, not electromagnetism."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0946' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Lodestone is naturally magnetised magnetite, an iron oxide with formula Fe₃O₄. It attracts iron and steel and lines up with the Earth''s magnetic poles, which led to the invention of the compass.', wrong = '{"0": "Haematite, Fe₂O₃, is a common iron ore but it is only weakly magnetic. It is not lodestone.", "1": "Iron pyrite, FeS₂, is an iron sulphide (fool''s gold), not an oxide, and it is not lodestone.", "2": "Rust is hydrated iron oxide formed by corrosion. It is not the natural magnetic mineral lodestone."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0947' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The force on a current-carrying wire in a magnetic field is F = BIL sin θ, where θ is the angle between the wire and the field. Substituting, F = 0.2 × 4 × 0.5 × sin 30° = 0.4 × 0.5 = 0.2 N.', wrong = '{"0": "0.1 N is half the correct value, as if sin 30° were applied twice.", "2": "0.35 N comes from using cos 30° instead of sin 30°: 0.4 × 0.866 ≈ 0.35 N. The force depends on the sine of the angle.", "3": "0.4 N is BIL, the force when the wire is perpendicular to the field (θ = 90°). At 30° it is only half of this."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0948' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The force on a current-carrying wire is F = BIL sin θ. With the wire at right angles to the field, sin 90° = 1, so F = BIL and B = F/(IL). Here IL = 5 × 0.3 = 1.5 A m, so B = 0.6 ÷ 1.5 = 0.4 T.', wrong = '{"0": "With B = 0.09 T the force would be 0.09 × 5 × 0.3 = 0.135 N, not 0.6 N.", "2": "0.9 T comes from multiplying 0.6 by 1.5 instead of dividing. It would give a force of 1.35 N.", "3": "2.5 T comes from dividing IL by F, 1.5 ÷ 0.6. The correct form is B = F/(IL)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0949' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In Fleming''s left-hand rule the thumb, first finger and second finger are held at right angles. The First finger points along the Field, the seCond finger along the Current, and the thuMb gives the force on the conductor, which is the direction of Motion.', wrong = '{"1": "The direction of an induced current is found with Fleming''s right-hand rule, used for generators, not the left-hand rule.", "2": "The magnetic field around the wire itself is found with the right-hand grip rule.", "3": "Fleming''s left-hand rule does not give an electric field. It gives the magnetic force on the current."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0950' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The wire''s own circular field adds to the external field on one side and partly cancels it on the other. The field lines on the strong side act like stretched bands that push the wire away, so it moves towards the side where the resultant field is weaker. This force is perpendicular to both the wire and the field.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the reverse. The crowded field lines on the stronger side push the wire away from that side, towards the weaker one.", "1": "The magnetic force on the wire is perpendicular to the wire, so it cannot act along its length.", "3": "The force is perpendicular to the external field, so the wire is not pushed along the field lines."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0951' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The force on a current-carrying conductor is F = IL × B, the vector (cross) product of L and B multiplied by the current. A cross product is always perpendicular to both of the vectors in it, so the force is at right angles to both the conductor (L) and the magnetic field B. Its size is BIL sin θ.', wrong = '{"0": "F = IL × B is perpendicular to B. A force parallel to the field never acts on a current-carrying wire.", "1": "F is perpendicular to L. If the wire lies along its own length direction the cross product gives no component along it.", "2": "A cross product does not point between its two vectors. It points at right angles to the plane containing both."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0952' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Magnetic flux is Φ = BA cos θ, where θ is the angle between the field and the normal to the loop. Substituting, Φ = 0.5 × 0.04 × cos 60° = 0.02 × 0.5 = 0.01 Wb.', wrong = '{"0": "The flux is zero only when the field lies parallel to the plane of the loop, which means θ = 90° between B and the normal. Here θ = 60°.", "2": "0.017 Wb comes from using sin 60° instead of cos 60°: 0.02 × 0.866 ≈ 0.017 Wb. The angle is measured from the normal, so cosine is used.", "3": "0.02 Wb is BA, the maximum flux when the normal points along B (θ = 0°). At 60° the flux is half of this."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0953' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Flux is Φ = BA cos θ, with θ measured between B and the normal (vector area) of the coil. When the field lies parallel to the plane of the coil, θ = 90° and cos 90° = 0, so no field lines pass through the coil and Φ = 0.', wrong = '{"0": "A field perpendicular to the plane is along the normal, so θ = 0° and the flux is maximum, Φ = BA.", "1": "A weak field still sends some field lines through the coil unless it lies parallel to the plane. The flux is small but not zero.", "2": "The number of turns affects the flux linkage NΦ, not whether the flux through each turn is zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0954' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Magnetic flux is Φ = BA, so 1 Wb = 1 T × 1 m². From F = BIL, B = F/(IL), so the tesla is N A⁻¹ m⁻¹. Multiplying by m² gives 1 Wb = (N A⁻¹ m⁻¹)(m²) = N m A⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "N A⁻¹ m⁻¹ is the tesla, the unit of flux density B. Flux needs an extra factor of area, m².", "2": "N A m⁻¹ puts the ampere in the numerator. From F = BIL, the ampere must be in the denominator.", "3": "N m⁻² is the pascal, the unit of pressure, not of magnetic flux."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0955' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Magnetic flux is defined as Φ = B · A = BA cos θ, where A is the vector area, a vector along the normal to the surface. The dot (scalar) product of two vectors has size but no direction, so flux is a scalar.', wrong = '{"0": "A cross product of two vectors gives another vector. Flux is defined with the dot product, not the cross product.", "1": "Flux can be negative when θ is greater than 90°. Anyway, a quantity being positive does not make it a scalar.", "3": "Flux is measured in webers. The tesla is the unit of flux density B, not of flux."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0956' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Flux linkage is the flux through one turn multiplied by the number of turns, NΦ. Converting 3 mWb = 3 × 10⁻³ Wb, the linkage is 200 × 3 × 10⁻³ = 0.6 Wb-turns.', wrong = '{"0": "0.015 comes from dividing 3 by 200, flux by turns. Flux linkage is the product NΦ, not a ratio.", "2": "6.0 comes from a wrong conversion, taking 3 mWb as 0.03 Wb. Milli means 10⁻³, so 3 mWb = 0.003 Wb.", "3": "66.7 comes from dividing the turns by the flux, 200 ÷ 3. The flux and turns must be multiplied."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0957' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The magnetic force on a moving charge is F = qvB sin θ. At right angles sin 90° = 1, so F = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 2 × 10⁶ × 0.5 = 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ N.', wrong = '{"1": "3.2 × 10⁻¹³ N is qv alone, 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 2 × 10⁶. It leaves out B = 0.5 T.", "2": "6.4 × 10⁻¹³ N comes from dividing by B = 0.5 instead of multiplying by it.", "3": "8.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ N is half the correct force, as if sin 90° were 0.5. At right angles sin θ = 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0958' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The magnetic force on a moving charge is F = qvB sin θ. With q, v and B unchanged, the force is proportional to sin θ. Since sin 90° ÷ sin 30° = 1 ÷ 0.5 = 2, the force at 90° is 2F.', wrong = '{"0": "F/2 has the change the wrong way round. The force is largest at 90°, since sin 90° is bigger than sin 30°.", "1": "The force depends on the angle through sin θ, so changing the angle from 30° to 90° changes the force.", "3": "√3F comes from cos 30°/sin 30°. The correct ratio is sin 90°/sin 30° = 2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0959' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The magnetic force supplies the centripetal force, so evB = mv²/r and r = mv/(eB). Substituting, r = (9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × 2.0 × 10⁶)/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 0.01) = 1.82 × 10⁻²⁴ ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻²¹ ≈ 1.1 × 10⁻³ m.', wrong = '{"1": "1.1 × 10⁻⁴ m is ten times too small, a slip in the powers of ten. 1.82 × 10⁻²⁴ ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻²¹ is about 1.1 × 10⁻³.", "2": "1.1 × 10⁻² m is ten times too large, a slip in the powers of ten, for example taking B as 0.001 T.", "3": "1.1 m is a thousand times too large. A light electron in a 0.01 T field turns in a path about a millimetre across."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0960' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The magnetic force F = qv × B is always perpendicular to the velocity. A force at right angles to motion does no work, so it changes only the direction of motion, not the speed. Hence the kinetic energy stays constant.', wrong = '{"0": "A force perpendicular to the motion does no work, so it cannot add kinetic energy to the particle.", "1": "The magnetic force has no component along the velocity, so it cannot slow the particle. It only bends its path.", "2": "The particle keeps moving in its circle at constant speed. Its kinetic energy never drops to zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0961' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The magnetic force on a moving charge is F = qv × B. With the same v and B, the vector v × B is the same for both, but the proton''s charge is positive and the electron''s is negative. So the forces point in opposite directions and they curve opposite ways.', wrong = '{"1": "Reversing the sign of the charge reverses the force. A proton and an electron cannot feel forces in the same direction here.", "2": "Both are charged and both move across the field, so each feels a force qvB.", "3": "The magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity, never along it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0962' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a velocity selector the electric and magnetic forces are opposite. A particle passes straight through when they balance, qE = qvB, so v = E/B. Here v = 6000 ÷ 0.3 = 2.0 × 10⁴ m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "1.8 × 10³ m s⁻¹ comes from multiplying E by B, 6000 × 0.3. The speed is the ratio E/B.", "2": "5.0 × 10⁻⁵ m s⁻¹ comes from dividing B by E, 0.3 ÷ 6000. The ratio is upside down.", "3": "6.0 × 10³ m s⁻¹ simply copies the value of E and ignores B."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0963' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The electric force qE does not depend on speed, but the magnetic force qvB grows with speed. At v = E/B they balance. For a faster particle qvB is larger than qE, so the magnetic force wins and the particle is deflected in its direction.', wrong = '{"0": "The electric force wins only for slower particles, v < E/B. For a faster particle qvB exceeds qE.", "1": "Only particles with exactly v = E/B pass straight through, because only then are the two forces equal.", "3": "Both forces act sideways to the motion. Neither one acts against the velocity to bring the particle to rest."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0964' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A particle goes straight through when qE = qvB. The charge q appears on both sides and cancels, giving v = E/B. Mass does not appear at all, so the selected speed depends only on E and B.', wrong = '{"0": "The charge cancels from qE = qvB, so the selected speed is the same for any charge.", "1": "Mass does not appear in the balance condition, so v = E/B is the same for light and heavy particles.", "2": "The length of the region does not enter v = E/B. Only the two field strengths fix the selected speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0965' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Passing straight through gives v = E/B = 4000 ÷ 0.2 = 2 × 10⁴ m s⁻¹. In the magnetic field alone, r = mv/(qB), so q/m = v/(Br) = E/(B²r) = 4000 ÷ (0.04 × 0.05) = 2.0 × 10⁶ C kg⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "With q/m = 1.0 × 10⁸ C kg⁻¹ the radius would be r = v ÷ (B × q/m) = 2 × 10⁴ ÷ (1 × 10⁸ × 0.2) = 1 mm, not 5 cm.", "2": "4.0 × 10⁵ C kg⁻¹ comes from E/(Br) = 4000 ÷ (0.2 × 0.05). B must be squared, since v = E/B brings in a second B.", "3": "8.0 × 10⁶ C kg⁻¹ comes from E/(Br²) = 4000 ÷ (0.2 × 0.0025). It squares r instead of B."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0966' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A rod moving across a magnetic field has an emf ε = vBL induced between its ends, because the field pushes the free charges along the rod. Substituting, ε = 5 × 0.3 × 0.4 = 0.6 V.', wrong = '{"0": "0.024 V comes from multiplying B and L but dividing by v: 0.3 × 0.4 ÷ 5. The emf grows with speed, so v must multiply.", "2": "1.5 V is vB = 5 × 0.3. It leaves out the length L = 0.4 m.", "3": "6.0 V is ten times too large, as if the rod were 4 m long instead of 0.4 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0967' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Only the component of velocity at right angles to the field produces an emf, so ε = vBL sin θ. Changing θ from 90° to 30° multiplies the emf by sin 30° ÷ sin 90° = 0.5, so it is half as large.', wrong = '{"1": "sin 30° is smaller than sin 90°, so the emf goes down, not up.", "2": "The emf depends on the angle through sin θ. Only the velocity component across the field cuts field lines.", "3": "The emf is zero only when the rod moves along the field (θ = 0°). At 30° part of its motion still cuts field lines."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0968' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Faraday''s law gives the average emf as ε = NΔΦ/Δt. The flux change per turn is 0.020 − 0.005 = 0.015 Wb. So ε = 100 × 0.015 ÷ 0.1 = 15 V.', wrong = '{"0": "0.15 V is ΔΦ/Δt for a single turn, 0.015 ÷ 0.1. It leaves out the 100 turns.", "1": "1.5 V is NΔΦ = 100 × 0.015 without dividing by the time of 0.1 s.", "3": "25 V comes from adding the two flux values, 100 × (0.020 + 0.005) ÷ 0.1. The emf depends on the change in flux."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0969' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With the coil perpendicular to the field, Φ = BA, so ε = NAΔB/Δt. The flux change per turn is 0.02 × 0.6 = 0.012 Wb. Then ε = 50 × 0.012 ÷ 0.2 = 3 V.', wrong = '{"0": "0.06 V is AΔB/Δt for one turn, 0.012 ÷ 0.2. It leaves out the 50 turns.", "1": "0.3 V is ten times too small. NΔΦ = 50 × 0.012 = 0.6 Wb-turns, and 0.6 ÷ 0.2 = 3 V.", "3": "6 V doubles the rate of change, as if the time were 0.1 s instead of 0.2 s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0970' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With the field fixed and perpendicular to the loop, the flux changes only because the area changes: ΔΦ/Δt = B(ΔA/Δt). So ε = 0.4 × 0.5 = 0.2 V.', wrong = '{"0": "0.08 V comes from squaring B, 0.4² × 0.5. The emf is simply B times the rate of change of area.", "2": "0.8 V comes from dividing B by the rate, 0.4 ÷ 0.5. The two must be multiplied.", "3": "1.25 V comes from dividing the rate by B, 0.5 ÷ 0.4. The two must be multiplied."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0971' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Faraday''s law gives ε = NΔΦ/Δt. Doubling N doubles the emf, and making the same ΔΦ happen in half the time doubles it again. So the emf becomes 2 × 2 = 4 times as large.', wrong = '{"1": "Twice as large counts only one of the two changes. Both doubling N and halving Δt double the emf.", "2": "Both changes increase the emf, so it cannot stay the same.", "3": "Both changes increase the emf. Halving the time makes the flux change faster, not slower."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0972' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The minus sign in Faraday''s law expresses Lenz''s law. It shows that the induced emf, and any current it drives, acts in the direction that opposes the change in flux producing it. This is a consequence of conservation of energy.', wrong = '{"0": "The sign shows direction relative to the flux change. The emf is positive or negative depending on whether the flux is rising or falling.", "1": "Faraday''s law applies equally to increasing and decreasing flux. The minus sign does not say which is happening.", "3": "The minus sign reflects conservation of energy. Energy is not created: work done against the opposing effect becomes electrical energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0973' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Lenz''s law the induced current opposes the change causing it, here the approach of the magnet. To oppose it, the near face of the coil becomes a north pole and repels the approaching north pole, so work must be done to push the magnet in.', wrong = '{"0": "A south pole would attract the magnet and help the motion. That would create energy from nothing, which Lenz''s law rules out.", "1": "Moving the magnet changes the flux through the coil, so an emf is induced and a current flows in a closed coil.", "2": "A north pole facing a north pole repels it. Two like poles cannot attract."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0974' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Pulling the north pole away reduces the flux through the coil. By Lenz''s law the induced current opposes this change, so the near face becomes a south pole. It attracts the receding north pole and resists its removal.', wrong = '{"0": "A north pole would repel the magnet and help it move away. That would assist the change instead of opposing it.", "1": "A north pole facing a north pole repels it. Like poles cannot attract.", "2": "A decreasing flux induces an emf just as an increasing flux does, so the coil does develop a pole."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0975' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If the induced current helped the change that caused it, a magnet pushed into a coil would be pulled in faster and faster, gaining kinetic energy from nothing. By opposing the change, the induced current makes us do work, and that work becomes electrical energy. So Lenz''s law follows from conservation of energy.', wrong = '{"1": "Conservation of charge is the basis of Kirchhoff''s first (junction) rule, not of Lenz''s law.", "2": "Lenz''s law is about the energy balance between mechanical work and electrical energy, not about momentum.", "3": "No mass is created or destroyed in electromagnetic induction, so conservation of mass does not explain the direction of induced current."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0976' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The induced current flows through the rod, and a current in a magnetic field feels a force F = ILB. By Lenz''s law this force opposes the rod''s motion. To keep the speed constant, an external force must balance it, and the work it does is converted into electrical energy.', wrong = '{"0": "Even on frictionless rails a force is needed, because the magnetic force on the induced current opposes the motion.", "1": "If the induced current sped the rod up, it would gain energy from nothing. This breaks conservation of energy and Lenz''s law.", "3": "The mass of the rod does not change as it moves. The extra force is needed to work against the magnetic drag."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0977' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The induced emf, ε = NΔΦ/Δt, depends on the number of turns and on how fast the flux changes, which increases with faster relative motion. The coil''s resistance does not appear in the formula. A higher resistance only reduces the current that the emf drives, so it does not increase the emf.', wrong = '{"0": "The emf is proportional to N, so more turns give a larger emf.", "1": "The emf is proportional to the rate of change of flux, so changing the flux faster increases it.", "3": "Moving the magnet faster changes the flux through the coil more quickly, which increases the emf."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0978' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An alternating current in the coil under the glass produces a rapidly changing magnetic flux that passes through the metal base of the pot. This changing flux induces currents in the metal, and their heating effect warms the pot and the water. The glass is not a conductor, so no currents flow in it.', wrong = '{"0": "The glass stays cool, so it is not supplying the heat. Heat is produced in the metal pot itself.", "1": "An induction cooker uses a changing magnetic flux from a coil, not microwaves. A microwave oven is a different device.", "2": "There is no flame in an induction cooker. Heating comes from currents induced in the pot."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0979' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A ferrofluid is a liquid that also shows magnetic properties. It is a colloidal suspension of tiny magnetic particles, usually iron oxide about 10 nm across, in a carrier liquid such as oil or water. The particles stay spread out rather than settling or dissolving.', wrong = '{"1": "Mercury is a liquid metal, but it is not magnetic and contains no suspended magnetic particles.", "2": "In a ferrofluid the magnetic particles are suspended as solid nanoparticles, not dissolved like an iron salt.", "3": "A ferrofluid is a liquid with suspended particles. It becomes magnetised only while a magnet is nearby."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0980' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A surfactant is a substance that reduces surface tension. Coating each magnetic nanoparticle with it stops the particles from clumping together, so they stay evenly spread through the carrier fluid instead of forming lumps.', wrong = '{"0": "The particles are only temporarily magnetised when a magnet is near. The coating does not make them permanent magnets.", "2": "A surfactant lowers surface tension, it does not raise it. Its job here is to keep the particles apart.", "3": "The particles stay suspended as solid nanoparticles. The coating does not dissolve them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0981' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Near a strong magnet the particles become magnetised and the magnetic forces pull the fluid upwards into chains along the field lines. Gravity and surface tension pull it back down. Spikes form where the magnetic forces win over the other two.', wrong = '{"0": "Electric forces and friction are not what shape the spikes. They come from magnetic forces competing with surface tension and gravity.", "1": "Buoyancy and viscosity alone cannot explain the spikes. The magnetic pull upward is the key force, against gravity and surface tension.", "3": "Nuclear forces act only inside atomic nuclei, so they play no part in the shape of a fluid."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0982' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The voice coil of a loudspeaker heats up while it works. The speaker''s magnetic field holds ferrofluid in place around the coil, where it absorbs and carries away heat. It also damps unwanted vibrations, which improves sound quality.', wrong = '{"0": "Ferrofluid particles are only temporarily magnetised, and the voice coil is an electromagnet driven by current, not a permanent magnet.", "1": "Raising the coil''s resistance is not its purpose. Ferrofluid is used for cooling and damping.", "2": "Ferrofluid does not store electrical energy. That is the job of a capacitor or battery."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0983' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ferrofluid particles can be steered by external magnets to a chosen place in the body. Particles carrying a drug can be guided straight to a tumour, which reduces side effects and makes treatment work better. Ferrofluids can also be used as contrast agents in MRI.', wrong = '{"1": "Blood pressure is measured with a sphygmomanometer, not with ferrofluids.", "2": "The heart''s electrical activity is recorded by an electrocardiogram (ECG).", "3": "Heat sterilisation is not given as a use of ferrofluids. Their medical uses are drug targeting and MRI contrast."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0984' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the seismometer a magnet hangs on a spring inside a coil fixed to the frame. During an earthquake the frame and coil move with the ground, but the heavy hanging mass tends to stay still by inertia. The relative motion changes the flux through the coil, inducing an emf by Faraday''s law.', wrong = '{"0": "The emf does not come from charges in the rock. It comes from relative motion between the magnet and the coil.", "2": "The spring only supports the hanging mass. Stretching a spring does not generate a current.", "3": "The effect is electromagnetic induction from a changing flux, not a voltage caused by heating."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0985' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Faraday''s law the induced emf depends on the rate of change of flux through the coil. The faster the magnet and coil move relative to each other, the faster the flux changes. So the emf, and the induced current, is proportional to the speed of the relative motion.', wrong = '{"0": "The mass provides the inertia that keeps the magnet nearly still. The emf itself depends on the rate of flux change, not directly on the mass.", "1": "The burial depth does not set the size of the emf. Only the speed of the relative motion does.", "3": "A large but slow movement changes the flux slowly, giving only a small emf. The emf depends on speed, not total distance."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0986' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In wireless charging an alternating current in the coil of the charging pad makes a changing magnetic flux. This flux links a coil inside the phone and induces an emf there, which charges the battery. This is electromagnetic induction.', wrong = '{"0": "Electrostatic induction only separates charges on a conductor near a charged body. It cannot transfer power between two coils.", "1": "The photoelectric effect needs light to free electrons from a metal. No light is involved in wireless charging.", "2": "Thermionic emission is the release of electrons from a hot filament. There is no heated filament in a wireless charger."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0987' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Faraday''s law says an emf is induced in a coil only while the magnetic flux linking it changes. The change may come from moving a magnet, moving the coil, or changing a current nearby. A constant flux, however strong, induces no emf.', wrong = '{"1": "A motionless magnet gives a constant flux through the coil. With no change in flux, no emf is induced.", "2": "A steady current produces a steady magnetic field, so the flux through the coil does not change and no emf is induced.", "3": "Heating the coil changes its resistance but not the magnetic flux through it, so it induces no emf."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0988' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Albert Einstein published the special theory of relativity in 1905. It is based on two postulates: the laws of physics are the same in all inertial frames, and the speed of light in free space is the same for all observers.', wrong = '{"0": "1687 is when Newton published his Principia, which set out the laws of motion.", "2": "1915 is when Einstein completed the general theory of relativity, which deals with gravity and accelerated frames.", "3": "1919 is the year of the solar eclipse observations that confirmed the bending of starlight predicted by general relativity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0989' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The ball shares the train''s horizontal velocity. The passenger moves with that same velocity, so the passenger sees the ball fall straight down. The ground observer sees the ball''s horizontal velocity combined with its free fall, so to them it follows a projectile (parabolic) path.', wrong = '{"1": "The ground observer also sees the ball''s horizontal velocity, which it shares with the train. So for them the path is curved, not straight down.", "2": "The ball and the passenger have the same horizontal velocity, so the ball does not drift backward in the passenger''s view.", "3": "This has the two views swapped. The passenger sees a straight drop and the ground observer sees the parabola."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0990' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An inertial frame of reference is a non-accelerating frame in which Newton''s first law, the law of inertia, holds. In it a body stays at rest or moves with uniform velocity unless an unbalanced force acts on it.', wrong = '{"1": "Objects in an inertial frame can move with uniform velocity. They need not stay at rest.", "2": "An accelerating frame is non-inertial, because objects in it seem to move without any applied force.", "3": "An inertial frame is defined by the law of inertia holding, not by gravity being absent."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0991' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A frame that accelerates or decelerates is non-inertial. In a car braking suddenly, passengers lurch forward even though no forward force acts on them, so the law of inertia does not hold inside it.', wrong = '{"0": "A car at constant velocity on a straight road is not accelerating, so it is an inertial frame.", "1": "A train at constant velocity is not accelerating, so it is an inertial frame.", "2": "A laboratory at rest on the ground is treated as inertial, since the Earth''s own acceleration is very small."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0992' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Earth rotates on its axis and revolves around the Sun, so strictly it accelerates and is a non-inertial frame. However, these accelerations are very small, so for most purposes the Earth can be treated as an inertial frame.', wrong = '{"0": "The Earth does move: it rotates daily and revolves around the Sun yearly.", "1": "Rotation and revolution both involve a changing direction of motion, which is acceleration. Its velocity is not exactly constant.", "2": "Special relativity applies in every inertial frame, not only on Earth."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0993' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If a frame moves with uniform velocity relative to an inertial frame, objects in it still obey the law of inertia, since no acceleration is added. So any frame moving with constant velocity relative to an inertial frame is also inertial.', wrong = '{"1": "Uniform motion does not make a frame non-inertial. Only acceleration does.", "2": "Any constant velocity keeps the frame inertial, whether the speed is small or large.", "3": "A frame need not be at rest to be inertial. Uniform motion is enough."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0994' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Special relativity applies to inertial frames, which move with constant velocity relative to one another. Einstein''s general theory of relativity extends this to frames that accelerate relative to each other, and to gravity.', wrong = '{"1": "Accelerating frames are the subject of the general theory of relativity, not the special theory.", "2": "Special relativity is about motion, especially at very high speeds. It is not limited to objects at rest.", "3": "Special relativity applies to all the laws of physics, not only to sound waves."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0995' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Special relativity rests on two postulates. First, the laws of physics are the same in all inertial frames. Second, the speed of light in free space has the same value c for all observers, whatever the motion of the source or the observer.', wrong = '{"0": "Relativity shows that time is not absolute. Moving clocks run slow, which is time dilation.", "1": "Relativity predicts that the mass of a body increases with its speed. It is not the same at all speeds.", "2": "This contradicts the second postulate. The speed of light does not depend on how fast its source moves."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0996' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first postulate says the laws of physics are the same in every inertial frame. So every experiment gives the same result whether a frame is at rest or moving uniformly, and no experiment can show which frame is really moving. Absolute uniform motion cannot be detected.', wrong = '{"0": "This contradicts the second postulate, which says the speed of light is the same in all inertial frames.", "1": "Since all inertial frames are equivalent, none can be shown to be absolutely at rest.", "3": "The first postulate says the opposite: the laws of physics are the same in all inertial frames."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0997' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For the ground observer the light travels at c in both directions, but the back door moves forward towards the light and the front door moves away from it. So the light reaches the back door first. Events that are simultaneous for the traveller are not simultaneous for the ground observer.', wrong = '{"0": "The front door moves away from the light, so the light takes longer to reach it. The back door opens first.", "1": "Both doors opening together is what the passenger inside the train sees, not the observer on the ground.", "2": "The light does reach both doors. Only the order of the openings differs between observers."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0998' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Proper time t₀ is the time between two events measured by an observer at rest relative to them, for example with a clock in the same frame as the events. Any observer moving relative to that frame measures a longer time t, which is time dilation.', wrong = '{"1": "No material observer can move at the speed of light, and such an observer would not measure proper time.", "2": "An observer moving relative to the events measures a dilated, longer time, not the proper time.", "3": "Proper time is defined by the observer being at rest relative to the events, not by any particular location."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0999' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The process lasts its proper time t₀ = 4.0 s in its own frame. A moving observer measures a dilated time t = t₀/√(1 − v²/c²). Here √(1 − 0.6²) = √0.64 = 0.8, so t = 4.0 ÷ 0.8 = 5.0 s.', wrong = '{"0": "2.4 s comes from multiplying by v/c, 4.0 × 0.6. Time dilation does not work this way, and the time gets longer, not shorter.", "1": "3.2 s comes from multiplying by √(1 − v²/c²) = 0.8. The moving observer measures a longer time, so t₀ is divided by 0.8.", "3": "6.7 s comes from dividing by v/c, 4.0 ÷ 0.6. The divisor should be √(1 − v²/c²) = 0.8."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1000' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The ship''s clock measures the proper time t₀ = 6.0 s. The Earth observer measures a dilated time t = t₀/√(1 − v²/c²). Here √(1 − 0.8²) = √0.36 = 0.6, so t = 6.0 ÷ 0.6 = 10 s.', wrong = '{"0": "3.6 s comes from multiplying by √(1 − v²/c²) = 0.6. The Earth observer sees the moving clock run slow, so the time is longer, not shorter.", "1": "4.8 s comes from multiplying by v/c, 6.0 × 0.8. This is not the time dilation formula.", "2": "7.5 s comes from dividing by v/c = 0.8. The divisor should be √(1 − v²/c²) = 0.6."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1001' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Time dilation is a property of time itself, not of any particular clock. So it applies to every timing process: physical, chemical and biological. Even the ageing of the human body would be slowed for a person moving at speeds close to that of light.', wrong = '{"0": "Time dilation is not limited to mechanical clocks. It affects every process that marks time.", "2": "A light clock is a handy way to derive the formula, but the result holds for every kind of clock and process.", "3": "Time dilation is real. It has been measured, for example with fast-moving unstable particles that live longer than they do at rest."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1002' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A moving object is shortened along its direction of motion: ℓ = ℓ₀√(1 − v²/c²), where ℓ₀ is the rest (proper) length. At 0.6c, √(1 − 0.36) = 0.8, so ℓ = 50 × 0.8 = 40 m.', wrong = '{"0": "30 m comes from multiplying by v/c, 50 × 0.6. The contraction factor is √(1 − v²/c²) = 0.8.", "2": "50 m is the rest length. An observer the rocket passes at 0.6c measures it as shorter along its motion.", "3": "62.5 m comes from dividing by 0.8. Moving lengths contract, they do not expand."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1003' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Length contraction happens only along the direction of motion. The mast stands at right angles to that direction, so the observer measures it at its full rest length, unchanged.', wrong = '{"0": "Only lengths along the direction of motion are shortened. The mast is perpendicular to the motion.", "1": "Relativity never makes moving lengths longer. Lengths along the motion shrink and perpendicular ones stay the same.", "3": "A length would approach zero only along the motion as v approached c. A perpendicular mast keeps its length."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1004' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The proper length ℓ₀ of an object is its length measured by an observer at rest relative to it. Any observer moving relative to the object measures a shorter length ℓ = ℓ₀√(1 − v²/c²) along the direction of motion.', wrong = '{"0": "An observer moving relative to the object measures a contracted length, which is shorter than the proper length.", "1": "No material observer can move at the speed of light, so this cannot define proper length.", "2": "Proper length depends on relative motion, not on how far away the observer is."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1005' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In special relativity the mass of a moving body increases with its speed: m = m₀/√(1 − v²/c²), where m₀ is the rest mass. At 0.6c, √(1 − 0.36) = √0.64 = 0.8, so m = 2.0 ÷ 0.8 = 2.5 kg.', wrong = '{"0": "1.6 kg comes from multiplying by 0.8. Mass increases with speed, so the rest mass is divided by 0.8.", "1": "2.0 kg is the rest mass. Relativity predicts that the mass is larger at 0.6c.", "3": "3.3 kg comes from dividing by v/c = 0.6. The divisor is √(1 − v²/c²) = 0.8."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1006' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Relativistic mass is m = m₀/√(1 − v²/c²). As v gets closer to c, the square root approaches zero and the mass grows without limit. Accelerating an infinite mass would need an infinite force and energy, so a material object can never reach c.', wrong = '{"1": "The mass increases without limit as v approaches c. It does not fall to zero.", "2": "Accelerating an object does not turn it into light. It simply becomes harder and harder to speed up.", "3": "Lengths along the motion contract as v approaches c. They do not become infinite."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1007' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'm = m₀/√(1 − v²/c²), so m = 2m₀ needs √(1 − v²/c²) = 1/2. Squaring, 1 − v²/c² = 1/4, so v²/c² = 3/4 and v = (√3/2)c ≈ 0.866c.', wrong = '{"0": "At 0.5c, √(1 − 0.25) ≈ 0.866, so the mass is only about 1.15 times the rest mass.", "1": "At 0.707c, v²/c² = 0.5 and the mass is √2 ≈ 1.41 times the rest mass, not double.", "3": "At 0.943c, v²/c² ≈ 0.889 and √(1 − 0.889) ≈ 0.333, so the mass is about three times the rest mass."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1008' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Relativistic effects depend on v/c. Even the Earth''s orbital speed, about 30 km s⁻¹, is only about one ten-thousandth of the speed of light, 3 × 10⁵ km s⁻¹. Everyday speeds are far smaller, so relativistic corrections are too tiny to notice and Newton''s laws work well.', wrong = '{"0": "Relativity applies to all motion and all physical laws, not only to light.", "2": "Everyday objects clearly have mass. The point is that their speeds are tiny compared with c.", "3": "Relativistic effects are never exactly zero at any speed. At everyday speeds they are just far too small to measure easily."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1009' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Einstein''s mass–energy relation gives the rest-mass energy as E₀ = m₀c². The mass must be in kilograms, so 1.0 g = 10⁻³ kg. Then E₀ = 10⁻³ × (3 × 10⁸)² = 10⁻³ × 9 × 10¹⁶ = 9 × 10¹³ J, an enormous energy from just one gram.', wrong = '{"0": "3 × 10⁵ J comes from using c instead of c², 10⁻³ × 3 × 10⁸. The speed of light must be squared.", "2": "9 × 10¹⁰ J comes from a wrong conversion, taking 1 g as 10⁻⁶ kg. One gram is 10⁻³ kg.", "3": "9 × 10¹⁶ J is the rest-mass energy of 1 kg. It forgets to convert 1.0 g into 10⁻³ kg."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1010' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By E = mc², a change in energy ΔE goes with a change in mass Δm = ΔE/c². Since c² ≈ 9 × 10¹⁶ m² s⁻², the energy changes in daily life give mass changes far too small to measure. Only nuclear reactions involve energies large enough to show it clearly.', wrong = '{"0": "E = mc² holds everywhere, including everyday processes. The mass change is real but tiny.", "1": "Energy changes happen all the time, such as heating water or burning fuel. They are not limited to nuclear reactions.", "3": "The mass change is not exactly zero. It is just immeasurably small because c² is so large."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1011' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In relativity, space and time are not separate and absolute. They are combined into a single four-dimensional continuum called space-time, made of the three dimensions of space plus time as the fourth dimension.', wrong = '{"0": "Relativity shows that space and time are linked and depend on the observer''s motion. They are not separate or absolute.", "1": "Moving clocks run slow (time dilation), but time does not stop for objects moving below the speed of light.", "2": "Space-time has three space dimensions and one time dimension, not four space dimensions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1012' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In Einstein''s general theory, a massive body like the Sun curves the space-time around it. Bodies and light rays move along geodesics, the ''straightest possible'' paths in curved space-time. A planet following such a path around the Sun appears to us as an orbit held by gravity.', wrong = '{"1": "An instantaneous pull acting at a distance is Newton''s picture of gravity. In Einstein''s picture the orbit comes from curved space-time.", "2": "Massive bodies curve space-time. It is flat only far from any mass.", "3": "Gravity does not depend on electric charge. Planets are essentially neutral, and their orbits are due to gravity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1013' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'General relativity predicted that the Sun''s gravity would bend starlight passing close to it by a definite amount. Measurements made during the solar eclipse of 1919 agreed with Einstein''s prediction rather than Newton''s, supporting his theory.', wrong = '{"0": "1687 is when Newton published his Principia, long before Einstein''s theories.", "1": "1905 is when Einstein published the special theory of relativity, which does not deal with gravity.", "3": "2015 is when gravitational waves were first detected, not the year starlight bending was measured."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1014' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The ship''s clocks are present at both departure and arrival, so they record the proper time t₀. Earth observers measure the dilated time t, so t₀ = t√(1 − v²/c²) = 8.0 × √(1 − 0.25) = 8.0 × 0.866 ≈ 6.9 years.', wrong = '{"0": "4.0 years comes from multiplying by v/c = 0.5. The correct factor is √(1 − v²/c²) ≈ 0.866.", "2": "8.0 years is the Earth time. Less time passes on the fast-moving ship.", "3": "9.2 years comes from dividing 8.0 by 0.866. That gives a longer time, but the ship''s clocks record a shorter one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1015' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By the first postulate of relativity, the laws of physics are the same in every inertial frame. A cabin moving at constant velocity is an inertial frame, so every experiment inside gives the same result as at rest. Uniform motion cannot be detected from inside.', wrong = '{"0": "A pendulum swings the same way in any inertial frame, so it cannot reveal the cabin''s speed.", "2": "By the second postulate, light travels at the same speed c in every inertial frame, moving or not.", "3": "Objects drift backwards only when the cabin accelerates. At constant velocity they stay where they are placed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1016' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the symbol ²³⁸₉₂U the lower number is the atomic number Z (protons) and the upper is the mass number A (protons plus neutrons). The neutron number is N = A − Z = 238 − 92 = 146.', wrong = '{"0": "92 is the atomic number, the number of protons, not neutrons.", "2": "238 is the mass number, the total number of nucleons (protons plus neutrons).", "3": "330 comes from adding Z to A, 238 + 92. The protons must be subtracted from A, not added."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1017' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Isotopes are atoms of the same element, so they have the same atomic number. All carbon nuclei have 6 protons, but carbon-12, carbon-13 and carbon-14 have 6, 7 and 8 neutrons. So they have different mass numbers.', wrong = '{"0": "Different numbers of protons would mean different elements, not isotopes of carbon.", "1": "Nuclei with the same mass number but different atomic numbers are isobars, not isotopes.", "3": "Nuclei with the same numbers of both protons and neutrons are the same nuclide, not different isotopes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1018' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The unified atomic mass unit is defined as exactly one-twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom. Its value is about 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg, and its energy equivalent is about 931 MeV.', wrong = '{"0": "A proton''s mass is about 1.0073 u, slightly more than 1 u, so it is not the definition.", "1": "A hydrogen atom''s mass is about 1.0078 u, slightly more than 1 u.", "3": "Oxygen-16 was once used as a standard, but the modern unit is based on carbon-12."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1019' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Using E = mc² with m = 1 u ≈ 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg gives about 1.49 × 10⁻¹⁰ J. Dividing by 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ J per MeV, this is about 931 MeV. So 1 u is equivalent to about 931 MeV.', wrong = '{"0": "0.511 MeV is the rest-mass energy of an electron, which is much lighter than 1 u.", "1": "1.02 MeV is twice the electron''s rest energy, the minimum photon energy for pair production.", "2": "9.31 MeV is a hundred times too small, a slip in the powers of ten."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1020' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'James Chadwick discovered the neutron in 1932. It is an uncharged particle in the nucleus with a mass almost equal to that of the proton. Its discovery explained why most nuclei are heavier than their protons alone could account for.', wrong = '{"0": "Rutherford discovered the nucleus and suggested that a neutral particle might exist, but Chadwick discovered the neutron.", "1": "Paul Dirac predicted the existence of antiparticles, such as the positron, from his theory of the electron.", "2": "Carl Anderson discovered the positron in 1932, not the neutron."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1021' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nuclei with atomic numbers greater than 82 are found to be naturally unstable. They emit α, β and γ radiation spontaneously, without any outside cause, which is called natural radioactivity.', wrong = '{"0": "Most light nuclei with Z below 20 are stable, so this does not describe naturally radioactive nuclei.", "1": "Many light nuclei with equal numbers of protons and neutrons, such as carbon-12 and oxygen-16, are stable.", "2": "Only ordinary hydrogen has no neutrons, and it is stable."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1022' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Stable nuclei can be made unstable by bombarding them with high-energy particles such as neutrons, protons or alpha particles. The radioisotopes produced then decay, and this is called artificial (induced) radioactivity.', wrong = '{"1": "Natural radioactivity is the spontaneous decay of nuclei that are unstable to begin with, without any bombardment.", "2": "Pair production is a high-energy gamma photon turning into an electron and a positron.", "3": "Annihilation is a particle meeting its antiparticle, with both turning into gamma photons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1023' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Alpha particles are positive, so they bend towards the negative plate. Beta particles (electrons) are negative and very much lighter, so they bend more strongly, towards the positive plate. Gamma rays are uncharged and pass straight through.', wrong = '{"0": "This reverses the charges. α is positive and goes towards the negative plate; β is negative and goes towards the positive plate.", "2": "γ rays carry no charge and are not deflected, and β particles are negative, so they bend towards the positive plate.", "3": "An α particle carries twice the charge of a β particle, but it is thousands of times heavier, so it bends much less."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1024' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An alpha particle is a helium nucleus, ⁴₂He. Emitting it lowers the mass number by 4 and the atomic number by 2. So 238 − 4 = 234 and 92 − 2 = 90, and element 90 is thorium: ²³⁴₉₀Th.', wrong = '{"0": "²³⁴₉₂U keeps Z = 92. Losing two protons lowers Z to 90, which changes the element.", "1": "²³⁸₉₀Th keeps A = 238. Alpha emission removes four nucleons, so A must fall to 234.", "3": "²³⁴₉₁Pa has Z lowered by only 1. Alpha decay lowers Z by 2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1025' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An alpha particle is the nucleus of a helium atom, ⁴₂He. It contains two protons and two neutrons and has no electrons, so its charge is +2e and its mass is about 4 u.', wrong = '{"0": "A helium atom has two electrons around its nucleus. An alpha particle has no electrons, which is why it carries a +2e charge.", "1": "A hydrogen nucleus is a single proton, with charge +e and mass about 1 u. An alpha particle is four times heavier.", "2": "A fast electron emitted from a nucleus is a β⁻ particle, not an alpha particle."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1026' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Alpha emission lowers Z by 2 and A by 4. Each β⁻ emission turns a neutron into a proton, raising Z by 1 and leaving A unchanged. After one α and two β⁻, Z changes by −2 + 2 = 0 and A by −4, so the final nucleus is an isotope of the original element.', wrong = '{"1": "The mass number cannot stay the same, because the alpha particle carries away 4 nucleons.", "2": "These numbers are mixed up. A falls by 4 from the α decay, and the two β⁻ decays restore Z.", "3": "The two β⁻ decays only cancel the α decay''s fall of 2 in Z. They do not raise Z above its starting value."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1027' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In alpha decay the mass of the parent nucleus is found to be greater than the total mass of the daughter nucleus plus the α particle. The missing mass Δm appears as energy, Q = Δmc², mostly as the kinetic energy of the α particle. This is the disintegration energy or Q-value.', wrong = '{"0": "The Q-value comes from a mass difference between parent and products, not from the α particle gaining mass in motion.", "2": "The daughter nucleus is lighter than the parent, since it has lost four nucleons and some mass-energy.", "3": "Energy is not created from nothing. It comes from the decrease in mass, by E = mc²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1028' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A nucleus contains no electrons. In β⁻ decay a neutron in the nucleus changes into a proton, and an electron and an antineutrino are created at the moment of emission. The created electron is the β particle, which keeps the total charge conserved.', wrong = '{"0": "β particles are not orbital electrons knocked out of the atom. They come from inside the nucleus, though they are identical to ordinary electrons.", "1": "The nucleus holds no electrons. The β electron is created at the moment of decay.", "3": "A proton changing into a neutron is β⁺ decay, which emits a positron, not an electron."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1029' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In β⁺ decay a proton in the nucleus changes into a neutron, emitting a positron and a neutrino. Z falls by 1 and A stays the same. So ²²₁₁Na becomes ²²₁₀Ne, since element 10 is neon.', wrong = '{"0": "²²₁₂Mg has Z raised by 1, which is the result of β⁻ decay, not β⁺ decay.", "1": "²¹₁₀Ne has A lowered by 1. In β decay no nucleons are lost, so A stays 22.", "2": "¹⁸₉F has A down by 4 and Z down by 2, which is what alpha decay of ²²₁₁Na would give."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1030' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In β⁺ decay a proton changes into a neutron, emitting a positron and a neutrino: p → n + e⁺ + ν. In β⁻ decay the electron is accompanied by an antineutrino instead. The neutrino shares the released energy with the positron.', wrong = '{"1": "The antineutrino goes with β⁻ decay, where a neutron becomes a proton and an electron is emitted.", "2": "A neutrino must be emitted along with the positron so that energy and momentum are conserved.", "3": "In β⁺ decay a proton becomes a neutron inside the nucleus. No proton is emitted."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1031' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A nuclide with too many neutrons for stability has an N/Z ratio that is too large. In β⁻ decay a neutron changes into a proton, so N falls by 1 and Z rises by 1. This lowers the N/Z ratio towards the stable value.', wrong = '{"0": "β⁺ decay changes a proton into a neutron, raising N/Z. It happens when the N/Z ratio is too small.", "2": "A neutrino is never emitted on its own in decay. It always comes with a β particle.", "3": "Absorbing an electron is not given in the textbook as the way to correct a high N/Z ratio. β⁻ emission is."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1032' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A given decay always releases the same total energy. In α decay it all goes to the α particle (and recoil), so every α has the same sharp energy. In β decay it is shared in varying proportions between the β particle and a neutrino or antineutrino, so the β energy ranges continuously from zero to a maximum.', wrong = '{"0": "The spread is not due to collisions inside the nucleus. It comes from sharing energy with the (anti)neutrino.", "1": "Energy is conserved in β decay. The (anti)neutrino carries away the part not taken by the β particle.", "3": "Being heavier does not explain why β energies form a continuous spread while α energies are sharp."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1033' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'β particles from a given nuclide come out with a range of energies, which seemed to break conservation of energy. To save the law, the neutrino was proposed: an uncharged, nearly massless particle that carries away the missing energy. It was later detected near a high-power nuclear reactor.', wrong = '{"0": "Alpha particles are deflected because they are charged. No new particle is needed to explain this.", "1": "Gamma rays are photons, which naturally have no charge. This needs no new particle.", "2": "The stability of the nucleus is explained by the strong nuclear force, not by the neutrino."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1034' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After an α or β decay the nucleus is often left in an excited state. It drops to a lower energy state by emitting a gamma photon, an electromagnetic wave with no charge and no mass. So Z and A stay the same and the element does not change.', wrong = '{"1": "Gamma rays carry no charge, so the atomic number does not change.", "2": "Gamma rays carry no nucleons, so the mass number does not change.", "3": "Since Z is unchanged, the nucleus remains the same element, only with less energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1035' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The textbook''s summary table of the three radiations lists a typical source for each: radon-222 for alpha particles, a strontium isotope for beta particles, and cobalt-60 for gamma rays.', wrong = '{"0": "Radon-222 is listed in the table as a typical alpha source.", "2": "Uranium-238 is an alpha emitter and is not the gamma source given in the table.", "3": "Carbon-12 is a stable isotope and emits no radiation at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1036' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a radium-226 nucleus emits an α particle, the daughter is radon-222, a different element. The change of one element into another during radioactive decay is called transmutation of elements.', wrong = '{"0": "Annihilation is a particle meeting its antiparticle and turning into energy, such as gamma photons.", "1": "Pair production is a gamma photon creating a particle and its antiparticle, such as an electron and a positron.", "3": "Ionization is the removal or addition of electrons to atoms. It does not change one element into another."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1037' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'There are four fundamental forces: strong, weak, electromagnetic and gravitational. Gravity is by far the weakest, yet its range is unlimited. Because masses only attract, it adds up over huge bodies and controls planets, stars and galaxies.', wrong = '{"0": "The strong nuclear force is the strongest of the four, and its range is very short, about the size of a nucleus.", "1": "The weak nuclear force has a very short range, even smaller than a nucleus, so it is not long-ranged.", "2": "The electromagnetic force also has an infinite range, but it is enormously stronger than gravity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1038' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The weak nuclear force is responsible for β decay. It can change the identity of particles, for example turning a neutron into a proton by changing a down quark into an up quark. It also governs how neutrinos interact with matter.', wrong = '{"1": "The strong force binds quarks into nucleons and holds nucleons together in the nucleus, but it does not cause β decay.", "2": "The electromagnetic force acts between charges. It cannot change a neutron into a proton.", "3": "Gravity is far too weak to matter inside nuclei and does not change one particle into another."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1039' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'James Clerk Maxwell wrote four equations linking electric and magnetic fields. They showed that electricity and magnetism are two aspects of a single electromagnetic force, and they predicted electromagnetic waves travelling at the speed of light.', wrong = '{"0": "Isaac Newton gave the laws of motion and the law of gravitation, not the unification of electricity and magnetism.", "2": "Albert Einstein developed the theories of relativity, building on Maxwell''s work.", "3": "Michael Faraday discovered electromagnetic induction, but it was Maxwell who wrote the unifying equations."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1040' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In pair production a photon''s energy turns into the rest mass of an electron and a positron. Each has rest-mass energy mₑc² = 0.511 MeV, so the photon needs at least 2 × 0.511 = 1.02 MeV.', wrong = '{"0": "0.511 MeV supplies the rest-mass energy of only one particle. Both the electron and the positron must be created.", "2": "2.04 MeV counts the rest-mass energy of each particle twice. The minimum is 2 × 0.511 = 1.02 MeV.", "3": "931 MeV is the energy equivalent of 1 u, which is far more than the mass of two electrons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1041' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A photon in empty space cannot turn into an electron and a positron while conserving both energy and momentum. Near a nucleus, the heavy nucleus recoils and takes up the extra momentum, so both laws can be satisfied.', wrong = '{"0": "The electron and positron have opposite charges, so the pair''s total charge is zero. No charge needs to be supplied.", "1": "The masses of the pair come from the photon''s energy, by E = mc², not from the nucleus.", "3": "A photon always travels at the speed of light and cannot be slowed down by the nucleus."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1042' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Part of the photon''s energy is used to create the rest masses of the pair, 2 × 0.511 = 1.02 MeV. The rest becomes their kinetic energy: 1.50 − 1.02 = 0.48 MeV, ignoring the small recoil energy of the nucleus.', wrong = '{"1": "0.99 MeV comes from subtracting only one rest-mass energy, 1.50 − 0.511. Two particles are created.", "2": "1.02 MeV is the energy used to create the two rest masses, not the kinetic energy left over.", "3": "1.50 MeV is the photon''s full energy. Some of it must become the rest mass of the pair."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1043' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When an electron and a positron at rest annihilate, their total rest energy 2 × 0.511 = 1.02 MeV becomes radiation. Their total momentum is zero, so two photons must fly off in opposite directions, each with about 0.51 MeV.', wrong = '{"0": "A single photon always carries momentum, so it could not leave a total momentum of zero. Two photons are needed.", "1": "Two photons of 1.02 MeV each would need 2.04 MeV, but only 1.02 MeV is available.", "2": "Electron–positron annihilation produces gamma photons. Its 1.02 MeV is far too little to make a neutron and a proton."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1044' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Every particle has an antiparticle with the same rest mass but opposite charge, and opposite magnetic properties. For example, the positron has the same mass as the electron but a charge of +e instead of −e.', wrong = '{"1": "Mass is never negative. An antiparticle has the same mass as its particle, and its charge is opposite, not the same.", "2": "An antiparticle has exactly the same mass as its particle, and its charge is opposite.", "3": "An antiparticle has the same mass as its particle. Only neutral particles have antiparticles with no charge."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1045' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Particles are grouped by their spin. Those with half-integer spin, such as electrons, protons and neutrons, obey Pauli''s exclusion principle and are called fermions. Particles with zero or whole-number spin are called bosons.', wrong = '{"0": "Bosons have zero or whole-number spin and do not obey Pauli''s exclusion principle.", "2": "Mesons are hadrons made of a quark and an antiquark, with integer spin, so they are bosons.", "3": "A photon has spin 1, so it is a boson, not a fermion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1046' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Leptons are particles that do not feel the strong nuclear force. They are the electron, the muon, the tau and their three neutrinos. The muon is a heavier relative of the electron, so it is a lepton.', wrong = '{"0": "A pion is a meson, made of a quark and an antiquark. Mesons are hadrons, not leptons.", "1": "A proton is a baryon, made of three quarks. Baryons are hadrons, not leptons.", "3": "A kaon is a meson, made of a quark and an antiquark, so it is a hadron, not a lepton."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1047' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hadrons are made of quarks and come in two kinds. Baryons, such as protons and neutrons, contain three quarks. Mesons, such as pions and kaons, contain one quark and one antiquark.', wrong = '{"0": "Three quarks make a baryon, such as a proton or neutron, not a meson.", "1": "Three antiquarks make an antibaryon, such as an antiproton.", "2": "Mesons are hadrons made of quarks. Leptons are separate fundamental particles and are not inside mesons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1048' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The charge of a hadron is the sum of its quark charges. For u, d and s: +2/3 − 1/3 − 1/3 = 0. So the particle is neutral; this combination is the lambda particle.', wrong = '{"1": "+1 e is the charge of uud, the proton. Here a strange quark of −1/3 e replaces one up quark.", "2": "−1 e would need three quarks of −1/3 e each, such as dds. Here one quark is an up quark.", "3": "+2/3 e counts only the up quark and leaves out the d and s charges."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1049' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An antiquark has the same mass as its quark but the opposite charge. The up quark has +2/3 e, so the anti-up quark has −2/3 e.', wrong = '{"0": "+2/3 e is the charge of the up quark itself. Its antiquark has the opposite sign.", "2": "−1/3 e is the charge of a down or strange quark, not of the anti-up quark.", "3": "+1/3 e is the charge of the anti-down (or anti-strange) quark."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1050' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 1964 Murray Gell-Mann and George Zweig proposed that hadrons are not fundamental. Instead, baryons such as protons and neutrons, and mesons such as pions, are built from smaller particles called quarks.', wrong = '{"0": "Paul Dirac predicted antiparticles and Carl Anderson discovered the positron. Neither proposed quarks.", "1": "Peter Higgs proposed the field that gives particles mass, and Einstein developed relativity. Neither proposed the quark model.", "3": "Chadwick discovered the neutron and Rutherford discovered the nucleus. They worked long before the quark model."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1051' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Quarks come in six flavours: up, down, strange, charm, bottom and top. The muon is not a quark at all. It is a lepton, a heavier relative of the electron that does not feel the strong force.', wrong = '{"0": "Charm is one of the six quark flavours, so it does belong to the list.", "1": "Bottom is one of the six quark flavours, so it does belong to the list.", "2": "Strange is one of the six quark flavours, found for example in kaons and the lambda particle."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1052' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A neutron is udd and a proton is uud. In β⁻ decay one down quark changes into an up quark, d → u + e⁻ + ν̄. The charge goes from −1/3 to +2/3, and the emitted electron keeps total charge conserved, turning the neutron into a proton.', wrong = '{"1": "An up quark changing into a down quark with positron emission is what happens in β⁺ decay, not β⁻ decay.", "2": "Quarks are never knocked out and seen on their own. In β decay a quark changes its flavour instead.", "3": "Both the neutron and the proton contain three quarks. No quark is gained; one changes flavour."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1053' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Quarks carry a kind of charge called colour, which comes in three types: red, green and blue. The strong force between colour charges, carried by gluons, is described by quantum chromodynamics (QCD), from the Greek word for colour.', wrong = '{"0": "Quantum electrodynamics (QED) describes the electromagnetic force between electric charges, carried by photons.", "2": "String theory is a proposed ''theory of everything'' in which particles are tiny vibrating strings. It is not the theory of the colour force.", "3": "General relativity is Einstein''s theory of gravity, not of the force between quarks."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1054' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each quark carries one of three colour charges: red, green or blue. A proton, like every baryon, has one quark of each colour. Red + green + blue combine to ''white'', so the proton as a whole is colourless.', wrong = '{"0": "Three quarks of the same colour would not cancel. A colourless baryon needs one of each colour.", "1": "Quarks do carry colour charge; it is the charge of the strong force. Only their combinations are colourless.", "3": "A quark with its antiquark is a meson, which is colourless as colour plus anti-colour. A proton is made of three quarks."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1055' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Higgs boson was discovered in 2012 at the Large Hadron Collider at CERN, near Geneva. It has a mass of about 125 GeV/c² and is linked to the Higgs field, which explains how other particles get their mass.', wrong = '{"0": "LIGO is an observatory that detects gravitational waves. It does not collide particles.", "1": "NASA is the United States space agency. The Large Hadron Collider is run by CERN in Europe.", "2": "Virgo is a gravitational-wave observatory in Italy, not a particle collider."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1056' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each fundamental force is carried by exchange particles (bosons). The weak nuclear force is carried by the W⁺, W⁻ and Z bosons. They interact strongly with the Higgs field, which gives them large masses.', wrong = '{"1": "Gluons carry the strong force that holds quarks together.", "2": "Photons carry the electromagnetic force between charged particles.", "3": "Gravitons are the proposed carriers of the gravitational force."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1057' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Particles get their mass by interacting with the Higgs field. Those that interact strongly with it, such as the W and Z bosons, get large masses. Photons do not interact with the Higgs field, so their rest mass is zero.', wrong = '{"0": "A strong interaction with the Higgs field gives a large mass, as for the W and Z bosons, not zero mass.", "2": "Photons are light, so they always travel at the speed of light, not more slowly.", "3": "Photons are not made of quarks. They are fundamental particles that carry the electromagnetic force."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1058' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some new particles made in high-energy collisions were called ''strange particles''. They are always created in pairs. For example, when a pion collides with a proton, a kaon (K⁰) and a lambda (Λ⁰) are produced together.', wrong = '{"0": "Strange particles are never produced singly in these collisions. They always appear in pairs.", "1": "They are produced in high-energy collisions such as pion–proton collisions, not in beta decay.", "3": "The example given is a pion striking a proton, not production from gamma rays."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1059' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Only about 5% of the universe is ordinary matter made of atoms (electrons, protons and neutrons). About 27% is unseen dark matter and about 68% is dark energy, so about 95% of the universe is still not understood.', wrong = '{"1": "About 27% is the share of dark matter, not of ordinary matter.", "2": "About 68% is the share of dark energy, not of ordinary matter.", "3": "95% is dark matter and dark energy together (27% + 68%), the part we know least about."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1060' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Most ordinary matter is hydrogen and helium inside stars. At the enormous temperatures there, the atoms are ionized into free nuclei and electrons, which is the plasma state. So most of the matter in the observable universe is plasma.', wrong = '{"0": "Solids such as planets and rocks make up only a tiny fraction of ordinary matter in the universe.", "1": "Liquids such as oceans are rare on a cosmic scale.", "2": "Most hydrogen and helium is in hot stars where it is ionized, not a neutral gas at room temperature."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1061' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'String theory proposes that fundamental particles are not points but tiny vibrating strings, which may be open or closed loops. The way a string vibrates sets the particle''s properties, such as its mass. It is offered as a possible ''theory of everything'' but is still unproved by experiment.', wrong = '{"1": "Point particles with no size are exactly what string theory replaces with tiny strings.", "2": "Quarks and leptons are themselves the fundamental particles. String theory says they are vibrating strings.", "3": "String theory is not about waves in an electric field. It describes all particles as vibrating strings."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1062' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Standard Model describes quarks and leptons, three forces (electromagnetic, strong and weak) carried by bosons, and the Higgs boson, which explains how particles get mass. It is incomplete because it cannot yet explain gravity, dark matter or dark energy.', wrong = '{"0": "The electromagnetic force, carried by photons, is one of the three forces the Standard Model does explain.", "2": "The strong nuclear force, carried by gluons, is included in the Standard Model.", "3": "The Standard Model explains mass through the Higgs field and the Higgs boson."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1063' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gas molecules move randomly in all directions, so for every molecule moving with +v there is one moving with −v. The plain average velocity is therefore zero and tells us nothing. Squaring first removes the signs, and the square root of the mean square gives the rms speed.', wrong = '{"1": "Squaring a speed above 1 m s⁻¹ makes the number larger, not smaller. The reason for squaring is to remove the signs.", "2": "Molecules move randomly in all directions. That is exactly why the plain average of their velocities cancels to zero.", "3": "Molecular speeds are only a few hundred metres per second, far below the speed of light."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1501' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kinetic theory gives P = ⅓(N/V)m<v²>. Since <K.E.> = ½m<v²>, we can write m<v²> = 2<K.E.>, so P = ⅓n × 2<K.E.> = ⅔n<K.E.>. Pressure is two-thirds of the translational kinetic energy per unit volume.', wrong = '{"0": "⅓ goes with m<v²>, not with ½m<v²>. Rewriting in terms of kinetic energy doubles the factor to ⅔.", "1": "3/2 is the factor in <K.E.> = (3/2)k_BT, not in the pressure formula.", "3": "P = n<K.E.> leaves out the factor ⅔, which comes from averaging motion over three directions and from K.E. = ½mv²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1502' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The average kinetic energy of gas molecules is proportional to absolute temperature, so heating makes them move faster. Faster molecules strike the walls more often and with a larger change in momentum at each hit. The force per unit area, the pressure, therefore rises.', wrong = '{"0": "Kinetic theory treats the size of the molecules as fixed and negligible. Heating does not make them larger.", "1": "The container is sealed, so the number of molecules stays the same.", "2": "Kinetic theory assumes no forces between molecules except during collisions, so attraction cannot explain the rise."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1503' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A popcorn kernel holds a little trapped moisture. When heated, this turns to steam, whose fast-moving molecules raise the pressure inside the kernel. The pressure eventually ruptures the hard cover, releasing the soft starch, which sets solid on cooling.', wrong = '{"0": "The starch comes out only after the cover breaks. It is the steam pressure inside that breaks the cover.", "2": "Heating raises the pressure inside the kernel, not the pressure of the air outside.", "3": "The kernel bursts because of a pressure build-up inside it, not because it loses its molecules."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1504' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Viscosity describes the internal friction of a fluid, its resistance to flow. In Brownian motion, small particles are pushed about by fluid molecules. In a less viscous fluid the particles meet less resistance, so their random Brownian movement is faster.', wrong = '{"1": "Density is mass per unit volume, a different property from viscosity.", "2": "The average speed of fluid molecules depends on temperature. Viscosity measures resistance to flow.", "3": "The boiling point is the temperature at which a liquid turns to vapour. It is unrelated to what viscosity measures."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1505' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'One mole contains Avogadro''s number of molecules, N_A = 6.02 × 10²³. So the number of moles is n = N/N_A = 3.01 × 10²³ ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ = 0.50 mol. A quick check: 3.01 is exactly half of 6.02.', wrong = '{"0": "2.0 mol comes from the inverted ratio, N_A ÷ N. 2.0 mol would contain 1.204 × 10²⁴ molecules.", "1": "1.81 × 10⁴⁷ mol comes from multiplying N by N_A instead of dividing.", "2": "0.25 mol would contain only 1.505 × 10²³ molecules, half the number given."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1506' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The average translational kinetic energy of a gas molecule is <K.E.> = (3/2)k_BT, so it is directly proportional to the absolute temperature. Halving T therefore halves the average kinetic energy.', wrong = '{"0": "A fourfold fall would need the absolute temperature to drop to a quarter, not to half.", "2": "The kinetic energy depends directly on temperature, so it must change when T changes.", "3": "A fall by √2 describes how the rms speed changes when T is halved, since v_rms ∝ √T. The kinetic energy itself halves."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1507' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each molecule has average kinetic energy (3/2)k_BT, and one mole has N_A molecules. Since N_Ak_B = R, the total is (3/2)RT = 1.5 × 8.31 × 300 ≈ 3.7 × 10³ J, about 3.7 kJ.', wrong = '{"0": "2.5 kJ is RT = 8.31 × 300 ≈ 2.49 kJ. The factor 3/2 is missing.", "1": "1.2 kJ is ½RT, the energy linked with motion along one direction only.", "3": "6.2 × 10⁻²¹ J is (3/2)k_BT, the kinetic energy of a single molecule, not of a whole mole."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1508' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Red dwarfs are low-mass stars that use their fuel very slowly and efficiently, so they evolve very slowly. The universe is not yet old enough for any of them to have finished its life. Models predict they will grow hotter and brighter, then end as low-mass white dwarfs.', wrong = '{"0": "Supernova explosions end the lives of stars about ten times the Sun''s mass, not of low-mass red dwarfs.", "1": "Red dwarfs are low-mass stars, not the most massive ones known.", "2": "No red dwarf has completed its life cycle yet, and they are expected to end as white dwarfs, not black holes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1509' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a low or intermediate mass star uses up its nuclear fuel, its core contracts into a dense white dwarf. Its outer layers are expelled into space as a glowing shell of gas called a planetary nebula.', wrong = '{"1": "A neutron star forms from the collapsed core of a much more massive star after a supernova.", "2": "A black hole forms from the collapse of a very massive star, not from the outer layers of a smaller one.", "3": "A protostar is an early stage of a forming star, not the remains of a dying one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1510' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the red giant stage, stars of at least about half the Sun''s mass become hot enough to fuse helium in their cores. Helium fusion builds heavier nuclei, mainly carbon and oxygen. Only more massive stars go on to fuse heavier elements in later stages, up to iron.', wrong = '{"0": "Hydrogen is the fuel of the earlier main-sequence stage. Helium fusion makes heavier nuclei, not hydrogen.", "1": "Fusion in stars builds nuclei only up to iron. Elements like uranium and thorium are not made by helium fusion.", "2": "Iron is reached only after several later fusion stages, and only in very massive stars."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1511' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Neutron stars are the densest known objects after black holes. Their matter is packed so tightly that it behaves like a degenerate gas, and their very strong gravity makes them hard to model.', wrong = '{"0": "An ideal gas has widely spaced particles. Neutron-star matter is packed together at extreme density.", "1": "Ordinary atoms cannot survive at neutron-star densities, since they are crushed together.", "3": "Neutron-star matter is described as a degenerate gas, not as a crystal of hydrogen."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1512' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A star stays stable only when its internal pressure roughly balances the inward gravitational pressure. Here gravity''s pressure, about 1.7 × 10¹⁴ Pa, is about seventeen times the internal pressure of 1 × 10¹³ Pa. Gravity wins, so the star contracts and may eventually collapse into a neutron star or black hole.', wrong = '{"0": "Expansion happens when the internal pressure is larger than the gravitational pressure, the opposite of this case.", "1": "Stable equilibrium needs the two pressures to be nearly equal. Here they differ by more than ten times.", "2": "A white dwarf is what remains of a smaller star after its fuel runs out. Here the reasoning predicts contraction and possible collapse."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1513' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Sun formed about 4.6 billion years ago from a huge cloud of gas and dust. The space between the Sun and Earth is almost empty, so heat cannot be carried by conduction or convection. The Sun''s energy reaches Earth mainly as electromagnetic radiation, which can cross a vacuum.', wrong = '{"0": "Conduction needs particles of a material in contact to pass energy along, and space provides almost none.", "2": "Convection needs a fluid that can flow and carry heat, and space is nearly empty.", "3": "Sound waves need a material medium and cannot travel through the vacuum of space."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1514' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From PV = ⅓Nm<v²>, the right side depends only on N, m and <v²>. At constant temperature the mean-square speed is fixed, and N and m do not change, so PV is constant (Boyle''s law). Squeezing the gas into less volume makes the molecules hit the walls more often, so P rises.', wrong = '{"1": "At constant temperature the average kinetic energy, and so the molecular speed, does not change.", "2": "Compressing a gas does not change the mass of its molecules.", "3": "The amount of gas is fixed. Only the space it fills becomes smaller, so the number of molecules stays the same."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1515' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A gas contains around 10²³ molecules, far too many to follow one by one. Statistical physics therefore uses probability theory to describe the system, for example how the molecules are spread over different speeds and energy states.', wrong = '{"1": "Applying Newton''s laws to every molecule separately is exactly what is impossible for about 10²³ particles.", "2": "Geometrical optics deals with light rays and lenses, not with large collections of molecules.", "3": "Nuclear reactions belong to nuclear physics, not to the statistical description of gases."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1516' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A vibration is one complete cycle of the motion. The body starts at one extreme, passes through the mean position to the other extreme, and comes back to where it started. For a pendulum swinging between points A and B, going from A to B and back to A is one vibration.', wrong = '{"0": "Moving from the mean position to one extreme is only a quarter of a vibration.", "2": "The maximum displacement from the mean position is the amplitude, not a vibration.", "3": "The number of oscillations made in one second is the frequency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1517' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The amplitude of a wave is the maximum displacement of the particles of the medium from their mean positions. It is a measure of the energy the wave carries: the greater the amplitude, the greater the energy, and vice versa.', wrong = '{"0": "Wave speed depends on the medium the wave travels through, not on its amplitude.", "1": "Frequency is the number of vibrations per second, and it does not depend on the amplitude.", "3": "Wavelength is the length of one complete wave, not the size of the disturbance."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1518' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Angular frequency is the angular displacement per unit time, ω = θ/t. In one complete cycle the angle is 2π radians and the time is the period T, so ω = 2π/T = 2πf. It is measured in rad s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "The number of vibrations per unit time is the frequency f, measured in hertz. Angular frequency is 2π times this.", "2": "The time taken for one vibration is the period T.", "3": "Maximum displacement per unit time is not a standard quantity in oscillation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1519' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Angular frequency and frequency are linked by ω = 2πf, so f = ω/2π = 4π ÷ 2π = 2 Hz. The period is T = 1/f = 1 ÷ 2 = 0.5 s.', wrong = '{"0": "f = 4 Hz comes from dividing ω by π instead of 2π.", "1": "f = 0.5 Hz and T = 2 s swap the values. A frequency of 0.5 Hz would need ω = π rad s⁻¹.", "2": "12.6 is ω itself (4π ≈ 12.6 rad s⁻¹), not the frequency in hertz."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1520' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the mean position the spring is neither stretched nor compressed, so the restoring force is zero. But the mass is moving at its greatest speed there. By inertia it keeps moving past the mean position until the compressed spring slows it down and stops it at the other extreme.', wrong = '{"1": "The restoring force is zero at the mean position, since F = −kx and x = 0 there.", "2": "The surface is smooth, so there is no friction. Friction would oppose the motion anyway, not drive it.", "3": "The spring constant k is a property of the spring and does not change with position."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1521' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The bob''s weight mg splits into mg sin θ along the arc and mg cos θ along the string. The bob does not move along the string, so the tension in the string balances mg cos θ. Only mg sin θ is left to pull the bob back towards the mean position.', wrong = '{"0": "mg cos θ is zero only when θ = 90°. For a normal swing it is nearly equal to mg.", "1": "mg cos θ equals mg sin θ only at θ = 45°, which is not generally the case.", "2": "It is mg sin θ, along the arc, that acts towards the mean position. mg cos θ acts along the string."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1522' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The period of a simple pendulum is T = 2π√(ℓ/g). Rearranging gives ℓ = gT²/4π². Substituting, ℓ = 9.8 × 2² ÷ (4 × 3.14²) = 39.2 ÷ 39.4 ≈ 0.99 m, about one metre.', wrong = '{"0": "About 0.25 m is the length for a period of 1 s, not 2 s.", "1": "About 1.99 m comes from dividing by 2π² instead of 4π².", "3": "About 3.97 m is the length for a period of 4 s, since ℓ grows as T²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1523' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The period of a pendulum is T = 2π√(ℓ/g). At high altitude g is slightly smaller, so T becomes slightly longer. Each swing takes more time, so the clock counts fewer swings in a day and runs slow, losing time.', wrong = '{"0": "A shorter period would need a larger g. At high altitude g is smaller, so the period gets longer.", "1": "The period does depend on g, as T ∝ 1/√g. It is the mass and amplitude it does not depend on.", "2": "A pendulum swings wherever gravity acts. At high altitude only its period changes slightly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1524' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a mass on a spring, ω² = k/m = 50 ÷ 0.5 = 100 s⁻². In SHM a = −ω²x, so a = −100 × 0.03 = −3.0 m s⁻². The minus sign shows the acceleration points towards the mean position, opposite to the displacement.', wrong = '{"1": "In SHM the acceleration is always opposite to the displacement, so with x positive it must be negative.", "2": "−0.3 m s⁻² comes from using ω = 10 rad s⁻¹ instead of ω² = 100 s⁻².", "3": "−30 m s⁻² is ten times too large. ω² = 100 s⁻², so a = −100 × 0.03 = −3.0 m s⁻²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1525' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Q is the projection of P on the diameter, so it can only move along that line. Its acceleration is therefore the component of P''s centripetal acceleration along the diameter: a = −x₀ω² cos θ = −ω²x. This is exactly the condition for SHM.', wrong = '{"0": "Q speeds up towards the centre and slows down towards the ends, so it must have an acceleration.", "1": "Only the component along the diameter belongs to Q. It equals P''s full centripetal acceleration only at the ends of the diameter.", "3": "The centripetal acceleration, and its component, always point towards the centre, which is Q''s mean position."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1526' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The displacement is found by substituting into x = x₀ cos ωt. At t = 1/3 s, the phase is ωt = π × 1/3 = π/3 rad, which is 60°. So x = 0.10 × cos 60° = 0.10 × 0.5 = 0.05 m, halfway between the mean position and the extreme.', wrong = '{"0": "0.087 m comes from using sin 60° instead of cos 60°: 0.10 × 0.866.", "2": "0.10 m is the displacement at t = 0, which is the amplitude.", "3": "The body reaches the mean position when ωt = 90°, at t = 0.5 s, not at 1/3 s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1527' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The phase constant φ tells where in its cycle the body is when timing starts. Its value is fixed by the displacement and velocity of the body at t = 0. The amplitude and period do not decide this starting point.', wrong = '{"0": "The mass affects ω through ω = √(k/m), but not the starting phase.", "1": "The amplitude is the size of the oscillation. It does not say where in the cycle the motion starts.", "2": "The period sets ω = 2π/T, not the phase at t = 0."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1528' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two systems oscillate in phase when they reach the same point of their cycles at the same time, for example both at the positive extreme together. This means their phase difference is 0°, or a whole cycle of 360°.', wrong = '{"1": "A 90° difference means one is at an extreme while the other is at the mean position.", "2": "A 180° difference means they are out of phase, always moving in opposite directions.", "3": "270° is three-quarters of a cycle, so the two are not at the same point at the same time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1529' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The force needed to stretch a spring grows steadily, from 0 at x = 0 to kx₀ at x = x₀ (Hooke''s law). For a force that rises uniformly, the average is (0 + kx₀)/2 = kx₀/2. The work done, and so the stored energy, is average force × x₀ = ½kx₀².', wrong = '{"0": "kx₀ is the final, maximum force at full stretch, not the average over the whole stretch.", "2": "2kx₀ is twice the largest force ever applied, so it cannot be the average.", "3": "½kx₀² is the work done, an energy measured in joules, not a force."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1530' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The total energy is E = ½kx₀². At x = x₀/2 the potential energy is ½k(x₀/2)² = ¼ × ½kx₀², a quarter of E. Since total energy is conserved, the kinetic energy is E − ¼E = ¾ of the total.', wrong = '{"0": "1/4 is the fraction that is potential energy at x₀/2, not kinetic energy.", "1": "K.E. and P.E. are equal, each half of E, at x = x₀/√2, not at x₀/2.", "3": "1/√2 is the displacement ratio at which the energies are equal, not an energy fraction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1531' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In free oscillations a body vibrates at its own natural frequency, with no outside periodic force acting. If there is no resistive force, no energy is lost, so both the total energy and the amplitude stay constant.', wrong = '{"0": "A steadily decreasing amplitude describes damped oscillations, where resistive forces remove energy.", "1": "A free oscillator vibrates at a fixed natural frequency, which does not increase.", "2": "Energy fed in by an external periodic force gives forced oscillations, not free ones."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1532' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In light damping the resistive forces are small, so energy is removed only gradually. The amplitude shrinks slowly over many oscillations before the motion stops, just as a playground swing left alone dies away slowly.', wrong = '{"1": "Critical damping brings the system back to rest in the shortest possible time without oscillating.", "2": "Heavy damping, like a pendulum in thick oil, stops the oscillation and the body creeps back to rest very slowly.", "3": "Resonance makes the amplitude grow large. It does not make it shrink slowly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1533' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Microwaves in the oven make the water molecules in food oscillate at the microwave frequency. The vibrating molecules collide with one another, and the friction of these collisions turns their energy into heat. Containers with no water in them are not heated directly.', wrong = '{"0": "Glass and plastic containers contain no water molecules, so the microwaves do not heat them directly.", "1": "The food is heated because it absorbs the microwave energy, not because the waves bounce off it.", "2": "Microwaves only make molecules oscillate. Their energy is far too small to cause nuclear reactions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1534' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Microwave ovens use very high frequency electromagnetic waves with a wavelength of about 12 cm. As a check, a typical oven frequency of 2.45 GHz gives λ = c/f = 3 × 10⁸ ÷ 2.45 × 10⁹ ≈ 0.12 m = 12 cm.', wrong = '{"0": "12 nm lies in the far ultraviolet region, far shorter than microwaves.", "1": "12 m is a radio wavelength, corresponding to about 25 MHz.", "2": "12 km is a very long radio wavelength, corresponding to only about 25 kHz."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1535' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In MRI the patient lies in a strong magnetic field and is exposed to radio-frequency radiation. Nuclei in the body oscillate in resonance with this radiation and absorb energy. The pattern of absorbed energy is used to build a computer-enhanced image of the body.', wrong = '{"0": "MRI uses radio waves, not X-rays. X-rays are used in ordinary radiography and CT scans.", "1": "MRI does not use sound waves to vibrate bones. Ultrasound imaging uses sound, but in a different way.", "3": "The heart''s beating rate plays no part in producing an MRI image."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1536' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If each push is given at the swing''s natural frequency, it comes at the same point of every cycle and always adds energy. The amplitude builds up with small pushes. This is resonance, where a periodic force matching the natural frequency gives a large amplitude.', wrong = '{"1": "Pushes much faster than the natural frequency would often come while the swing is moving towards the pusher, opposing its motion.", "2": "Randomly timed pushes sometimes add energy and sometimes remove it, so the amplitude does not build up steadily.", "3": "A single hard push gives only one free oscillation, which then dies away by damping."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1537' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Aeroplane wings are acted on by aerodynamic forces, turbulence and engine vibrations. If the frequency of any of these matches the natural frequency of the wings, resonance occurs and the vibrations build up to a large amplitude, which can damage the structure. Designers therefore avoid such matching.', wrong = '{"0": "Resonance does not change the mass of the wing. It only increases the size of its vibrations.", "2": "The danger of resonance is large wing vibrations, not the engine stopping.", "3": "The risk is structural damage from growing vibrations, not an instant loss of all lift."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1538' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sound from the transducer reflects off the reflector, and the incident and reflected waves form a standing wave. Sound pressure is maximum at the antinodes and minimum at the nodes. This pressure difference pushes small particles into the nodes, which act as acoustic traps and hold them up against gravity.', wrong = '{"0": "The higher pressure at the antinodes pushes particles away, towards the low-pressure nodes.", "1": "The particles float in the air between the transducer and reflector, without touching the transducer.", "2": "The particles float in mid-air at the nodes, without touching the reflector."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1539' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 1860 James Clerk Maxwell treated light as an electromagnetic wave. Oscillating charges produce a changing electric field, which produces a changing magnetic field, and each keeps regenerating the other. The wave can therefore travel even through a vacuum.', wrong = '{"0": "Huygens proposed a wave theory of light in 1678, but he did not describe light as electromagnetic.", "1": "Newton proposed the corpuscular theory, in which light is a stream of tiny particles.", "3": "Young showed interference of light in 1801, proving it is a wave, but he did not identify it as electromagnetic."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1540' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A wavefront is a surface on which all points vibrate in phase. Waves from a point source spread out equally in all directions, so each wavefront is a sphere centred on the source. Very far from the source, a small part of such a sphere is nearly flat, a plane wavefront.', wrong = '{"0": "Plane wavefronts come from a plane source or from a very distant source, such as sunlight reaching Earth.", "2": "Cylindrical wavefronts come from a long line source in three dimensions, not from a point.", "3": "Straight-line wavefronts are the two-dimensional picture of plane waves, as from a vibrating bar in a ripple tank."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1541' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For an interference pattern, the waves must be of the same type, coherent (with a constant phase difference) and moving in the same or nearly the same direction. Equal amplitudes are not required. Unequal amplitudes still interfere; the dark fringes are just not completely dark, so the contrast is lower.', wrong = '{"0": "This is a real condition. Sound interferes with sound and light with light, but not one with the other.", "1": "This is a real condition. Without a constant phase difference the pattern shifts too fast to be seen.", "2": "This is a real condition. The waves must travel in the same or nearly the same direction to overlap and interfere."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1542' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Independent sources have no fixed phase relation, so the phase difference between their waves keeps changing randomly. The fringe pattern then shifts about far too quickly for the eye to follow, and the screen just looks evenly lit, with no steady bright and dark bands.', wrong = '{"1": "A steady pattern of sharp fringes needs coherent sources with a constant phase difference.", "2": "Light from both sources still reaches the screen, so it is lit, not completely dark.", "3": "Without coherence no stable fringe of any order forms, including the central one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1543' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Coherent sources keep a constant phase difference. This is possible only if both waves have the same frequency and it stays constant. If the frequencies differ, the waves keep drifting in and out of phase and the phase difference changes all the time.', wrong = '{"0": "Equal amplitudes improve the contrast of the fringes, but they are not what makes sources coherent.", "1": "The direction of travel is a separate condition for seeing interference. It does not make sources coherent.", "3": "A sound wave and a light wave are different types of wave and cannot interfere at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1544' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Bright fringes form where the waves from the two slits arrive in phase, which happens when the path difference is a whole number of wavelengths, mλ. The central fringe has zero path difference, so the first bright fringe beside it has a path difference of λ.', wrong = '{"0": "Zero path difference gives the central bright fringe, not the next one beside it.", "1": "A path difference of λ/2 makes the waves arrive out of phase, giving the first dark fringe.", "2": "A path difference of 3λ/2 gives the second dark fringe, not a bright one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1545' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Young''s experiment also works with a bright light bulb, as Young himself did in 1801 with no laser. A laser is more convenient because its light is concentrated into a narrow, more intense beam, so the fringes are bright and easy to see.', wrong = '{"0": "A laser gives nearly monochromatic light, a single wavelength, not many wavelengths.", "1": "Light from any source, including a bulb, is diffracted by narrow slits.", "2": "The two slits are still needed to split the laser light into two coherent sources."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1546' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The fringes lie close to the centre of the screen, so their distances y are tiny compared with the slit-to-screen distance L, typically about a metre. The angle θ is then very small, and for small angles sin θ ≈ tan θ = y/L. This leads to y = mλL/d.', wrong = '{"0": "The slit separation is a fraction of a millimetre, far smaller than the screen distance, not larger.", "1": "The approximation holds for any colour of light, as long as the angle is small.", "3": "Narrow slits are used, and slit width is not the reason the small-angle approximation holds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1547' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Light reflected from the top and bottom surfaces of a thin film interferes. Whether a colour is reinforced depends on the path difference, which is set by the film''s thickness. Since the thickness varies from place to place, different colours are reinforced at different places, giving complicated coloured patterns.', wrong = '{"1": "If all colours were absorbed equally, no colours would be seen at all.", "2": "Refraction alone does not produce these colours. They come from interference between light reflected at the film''s two surfaces.", "3": "A soap film does not produce its own light. It only reflects the light falling on it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1548' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Newton''s rings come from interference in the thin air film between a curved lens and a flat glass plate. With monochromatic light, such as the yellow light of a sodium lamp, clear alternate bright and dark rings are seen.', wrong = '{"1": "A filament bulb gives white light, which produces overlapping coloured rings instead of clear bright and dark ones.", "2": "Sunlight is white light, a mixture of many wavelengths, not monochromatic.", "3": "A candle flame gives a broad range of wavelengths, not a single one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1549' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A diffraction grating is made by using a diamond point to scratch many fine, equally spaced grooves on a glass or metal surface. Gratings can also be made by photographically reducing a pattern of black and white stripes. The first grating was made by Fraunhofer using fine wires.', wrong = '{"0": "Drilling holes in a mirror with a laser is not a method given for making gratings.", "1": "Melting glass into threads is not a way of making gratings.", "3": "A thin oil film on water shows thin-film interference colours. It is not a diffraction grating."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1550' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The grating spacing d is the reciprocal of the number of slits per unit length. With 1000 slits in 1 mm, d = 1 mm ÷ 1000 = 10⁻³ mm = 10⁻⁶ m = 1000 nm. This is of the same order as visible wavelengths, 400 to 700 nm.', wrong = '{"0": "1000 mm comes from multiplying instead of taking the reciprocal. The spacing must be smaller than 1 mm.", "1": "1 nm is a thousand times too small. 10⁻⁶ m is 1000 nm.", "2": "1 mm is the length that contains all 1000 slits, not the gap between neighbouring slits."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1551' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For the same slit spacing, a grating has its principal maxima at the same angles as a double slit, given by d sin θ = mλ. But with thousands of slits the maxima become very narrow, sharp and bright. Their angles can be measured very precisely, giving a precise wavelength.', wrong = '{"0": "More slits make the maxima narrower and brighter, not wider and fainter.", "2": "A grating still produces a central, zero-order maximum, at θ = 0.", "3": "Ordinary gratings are used with visible light. Crystals act as gratings for X-rays."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1552' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 1914 the father and son W.H. Bragg and W.L. Bragg began studying the atomic structure of crystals with X-rays. They showed that X-rays reflect from planes of atoms in a crystal, which led to the Bragg equation 2d sin θ = nλ.', wrong = '{"0": "Young and Fresnel worked on interference and diffraction of visible light in the early 1800s, not X-rays.", "1": "Newton and Huygens proposed early theories of light in the 1600s, long before X-rays were discovered.", "2": "Fraunhofer made the first grating and Maxwell described electromagnetic waves. Neither began X-ray crystallography."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1553' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electric potential is a scalar, so the potentials from the charges simply add, keeping their signs. Since all are 0.30 m from P, V = 9 × 10⁹ × (2 + 3 − 1) × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 0.30 = 36 ÷ 0.30 = 120 V.', wrong = '{"0": "180 V treats all three charges as positive (6 nC), ignoring the sign of the −1 nC charge.", "2": "60 V is the potential from the +2 nC charge alone, 9 × 10⁹ × 2 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 0.30.", "3": "The charges do not cancel. Their algebraic sum is +4 nC, not zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1554' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The potential of a point charge is V = q/(4πε₀r), which is inversely proportional to r, not r². Making the distance four times larger makes the potential one-quarter as large: 400 ÷ 4 = 100 V.', wrong = '{"1": "25 V uses a 1/r² dependence, which is how the electric field varies, not the potential.", "2": "Moving farther from the charge lowers the potential. 1600 V would mean it rose four times.", "3": "200 V would need the distance only to double, from r to 2r."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1555' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For V to be zero outside the charges, the point must be nearer the smaller charge, so it lies beyond the −3 μC charge. Let it be y beyond that charge, and 40 + y from the +9 μC charge. Then 9/(40 + y) = 3/y, so 9y = 120 + 3y and y = 20 cm.', wrong = '{"0": "Beyond the +9 μC charge, the larger charge is always closer, so its potential always outweighs that of −3 μC and V cannot be zero.", "1": "At 10 cm beyond, 9/50 = 0.18 but 3/10 = 0.30, so the potentials do not cancel.", "2": "At 60 cm beyond, 9/100 = 0.09 but 3/60 = 0.05, so the potentials do not cancel."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1556' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A positive charge is pushed along the field, from higher to lower potential. A negative charge feels a force opposite to the field, so it moves from lower to higher potential. In doing so it loses electric potential energy and gains kinetic energy.', wrong = '{"0": "Moving from higher to lower potential is how a free positive charge moves, not a negative one.", "1": "A free charge does not stay on an equipotential surface. The field pushes it across the equipotentials.", "2": "A charge in an electric field always feels a force qE, so when released it accelerates."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1557' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The kinetic energy gained by a charge accelerated through a potential difference is qΔV. With q = 2e and ΔV = 1000 V, the energy is 2e × 1000 V = 2000 eV, which is 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ J.', wrong = '{"1": "1000 eV is the energy gained by a particle of charge e. The alpha particle carries 2e, so it gains twice as much.", "2": "A charge of 2e doubles the energy gained. It does not halve it.", "3": "1.6 × 10⁻¹⁶ is the energy in joules for a charge e through 1000 V. It is not a value in electronvolts."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1558' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The potential energy of two point charges is U = (1/4πε₀)(Qq/r). Substituting, U = 9 × 10⁹ × (2 × 10⁻⁶)(−3 × 10⁻⁶) ÷ 0.30 = −0.054 ÷ 0.30 = −0.18 J. Unlike charges attract, so the potential energy is negative.', wrong = '{"0": "The product of a positive and a negative charge is negative, so U must be negative for unlike charges.", "2": "−0.60 J comes from dividing by r² = 0.09 m². Potential energy depends on 1/r, not 1/r².", "3": "−1.8 J is ten times too large, a slip in the powers of ten."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1559' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Applying Gauss''s law to a parallel plate capacitor with surface charge density σ shows that the field between the oppositely charged plates is uniform with E = σ/ε₀. Combining this with V = Ed and Q = σA gives C = Aε₀/d.', wrong = '{"0": "σ/2ε₀ is the field of a single isolated charged sheet. Between two oppositely charged plates the fields add to σ/ε₀.", "1": "σε₀ has units of C² N⁻¹ m⁻⁴, which are not the units of electric field.", "3": "ε₀/σ inverts the relation and also has the wrong units."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1560' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The capacitance of an isolated sphere is C = 4πε₀ε_rR. Surrounding it with a medium of dielectric constant ε_r multiplies its capacitance by ε_r. So the new capacitance is 10 pF × 2.5 = 25 pF.', wrong = '{"0": "4 pF comes from dividing by 2.5. A dielectric increases capacitance, it does not reduce it.", "1": "The surrounding medium does change the capacitance, through the factor ε_r.", "2": "12.5 pF comes from adding 2.5 pF instead of multiplying by 2.5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1561' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Capacitance is defined as C = Q/V, so its unit is the coulomb per volt. A capacitor has a capacitance of 1 farad if a charge of 1 C on it gives a potential difference of 1 V. The unit is named after Michael Faraday.', wrong = '{"1": "A volt per coulomb is the reciprocal of the farad.", "2": "A joule per coulomb is the volt, the unit of potential difference.", "3": "A coulomb per second is the ampere, the unit of current."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1562' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Charge is conserved when the capacitors are joined. At first Q = 60 μF × 40 V = 2400 μC. Afterwards the 60 μF capacitor holds 60 × 24 = 1440 μC, so 960 μC has moved to the second one. Its capacitance is C₂ = 960 μC ÷ 24 V = 40 μF.', wrong = '{"0": "100 μF is the total capacitance of the pair, 2400 μC ÷ 24 V, not the second capacitor alone.", "1": "36 μF does not conserve charge: 60 × 24 + 36 × 24 = 2304 μC, not 2400 μC.", "3": "With 90 μF the total would be 150 μF, and the common voltage would fall to 2400 ÷ 150 = 16 V, not 24 V."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1563' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a series connection the same charge Q flows onto every capacitor. Since V = Q/C, the capacitor with the smaller capacitance has the larger potential difference across it. The two voltages add up to the battery voltage.', wrong = '{"0": "With equal charge, V = Q/C is smaller for the larger capacitor, not larger.", "1": "Equal potential differences, each equal to the battery voltage, describe a parallel connection.", "3": "In series every capacitor carries the same charge, so the larger one does not store more charge."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1564' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For capacitors in series, 1/C = 1/C₁ + 1/C₂ + 1/C₃. Here 1/C = 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/6 = 3/6 + 2/6 + 1/6 = 1, so C = 1 μF. A series combination is always smaller than the smallest capacitor.', wrong = '{"0": "11 μF is the sum 2 + 3 + 6, which is the equivalent capacitance in parallel, not in series.", "2": "3.7 μF is the plain average of the three values, which has no meaning for a combination.", "3": "6 μF is the largest single value. A series combination is smaller than even the smallest capacitor."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1565' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If the plates of a charged capacitor are joined with a thick wire, charge flows through the wire until the plates are neutral, and the wire becomes hot. Heat is a form of energy, so the charged capacitor must have stored that energy in its electric field.', wrong = '{"0": "Charging only moves electrons from one plate to the other. It does not make the plates measurably heavier.", "1": "Capacitance depends on the plate area, separation and dielectric, not on whether the capacitor is charged.", "2": "A charged capacitor does not give out light. Its energy is shown by the heating when it discharges."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1566' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For two capacitors in series, C_eq = C₁C₂/(C₁ + C₂) = (3 × 6)/(3 + 6) = 2 μF. The energy stored is ½C_eqV² = ½ × 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 90² = 10⁻⁶ × 8100 = 8.1 × 10⁻³ J.', wrong = '{"1": "3.6 × 10⁻² J uses the parallel value, 9 μF: ½ × 9 × 10⁻⁶ × 8100. The capacitors are in series.", "2": "1.6 × 10⁻² J leaves out the factor ½: 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 8100 ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻² J.", "3": "2.4 × 10⁻² J uses only the 6 μF capacitor with the full 90 V across it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1567' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Resistance is R = V/I, and since I = q/t, R = Vt/q. Capacitance is C = q/V. Multiplying, RC = (Vt/q)(q/V) = t. So one ohm times one farad is one second, which is why RC is called the time constant.', wrong = '{"0": "A volt is a joule per coulomb. Ω × F leaves only a unit of time.", "1": "An ampere is a coulomb per second, not the product of ohm and farad.", "2": "A coulomb is the unit of charge. Ω × F = s, a unit of time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1568' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The time constant is τ = RC = 2000 × 50 × 10⁻⁶ = 0.10 s, so 0.10 s is exactly one time constant. During discharge V = V₀e^(−t/RC), so after one time constant V = 20 ÷ 2.718 ≈ 7.4 V, about 37% of the starting voltage.', wrong = '{"0": "2.7 V is V₀e⁻², the voltage after two time constants (0.20 s), not one.", "1": "12.6 V is about 63% of V₀, the fraction reached after one time constant when charging, not what remains when discharging.", "2": "10 V is half of V₀, reached after about 0.69RC ≈ 0.069 s, before one full time constant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1569' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'During discharge Q = Q₀e^(−t/RC). The current, and so the rate at which charge falls, depends on how much charge is left. So the graph falls steeply at first and then flattens out more and more, like water draining from a tank.', wrong = '{"0": "A constant rate of fall would need a constant current, but the current falls as the charge falls.", "2": "A curve that rises steeply and then levels off describes a capacitor charging, not discharging.", "3": "A horizontal line would mean the charge stays the same, but it decreases with time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1570' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'During discharge the charge falls as Q = Q₀e^(−t/RC). Since V = Q/C and I = V/R, the voltage and the current are proportional to the charge. So all three decay exponentially, falling by the same factor in the same time, with the same time constant RC.', wrong = '{"0": "The voltage and current also decay, since V = Q/C and I = V/R are proportional to the charge.", "1": "The charge and voltage also decay, with the same time constant as the current.", "3": "The decay is exponential, fast at first and then slower, not a straight-line fall."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1571' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The time constant RC is the time for the charge to fall to about 37% of its starting value. A larger RC means this takes longer: a bigger resistance limits the current, and a bigger capacitance holds more charge. So the capacitor discharges more slowly.', wrong = '{"1": "A smaller time constant, not a larger one, gives a faster discharge.", "2": "Instant discharge would need RC = 0. A larger RC makes discharge slower.", "3": "Discharge begins as soon as the circuit is closed, whatever the value of RC. It just proceeds more slowly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1572' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the fan is switched on, the capacitor gives the motor an extra boost of power. This helps the motor overcome static friction and the initial resistance due to inertia, so it starts spinning quickly. Capacitors do the same job in motors of washing machines, blenders and water pumps.', wrong = '{"0": "The speed setting is controlled by the fan''s regulator. The capacitor does not store it.", "2": "The fan motor runs on AC. This capacitor is not a rectifier and does not convert AC to DC.", "3": "Cooling the motor is not the capacitor''s job. It helps the motor start."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1573' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A start capacitor gives the compressor motor a ''jump start'' and is then disconnected by a relay once the motor reaches speed. A run capacitor, by contrast, stays connected in the circuit while the motor runs, which improves energy efficiency.', wrong = '{"0": "Working only at start-up and then being cut out by a relay describes the start capacitor, not the run capacitor.", "1": "A capacitor stores electric charge and energy, not the refrigerant gas.", "3": "A flash produced by a capacitor is used in camera flashguns, not for a fridge''s interior light."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1574' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a camera flashgun a capacitor is charged and stores energy. When the picture is taken, it discharges rapidly into the flash tube. The large current ionises the xenon gas inside the tube, which gives a brief, very bright flash of light.', wrong = '{"1": "Neon is used in glowing signs and indicator lamps, not in the flash tube described.", "2": "The gas in a camera flash tube is xenon, not hydrogen.", "3": "Carbon dioxide is not used in flash tubes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1575' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In ECG and EEG machines, capacitors filter out unwanted signals so the small signals from the heart or brain are recorded clearly. In imaging machines such as CT and MRI, they help filter out noise and improve image clarity.', wrong = '{"0": "X-rays are produced in an X-ray tube, not by a capacitor.", "1": "Patient records are stored on digital memory devices, not in capacitors.", "2": "The magnetic field in MRI is produced by large electromagnets, not directly by capacitors."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1576' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An alternating current reverses its direction every half-cycle. For it to count as alternating, each cycle must have two exactly symmetrical halves, one positive and one negative, and the peak value must stay the same in every cycle.', wrong = '{"1": "Unequal positive and negative halves do not fit the symmetrical waveform that defines AC.", "2": "The peak value of AC stays constant from cycle to cycle. It does not grow.", "3": "A current with no negative values never reverses direction, so it is not alternating."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1577' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Near a high-voltage line the electric field can exceed the strength that air can withstand. The air around the conductor is then ionised, which is called corona discharge. It can give a faint glow, radio noise and a hissing or crackling sound.', wrong = '{"0": "The sound is linked to ionisation of the air by the strong field, not to the wires vibrating at mains frequency.", "1": "Birds sitting on the wires do not cause the hissing sound.", "3": "Damp weather can make corona worse, but the cause is ionisation of the air by the strong electric field."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1578' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a heating element, P = VI with rms values, so I = P/V = 1000 W ÷ 250 V = 4 A. Its resistance is R = V/I = 250 ÷ 4 = 62.5 Ω. As a check, R = V²/P = 62 500 ÷ 1000 = 62.5 Ω.', wrong = '{"1": "0.25 A comes from dividing V by P instead of P by V, and 1000 Ω follows from that wrong current. Such an element would use only 62.5 W.", "2": "4 A is correct, but the resistance is R = V/I = 62.5 Ω, not 1000 Ω.", "3": "250 A with 1 Ω would mean a power of 250 × 250 = 62.5 kW, not 1 kW."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1579' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A choke is a coil of thick copper wire on a laminated soft-iron core. It has a large inductance but very small resistance, so the current lags the voltage by nearly 90°. The power factor cos θ is then almost zero, and very little power is wasted as heat.', wrong = '{"0": "A choke has a very large inductance, made larger by its iron core, not an inductance near zero.", "2": "A choke is a copper coil on an iron core. It is not a semiconductor device.", "3": "With DC, X_L = 0 and a choke acts as a small resistance. It is used in AC circuits."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1580' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the current through the choke is suddenly interrupted, the rapid change of current induces a large self-induced emf in it. This brief high voltage is enough to break down the gas in the tube and start the discharge. After that the choke limits the current.', wrong = '{"0": "The choke does not start the lamp by heating the tube. It supplies a brief high voltage.", "1": "A choke stores energy in its magnetic field, not as light.", "3": "A choke does not convert AC to DC. Rectification is done by diodes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1581' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An AC supply keeps reversing, so the capacitor is charged first one way and then the other. Each time, electrons flow onto one plate and off the other through the connecting wires, then back again. This to-and-fro flow in the wires is an alternating current, even though no charge crosses the gap.', wrong = '{"0": "The dielectric stays an insulator. No charge crosses the gap between the plates.", "1": "An ideal capacitor does not leak charge through its dielectric.", "2": "A capacitor blocks steady DC, so there is no DC current for the ammeter to read."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1582' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nikola Tesla, born in 1856, pioneered the generation, transmission and use of alternating current. AC can be stepped up to high voltage by transformers and sent over long distances with low losses, which is why Tesla is often called the father of AC.', wrong = '{"0": "Faraday discovered electromagnetic induction, the basis of generators, but he did not develop AC power systems.", "1": "Joseph Henry worked on self-induction, and the unit of inductance, the henry, is named after him.", "2": "Thomas Edison promoted direct current supply systems, not alternating current."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1583' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Impedance is Z = V_rms/I_rms. Each rms value is the peak value divided by √2, so the √2 cancels and the ratio of peak values gives the same answer. Z = 200 V ÷ 10 A = 20 Ω.', wrong = '{"1": "2000 comes from multiplying voltage by current. That gives a power-like quantity, not an impedance.", "2": "0.05 Ω comes from dividing the current by the voltage, 10 ÷ 200.", "3": "14 Ω comes from dividing only the voltage by √2, giving 141 V ÷ 10 A, while leaving the current as a peak value."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1584' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A capacitor stores energy in its electric field during one quarter-cycle and gives it back in the next, so its average power is zero. The only power used in an RC circuit is dissipated as heat in the resistor: P = VI cos θ = I²R.', wrong = '{"0": "A capacitor takes in energy and returns it each cycle, so it dissipates no power on average.", "1": "The capacitor takes no net power, so the power is not shared equally between the two.", "2": "The resistor turns electrical energy into heat, so the average power is not zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1585' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The reactances are X_C = 1/(ωC) and X_L = ωL. At low frequency X_C is large and X_L is small. Well below resonance the capacitive reactance dominates, so the circuit behaves mainly like an RC series circuit, with the current leading the voltage.', wrong = '{"1": "X_L dominates at high frequency, above resonance. Below resonance X_C is the larger one.", "2": "X_L equals X_C only at the resonant frequency, where the circuit acts like a pure resistor.", "3": "An open circuit has infinite impedance, not zero, and neither describes this circuit."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1586' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The phase angle is given by tan θ = (V_L − V_C)/V_R = (40 − 16) ÷ 32 = 24 ÷ 32 = 0.75, so θ ≈ 37°. Since V_L is larger than V_C, the circuit is inductive and the voltage leads the current. The source voltage is √(32² + 24²) = 40 V.', wrong = '{"0": "53° is tan⁻¹(32/24), the complementary angle. Also, in an inductive circuit the current lags, it does not lead.", "1": "A 90° phase angle would need V_R = 0, a circuit with no resistance.", "3": "The phase angle is zero only at resonance, when V_L = V_C. Here 40 V is not equal to 16 V."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1587' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an RLC circuit the resistor dissipates energy, so it damps the oscillations of the current, much as a shock absorber damps a car''s spring. A higher resistance means more damping, so the current oscillations die away faster. R does not change the resonant frequency.', wrong = '{"0": "Increasing R adds damping. Damping could only be removed by reducing R towards zero.", "2": "The resonant frequency is f₀ = 1/(2π√LC). It depends on L and C, not on R.", "3": "The circuit resonates only at the one frequency where X_L = X_C, whatever the resistance."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1588' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At resonance in a parallel RLC circuit, the impedance is maximum, so the current drawn from the source is minimum. But large currents surge back and forth between the inductor and the capacitor. Each of these branch currents can be bigger than the small source current, which is called current magnification.', wrong = '{"0": "At parallel resonance the source current is a minimum, not a maximum, and the branch currents are large, not zero.", "1": "The currents are not equal. The inductor and capacitor branches carry much larger currents than the source.", "3": "At resonance large currents flow in both the inductor branch and the capacitor branch."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1589' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At resonance X_L = X_C, so the effects of the inductor and capacitor cancel and the circuit behaves as purely resistive. The voltage and current are then in phase, θ = 0, and the power factor is cos 0° = 1.', wrong = '{"0": "A power factor of zero belongs to a purely reactive circuit, where current and voltage are 90° apart.", "2": "A power factor of 0.5 would mean a 60° phase difference between voltage and current.", "3": "The power factor is cos θ, which can never be larger than 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1590' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A phone battery stores energy as direct current and must be charged with DC. The wall outlet supplies AC. The charger''s full-wave bridge rectifier converts the AC into DC, using both halves of each cycle, so the battery can be charged.', wrong = '{"0": "Phone batteries store and are charged with direct current, not alternating current.", "1": "A rectifier changes AC into DC. Stepping the voltage up or down is the job of a transformer.", "3": "A bridge rectifier is made of four diodes, so it does not remove the need for them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1591' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A PN junction diode conducts a large current when forward biased but almost none when reverse biased. Since it lets current pass in only one direction, it can turn alternating current into current that flows one way, which is rectification.', wrong = '{"0": "A device that conducts equally in both directions could not turn AC into one-way current.", "1": "A diode does not change the frequency of the supply. It only blocks current in one direction.", "2": "Storing charge is the job of a capacitor. A diode rectifies because it conducts in one direction only."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1592' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The opposition of an inductor to AC is its inductive reactance, X_L = 2πfL. Rearranging, L = X_L ÷ (2πf). Here 2πf = 2 × 3.14 × 50 = 314 s⁻¹, so L = 160 ÷ 314 ≈ 0.51 H.', wrong = '{"0": "3.2 H comes from 160 ÷ 50, which leaves out the factor 2π.", "1": "8000 H comes from multiplying X_L by f instead of dividing.", "3": "With 0.05 H the reactance at 50 Hz would be 2π × 50 × 0.05 ≈ 15.7 Ω, only a tenth of 160 Ω."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1593' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The energy stored in an inductor''s magnetic field is U = ½LI². Self-inductance L is always positive, and I² is never negative whichever way the current flows. So the stored energy can never be negative.', wrong = '{"1": "Self-inductance is a positive property of a coil, set by its turns, size and core.", "2": "A current must flow through the inductor for any energy to be stored at all.", "3": "An inductor stores energy in its magnetic field, not in an electric field."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1594' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Mutual inductance is M = ε_s ÷ (ΔI_p/Δt), the emf induced in the secondary per unit rate of change of current in the primary. So M = 1 henry when a primary current changing at 1 A s⁻¹ induces an emf of 1 V in the secondary. The unit is named after Joseph Henry.', wrong = '{"0": "Mutual inductance has nothing to do with a resistance joining the two coils.", "1": "Equal numbers of turns do not define the henry. M depends on the rate of change of current and the induced emf.", "2": "A steady current produces a steady flux, which induces no emf at all in the secondary."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1595' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Photon energy is E = hc/λ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.0 × 10⁸) ÷ (620 × 10⁻⁹) ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Dividing by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J per eV gives about 2.0 eV, typical of visible (red-orange) light.', wrong = '{"1": "3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ is the photon energy in joules. It has not been converted into electronvolts.", "2": "0.5 eV is the reciprocal of the correct answer, as if the conversion were done upside down.", "3": "20 eV is ten times too large. Photons of that energy would be far into the ultraviolet."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1596' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each photon has energy E = hc/λ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.0 × 10⁸) ÷ (663 × 10⁻⁹) = 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. The number emitted per second is power ÷ E = 2.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ ≈ 6.7 × 10¹⁵.', wrong = '{"0": "3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J is the energy of one photon, not the number of photons per second.", "1": "6.7 × 10¹² comes from taking the power as 2.0 μW instead of 2.0 mW.", "3": "1.5 × 10¹⁸ photons per second would need a power of about 0.45 W, far more than 2.0 mW."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1597' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the photocell the electrodes are separated by a vacuum, so the battery alone cannot drive a current across the gap. Current flows only while light falls on the cathode and stops as soon as the light is cut off. This shows the light releases the electrons that carry the current.', wrong = '{"0": "If the battery alone drove the current, it would keep flowing after the light was cut off.", "2": "The metal cathode has an enormous supply of free electrons and does not run out.", "3": "The stopping potential matters only when the anode is made negative. It is not why the current stops here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1598' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In quantum theory light arrives as photons, each with energy hf. A single photon gives all its energy to one electron at once. If hf is above the work function, that electron escapes immediately, however weak the light. The wave picture wrongly predicted a delay while energy built up.', wrong = '{"0": "A slow build-up of energy is the wave-theory prediction, which experiments contradict.", "2": "Intensity and frequency are separate. Weak light can have any frequency.", "3": "Photoemission is not a heating effect. It happens instantly, before any heating could matter."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1599' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The stopping potential gives the maximum kinetic energy: K.E._max = eV₀ = 0.60 eV. Einstein''s equation is hf = φ + K.E._max, so φ = hf − K.E._max = 2.48 − 0.60 = 1.88 eV.', wrong = '{"0": "3.08 eV comes from adding the photon energy and the kinetic energy instead of subtracting.", "1": "0.60 eV is the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electrons, not the work function.", "2": "2.48 eV is the energy of each incoming photon, not the energy needed to free an electron."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1600' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In pair production the photon''s energy becomes the rest mass of an electron and a positron. Each has rest energy 0.51 MeV, so the photon needs at least 2 × 0.51 = 1.02 MeV. Any extra energy becomes the kinetic energy of the pair.', wrong = '{"0": "0.51 MeV provides the rest energy of only one particle, but a pair must be created.", "1": "2.04 MeV is twice the minimum. It would create the pair and leave 1.02 MeV as kinetic energy.", "2": "0.255 MeV is half of one particle''s rest energy, far too little to create a pair."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1601' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The de Broglie wavelength is λ = h/p. The ball''s momentum is p = mv = 0.050 kg × 20 m s⁻¹ = 1.0 kg m s⁻¹, so λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ÷ 1.0 ≈ 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ m. This is far too small for any wave effect to be detected.', wrong = '{"1": "6.6 × 10⁻³⁷ m comes from taking the mass as 50 kg instead of 50 g = 0.050 kg.", "2": "1.3 × 10⁻³² m comes from dividing h by the mass only, leaving out the speed.", "3": "3.3 × 10⁻³⁵ m comes from dividing h by the speed only, leaving out the mass."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1602' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After electron diffraction was shown, diffraction patterns were also observed with protons, neutrons, hydrogen atoms and helium atoms. This gives strong evidence that all material particles have a wave nature, as de Broglie proposed.', wrong = '{"0": "Photons are not the only other example. Material particles such as protons and neutrons also diffract.", "1": "Light and sound are already known to be waves. The point is that material particles diffract too.", "3": "Many particles besides electrons, including protons, neutrons and whole atoms, show diffraction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1603' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Light travels from source to detector as a wave, which is why it shows interference and diffraction. When it interacts with matter, as in the photoelectric effect, it behaves as a stream of photons. Both pictures are needed, but a single experiment shows only one of them.', wrong = '{"0": "This swaps the two roles. Light travels as a wave and interacts with matter as photons.", "2": "The photoelectric effect cannot be explained by waves alone. It needs the photon picture.", "3": "Interference and diffraction cannot be explained by particles alone. They need the wave picture."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1604' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'J. J. Thomson measured e/m by deflecting electrons with electric and magnetic fields. This treats the electron as a charged particle with a definite mass, so its particle nature is assumed. Its wave nature is shown by a different experiment, electron diffraction.', wrong = '{"1": "The wave nature of the electron is shown by electron diffraction (Davisson and Germer), not by the e/m experiment.", "2": "No single experiment shows both the wave and particle natures at the same time.", "3": "The experiment depends on treating the electron as a particle with charge and mass moving in fields."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1605' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an electron microscope, the final magnified image of the specimen formed by the projector lens can be recorded on film. This photograph of the image is called a micrograph.', wrong = '{"0": "A spectrogram is a record of a spectrum, not of a microscope image.", "1": "A radiograph is an image made with X-rays, as in medical X-ray pictures.", "3": "A hologram is a three-dimensional interference record made with laser light."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1606' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each element has its own set of electron energy levels. When its vapour is excited, it emits light only at wavelengths matching the gaps between those levels, giving a pattern of lines unique to that element. Matching an observed pattern identifies the element, like a fingerprint.', wrong = '{"0": "If every element gave the same lines, spectra could not tell elements apart.", "2": "A spectrum containing all wavelengths is a continuous spectrum, not a line spectrum.", "3": "All elements produce line spectra. Hydrogen simply has the simplest one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1607' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For n = 3, 1/λ = R_H(1/4 − 1/9) = R_H × 5/36 = 1.097 × 10⁷ × 0.1389 ≈ 1.524 × 10⁶ m⁻¹. Taking the reciprocal gives λ ≈ 6.56 × 10⁻⁷ m, about 656 nm, the red line of hydrogen.', wrong = '{"0": "About 486 nm is the second Balmer line, from n = 4 to n = 2.", "1": "About 365 nm is the Balmer series limit, when n becomes very large.", "2": "About 122 nm is the first Lyman line, from n = 2 to n = 1, in the ultraviolet."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1608' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The photon energy equals the energy difference: ΔE = E₂ − E₁ = −5.45 × 10⁻¹⁹ − (−21.8 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 16.35 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Then λ = hc/ΔE = 1.99 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 1.635 × 10⁻¹⁸ ≈ 1.22 × 10⁻⁷ m = 122 nm. Lines ending on n = 1 form the Lyman series in the ultraviolet.', wrong = '{"1": "Balmer lines, such as 656 nm, come from transitions ending on n = 2, not n = 1.", "2": "About 91 nm comes from using the whole of |E₁|, the ionisation energy. That is the Lyman series limit.", "3": "Infrared lines such as 1875 nm come from transitions ending on n = 3, the Paschen series."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1609' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Atoms of the gas absorb only photons whose energy exactly matches a gap between their energy levels. These are the same gaps that produce emitted photons when the atoms are excited. So the dark absorption lines appear at exactly the wavelengths of bright lines in the element''s emission spectrum.', wrong = '{"0": "The same energy gaps cause both kinds of line, so the wavelengths match rather than differ.", "2": "An absorption spectrum does have lines: dark lines on a continuous bright background.", "3": "The dark lines appear wherever the energy gaps place them, including in the visible region."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1610' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An atom absorbs energy only in definite amounts, which raises the electron to an excited state. Excited states are very short-lived: the electron soon drops back to a lower level and emits a photon whose energy equals the gap. These photons make up the emission spectrum.', wrong = '{"0": "Excited states last only a very short time before the electron falls back.", "2": "Photons are emitted exactly when the electron falls from an excited state to a lower one.", "3": "The ground state is the lowest energy state. Excited states have higher energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1611' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To locate an electron to within Δx, light of wavelength about Δx must be used, so a shorter wavelength gives a smaller Δx. But a photon''s momentum is h/λ, so shorter-wavelength photons hit the electron harder and make its momentum more uncertain. Overall Δx Δp ≈ h.', wrong = '{"1": "The uncertainty principle says Δx and Δp cannot both be made perfectly certain at the same time.", "2": "A photon striking an electron transfers momentum to it and changes its motion.", "3": "The charge of an electron is fixed at −e. Light does not change it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1612' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By the uncertainty principle Δx Δp ≈ h. Confining an electron to Δx ≈ 10⁻¹⁴ m gives Δp ≈ h/Δx ≈ 6.6 × 10⁻²⁰ kg m s⁻¹, which corresponds to an enormous energy, of the order of hundreds of MeV towards the GeV range. Electrons in atoms never show such energies, so electrons cannot exist inside the nucleus.', wrong = '{"0": "The uncertainty principle says nothing about negative mass. Mass is never negative.", "1": "Confining the electron makes its momentum and energy very large, not zero.", "3": "Confining an electron does not turn it into a proton. The argument is about the huge energy it would need."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1613' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Control rods are made of cadmium because it can absorb a large number of neutrons without itself becoming unstable or radioactive. Pushing the rods further into the core removes more neutrons, leaving fewer to cause fission, so the rate of the chain reaction is controlled.', wrong = '{"0": "Control rods remove neutrons from the core. They do not speed neutrons up.", "2": "Releasing extra neutrons would make the chain reaction run faster, the opposite of control.", "3": "Slowing neutrons down is the job of the moderator, such as heavy water or graphite, not the control rods."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1614' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To shut a reactor down, the control rods are allowed to fall fully into the core. They then absorb so many neutrons that too few remain to cause further fission, and the chain reaction stops.', wrong = '{"0": "Shut-down is done with the control rods, which absorb neutrons, not by removing the moderator.", "2": "Adding more fuel would make the chain reaction harder to stop, not easier.", "3": "Coolant carries heat away. Pumping it into the turbine does not reduce the number of neutrons in the core."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1615' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Pakistan has three nuclear power plants operating at Karachi and four at Chashma (Mianwali), with a total capacity of more than 3000 MW. All of them are pressurized water reactors (PWR), and at Chashma the cooling water comes from the Indus river.', wrong = '{"0": "The plants are not described as fast breeder reactors but as pressurized water reactors.", "1": "No fusion reactor yet produces electricity for the grid. Fusion reactors are still experimental.", "3": "They are pressurized water reactors, which do use a moderator."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1616' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the Karachi Nuclear Power Plant (KANUPP), heavy water acts as the moderator and also carries heat from the reactor core to the heat exchanger. The steam coming out of the turbine is cooled using sea water, which is easily available on the coast.', wrong = '{"0": "Heavy water is the moderator and carries heat from the core. It is not used to cool the turbine steam.", "1": "Liquid sodium is a coolant in some fast reactors, not at KANUPP.", "3": "Air alone is not used. The plant cools its turbine steam with sea water."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1617' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Natural uranium contains only about 0.7% uranium-235, so early reactors used fuel enriched to about 2.4 to 3.0% U-235. Nowadays plutonium-239 and uranium-233 are also used as fission fuels.', wrong = '{"1": "Iron-56 is among the most stable nuclei, and carbon-12 is also stable. Neither is a fission fuel.", "2": "Cadmium and boron absorb neutrons strongly, so they are used to control a reactor, not to fuel it.", "3": "Deuterium and tritium are fuels for fusion, not for fission reactors."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1618' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Spent reactor fuel still contains intensely radioactive substances, including plutonium-239. Its half-life is about 24 000 years, so waste containing it stays dangerous for a very long time and is very hard to dispose of safely.', wrong = '{"0": "3.8 days is the half-life of radon gas.", "1": "1620 years is the half-life of radium-226.", "3": "23.5 minutes is the half-life of uranium-239."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1619' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Light nuclei repel each other strongly because they are positively charged. At temperatures of millions of degrees they move fast enough to overcome this repulsion and fuse. Fusion brought about by such extreme temperature is called a thermonuclear reaction.', wrong = '{"0": "A chain reaction is the self-sustaining process in fission, where neutrons from one fission cause more fissions.", "2": "Fission splits a heavy nucleus into lighter ones. It is the opposite of joining light nuclei.", "3": "Annihilation is a particle meeting its antiparticle and turning into energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1620' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fusion needs temperatures of millions of degrees. The only way such a temperature has been reached for uncontrolled fusion is by exploding an atomic (fission) bomb. Its heat, for a very short time, triggers fusion in the hydrogen bomb.', wrong = '{"1": "An electric spark cannot produce temperatures of millions of degrees.", "2": "Fusion needs extremely high temperatures, not cooling to near absolute zero.", "3": "Radioactive tracers are used to follow processes, for example in the body. They cannot start fusion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1621' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An ''artificial sun'' is an experimental fusion reactor. China''s EAST and HL-2M/HL-3 use strong magnetic fields to hold hydrogen isotopes heated to over 100 million °C, hotter than the Sun''s core. They try to copy the fusion that powers the Sun, as a future source of clean energy.', wrong = '{"0": "They do not reflect sunlight. They are fusion experiments that produce their own heat.", "1": "The Sun is powered by fusion of light nuclei, not fission of uranium.", "3": "They are reactors built on the ground in China, not satellites in orbit."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1622' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The activity of a source is the number of nuclei decaying per second. A detector placed near it records a fixed fraction of the emitted radiation, so the count rate is directly proportional to the activity. Both fall with time in the same way.', wrong = '{"0": "The count rate depends on how many nuclei decay per second, so it cannot be independent of the activity.", "2": "After one half-life the count rate is halved, not reduced to zero.", "3": "More decays per second give more counts, not fewer, so the relation is direct, not inverse."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1623' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In each half-life, half of the nuclei that remain decay. So the number left goes N₀/2, N₀/4, N₀/8 and so on. It gets smaller and smaller but never reaches zero in a finite time; in theory an infinite time would be needed for all to decay.', wrong = '{"0": "Decay does not stop after a few half-lives. It keeps going, halving the remainder each time.", "2": "The sample does not create new nuclei of the same kind. The number of original nuclei only decreases.", "3": "The half-life of a nuclide is constant. It does not change as the sample gets older."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1624' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The decay law is ΔN = −λNΔt. Rearranging, λ = (ΔN/N)/Δt, which is the fraction of the nuclei present that decay per unit time. Its unit is s⁻¹, and a larger λ means faster decay.', wrong = '{"0": "The time for half the nuclei to decay is the half-life, related to λ by T½ = 0.693/λ.", "1": "The number of nuclei at the start is N₀, not the decay constant.", "3": "The decay constant is a rate, measured in s⁻¹, not an energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1625' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '24 days is 24 ÷ 8 = 3 half-lives. The number left undecayed is 1600 × (½)³ = 1600 ÷ 8 = 200. So the number that have decayed is 1600 − 200 = 1400.', wrong = '{"0": "200 is the number left undecayed after 24 days. The question asks how many have decayed.", "1": "800 is the number decayed after one half-life (8 days) only.", "2": "1200 is the number decayed after two half-lives (16 days)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1626' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Uranium-238 has a half-life of about 4.5 × 10⁹ years, roughly the age of the Earth. This is far longer than radium-226 (1620 years), radon (3.8 days) or uranium-239 (23.5 minutes).', wrong = '{"0": "Radon gas has a half-life of only 3.8 days.", "1": "Radium-226 has a half-life of 1620 years, very short compared with uranium-238.", "3": "Uranium-239 has a half-life of only 23.5 minutes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1627' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First find the number of moles: 2.41 μg = 2.41 × 10⁻⁶ g, and 2.41 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 241 = 1.0 × 10⁻⁸ mol. Then N = moles × N_A = 1.0 × 10⁻⁸ × 6.02 × 10²³ = 6.02 × 10¹⁵ nuclei.', wrong = '{"1": "6.02 × 10²¹ comes from treating 2.41 μg as 2.41 g, a factor of a million too large.", "2": "1.45 × 10²⁰ nuclei would have a mass of about 58 mg, far more than 2.41 μg.", "3": "6.02 × 10¹⁸ comes from treating 2.41 μg as 2.41 mg, a factor of a thousand too large."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1628' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A radioactive isotope has the same chemistry as the stable isotope of the element, so the body treats both alike. But the radioactive atoms emit radiation, so a detector outside the body can follow where they go. This makes them useful ''tagged'' atoms, or tracers.', wrong = '{"1": "If it behaved differently from the stable isotope, it would not show where the normal substance goes.", "2": "A tracer is useful precisely because it decays and gives out radiation that can be detected.", "3": "A tracer must give radiation that a detector picks up, otherwise it could not be traced."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1629' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Different organs take up particular elements. Radio-iodine collects mostly in the thyroid gland, phosphorus in the bones and cobalt in the liver. So a phosphorus tracer collects in the bones and can be used to study them.', wrong = '{"0": "Cobalt, not phosphorus, is the element named as collecting in the liver.", "2": "Iodine, not phosphorus, is absorbed mostly by the thyroid gland.", "3": "Phosphorus is not linked with the lungs. It collects in the bones."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1630' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Experiments show that cells which multiply rapidly, like cancer cells, absorb more radiation and are more easily destroyed by ionising radiation than normal cells. This is why radiotherapy can kill a tumour while doing less harm to healthy tissue.', wrong = '{"0": "If cancer cells were unaffected by radiation, radiotherapy could not work.", "2": "Normal cells absorb some radiation and can be damaged, but cancer cells absorb more.", "3": "Radiation affects cells in any tissue it passes through, not only those in the blood."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1631' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For skin cancer, phosphorus-32 or strontium-90 may be used. Both emit beta particles with a short range, suitable for treating the surface. The dose has to be controlled carefully to avoid damaging healthy tissue.', wrong = '{"0": "Iodine-131 is used for thyroid problems and sodium-24 for blood circulation and plasma volume, not skin cancer.", "1": "Uranium-235 and plutonium-239 are fission fuels, not sources for treating skin cancer.", "2": "Carbon-12 and oxygen-16 are stable isotopes and give out no radiation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1632' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ionising radiation does not pick out only cancer cells. As it passes through the body it also damages some healthy cells and tissues. This damage to healthy tissue is why patients undergoing radiation treatment often feel ill.', wrong = '{"1": "External radiation treatment does not make the patient permanently radioactive.", "2": "Radiation does not cool the body. Its harmful effect comes from ionising cells.", "3": "Radiation treatment does not remove all of the patient''s red blood cells."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1633' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In some cancer treatments small radioactive sources called ''seeds'' are implanted directly into the malignant tissue. Their radiation has a short range, so it treats the tumour locally while sparing most of the healthy tissue around it.', wrong = '{"0": "The seeds are implanted into the tumour, not swallowed to coat the stomach.", "2": "They are not sprayed on the skin, and they give no protection like a sunscreen.", "3": "They are placed inside the malignant tissue, not mixed with the patient''s food."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1634' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = '''Piezoelectric'' comes from the Greek ''piezein'', meaning ''to press''. Squeezing a piezoelectric crystal produces a potential difference across it. The reverse also works: an alternating voltage makes the crystal vibrate, which is how ultrasound transducers work.', wrong = '{"0": "The word has nothing to do with shining or giving out light.", "2": "Vibration is how a piezoelectric transducer is used, but ''piezein'' means ''to press''.", "3": "''To slice'' is the meaning of the Greek ''tomos'', as in tomography."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1635' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gamma rays have very high energy and the greatest penetrating power of the nuclear radiations. In PET scans the gamma photons pass out of the body to the detectors, and in cancer treatment they can reach and destroy tumours deep inside the body.', wrong = '{"0": "Gamma rays have the highest frequencies in the electromagnetic spectrum, not low ones.", "2": "Gamma rays are ionising radiation, which is exactly why they can destroy cancer cells.", "3": "Gamma rays have the shortest wavelengths in the electromagnetic spectrum, not long ones."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1636' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Acoustic impedance is set by the density of the tissue and the speed of sound in it. When ultrasound meets a boundary between two tissues, the bigger the difference in their acoustic impedances, the more sound is reflected as an echo.', wrong = '{"0": "Colour and temperature do not set the acoustic impedance of a tissue.", "1": "Electrical resistance has nothing to do with how sound waves are reflected.", "3": "Atomic number matters for how X-rays are absorbed, not for how ultrasound is reflected."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1637' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Obstetrics is the branch of medicine dealing with pregnancy. Ultrasound is used there to monitor the growth of the fetus, estimate its gestational age and find abnormalities. It is preferred because it uses sound waves, not ionising radiation, so it is safe for mother and baby.', wrong = '{"1": "Destroying kidney stones is not an obstetric use, and it is not described as the use of ultrasound in pregnancy.", "2": "Obstetrics deals with pregnancy, not with imaging the skulls of adults.", "3": "The radioactivity of a tracer is measured with radiation detectors such as gamma cameras, not with ultrasound."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1638' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Bremsstrahlung is German for ''braking radiation''. In an X-ray tube, fast electrons are slowed down sharply as they pass near nuclei in the target. A decelerating charge emits radiation, and this gives the continuous part of the X-ray spectrum.', wrong = '{"1": "Characteristic radiation is the line spectrum produced when electrons fall into vacant inner shells of target atoms.", "2": "Bremsstrahlung comes from decelerating electrons, not from radioactive decay of nuclei.", "3": "Bremsstrahlung in an X-ray tube produces X-rays, not visible light."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1639' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a CT (computed tomography) machine the X-ray tube rotates around the patient while the detectors stay in place. This collects X-ray data from all sides of one slice of the body. A computer combines the data into a cross-sectional image, and the patient is moved along for the next slice.', wrong = '{"0": "Images from many different angles are needed, so the X-ray tube cannot stay fixed.", "2": "The patient lies still while the X-ray tube rotates around them.", "3": "CT uses X-rays together with computer processing, not ultrasound."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1640' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Radiotherapy uses radiation to destroy cancer cells in two main ways. In teletherapy the radiation comes from a machine outside the patient''s body and is aimed at the tumour. In brachytherapy a radioactive source is placed inside or close to the tumour.', wrong = '{"0": "Brachytherapy places a radioactive source inside or close to the tumour, not in a machine outside the body.", "2": "Tomography is an imaging technique that produces images of slices of the body, not a treatment.", "3": "Echocardiography is ultrasound imaging of the heart, not radiotherapy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1641' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Earth''s climate system has five parts: the atmosphere (air), the hydrosphere (water), the cryosphere (ice and permafrost), the lithosphere (the Earth''s upper rocky layer) and the biosphere (living things). So the lithosphere is the upper rocky layer.', wrong = '{"0": "The layer of air around the Earth is the atmosphere.", "1": "All the living things on Earth make up the biosphere.", "3": "Oceans, lakes and rivers belong to the hydrosphere."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1642' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The biosphere is the living part of the Earth''s climate system, made up of all plants, animals and other organisms. It interacts with the atmosphere, hydrosphere, cryosphere and lithosphere, so a change in one part can cause changes in the others.', wrong = '{"0": "The cryosphere is the frozen part of the Earth, its ice and permafrost.", "2": "The lithosphere is the Earth''s upper rocky layer.", "3": "The hydrosphere is the water of the Earth, such as oceans, lakes and rivers."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1643' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An ideal blackbody absorbs all radiation falling on it. The radiation it emits when heated depends only on its temperature, not on its material or shape. As the temperature rises it emits more energy, and the peak of its spectrum shifts to shorter wavelengths.', wrong = '{"0": "The shape of the body does not change the spectrum of blackbody radiation.", "1": "An ideal blackbody''s spectrum is the same whatever material it is made of.", "3": "Surface colour affects real bodies, but an ideal blackbody''s radiation depends only on temperature."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1644' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Satellite remote sensing follows these steps: source and illumination, interaction with the atmosphere, interaction with the target, recording by the sensor, transmission and reception at a ground station, and finally interpretation and analysis. So transmission and reception comes right after recording.', wrong = '{"0": "Source and illumination is the very first step, when radiation first lights up the target.", "1": "Interaction with the target happens before the sensor records the radiation.", "2": "Interpretation and analysis is the last step, after the data has been transmitted and processed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-1645' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The slope of a displacement-time graph is Δs/Δt, the change in displacement divided by the time taken. This is the definition of velocity. A steeper line therefore means a greater velocity, and a horizontal line means zero velocity.', wrong = '{"0": "Acceleration is the slope of a velocity-time graph, not a displacement-time graph.", "2": "Displacement is read directly from the graph''s value at a given time, not from its slope.", "3": "Force cannot be read from a displacement-time graph alone, since it also needs the mass."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6001' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A line parallel to the time axis means the displacement stays the same as time passes. Its slope, which is the velocity, is zero. So the body is at rest.', wrong = '{"1": "Uniform velocity gives a straight line that slopes, since the displacement changes steadily.", "2": "Uniform acceleration gives a curved graph (a parabola) whose slope keeps changing.", "3": "Decreasing velocity gives a curve that becomes less steep with time, not a flat line."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6002' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The slope of a displacement-time graph gives the velocity. If the curve gets steeper as time passes, the slope is increasing, so the velocity is increasing. The body is therefore speeding up, or accelerating.', wrong = '{"0": "A body at rest has a horizontal displacement-time graph with zero slope.", "1": "Constant velocity gives a straight line of constant slope, not a curve.", "2": "A body slowing down would give a curve that becomes less steep with time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6003' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'On a displacement-time graph, velocity equals the slope, tan θ. So v₁/v₂ = tan 30° ÷ tan 60° = (1/√3) ÷ √3 = 1/3. The ratio of velocities is 1 : 3.', wrong = '{"0": "1 : 2 compares the angles 30° and 60° directly instead of their tangents.", "1": "1 : √3 uses only one factor of √3. tan 30° = 1/√3 and tan 60° = √3, so the ratio is 1/3.", "3": "2 : 1 inverts the ratio of the angles, and the angles are not what should be compared."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6004' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity. It is uniform when the velocity changes by equal amounts in equal intervals of time, for example by 2 m s⁻¹ every second. The velocity-time graph is then a straight line.', wrong = '{"0": "A constant velocity means zero acceleration, not uniform acceleration.", "2": "Covering equal distances in equal times means uniform velocity, with no acceleration.", "3": "If the acceleration itself changes, the acceleration is variable, not uniform."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6005' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, which is the slope of a velocity-time graph. When the graph is curved, the slope changes from point to point. The acceleration at a particular instant is the slope of the tangent drawn to the curve at that instant.', wrong = '{"1": "The area under a velocity-time graph gives the displacement, not the acceleration.", "2": "Dividing the velocity by 2 has no physical meaning here.", "3": "The length of the curve has no physical meaning on a velocity-time graph."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6006' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The distance covered equals the area under the velocity-time graph. Starting from rest with uniform acceleration, the graph is a straight line from 0 to 20 m s⁻¹ over 10 s, forming a triangle. Area = ½ × 10 s × 20 m s⁻¹ = 100 m.', wrong = '{"0": "200 m is the area of the rectangle 10 × 20. It forgets the factor ½ for a triangle.", "1": "50 m is half of the correct value, as if the factor ½ were applied twice.", "2": "400 m doubles the rectangle''s area instead of halving it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6007' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Only the vertical part of the velocity, v sin θ, decides the height. The maximum height is H = v²sin²θ/2g. Here sin 30° = 0.5, so H = (400 × 0.25) ÷ 20 = 5 m.', wrong = '{"0": "10 m comes from using sin θ instead of sin²θ: (400 × 0.5) ÷ 20.", "1": "15 m comes from using sin²60° = 0.75 instead of sin²30° = 0.25.", "3": "20 m is v²/2g, the height reached if the ball were thrown straight up."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6008' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The time of flight depends on the vertical component of velocity: T = 2v sin θ/g. Substituting, T = (2 × 20 × 0.5) ÷ 10 = 2 s. The ball takes 1 s to rise and 1 s to fall.', wrong = '{"0": "1 s is only the time to reach the highest point. The ball takes the same time again to come down.", "2": "About 3.5 s comes from using cos 30° instead of sin 30°: 2 × 20 × 0.866 ÷ 10.", "3": "4 s is the time of flight for a ball thrown straight up at 20 m s⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6009' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With no air resistance, the horizontal velocity v cos θ stays the same throughout the flight. At the highest point the vertical velocity is zero, so only the horizontal velocity remains. Gravity acts all the time, so the acceleration is still g downward.', wrong = '{"1": "The acceleration is never zero in free flight, since gravity always acts. The velocity is not zero either.", "2": "The velocity is not zero at the top, because the horizontal component is still there unless thrown straight up.", "3": "It is the vertical velocity that becomes zero at the top. Only the horizontal velocity remains."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6010' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The range of a projectile is R = v²sin 2θ/g, and at 45° this is v²sin 90°/g = v²/g. The maximum height is H = v²sin²θ/2g = v² × ½ ÷ 2g = v²/4g. Comparing the two, H = R/4.', wrong = '{"0": "The height equals the range only for a launch angle of about 76°, where tan θ = 4.", "1": "R/2 forgets the factor sin²45° = ½ in the height formula, giving v²/2g.", "2": "At 45° the maximum height is much less than the range, so it cannot be 2R."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6011' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Before firing, the gun and bullet are at rest, so their total momentum is zero. After firing it must still be zero: 0.020 × 400 = 4 × v. So 8 = 4v and v = 2 m s⁻¹, with the gun moving opposite to the bullet.', wrong = '{"0": "1 m s⁻¹ is half the correct value. 8 kg m s⁻¹ ÷ 4 kg = 2 m s⁻¹.", "1": "4 m s⁻¹ would need a 40 g bullet: 0.040 × 400 = 16 = 4 × 4.", "3": "8 is the bullet''s momentum in kg m s⁻¹, not the gun''s speed. It must be divided by the gun''s mass."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6012' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The body is at rest, so the total momentum before the explosion is zero, and it must still be zero afterwards. So 1 × 6 = 2 × v, giving v = 3 m s⁻¹. The two momenta must cancel, so the 2 kg piece moves in the opposite direction.', wrong = '{"0": "6 m s⁻¹ in the same direction would give a total momentum of 6 + 12 = 18 kg m s⁻¹, not zero.", "2": "12 m s⁻¹ comes from multiplying 6 by 2 instead of dividing. It would give 24 kg m s⁻¹, not 6.", "3": "If both pieces moved the same way, their momenta would add instead of cancelling, so the total could not be zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6013' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Newton''s third law, during a collision each body pushes the other with a force that is equal in size and opposite in direction, and both forces act for the same time. Since change in momentum = force × time, the two momentum changes are equal and opposite. So the total momentum of the pair stays the same.', wrong = '{"1": "Bodies usually keep moving after a collision. Conservation of momentum does not require them to stop.", "2": "By the third law the two forces are always equal in size, whatever the masses.", "3": "Momentum is conserved because of the equal and opposite mutual forces, not because friction cancels forces."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6014' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a perfectly elastic collision the relative speed of approach equals the relative speed of separation. A approaches B at 5 m s⁻¹, so afterwards v_B − v_A = 5. With v_B = 4 m s⁻¹, v_A = 4 − 5 = −1 m s⁻¹, so A moves backward at 1 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "If A moved forward at 1 m s⁻¹, the balls would separate at only 4 − 1 = 3 m s⁻¹, not 5 m s⁻¹.", "1": "If A also moved at 4 m s⁻¹ forward, the balls would not separate at all, which cannot happen in an elastic collision.", "2": "A would stop only if the masses were equal, and then B would move off at 5 m s⁻¹, not 4 m s⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6015' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a one-dimensional elastic collision both momentum and kinetic energy are conserved. For two equal masses with one at rest, the only way to satisfy both is for them to exchange velocities. The moving ball stops, and the other moves off with its speed.', wrong = '{"0": "Moving off together is what happens in a perfectly inelastic collision, where kinetic energy is lost.", "1": "If both rebounded at the original speed, the total momentum and kinetic energy would increase, which is impossible.", "3": "If the first ball kept half its speed, momentum and kinetic energy could not both be conserved."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6016' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Momentum is conserved: 2 × 3 = 3 × v, so the common velocity is 2 m s⁻¹. Kinetic energy before is ½ × 2 × 3² = 9 J, and after is ½ × 3 × 2² = 6 J. So 9 − 6 = 3 J is lost, turned into heat, sound and deformation.', wrong = '{"0": "Kinetic energy is not conserved when trolleys stick together. Some always turns into other forms.", "2": "6 J is the kinetic energy that remains after the collision, not the amount lost.", "3": "9 J is the kinetic energy before the collision. Not all of it is lost, since the trolleys keep moving."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6017' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At constant speed the push balances friction, so work is done against friction all the way. That work is W = f × d = 20 N × 5 m = 100 J, and it is dissipated as heat in the box, the floor and the surroundings.', wrong = '{"1": "4 comes from dividing the force by the distance, 20 ÷ 5. Work is force multiplied by distance.", "2": "25 comes from adding the force and the distance, 20 + 5. Work is their product.", "3": "Constant speed means no gain in kinetic energy, but work is still done against friction and turns into heat."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6018' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At constant speed the engine''s driving force equals the total resistive force, 1500 N. Power is P = Fv = 1500 N × 20 m s⁻¹ = 30 000 W, which is 30 kW.', wrong = '{"0": "75 W comes from dividing the force by the speed, 1500 ÷ 20. Power is force multiplied by speed.", "1": "3 kW is ten times too small. 1500 × 20 = 30 000 W.", "2": "300 kW is ten times too large. 1500 × 20 = 30 000 W = 30 kW."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6019' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Efficiency = useful output ÷ input × 100% = 400 ÷ 500 × 100% = 80%. The power wasted is input minus useful output, 500 − 400 = 100 W, mainly as heat and sound.', wrong = '{"0": "125% comes from dividing input by output. No machine can have an efficiency above 100%.", "1": "80% is correct, but 400 W is the useful output, not the power wasted.", "3": "100 W wasted is correct, but 20% is the fraction wasted, not the efficiency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6020' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gravitational potential energy is mgh, with h measured from the chosen zero level. The stone is 2 m below ground level, so h = −2 m. PE = 2 × 10 × (−2) = −40 J.', wrong = '{"0": "+40 J would be the energy of the stone 2 m above ground. Below the reference level the PE is negative.", "2": "The PE is zero only at the reference level itself, here the ground surface.", "3": "−4 J comes from leaving out g: 2 × (−2). PE is mgh, so g = 10 m s⁻² must be included."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6021' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'As the ball speeds up, the drag force from the oil grows with its speed. Terminal velocity is reached when drag plus upthrust exactly balances the weight. The net force is then zero, so there is no acceleration and the ball falls at constant speed.', wrong = '{"1": "The weight of the ball stays the same throughout. It is balanced, not removed.", "2": "At terminal velocity the ball keeps moving, at a steady speed. It does not stop.", "3": "The oil exerts the drag and upthrust that balance the weight. Without them there would be no terminal velocity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6022' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At terminal velocity the weight, which is proportional to r³, is balanced by the Stokes drag 6πηrv, which is proportional to rv. So v ∝ r³/r = r². Doubling the radius makes the terminal velocity 2² = 4 times as large.', wrong = '{"0": "A larger sphere falls faster in a viscous fluid, not slower.", "1": "Double would follow only if v ∝ r. It ignores that the weight grows as r³ while drag grows only as r.", "2": "The terminal velocity depends on the radius, as v ∝ r², so it cannot stay the same."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6023' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Turbulent flow is irregular and full of eddies, with the fluid mixing in a disorderly way. It sets in when the flow speed rises above a critical value; below that the flow is laminar (streamline). Most practical flows, such as rivers and air around moving cars, are turbulent.', wrong = '{"0": "Low speeds favour smooth laminar flow. Turbulence appears at high speeds.", "1": "Smooth parallel layers describe laminar (streamline) flow, not turbulent flow.", "3": "Turbulence can occur in blood vessels, for example where an artery is narrowed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6024' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For an incompressible fluid the volume flowing per second is the same everywhere, so A₁v₁ = A₂v₂ (equation of continuity). Then v₂ = A₁v₁/A₂ = (4 × 2) ÷ 1 = 8 m s⁻¹. The area falls to a quarter, so the speed rises four times.', wrong = '{"0": "0.5 m s⁻¹ inverts the area ratio. In a narrower pipe the water must move faster, not slower.", "2": "If the speed stayed 2 m s⁻¹, less water would pass through the narrow part each second than enters the pipe.", "3": "4 m s⁻¹ only doubles the speed, but the area has fallen to a quarter, so the speed rises four times."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6025' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By the equation of continuity, Av stays constant, so blood flows faster where the artery is narrower. By Bernoulli''s principle, where a fluid moves faster its pressure is lower. So in the narrowed part the blood moves faster and its pressure falls.', wrong = '{"1": "In a narrower section the speed increases, not decreases, since Av must stay constant.", "2": "By Bernoulli''s principle, a higher speed goes with a lower pressure, not a higher one.", "3": "Blood still flows through the narrowing, and does so faster."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6026' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sound is a mechanical wave that travels by making particles of a medium vibrate. When the air is pumped out, there are no particles to carry the sound, so the bell cannot be heard. Light is an electromagnetic wave that needs no medium, so the bell can still be seen.', wrong = '{"0": "Light is an electromagnetic wave, not a mechanical wave. That is why it can cross a vacuum.", "1": "Light travels far faster than sound, about 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ compared with about 340 m s⁻¹.", "2": "The sound fades as the air is removed, though the glass is unchanged. It is the lack of air that stops the sound."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6027' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Polarisation means restricting the vibrations of a wave to one plane. This is possible only for transverse waves, which vibrate at right angles to their direction of travel. In sound waves the air particles vibrate along the direction of travel, so sound is longitudinal and cannot be polarised.', wrong = '{"0": "Transverse waves can be polarised. Sound in air is not transverse.", "1": "Sound is a mechanical wave, not an electromagnetic wave. Electromagnetic waves are transverse and can be polarised.", "3": "Speed has nothing to do with whether a wave can be polarised."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6028' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Newton assumed the compressions and rarefactions in a sound wave happen at constant temperature, so he used the isothermal elasticity P. In fact they happen so quickly that heat cannot flow in or out, so the changes are adiabatic. Laplace corrected this by using γP, which gives the measured value.', wrong = '{"0": "Sound travels through air precisely because air is compressible. Newton did not assume otherwise.", "2": "Newton correctly treated sound as a longitudinal pressure wave, not a transverse one.", "3": "Newton used the correct density of air. The error was in assuming isothermal changes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6029' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Laplace pointed out that the changes in a sound wave are adiabatic, so the elasticity of the gas is γP, not P. The speed of sound is then v = √(γP/ρ). For air γ ≈ 1.4, which gives about 332 m s⁻¹ at 0 °C, matching experiment.', wrong = '{"1": "v = √(P/ρ) is Newton''s original formula, which gives about 280 m s⁻¹, too low.", "2": "γ belongs inside the square root, multiplying P, because it corrects the elasticity.", "3": "√(ρ/γP) is the reciprocal of the correct expression and has units of s m⁻¹, not a speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6030' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The speed of sound is v = √(γP/ρ). At constant temperature, doubling the pressure also doubles the density (Boyle''s law), so P/ρ stays the same. The speed of sound is therefore unchanged; it depends on temperature, not on pressure alone.', wrong = '{"0": "Doubling the speed would need P/ρ to increase four times, but here it does not change at all.", "1": "√2 times assumes only P doubles. The density doubles too, so the ratio P/ρ is unchanged.", "2": "At constant temperature the speed does not depend on pressure, so it does not halve."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6031' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The speed of sound in air rises by about 0.61 m s⁻¹ for each degree Celsius. From 0 °C to 20 °C, the increase is 0.61 × 20 = 12.2 m s⁻¹. So v = 332 + 12.2 ≈ 344 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "332 m s⁻¹ is the speed at 0 °C. Warming the air increases the speed of sound.", "1": "352 m s⁻¹ comes from adding 1 m s⁻¹ per °C instead of 0.61 m s⁻¹ per °C.", "3": "320 m s⁻¹ comes from subtracting the increase. Sound travels faster, not slower, in warmer air."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6032' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The speed of sound in a gas depends on its density: v = √(γP/ρ), so at the same pressure and temperature v ∝ 1/√ρ. Humid air has some N₂ (M = 28) and O₂ (M = 32) molecules replaced by lighter H₂O molecules (M = 18), so its density is lower and sound travels slightly faster.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the opposite of the truth. Water vapour (M = 18) is lighter than N₂ and O₂, so it makes air less dense, not denser.", "2": "The comparison is made at the same temperature, so temperature is not the cause. The higher speed comes from the lower density of humid air.", "3": "Molecules do not pass sound on by colliding harder. The small increase in speed is explained by the lower density of humid air."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6033' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The speed of sound in a gas is proportional to the square root of its absolute temperature, v ∝ √T. If T becomes 4T, the speed becomes √4 = 2 times larger, so the new speed is 2v.', wrong = '{"1": "v/2 would mean the speed falls when the gas is heated. In fact sound travels faster in a hotter gas, since v ∝ √T.", "2": "The speed of sound in a gas does depend on temperature. Hotter molecules move faster and pass the disturbance on more quickly.", "3": "4v treats v as directly proportional to T. The speed depends on √T, so a 4 times rise in T only doubles v."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6034' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If the point is at angle ωt, its projection on a diameter is x = A cos ωt. This position swings between +A and −A, and its acceleration is −ω²x, always towards the centre and proportional to the displacement. That is simple harmonic motion with amplitude A and angular frequency ω.', wrong = '{"0": "The projection does not move at constant speed. It moves fastest (Aω) at the centre and stops for an instant at each end of the diameter.", "1": "2A is the full path length from one end of the diameter to the other. The amplitude is the maximum displacement from the centre, which is A.", "2": "The projection does not stay still. As the point goes round, its shadow on the diameter moves to and fro between the two ends."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6035' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the circling point crosses the diameter''s middle, its velocity (magnitude Aω) is parallel to the diameter, so the projection gets the full speed Aω. At the ends of the diameter the velocity is perpendicular to it, so the projection is momentarily at rest. So the greatest speed is Aω, at the centre.', wrong = '{"0": "At the ends the circling point moves at right angles to the diameter, so the projection''s speed there is zero, not Aω.", "1": "The place is right but the value is wrong. Aω² is the maximum acceleration, which occurs at the ends, not a speed.", "3": "At the ends the acceleration is greatest (ω²A), not zero. This option also does not give the place of greatest speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6036' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Heat flows because of a temperature difference, always from the hotter body to the colder one. The amount of internal energy does not decide the direction. The cup at 100 °C is hotter than the tub at 20 °C, so heat flows from the cup to the tub until both reach the same temperature.', wrong = '{"0": "The tub has more internal energy only because it holds much more water. Heat flow depends on temperature, and the tub is colder.", "2": "Heat stops flowing only when the temperatures are equal. Here there is an 80 °C difference, so heat must flow.", "3": "Thermal equilibrium means equal temperatures, not equal internal energies. Heat also flows the other way, from cup to tub."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6037' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Specific heat c is the heat needed per kilogram per kelvin, while molar specific heat C is per mole per kelvin. Since one mole has mass M, C = M × c = 0.018 kg mol⁻¹ × 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ = 75.6 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "4200 is the specific heat per kilogram. A mole of water is only 0.018 kg, so it needs much less heat per kelvin.", "2": "233 000 comes from dividing 4200 by 0.018 instead of multiplying. Per mole the value must be smaller than per kilogram.", "3": "18 is the molar mass of water in g mol⁻¹. It is not a heat capacity and has the wrong units."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6038' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At constant volume the gas does no work, so all the heat supplied raises its internal energy and temperature. At constant pressure the gas expands and does work PΔV on its surroundings, so extra heat is needed for the same rise in temperature. Hence Cp is greater than Cv.', wrong = '{"0": "Heating a gas does not change the mass of its molecules. The difference comes from the work of expansion.", "1": "This is reversed. Work is done at constant pressure because the gas expands; it is at constant volume that no work is done.", "2": "In both cases heat is supplied to the gas. At constant pressure part of it goes into the work of expansion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6039' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Work done by a gas expanding at constant pressure is W = PΔV. Convert litres to cubic metres: ΔV = 3.0 − 1.0 = 2.0 L = 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³. So W = 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa × 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³ = 400 J.', wrong = '{"0": "200 J uses a volume change of 1 L. The gas actually expands by 2 L, from 1.0 L to 3.0 L.", "1": "600 J comes from multiplying the pressure by the final volume of 3 L. Work depends on the change in volume, 2 L.", "3": "4 × 10⁵ J forgets to convert litres into m³. 1 L = 10⁻³ m³, so the answer is 1000 times smaller."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6040' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first law of thermodynamics says Q = ΔU + W, where Q is heat given to the gas and W is work done by the gas. So ΔU = Q − W = 500 J − 200 J = +300 J. The gas keeps 300 J as extra internal energy.', wrong = '{"0": "+700 J adds the work instead of subtracting it. Work done by the gas is energy leaving it, so it lowers ΔU.", "2": "−300 J has the sign reversed. The gas takes in 500 J and gives out only 200 J, so its internal energy rises.", "3": "−700 J reverses the sign of the heat and adds the work. The gas absorbs heat, so Q is positive."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6041' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a rigid container the volume cannot change, so the gas does no work (W = PΔV = 0). The first law Q = ΔU + W then gives Q = ΔU. All the heat supplied goes into raising the internal energy, and so the temperature of the gas rises.', wrong = '{"1": "Work by a gas needs a change in volume. In a rigid container ΔV = 0, so no work is done at all.", "2": "The heat supplied must go somewhere. With no work done, it all increases the internal energy.", "3": "Internal energy rises, so the temperature rises too. A fall in temperature would need energy to leave the gas."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6042' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a monatomic ideal gas Cv = (3/2)R, since its energy is only translational kinetic energy. Using Cp − Cv = R gives Cp = (3/2)R + R = (5/2)R. So γ = Cp/Cv = (5/2)/(3/2) = 5/3 ≈ 1.67.', wrong = '{"0": "1.40 (7/5) is γ for a diatomic gas such as air, where Cv = (5/2)R. It does not fit a monatomic gas.", "1": "1.33 (4/3) is γ for some polyatomic gases, which have more ways to store energy. It is not the monatomic value.", "2": "1.50 is just Cv/R. It is not the ratio Cp/Cv, which works out to 5/3."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6043' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Per mole of ideal gas, the extra heat needed at constant pressure equals the work of expansion, PΔV = RΔT for a 1 K rise. So Cp − Cv = R, the universal gas constant, which is 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ for every ideal gas.', wrong = '{"0": "Cp and Cv are not equal for a gas. At constant pressure extra heat is needed to do expansion work.", "1": "4.18 is the number of joules in one calorie (the mechanical equivalent of heat). It is not the gas constant.", "3": "1.67 is the ratio Cp/Cv for a monatomic gas. It has no units, while the difference Cp − Cv is R."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6044' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The field of a positive charge points away from it. At the midpoint, each equal charge is the same distance away, so both fields have the same size, kq/r², but point in opposite directions. They cancel completely, so the resultant field there is zero (a neutral point).', wrong = '{"0": "The fields would add only if they pointed the same way. Here they point in opposite directions and cancel.", "2": "A zero field has no direction. The two equal fields cancel, so there is no net push towards either charge.", "3": "A field becomes very large only very close to a point charge. The midpoint is a finite distance from both."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6045' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each charge is d/2 from the midpoint, so each field is kq/(d/2)² = 4kq/d². The field of +q points away from +q and the field of −q points towards −q, so both point towards −q and add. The total is 4kq/d² + 4kq/d² = 8kq/d², towards −q.', wrong = '{"1": "Fields cancel at the midpoint only for like charges. For +q and −q both fields point the same way and add.", "2": "kq/d² uses the full distance d instead of d/2, ignores the second charge and gives the wrong direction.", "3": "4kq/d² counts only one charge. The direction is also wrong, since the field points towards −q."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6046' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Between two like charges the null point lies on the line joining them. At distance x from +4q: k(4q)/x² = kq/(3 − x)². Taking square roots, 2/x = 1/(3 − x), so 6 − 2x = x and x = 2 m. It lies 1 m from +q, nearer the smaller charge, as expected.', wrong = '{"0": "At 1 m from +4q the distance to +q is 2 m. The fields are 4kq and kq/4, so the +4q field is 16 times larger and they do not cancel.", "1": "1.5 m is the midpoint, which is the null point only for equal charges. Here +4q''s field would be four times larger.", "2": "At 2.5 m the distances are 2.5 m and 0.5 m, giving fields 0.64kq and 4kq. The field of +q is larger, so they do not cancel."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6047' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Field lines start on positive charges and go outward. For two equal positive charges the lines repel each other and bend away, and none reach the midpoint, where the two fields cancel. This point of zero field midway between the charges is called the neutral point.', wrong = '{"0": "Lines run from one charge to the other only for opposite charges, from + to −. Like charges push their lines apart.", "1": "Field lines never cross. At any point the field has only one direction, so two lines cannot pass through the same point.", "3": "Electric field lines from static charges begin on positive and end on negative charges (or at infinity). They do not form closed loops."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6048' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Using Gauss''s law with a closed cylinder through an infinite sheet, the flux through the two ends gives 2EA = σA/ε₀, so E = σ/2ε₀. The answer contains no distance, so the field is uniform: the same at all distances and perpendicular to the sheet on both sides.', wrong = '{"0": "A 1/r² decrease belongs to a point charge. For an infinite sheet the field lines stay parallel, so the field does not weaken.", "2": "A charged sheet does produce a field, of size σ/2ε₀. It is zero only inside a conductor.", "3": "Nothing makes the field grow with distance. For an infinite sheet it stays constant at σ/2ε₀."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6049' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each plate alone gives a uniform field σ/2ε₀. Between the plates the two fields point the same way, from the positive plate to the negative plate, and add to σ/ε₀. Outside the plates they point in opposite directions and cancel, so the field is uniform σ/ε₀ only between the plates.', wrong = '{"1": "The fields cancel outside the plates, not between them. Between the plates they add.", "2": "σ/2ε₀ is the field of a single sheet. Between two oppositely charged plates both fields add.", "3": "2σ/ε₀ counts each plate as giving σ/ε₀. Each sheet gives only σ/2ε₀, so the total is σ/ε₀."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6050' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a medium Coulomb''s law becomes F = q₁q₂/(4πε₀εᵣr²). The relative permittivity εᵣ of the medium divides the force. For water εᵣ ≈ 80, so with the same charges and distance the force becomes F/80.', wrong = '{"0": "A dielectric medium weakens the force between charges. It divides F by εᵣ rather than multiplying it.", "1": "The force does depend on the medium. Only in vacuum (εᵣ = 1), and very nearly in air, is it unchanged.", "2": "The force is divided by εᵣ itself, not by its square root. So the factor is 80, not √80."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6051' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ohm''s law says the current in a conductor is proportional to the voltage across it, so V/I = R is constant. This holds only if the physical state of the conductor stays the same. If its temperature rises, its resistance rises too, and V/I is no longer constant.', wrong = '{"0": "A very large current heats the conductor and raises its resistance. That spoils the constant V/I ratio.", "1": "Semiconductors are non-ohmic. Their resistance changes with temperature and with the applied voltage.", "3": "Whether the voltage is alternating is not the condition. Ohm''s law needs the physical conditions, especially temperature, to stay constant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6052' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'As the current through a filament lamp increases, the tungsten filament gets very hot. The resistance of a metal rises with temperature, so each extra volt produces a smaller increase in current. The I–V graph therefore bends towards the V axis, and the lamp is a non-ohmic device.', wrong = '{"0": "This is reversed. The resistance of a metal rises as it heats up, which is why the current grows less than in proportion.", "2": "An ohmic conductor gives a straight-line I–V graph through the origin. A curved graph shows the lamp does not obey Ohm''s law.", "3": "The current keeps rising with voltage. It just rises less steeply than it would for a fixed resistance."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6053' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A source delivers maximum power to its load when the load resistance equals the internal resistance, R = r = 2 Ω. Then I = E/(R + r) = 12/4 = 3 A and P = I²R = 3² × 2 = 18 W. A quick check: P(max) = E²/4r = 144/8 = 18 W.', wrong = '{"1": "72 W is E²/r = 144/2. That is the power wasted inside the battery when it is short-circuited, not the power in the load.", "2": "36 W is E²/2r. The correct maximum load power is E²/4r = 18 W.", "3": "6 is E/r = 12/2, the short-circuit current in amperes. It is not a power."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6054' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Maximum power transfer happens when the load resistance equals the internal resistance. The same current flows through both, so the power wasted inside the source equals the power delivered to the load, and the efficiency is only 50%. Power stations aim for high efficiency instead, so they are not run under this condition.', wrong = '{"0": "Current does flow under this condition. With R = r it equals E/2r.", "1": "At maximum power transfer the load voltage is only half the emf, because half is dropped across the internal resistance.", "2": "Internal resistance is fixed by the source itself. Choosing a load does not make it zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6055' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electrical energy = power × time. Working in kilowatts and hours, 2 kW × 3 h = 6 kWh. One kWh is the ''unit'' on an electricity bill, so 6 units are used. The cost is 6 units × Rs 50 per unit = Rs 300.', wrong = '{"0": "The energy of 6 kWh is right, but Rs 150 uses Rs 25 per unit. At Rs 50 per unit the cost is Rs 300.", "1": "1.5 kWh comes from dividing time by power (3 ÷ 2) instead of multiplying them. Energy is power × time = 6 kWh.", "3": "5 kWh comes from adding 2 and 3. Energy is found by multiplying power by time, giving 6 kWh."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6056' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A kilowatt-hour is the energy used by a 1 kW device in 1 hour. Converting to SI units: 1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 × 10⁶ J. It is a unit of energy, used for electricity bills.', wrong = '{"0": "1000 J is the energy of 1 kW for only one second. An hour has 3600 seconds.", "2": "3600 J is one watt-hour (1 W × 3600 s). A kilowatt-hour is 1000 times more.", "3": "kJ per second is a unit of power, not energy. The kWh measures energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6057' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The force on a current-carrying wire at right angles to a field is F = BIL, so B = F/(IL). One tesla is the field that gives a force of 1 N on a 1 m wire carrying 1 A. So 1 T = 1 N A⁻¹ m⁻¹, which also equals 1 Wb m⁻².', wrong = '{"1": "N A m puts current and length on top. Since B = F/(IL), they belong in the denominator.", "2": "The tesla is a weber per square metre (flux per area), Wb m⁻², not Wb m.", "3": "A m⁻¹ is the unit of magnetic field strength H, not of flux density B."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6058' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The magnetic force provides the centripetal force: qvB = mv²/r. This gives r = mv/(qB), so with m, q and B fixed the radius is directly proportional to the speed. Doubling v doubles the radius to 2r.', wrong = '{"0": "r/2 would mean a faster particle bends more sharply. In fact r = mv/qB grows with speed.", "1": "The radius does depend on speed, since r = mv/qB. Only the period stays the same.", "2": "4r treats r as proportional to v². The formula r = mv/qB shows r ∝ v."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6059' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The time for one revolution is T = 2πr/v. Putting in r = mv/(qB) gives T = 2πm/(qB), in which v cancels. A faster particle moves in a proportionally larger circle, so the period is the same at all speeds. This is the idea behind the cyclotron.', wrong = '{"0": "A faster particle covers a larger circle, but its higher speed exactly makes up for this. T stays the same.", "1": "The period does not fall with speed. In T = 2πm/qB the speed does not appear.", "3": "The radius cancels out as well. T depends only on m, q and B."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6060' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The magnetic force on a moving charge is F = qvB sin θ, where θ is the angle between v and B. For motion parallel to the field θ = 0° and sin 0° = 0. So the force is zero and the particle continues in a straight line at constant speed.', wrong = '{"0": "qvB is the force when the particle moves at right angles to the field (θ = 90°). Here θ = 0°.", "2": "qvB/2 would need sin θ = 0.5, that is θ = 30°. For parallel motion sin θ = 0.", "3": "The magnetic force is always perpendicular to both v and B. It is never along the field."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6061' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The magnetic force on a moving charge, F = qvB sin θ, always acts at right angles to the velocity. Work is done only by a force component along the motion, so this force does no work. It changes only the direction of motion, and the speed and kinetic energy stay constant.', wrong = '{"1": "The force is not always zero. It is zero only when the particle moves parallel to B.", "2": "The size of the charge does not matter. Even a large charge gets a force perpendicular to its velocity.", "3": "Uniformity is not the reason. The force is perpendicular to v in any magnetic field, so no work is done."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6062' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The changing flux in a transformer core induces circulating currents in the iron itself, called eddy currents, which waste energy as heat. Thin insulated sheets (laminations) break up the paths of these currents and make them very small. Soft iron is used because it is easy to magnetise and demagnetise, which reduces hysteresis loss.', wrong = '{"0": "Eddy currents only heat the core and waste energy. Laminations are used to reduce them, not increase them.", "1": "No core design lets a transformer work on steady DC. It needs a changing flux, which only AC provides.", "2": "The laminations are in the iron core, not the copper coils. Higher coil resistance would only waste more energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6063' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For an ideal transformer Vs/Vp = Ns/Np. So Ns = Np × Vs/Vp = 2000 × 11/220 = 2000 ÷ 20 = 100 turns. A step-down transformer has fewer turns on the secondary than on the primary.', wrong = '{"0": "200 turns would give 22 V, since 2000 × 22/220 = 200. The required output is 11 V.", "1": "40 000 turns uses the ratio upside down, 2000 × 220/11. That would step the voltage up, not down.", "3": "1100 turns has no basis in the turns ratio. The secondary needs 1/20 of the primary turns."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6064' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Faraday''s law an emf is induced only when the magnetic flux linking a coil changes. A steady DC in the primary makes a constant flux in the core, so after switching on no emf is induced in the secondary. AC works because it keeps changing, giving a continuously changing flux.', wrong = '{"0": "The size of the DC does not matter. Even a large steady current makes an unchanging flux and induces no emf.", "2": "The core does not melt. The real problem is that a constant flux cannot induce an emf.", "3": "DC connected to the primary flows only in the primary. Nothing is passed to the secondary without a changing flux."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6065' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a fixed power P = VI, raising the voltage ten times cuts the current to one tenth. The heat lost in the cables is P(loss) = I²R. With I reduced to I/10, the loss becomes (1/10)² = 1/100 of the original.', wrong = '{"1": "Raising the voltage lowers the current for the same power, so cable losses fall rather than rise.", "2": "1/10 forgets that the loss depends on I², not on I. One tenth of the current gives one hundredth of the loss.", "3": "The cable resistance is unchanged, but the current is smaller, so I²R loss drops sharply."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6066' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a given power, high voltage means low current, and the heat lost in long cables, I²R, becomes small. So step-up transformers raise the voltage at the power station for transmission. Near homes, step-down transformers lower it to a safe working value such as 220 V.', wrong = '{"0": "High voltage is dangerous, not safe. That is exactly why it is stepped down before reaching homes.", "1": "Transformers do not generate electricity. They only change the voltage of an AC supply.", "2": "Cables can carry low voltage. The reason for high voltage is to reduce energy loss in the cables."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6067' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The emf is greatest when the flux through the coil changes fastest. When the coil''s plane is parallel to B, its sides move straight across the field lines, so ε reaches its peak NBAω. At that instant the flux through the coil is actually zero, but it is changing at the maximum rate.', wrong = '{"0": "With the plane perpendicular to B the flux through the coil is maximum, but the sides move along the field lines, so the flux is not changing and ε = 0.", "1": "At 45° the emf is NBAω sin 45°, about 71% of the maximum. It is not the greatest value.", "3": "A conductor moving along the field cuts no field lines, so it has no induced emf."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6068' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an AC generator a coil is turned in a magnetic field by a turbine or engine, which supplies mechanical energy. The changing flux induces an alternating emf by electromagnetic induction. So the generator changes mechanical energy into electrical energy.', wrong = '{"0": "Converting electrical energy into mechanical energy is what a motor does, the reverse of a generator.", "2": "Turning chemical energy into electrical energy is what a cell or battery does.", "3": "Turning heat or electrical energy into light is what a lamp does, not a generator."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6069' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A diode is forward biased when the p-side is joined to the positive terminal and the n-side to the negative. The applied voltage opposes the barrier potential, so the depletion region narrows. Once about 0.7 V is exceeded for a silicon diode, a large current flows.', wrong = '{"1": "Joining the p-side to the negative terminal is reverse bias. The barrier then increases and almost no current flows.", "2": "If both sides are at the same potential, no voltage is applied across the junction, so it is not biased.", "3": "With no battery the diode is unbiased. Forward bias needs an applied voltage in the correct direction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6070' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In reverse bias the p-side is joined to the negative terminal and the n-side to the positive. This pulls the majority carriers away from the junction, so the depletion region widens and the barrier rises. Only a very small leakage current, carried by minority carriers, flows.', wrong = '{"0": "A narrower depletion region and a large current describe forward bias, not reverse bias.", "1": "A diode conducts well in one direction only. In reverse bias it allows just a tiny leakage current.", "2": "Reverse bias adds to the barrier potential. It becomes larger, not zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6071' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a PN junction forms, electrons and holes near the junction combine and disappear. This leaves fixed positive donor ions on the n-side and fixed negative acceptor ions on the p-side, with almost no free charge carriers. Their electric field creates the barrier potential.', wrong = '{"0": "The free electrons and holes in this region have recombined, which is why it is called the depletion region.", "1": "With almost no free charge carriers, the depletion region acts nearly like an insulator, not a good conductor.", "3": "The depletion region forms as soon as the junction is made, even with no bias. Biasing only changes its width."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6072' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Radioactive decay is a nuclear process. It happens spontaneously, and the rate depends only on the nuclide and the number of nuclei present. Temperature, pressure and chemical bonding affect the electrons around the nucleus, not the nucleus itself, so they do not change the decay rate.', wrong = '{"0": "Heating only makes atoms move faster. It does not reach the nucleus, so the decay rate stays the same.", "2": "Pressure acts on whole atoms and molecules, not on the nucleus. It has no effect on the decay rate.", "3": "Forming a compound involves only the outer electrons. The nucleus keeps decaying at the same rate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6073' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each nucleus has a fixed chance of decaying in each second, called the decay constant λ. It is impossible to say which nucleus will decay next or exactly when. With very large numbers of nuclei, though, the overall rate A = λN is predictable, which is why the half-life is constant.', wrong = '{"1": "Nuclei do not all decay together. They decay one by one at unpredictable times.", "2": "The half-life of a given nuclide is a fixed constant. It is the individual decays that are random.", "3": "The chance of decay per nucleus is the same whatever the sample size. A bigger sample just has more decays per second."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6074' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'During a collision the bodies push on each other only with internal forces, so with no external force the total momentum is conserved. Momentum is a vector, so its x and y components are each conserved separately. When the bodies stick together the collision is perfectly inelastic, and some kinetic energy is changed into heat and sound.', wrong = '{"0": "Kinetic energy is not conserved when bodies stick together. Part of it is lost as heat, sound and deformation.", "1": "Momentum is conserved in every direction, so the y-component is conserved as well as the x-component.", "2": "Momentum is conserved in any isolated collision, elastic or inelastic. Only kinetic energy is lost here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6075' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Conserve momentum in each direction. pₓ = 2 × 3 = 6 kg m s⁻¹ and p_y = 1 × 6 = 6 kg m s⁻¹, so the total momentum is √(6² + 6²) = √72 ≈ 8.49 kg m s⁻¹. The combined mass is 3 kg, so v = 8.49/3 ≈ 2.8 m s⁻¹, at 45° to the x-axis.', wrong = '{"0": "2.0 m s⁻¹ uses only the x-momentum, 6/3. The y-momentum must be added as a perpendicular component.", "1": "3.0 m s⁻¹ adds the two speeds and divides by the total mass, 9/3. Speeds of different masses cannot be added like this.", "3": "4.0 m s⁻¹ adds the momenta as if they were along one line, 12/3. They are at right angles, so they combine by Pythagoras."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6076' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the Doppler effect for light, a source moving away from the observer shows a longer wavelength (lower frequency) than the same line measured at rest. Here the line is seen at 478 nm instead of 397 nm, a shift towards the red end of the spectrum. This red shift shows the galaxy is moving away from the Earth.', wrong = '{"0": "A source moving towards the Earth shows a blue shift, a shorter wavelength. Here the wavelength has increased.", "2": "A galaxy at rest relative to the Earth would show the line at the laboratory value, 397 nm, with no shift.", "3": "A Doppler shift tells us about motion along the line of sight, not about temperature."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6077' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In Doppler ultrasound, sound waves reflect off moving red blood cells and come back with a changed frequency. The size of this shift gives the speed and direction of blood flow. Doctors use it to find narrowed valves or vessels (stenosis) and leaking valves (regurgitation).', wrong = '{"1": "Body temperature is measured with a thermometer. The Doppler effect measures speed of motion, not heat.", "2": "X-ray images of bones use X-rays absorbed by dense tissue. They do not use the Doppler shift of ultrasound.", "3": "Blood sugar is measured by a chemical test on a blood sample, not by ultrasound."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-6078' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ionizing radiation knocks electrons out of atoms, producing ions and highly reactive free radicals inside cells. These break chemical bonds in important molecules such as DNA. The damage can kill cells or make them grow abnormally, while the heating it causes is negligible.', wrong = '{"1": "Even a harmful dose deposits very little energy, warming tissue by only a tiny fraction of a degree. Heating is not the cause of harm.", "2": "Alpha, beta and gamma radiation do not make the body radioactive. Only neutron exposure can do that, and it is not the main harm.", "3": "Radiation does not measurably change the mass of the cells it passes through. The harm is chemical damage from ionization."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7001' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cells that divide rapidly are the most sensitive to ionizing radiation, and cancer cells divide much faster than most normal cells. A carefully aimed and controlled dose therefore kills tumour cells more readily. Some healthy tissue is still damaged, which is why patients often feel unwell during treatment.', wrong = '{"0": "If tumour cells absorbed no gamma radiation, radiotherapy could not affect them at all.", "2": "All living cells contain DNA, including cancer cells. Damage to this DNA is how radiation kills them.", "3": "Tumours can lie deep in the body. Penetrating gamma beams or implanted sources are used to reach them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7002' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Absorbed dose is the energy of radiation absorbed per kilogram of tissue, so its unit is J kg⁻¹. In SI this unit is called the gray: 1 Gy = 1 J kg⁻¹. It measures only the energy absorbed, not how harmful each type of radiation is.', wrong = '{"0": "The sievert measures equivalent dose, which multiplies the absorbed dose by a factor for how harmful the radiation type is.", "1": "The becquerel measures the activity of a source, one decay per second. It says nothing about energy absorbed.", "3": "The curie is an old unit of activity (3.7 × 10¹⁰ decays per second), not a unit of dose."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7003' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The gray measures only the energy absorbed per kilogram. The sievert measures equivalent dose, which is the absorbed dose multiplied by a radiation weighting factor. For the same energy absorbed, alpha particles do about 20 times as much biological damage as gamma rays, so they give more sieverts per gray.', wrong = '{"0": "Decays per second is the activity of a source, measured in becquerels, not a dose.", "1": "The sievert applies to all ionizing radiations, including alpha, beta, gamma and neutrons, not just gamma.", "2": "The energy of a single photon is given in joules or electronvolts. The sievert is a unit of dose to tissue."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7004' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Genetic (hereditary) effects come from radiation damage to the DNA in sperm or egg cells. These mutations can be passed on and appear in the person''s children or later generations. Somatic effects, such as burns, cataracts and cancer, appear in the exposed person''s own body.', wrong = '{"1": "Somatic effects appear in the body of the person who was exposed, such as burns or cancer. They are not passed to children.", "2": "Harmful radiation doses produce almost no heating, and heating is not what is inherited.", "3": "The photoelectric effect is the emission of electrons from a metal surface when light falls on it. It is not a biological effect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7005' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Alpha particles are the most strongly ionizing and least penetrating radiation. Outside the body the dead outer layer of skin stops them, so they hardly reach living cells. Once swallowed or inhaled, all their energy is dumped into a tiny volume of living tissue, causing intense local damage.', wrong = '{"0": "Alpha particles are helium nuclei. They do not change into gamma rays inside the body.", "2": "Alpha particles have the shortest range of the three radiations, a few cm in air and a fraction of a mm in tissue. Gamma rays travel much farther.", "3": "Where a swallowed isotope collects depends on its chemistry, not on the charge of the alpha particles it emits."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7006' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gamma rays from a small point source spread out over a sphere, so the intensity falls as 1/r² (the inverse square law). Moving from 1 m to 3 m makes the dose rate (1/3)² = 1/9 of the original. Distance, shielding and short exposure time are the main ways to reduce dose.', wrong = '{"0": "1/3 assumes the intensity falls as 1/r. For a point source it falls as 1/r², giving 1/9.", "1": "1/6 has no basis. Tripling the distance divides the intensity by 3², which is 9.", "3": "Penetrating power does not stop the rays from spreading out. Their intensity still falls with the square of distance."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7007' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Radioactive iodine-131 behaves chemically just like ordinary iodine, and the thyroid gland absorbs iodine strongly. So the isotope collects in the thyroid, and its beta and gamma radiation destroy overactive or cancerous thyroid tissue. Its half-life of about 8 days keeps the exposure short.', wrong = '{"0": "Iodine-131 emits beta particles and gamma rays, which are ionizing radiation, not harmless visible light.", "1": "The thyroid takes up iodine strongly. That selective uptake is the whole reason iodine-131 is used.", "2": "Iodine-131 has a half-life of about 8 days, not millions of years. A very long half-life would give too weak a dose."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7008' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gamma rays are very penetrating, so they pass through sealed plastic packs and kill bacteria inside by damaging their DNA. Items can be sterilized after packing and without heat, which is safe for heat-sensitive plastics. The irradiated items do not become radioactive.', wrong = '{"1": "Gamma irradiation does not make the instruments radioactive. It kills microorganisms by damaging their DNA.", "2": "If the wrapping absorbed all the rays, nothing inside would be sterilized. The rays must pass through it.", "3": "Gamma sterilization works on plastics, dressings and even food, not just metal objects."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7009' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Energy in kWh = power in kW × time in hours. Kettle: 1.5 kW × (20/60) h = 0.5 kWh a day. Bulb: 0.06 kW × 5 h = 0.3 kWh a day. Total = 0.8 kWh a day, so in 30 days 0.8 × 30 = 24 kWh.', wrong = '{"0": "15 kWh counts only the kettle (0.5 kWh × 30 days) and leaves out the bulb.", "2": "9 kWh counts only the bulb (0.3 kWh × 30 days) and leaves out the kettle.", "3": "18 kWh treats 20 minutes as 0.2 h. Twenty minutes is one third of an hour, about 0.33 h."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7010' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First find the heater''s resistance from its rating: R = V²/P = 220²/1000 = 48.4 Ω. On 110 V, P = V²/R = 110²/48.4 = 250 W. Since P = V²/R, halving the voltage reduces the power to one quarter.', wrong = '{"0": "500 W assumes power is proportional to voltage. With R fixed, P ∝ V², so half the voltage gives one quarter of the power.", "1": "The 1000 W rating holds only at 220 V. At a lower voltage less current flows and the power falls.", "3": "125 W is 1000 ÷ 8, which would need P ∝ V³. The correct factor is (1/2)² = 1/4."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7011' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Energy used = power × time = 2.5 kW × 8 h = 20 kWh. Each kWh is one unit on the bill, costing Rs 50. So the cost is 20 × Rs 50 = Rs 1000. A quick check: one hour costs 2.5 × 50 = Rs 125, and 8 hours cost Rs 1000.', wrong = '{"0": "Rs 400 is 50 × 8. It forgets to multiply by the power of 2.5 kW.", "1": "Rs 2000 is double the correct cost. It would need 40 kWh, but the energy used is only 20 kWh.", "2": "Rs 125 is 2.5 × 50. It forgets to multiply by the 8 hours of use."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7012' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Momentum is a vector, so the reversal of direction matters. Taking the rebound direction as positive, Δp = 0.5 × 8 − 0.5 × (−12) = 4 + 6 = 10 kg m s⁻¹. The average force is F = Δp/Δt = 10/0.05 = 200 N.', wrong = '{"1": "40 N uses the change in speed, 12 − 8 = 4 m s⁻¹. The ball reverses direction, so the velocity change is 12 + 8 = 20 m s⁻¹.", "2": "80 N uses only the rebound momentum, 0.5 × 8 = 4 kg m s⁻¹. The full change in momentum is 10 kg m s⁻¹.", "3": "400 N would need a contact time of 0.025 s. With 0.05 s the force is 10/0.05 = 200 N."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7013' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Every second, 2 kg of sand lands on the belt with no horizontal speed and must be brought up to 3 m s⁻¹. Force = rate of change of momentum = v × (Δm/Δt) = 3 × 2 = 6 N. Here the velocity stays constant but the moving mass increases, so F = ma alone would wrongly give zero.', wrong = '{"0": "1.5 N divides the speed by the mass rate (3/2). The force is speed × mass per second, 3 × 2 = 6 N.", "2": "The belt''s speed is constant, but new sand keeps gaining horizontal momentum every second, and that needs a force.", "3": "18 N multiplies by 3 twice (3 × 3 × 2). The correct force is just 3 × 2 = 6 N."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7014' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Newton''s second law in its general form says force equals the rate of change of momentum, F = Δp/Δt. If the mass is constant this becomes F = mΔv/Δt = ma. When the mass changes, as for a rocket burning fuel, a conveyor belt gaining sand or a water jet, only the momentum form gives the correct force.', wrong = '{"0": "The law is not limited to bodies at rest. It covers all motion, and a body stays at rest only if no net force acts.", "1": "Both forms of the law measure force in newtons. The units are not the difference.", "3": "Force is the rate of change of momentum, not momentum itself. They have different units, N and kg m s⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7015' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The thrust of a rocket = (mass of gas ejected per second) × (exhaust speed). To just lift off, the thrust must at least equal the weight, mg = 300 × 10 = 3000 N. So Δm/Δt = 3000/1500 = 2 kg s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "0.2 kg s⁻¹ is 300/1500. It uses the mass instead of the weight, forgetting to multiply by g.", "1": "20 kg s⁻¹ uses a weight of 30 000 N, ten times too large. The weight is 300 × 10 = 3000 N.", "2": "5 kg s⁻¹ is 1500/300, the numbers divided the wrong way and without g."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7016' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A small strip under a force-time graph has area F × Δt, which is the impulse. By Newton''s second law in momentum form, F = Δp/Δt, so F × Δt = Δp. Adding up all the strips, the total area equals the change in momentum of the body.', wrong = '{"1": "Change in kinetic energy equals the work done, which is the area under a force-displacement graph, not force-time.", "2": "Work done is the area under a force-displacement graph. Force × time gives impulse, not work.", "3": "Acceleration is the slope of a velocity-time graph. It is not an area under a force-time graph."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7017' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Launch angles of 30° and 60° add up to 90°, so with the same speed they give the same range. Their times of flight differ, though, since T = 2u sin θ/g. So t₃₀ : t₆₀ = sin 30° : sin 60° = (1/2) : (√3/2) = 1 : √3.', wrong = '{"0": "Equal ranges do not mean equal times. The 60° ball goes higher and stays in the air longer.", "2": "1 : 2 is the ratio of the angles themselves. Time of flight depends on sin θ, not on θ.", "3": "√3 : 1 is the ratio upside down. The ball launched at 60° takes longer, so t₃₀ is the smaller."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7018' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The maximum height of a projectile is H = u² sin²θ/2g. With the same speed, H depends only on sin²θ. So H₃₀ : H₆₀ = sin²30° : sin²60° = (1/4) : (3/4) = 1 : 3, even though the two ranges are equal.', wrong = '{"0": "1 : √3 is the ratio of sin θ, which gives the times of flight. Height depends on sin²θ.", "1": "1 : 2 is just the ratio of the angles, 30 : 60. Height depends on sin²θ, not on θ itself.", "3": "Equal ranges do not mean equal heights. The 60° projectile rises three times as high."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7019' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For angles θ and (90° − θ), the times of flight are t₁ = 2u sin θ/g and t₂ = 2u cos θ/g. Their product is 4u² sin θ cos θ/g² = 2(u² sin 2θ/g)/g. Since the range is R = u² sin 2θ/g, this gives t₁t₂ = 2R/g.', wrong = '{"0": "R/g misses the factor 2 that comes from 4 sin θ cos θ = 2 sin 2θ.", "1": "R/2g is four times smaller than the correct 2R/g.", "2": "4R/g forgets that 2 sin θ cos θ = sin 2θ, so it counts the factor 2 twice."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7020' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ball A approaches B at the relative speed 2.0 − 0.5 = 1.5 m s⁻¹. In a perfectly elastic collision the relative speed of separation equals the relative speed of approach, whatever the masses. So the balls separate at 1.5 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "2.5 m s⁻¹ adds the speeds, which applies only when the balls move towards each other. Here they move the same way.", "2": "2.0 m s⁻¹ uses only A''s speed and ignores that B is also moving forward.", "3": "0.5 m s⁻¹ is just B''s speed. The relative speed is the difference, 1.5 m s⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7021' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a perfectly elastic collision the speed of separation equals the speed of approach. In a perfectly inelastic one the bodies stick, so the separation speed is zero. Separating at half the approach speed lies in between, so the collision is inelastic: momentum is conserved but some kinetic energy is lost.', wrong = '{"0": "A perfectly elastic collision needs the speed of separation to equal the speed of approach, not half of it.", "2": "In a perfectly inelastic collision the bodies stick and move together, so they do not separate at all.", "3": "Momentum is conserved in every collision of an isolated system. Only kinetic energy may be lost."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7022' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For an elastic head-on collision with the second ball at rest: v₁ = (m₁ − m₂)u/(m₁ + m₂) = (−1/3) × 6 = −2 m s⁻¹ and v₂ = 2m₁u/(m₁ + m₂) = (2/3) × 6 = +4 m s⁻¹. Check: momentum 1 × (−2) + 2 × 4 = 6, and separation speed 4 − (−2) = 6 = approach speed.', wrong = '{"0": "+2 and +2 means the balls move off together. That is a perfectly inelastic result, in which kinetic energy is lost.", "1": "0 and +3 conserves momentum but halves the kinetic energy (18 J to 9 J). The separation speed 3 is not 6.", "3": "−3 and +4.5 conserves momentum, but the separation speed 7.5 m s⁻¹ exceeds 6 m s⁻¹, so kinetic energy would increase."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7023' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The horizontal and vertical motions are independent. Vertically the stone starts from rest: 45 = ½ × 10 × t², so t² = 9 and t = 3 s. Horizontally the speed stays 15 m s⁻¹, so it lands x = 15 × 3 = 45 m from the foot of the cliff.', wrong = '{"0": "30 m uses a fall time of 2 s. Falling 45 m from rest takes 3 s.", "1": "90 m is double the correct distance, as if the stone stayed in the air for 6 s.", "2": "67.5 m is 15 × 4.5, using t² = 4.5 (from forgetting the ½ in ½gt²) as if it were the time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7024' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The stone takes t = 3 s to fall 45 m (45 = ½ × 10 × t²). At impact its vertical velocity is gt = 10 × 3 = 30 m s⁻¹, while its horizontal velocity is still 15 m s⁻¹. So tan θ = 30/15 = 2, and θ = tan⁻¹ 2 ≈ 63° below the horizontal.', wrong = '{"1": "45° would need equal horizontal and vertical velocities. At impact the vertical velocity is twice the horizontal.", "2": "tan⁻¹ ½ uses horizontal over vertical. The angle with the horizontal uses vertical over horizontal.", "3": "tan⁻¹ 3 divides the height (45 m) by the horizontal speed (15 m s⁻¹), mixing a distance with a speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7025' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The vertical component is 20 sin 30° = 10 m s⁻¹ upward. Taking up as positive, the ground is 25 m below: −25 = 10t − 5t², so t² − 2t − 5 = 0. The positive root is t = 1 + √6 ≈ 3.45 s.', wrong = '{"0": "2.00 s is the time to rise and come back down to the top of the tower. The ball must still fall another 25 m.", "2": "2.24 s (√5 s) is the time to fall 25 m from rest. It ignores the ball''s upward start.", "3": "4.45 s is 2 + √6, a slip in solving the quadratic. The roots of t² − 2t − 5 = 0 are 1 ± √6."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7026' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gravitational potential energy is mgh, where h is measured from the chosen zero level. The floor is 0.8 m below the table top, so h = −0.8 m. PE = 2 × 10 × (−0.8) = −16 J. The negative sign simply means the ball is below the reference level.', wrong = '{"0": "+16 J has the right size but ignores that the ball is below the zero level, where h is negative.", "1": "0 J would be correct only if the floor were the reference level. Here the table top is.", "3": "−30 J would need the floor to be 1.5 m below the table. It is only 0.8 m below."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7027' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With the zero of potential energy at infinity, U = −GMm/r is negative at every finite distance. A higher orbit means a larger r, which makes U less negative, so U increases. Work has to be done against gravity to lift the satellite.', wrong = '{"0": "U becomes more negative only when the satellite moves closer to the Earth, not farther away.", "1": "U depends on r, so moving to a different orbit changes it.", "2": "U = −GMm/r reaches zero only at infinity. At any finite distance it stays negative."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7028' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For two forces at angle θ, R² = F₁² + F₂² + 2F₁F₂ cos θ. With F₁ = F₂ = R = F: F² = 2F² + 2F² cos θ, so cos θ = −½ and θ = 120°. A quick check: three equal forces at 120° to each other balance.', wrong = '{"1": "At 60° the resultant is 2F cos 30° = √3 F, about 1.73F, not F.", "2": "At 90° the resultant is √(F² + F²) = √2 F, about 1.41F.", "3": "At 150° the resultant is 2F cos 75°, about 0.52F, which is less than F."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7101' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Take P along the x-axis and let Q make angle θ with it. For the resultant to be perpendicular to P, its x-component must be zero: P + Q cos θ = 0. The resultant is then just Q sin θ, and R² = Q² − Q² cos²θ = Q² − P², so R = √(Q² − P²).', wrong = '{"0": "√(P² + Q²) is the resultant when P and Q are themselves perpendicular, not when R is perpendicular to P.", "2": "Q − P is the resultant of opposite forces, and that resultant lies along P, not at right angles to it.", "3": "P + Q is the resultant of parallel forces, which also lies along P."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7102' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The magnitudes of the two products are |A × B| = AB sin θ and A · B = AB cos θ. The condition gives AB sin θ = √3 AB cos θ. Dividing both sides by AB cos θ, tan θ = √3, so θ = 60°.', wrong = '{"0": "At 30°, tan θ = 1/√3, so the cross product would be only 1/√3 times the dot product, not √3 times.", "1": "At 45°, sin θ = cos θ, so the two products would be equal, not in the ratio √3.", "3": "At 90° the dot product is zero, so |A × B| could not equal √3 times it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7103' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two vectors are perpendicular when their dot product is zero, since cos 90° = 0. Multiply matching components and add: A · B = (2)(1) + (3)(−2) + (−1)(c) = 2 − 6 − c. Setting this to zero gives −4 − c = 0, so c = −4.', wrong = '{"0": "c = 4 drops the minus sign of the k̂ component of A, writing +c instead of −c.", "1": "c = −8 takes the y-product as +6 and also drops the minus sign of −k̂.", "2": "c = 8 takes the y-product 3 × (−2) as +6 instead of −6."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7104' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For equal magnitudes A at angle θ: |A + B|² = 2A²(1 + cos θ) and |A − B|² = 2A²(1 − cos θ). Squaring the condition gives 1 + cos θ = 3(1 − cos θ), so 4 cos θ = 2, cos θ = ½ and θ = 60°.', wrong = '{"1": "At 30° the ratio |A + B|/|A − B| is about 3.7, not √3 ≈ 1.73.", "2": "At 45° the ratio |A + B|/|A − B| is about 2.4, not √3.", "3": "At 90° the sum and the difference have equal magnitudes, so the ratio would be 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7105' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Take moments about the hinge, so the unknown hinge force has no moment. The weight of a uniform beam acts at its centre, 1.0 m from the hinge. For balance, T × 1.5 = 200 × 1.0, so T = 200/1.5 ≈ 133 N.', wrong = '{"0": "100 N would be the tension if the rope were tied at the free end, 2 m from the hinge.", "2": "150 N comes from 200 × 1.5/2, with the distances placed the wrong way round.", "3": "267 N is 200 × 2/1.5, which places the weight at the far end instead of at the centre."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7106' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The lamp''s weight is 10 × 10 = 100 N. Each cord pulls at 30° to the horizontal, so its vertical component is T sin 30° = T/2. For balance, 2 × T/2 = 100 N, so T = 100 N. Cords at a shallow angle need large tensions.', wrong = '{"0": "50 N just halves the weight, which would be true only if the cords hung vertically.", "1": "57.7 N uses cos 30°, treating the 30° as measured from the vertical. It is measured from the ceiling.", "3": "200 N forgets that two cords share the load."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7107' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The area of a parallelogram with adjacent sides A and B is |A × B|. For vectors in the xy-plane, |A × B| = |AₓB_y − A_yBₓ| = |3 × 5 − 4 × (−2)| = 15 + 8 = 23 square units.', wrong = '{"0": "7 comes from 15 − 8, forgetting that Bₓ is −2, so the second term adds.", "1": "11.5 is half of 23, the area of the triangle formed by A and B, not the parallelogram.", "2": "26.9 is |A||B| = 5 × √29, which would be the area only if the vectors were perpendicular."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7108' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The component of A along B is A · B̂, the dot product of A with the unit vector in the direction of B. A · B = 4 × 1 + 3 × 1 = 7 and |B| = √(1² + 1²) = √2. So the component = 7/√2 ≈ 4.95.', wrong = '{"1": "7 is A · B. It must still be divided by |B| = √2 to give the component.", "2": "3.5 divides by 2 instead of by |B| = √2.", "3": "9.9 multiplies 7 by √2 instead of dividing by it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7109' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Work is the dot product of force and displacement. The displacement is d = (4 − 1)î + (−2 − 2)ĵ = 3î − 4ĵ m. So W = F · d = 3 × 3 + 4 × (−4) = 9 − 16 = −7 J. The negative sign means the force partly opposes the motion.', wrong = '{"0": "25 J takes the y-displacement as +4 instead of −4, giving 9 + 16.", "2": "7 J has the right size but the wrong sign. The y-part of the work is −16 J, larger than +9 J.", "3": "−25 J has no basis in F · d. The correct sum is 9 − 16 = −7 J."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7110' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the first 3 s the body falls ½ × 10 × 3² = 45 m. The distance fallen in the t-th second is (g/2)(2t − 1) = 5(2t − 1). Setting 5(2t − 1) = 45 gives 2t − 1 = 9, so t = 5 s.', wrong = '{"0": "In the 4th second the body falls 5(2 × 4 − 1) = 35 m, less than 45 m.", "1": "In the 6th second it falls 5(2 × 6 − 1) = 55 m, more than 45 m.", "3": "9 s comes from setting 5t = 45, which forgets that the distance in the t-th second is 5(2t − 1)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7111' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From rest, the distance in the n-th second is (a/2)(2n − 1). For the 3rd second this is (a/2)(5) = 5a/2. The distance in the first 3 s is ½a(3)² = 9a/2. So the ratio is 5 : 9.', wrong = '{"0": "1 : 3 is 3a/2 : 9a/2, which uses the 2nd second instead of the 3rd.", "1": "9 : 5 is the correct ratio upside down.", "2": "3 : 5 compares the 2nd second with the 3rd second, not with the first 3 seconds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7112' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Distance is the area under the velocity-time graph. The top speed is 2 × 10 = 20 m s⁻¹. Accelerating: ½ × 10 × 20 = 100 m. Steady speed: 20 × 20 = 400 m. Braking: ½ × 5 × 20 = 50 m. Total = 100 + 400 + 50 = 550 m.', wrong = '{"1": "500 m leaves out the 50 m covered while the car slows down to rest.", "2": "600 m uses 10 s for the braking stage. Braking takes 5 s, so that triangle is only 50 m.", "3": "450 m leaves out the 100 m covered during the first 10 s of acceleration."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7113' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Average speed = total distance ÷ total time. Let each half be d km. Time = d/40 + d/60 = (3d + 2d)/120 = d/24 h. So average speed = 2d ÷ (d/24) = 48 km h⁻¹. With equal distances the car spends longer at the slower speed, so the answer is below 50.', wrong = '{"0": "50 km h⁻¹ is the simple mean of the two speeds. That works only if equal times, not equal distances, are spent at each speed.", "2": "45 km h⁻¹ has no basis in the data. Total distance over total time gives 48 km h⁻¹.", "3": "52 km h⁻¹ is above the simple mean, which would need more time at 60 km h⁻¹. In fact more time is spent at 40 km h⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7114' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Heights above the launch point: y₁ = 30t − 5t² and y₂ = 30(t − 2) − 5(t − 2)². Setting y₁ = y₂ and simplifying gives 20t − 80 = 0, so t = 4 s. Both balls are then 40 m up, the first falling and the second still rising.', wrong = '{"0": "At 3 s the first ball is at its highest point, 45 m, while the second is only 25 m up.", "1": "At 5 s the first ball has fallen to 25 m, while the second is at 45 m. They have already passed.", "3": "At 2 s the second ball is only just being thrown, while the first is 40 m up."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7115' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the highest point the vertical velocity is zero, so only the horizontal component u cos θ remains, and it stays constant throughout. Setting u cos θ = u/2 gives cos θ = ½, so θ = 60°.', wrong = '{"0": "At 30° the speed at the top would be u cos 30° ≈ 0.87u, not 0.5u.", "1": "At 45° the speed at the top would be u cos 45° ≈ 0.71u.", "2": "At 75° the speed at the top would be u cos 75° ≈ 0.26u, which is too small."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7116' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The maximum height is H = u² sin²θ/2g and the range is R = 2u² sin θ cos θ/g. Dividing, H/R = sin θ/(4 cos θ) = tan θ/4. For H = R this ratio must be 1, so tan θ = 4, an angle of about 76°.', wrong = '{"1": "tan θ = ¼ would give H/R = 1/16, so the height would be far smaller than the range.", "2": "At 45°, tan θ = 1 and H/R = ¼. That gives the maximum range, not H = R.", "3": "tan θ = 2 gives H/R = ½, so the height would be only half the range."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7117' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The velocity is perpendicular to u when v · u = 0. With v = u − gt (vector), v · u = u² − gt(u sin θ) = 0, so t = u/(g sin θ) = 20/(10 × 0.866) ≈ 2.31 s. This is before the projectile lands at 3.46 s, so it really happens.', wrong = '{"0": "1.73 s is the time to reach the top, where the velocity is horizontal. That is at 60° to u, not 90°.", "2": "3.46 s is the whole time of flight, 2u sin θ/g. At landing the velocity is at 120° to u.", "3": "4.00 s uses sin 30° instead of sin 60°. It is also after the projectile has landed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7118' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The velocity becomes perpendicular to u at t = u/(g sin θ). This must happen before landing, at 2u sin θ/g. So u/(g sin θ) ≤ 2u sin θ/g, which needs sin²θ ≥ ½, that is θ ≥ 45°. Of the choices only 60° meets this.', wrong = '{"0": "At 20° the velocity can only turn through 40° before landing, so it never becomes perpendicular to u.", "1": "At 35° the velocity turns through only 70° before landing, short of 90°.", "3": "At 40° the velocity turns through only 80° before landing. The minimum angle is 45°."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7119' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The greatest range is reached at 45°: R(max) = u²/g = 80 m. Fired straight up, the bullet rises until its speed is zero, so u² = 2gH and H = u²/2g. Therefore H = R(max)/2 = 80/2 = 40 m.', wrong = '{"0": "80 m takes the vertical height equal to the maximum range. Straight up the height is u²/2g, half of u²/g.", "1": "20 m is the greatest height reached when fired at 45°, u²/4g. Fired vertically it rises twice as high.", "2": "160 m doubles the range instead of halving it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7120' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A lift moving up but slowing down has a downward acceleration of 2 m s⁻². For the person, mg − N = ma, so N = m(g − a) = 60 × (10 − 2) = 480 N. The scale shows N. Only the direction of acceleration matters, not the direction of motion.', wrong = '{"1": "600 N is the person''s true weight, read only when the lift has no acceleration.", "2": "720 N, 60 × (10 + 2), is the reading for an upward acceleration. Slowing while rising is a downward acceleration.", "3": "120 N is just ma = 60 × 2. The scale reads the normal force, which is m(g − a)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7121' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The heavier mass falls and the lighter one rises with a = (m₁ − m₂)g/(m₁ + m₂) = (3 − 2) × 10/5 = 2 m s⁻². For the 2 kg mass: T − 20 = 2 × 2, so T = 24 N. Check with the 3 kg mass: 30 − T = 3 × 2, also giving T = 24 N.', wrong = '{"0": "20 N is the weight of the 2 kg mass. The string must pull harder than that to accelerate it upward.", "2": "30 N is the weight of the 3 kg mass. If the tension were this large, the 3 kg mass would not accelerate.", "3": "25 N is the average of the two weights. The correct value is 2m₁m₂g/(m₁ + m₂) = 24 N."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7122' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Both blocks move together with a = F/(total mass) = 12/(2 + 4) = 2 m s⁻². The only horizontal force on the 4 kg block is the push from the 2 kg block. So that contact force is 4 × 2 = 8 N.', wrong = '{"0": "4 N is the net force on the 2 kg block (12 − 8 N), not the force it exerts on the 4 kg block.", "1": "12 N is the applied force. If all of it passed on, the 2 kg block would have no net force to accelerate it.", "3": "6 N splits the force equally between the blocks. The share depends on mass, so the heavier block needs more."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7123' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The hanging mass pulls with its weight, 1 × 10 = 10 N. Kinetic friction on the block is μkN = 0.2 × 4 × 10 = 8 N. The net force moves both masses: a = (10 − 8)/(4 + 1) = 0.4 m s⁻².', wrong = '{"0": "2.0 m s⁻² is 10/5, which ignores the 8 N of friction on the table.", "1": "1.6 m s⁻² divides the friction force by the total mass. The net force is 10 − 8 = 2 N.", "2": "The pull of 10 N is larger than the 8 N of friction, so there is a net force and the system accelerates."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7124' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Momentum is conserved: 0.05 × 400 = (0.05 + 1.95)v, so v = 20/2.0 = 10 m s⁻¹. Kinetic energy before = ½ × 0.05 × 400² = 4000 J; after = ½ × 2.0 × 10² = 100 J. The loss is 3900 J, which is 3900/4000 = 97.5%.', wrong = '{"1": "2.5% is the fraction of kinetic energy that remains, 100 J out of 4000 J, not the fraction lost.", "2": "50% is lost only when the bullet and block have equal masses. Here the block is 39 times heavier.", "3": "75% has no basis in the data. The loss fraction is M/(m + M) = 1.95/2.0 = 97.5%."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7125' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The object starts at rest, so the two pieces get equal and opposite momenta: 1 × 20 = 2 × v, so v = 10 m s⁻¹. Kinetic energy released = ½ × 1 × 20² + ½ × 2 × 10² = 200 + 100 = 300 J.', wrong = '{"0": "200 J counts only the kinetic energy of the 1 kg piece and leaves out the 100 J of the 2 kg piece.", "2": "400 J gives both pieces the same 200 J. With equal momenta, the lighter piece gets the larger share of energy.", "3": "600 J is double the correct value. It would need the 2 kg piece to move at 20 m s⁻¹, breaking momentum conservation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7126' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By momentum conservation the gun recoils with V = (0.02 × 300)/4 = 1.5 m s⁻¹. Its kinetic energy is ½ × 4 × 1.5² = 4.5 J. The gun and bullet have equal momenta, but the light bullet carries almost all the energy, 900 J.', wrong = '{"0": "900 J is the bullet''s kinetic energy, ½ × 0.02 × 300², not the gun''s.", "1": "6 is the gun''s momentum in kg m s⁻¹ (4 × 1.5). It is not an energy.", "3": "9 J is 4 × 1.5², which forgets the ½ in ½mv²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7127' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For an elastic head-on collision with a ball at rest, v₂ = 2m₁v/(m₁ + m₂) = 2mv/(4m) = v/2. Its kinetic energy is ½(3m)(v/2)² = (3/8)mv², which is 3/4 of the original ½mv². The general formula 4m₁m₂/(m₁ + m₂)² gives the same 3/4.', wrong = '{"0": "1/4 is the fraction the first ball keeps, since it rebounds at v/2. It is not the fraction transferred.", "1": "1/2 assumes the energy is shared equally. The share depends on the masses.", "2": "1/3 is just the mass ratio m : 3m. The energy fraction is 4 × 1 × 3/(1 + 3)² = 3/4."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7128' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Take the 2 kg ball''s direction as positive. Momentum: 2(3) + 1(−3) = 3, so 2v₁ + v₂ = 3. Elastic: separation speed = approach speed, so v₂ − v₁ = 6. Solving gives v₁ = −1 m s⁻¹ and v₂ = +5 m s⁻¹, so the 1 kg ball moves at 5 m s⁻¹ in the 2 kg ball''s original direction.', wrong = '{"1": "3 m s⁻¹ would need the 2 kg ball to stop dead. The kinetic energy would fall from 13.5 J to 4.5 J, which an elastic collision cannot do.", "2": "1 m s⁻¹ is the speed of the 2 kg ball after the collision, and it moves backwards, not forwards.", "3": "6 m s⁻¹ is the relative speed of the two balls, not the velocity of the 1 kg ball."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7129' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The area under a force-time graph is the impulse, which equals the change in momentum. Here the graph is a triangle: ½ × 4 s × 10 N = 20 N s. Starting from rest, v = Δp/m = 20/2 = 10 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "20 m s⁻¹ takes the impulse of 20 N s as the speed, forgetting to divide by the 2 kg mass.", "2": "5 m s⁻¹ halves the correct answer a second time, as if the triangle''s ½ were applied twice.", "3": "40 m s⁻¹ uses the rectangle 10 × 4 = 40 N s instead of the triangle, and forgets the mass."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7130' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The acceleration is (−12 − 20)/8 = −4 m s⁻². The body stops at t = 20/4 = 5 s after covering ½ × 5 × 20 = 50 m. It then moves back for 3 s, covering ½ × 3 × 12 = 18 m. Total distance = 50 + 18 = 68 m, while the displacement is only 32 m.', wrong = '{"0": "32 m is the displacement, 50 − 18 m. Distance counts both parts of the path as positive.", "1": "50 m counts only the forward motion and leaves out the 18 m travelled back.", "3": "80 m is ½ × 20 × 8, as if the body slowed steadily to rest over the full 8 s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7131' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Both balls start with the same speed u at the same height and both gain kinetic energy mgh, so by energy conservation v² = u² + 2gh for both: they land with equal speeds. The times differ: B starts with downward velocity u, while A has zero vertical velocity, so B reaches the ground first.', wrong = '{"0": "They cannot land together, because ball B starts with a downward velocity u while ball A starts with none.", "1": "A starts with no downward speed, so it takes longer to fall. Its landing speed equals B''s.", "2": "Both balls start with the same kinetic energy and gain the same mgh, so they land with equal speeds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7132' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The largest static friction possible is μsN = 0.5 × 5 × 10 = 25 N. The 20 N push is less than this, so the box does not move. Static friction then adjusts to balance the push exactly, so the friction force is 20 N.', wrong = '{"1": "25 N is only the maximum static friction. Friction is never larger than needed to stop motion.", "2": "A push of 20 N is acting, and the box stays still, so friction must be acting to oppose it.", "3": "5 N is 25 − 20, the margin before slipping. It is not a force acting on the box."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7133' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Consider the trailer on its own. The tow bar pulls it forward with T, and resistance of 200 N acts backward. Newton''s second law gives T − 200 = 500 × 2, so T = 1200 N. For the whole system the car''s driving force would be 1500 × 2 + 500 = 3500 N.', wrong = '{"0": "1000 N is just 500 × 2. It ignores the 200 N of resistance acting on the trailer.", "2": "1167 N shares the 3500 N driving force by mass. That ignores where the resistive forces actually act.", "3": "3500 N is the car''s driving force on the whole car-trailer system, not the tow bar tension."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7134' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Use energy conservation with friction. Potential energy lost = mgh = 2 × 10 × 5 = 100 J. Kinetic energy gained = ½ × 2 × 8² = 64 J. The difference, 100 − 64 = 36 J, is the work done against friction, which appears as heat.', wrong = '{"0": "64 J is the kinetic energy the block gains at the bottom, not the energy lost to friction.", "1": "100 J is the total potential energy lost. Part of it became kinetic energy, so not all of it is heat.", "3": "164 J adds the potential and kinetic energies. The heat is their difference, 100 − 64 J."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7135' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = '600 kg per minute is 10 kg per second. Each second the pump gives this water potential energy mgh = 10 × 10 × 20 = 2000 J and kinetic energy ½mv² = ½ × 10 × 10² = 500 J. So the output power is 2000 + 500 = 2500 W.', wrong = '{"0": "2000 W counts only the lift of the water and ignores the kinetic energy it leaves with.", "1": "500 W counts only the kinetic energy and ignores the 20 m lift.", "2": "150 000 J is the energy delivered per minute. Power is energy per second, so divide by 60."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7136' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At steady speed the driving force balances the weight component down the slope plus the resistance. mg sin θ = 1000 × 10 × 0.1 = 1000 N, so F = 1000 + 500 = 1500 N. Power P = Fv = 1500 × 20 = 30 000 W = 30 kW.', wrong = '{"1": "10 kW is 500 × 20, which counts only the resistance and ignores the climb.", "2": "20 kW is 1000 × 20, which counts only the weight component along the slope and ignores resistance.", "3": "200 kW uses the full weight mg = 10 000 N instead of its component along the slope, mg sin θ."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7137' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Useful output power = mgh/t = 400 × 10 × 12/30 = 1600 W. Efficiency = output ÷ input, so input = output ÷ efficiency = 1600/0.8 = 2000 W. The input must always be larger than the useful output.', wrong = '{"0": "1600 W is the useful output power. The motor wastes 20% of its input, so the input is larger.", "2": "1280 W multiplies the output by 0.8 instead of dividing. Input power must exceed output power.", "3": "1920 W adds 20% of 1600 W. The correct step is to divide by 0.8, which gives 2000 W."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7138' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a varying force, work done is the area under the force-displacement graph. For the first 4 m the graph is a triangle: ½ × 4 × 20 = 40 J. For the next 2 m it is a rectangle: 20 × 2 = 40 J. Total work = 80 J.', wrong = '{"0": "120 J treats the force as 20 N over the whole 6 m. During the first 4 m it was smaller.", "1": "40 J counts only one of the two parts, either the triangle or the rectangle.", "3": "60 J uses an average force of 10 N over all 6 m. The force is a steady 20 N over the last 2 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7139' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kinetic energy and momentum are linked by KE = p²/2m, so for a fixed mass KE ∝ p². A 20% rise in momentum multiplies p by 1.2, so KE is multiplied by 1.2² = 1.44. That is an increase of 44%.', wrong = '{"0": "20% treats KE as directly proportional to p. Since KE ∝ p², the rise is larger.", "1": "40% just doubles 20%. It misses the extra 0.2² = 0.04 that comes from squaring 1.2.", "2": "21% comes from 1.1² = 1.21, as if the momentum had risen by only 10%."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7140' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kinetic energy and momentum are related by K = p²/2m, so p = √(2mK). With equal K, momentum is proportional to √m. So p₁ : p₂ = √1 : √4 = 1 : 2, and the heavier body has the larger momentum.', wrong = '{"1": "1 : 4 treats momentum as directly proportional to mass. With equal kinetic energy, p ∝ √m.", "2": "2 : 1 is the ratio upside down. The heavier 4 kg body has twice the momentum.", "3": "1 : 16 squares the mass ratio. The momentum ratio is the square root of 1 : 4."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7141' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Falling through L from rest, the bob gains v² = 2gL = 2 × 10 × 2 = 40 m² s⁻². At the lowest point the tension must support the weight and supply the centripetal force: T = mg + mv²/L = 5 + 0.5 × 40/2 = 15 N. This is 3mg whatever the string length.', wrong = '{"0": "5 N is just the weight. The moving bob also needs a centripetal force towards the pivot.", "2": "10 N is only the centripetal force mv²/L. The tension must also hold up the 5 N weight.", "3": "20 N is 4mg, which is too large. The correct tension is mg + 2mg = 3mg = 15 N."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7142' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Taking zero at infinity, the absolute potential energy at Earth''s surface is −GMm/R. Since GM = gR², this equals −mgR. To reach zero at infinity the probe needs at least mgR = 1000 × 10 × 6.4 × 10⁶ = 6.4 × 10¹⁰ J.', wrong = '{"0": "3.2 × 10¹⁰ J is mgR/2, the energy needed to rise to a height R above the surface, not to infinity.", "1": "6.4 × 10⁷ J uses the radius in km (6400) instead of metres.", "3": "1.28 × 10¹¹ J is double the correct value, 2mgR."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7143' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Using U = −GMm/r: at the surface r = R and at height R, r = 2R. So ΔU = −GMm/2R − (−GMm/R) = GMm/2R. With GM = gR², this is mgR/2. The simple formula mgh would overestimate it because g weakens with height.', wrong = '{"0": "mgR uses mgh with g constant all the way up. In fact g falls with height, so less energy is needed.", "1": "2mgR has no basis. The increase is less than mgR, not more.", "2": "mgR/4 uses the weaker field at height R, g/4, as if it acted over the whole climb."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7144' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First convert the speed: 72 km h⁻¹ = 72 × 1000/3600 = 20 m s⁻¹. Friction does work equal to the car''s kinetic energy: μmg × d = ½mv². The mass cancels, so d = v²/(2μg) = 20²/(2 × 0.5 × 10) = 400/10 = 40 m.', wrong = '{"1": "20 m uses v²/(4μg), which doubles the denominator.", "2": "80 m uses v²/(μg), which forgets the ½ in ½mv².", "3": "518 m uses 72 as the speed without converting km h⁻¹ into m s⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7145' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The kinetic energy just after a bounce is proportional to the height it can rise to. So the fraction kept is 12.8/20 = 0.64, and 36% is lost. Since KE ∝ v², the speed ratio is √0.64 = 0.8.', wrong = '{"0": "64% is the energy that remains, not the energy lost. 0.64 is the energy ratio, not the speed ratio.", "2": "The 36% loss is right, but 0.64 is the energy ratio. The speed ratio is its square root, 0.8.", "3": "The speed ratio 0.8 is right, but 20% is the drop in speed. The drop in energy is 36%."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7146' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Average power = work done ÷ time. With no friction, all the work becomes kinetic energy: ½ × 2000 × 20² = 400 000 J. So average power = 400 000/10 = 40 000 W = 40 kW. At the end the power is F × v = 4000 × 20 = 80 kW, twice the average, because power rises with speed.', wrong = '{"0": "80 kW is the power at the moment the car reaches 20 m s⁻¹. The average over the 10 s is half of this.", "1": "20 kW is half the correct average power.", "3": "4 is the driving force in kilonewtons (2000 × 2 = 4000 N). It is a force, not a power."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7147' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By the work-energy theorem, the work done by the plank equals the bullet''s loss of kinetic energy. ΔKE = ½ × 0.01 × (500² − 300²) = ½ × 0.01 × 160 000 = 800 J. Since F × d = 800 J and d = 0.1 m, F = 8000 N.', wrong = '{"0": "800 is the energy lost in joules. It must still be divided by the 0.1 m thickness to give the force.", "1": "4000 N uses (500 − 300)² instead of 500² − 300², and also drops the ½.", "2": "16 000 N forgets the ½ in ½mv², doubling the energy lost."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7148' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Starting from rest with constant angular acceleration, the angle turned is θ = ½αt² = ½ × 2 × 10² = 100 rad. One revolution is 2π rad, so the number of revolutions is 100/(2π) ≈ 100/6.28 ≈ 15.9.', wrong = '{"1": "100 is the angle in radians. It must be divided by 2π to give revolutions.", "2": "31.8 divides by π instead of 2π. One revolution is 2π radians.", "3": "50 divides the 100 rad by 2 instead of by 2π."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7149' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Convert rpm to rad s⁻¹: 120 rpm = 4π rad s⁻¹ and 360 rpm = 12π rad s⁻¹. Angular acceleration α = (12π − 4π)/4 = 2π rad s⁻². Tangential acceleration aₜ = rα = 0.5 × 2π = π ≈ 3.14 m s⁻².', wrong = '{"0": "6.28 is α in rad s⁻². It must be multiplied by r = 0.5 m to give the rim''s linear acceleration.", "2": "1.57 m s⁻² is π/2, half the correct value.", "3": "12.6 m s⁻² is 4π, which multiplies α by 2 instead of by r = 0.5 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7150' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'On a flat curve only friction provides the centripetal force, so mv²/r must not exceed μmg. The mass cancels, so the maximum speed is v = √(μgr) = √(0.8 × 10 × 50) = √400 = 20 m s⁻¹. Above this speed the car skids outward.', wrong = '{"0": "40 m s⁻¹ is √(4μgr), double the correct speed. It would need four times the friction.", "1": "14.1 m s⁻¹ is √(0.4 × 10 × 50), which uses half the coefficient of friction.", "3": "28.3 m s⁻¹ is √(1.6 × 10 × 50), which uses double the coefficient of friction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7151' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'On a banked curve with no friction, the horizontal part of the normal force gives the centripetal force. This leads to tan θ = v²/(rg) = 15²/(90 × 10) = 225/900 = 0.25. So θ = tan⁻¹ 0.25 ≈ 14°.', wrong = '{"0": "tan⁻¹ 0.5 would suit a radius of 45 m at this speed, not 90 m.", "1": "45° needs tan θ = 1, that is v² = rg, a speed of 30 m s⁻¹.", "2": "tan⁻¹ 4 uses rg/v², the ratio upside down."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7152' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the top, the minimum speed is when the tension just becomes zero and gravity alone gives the centripetal force: v_t² = gr. From bottom to top the stone rises 2r, so v_b² = v_t² + 4gr = 5gr = 5 × 10 × 0.9 = 45. Thus v_b = √45 ≈ 6.7 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "3.0 m s⁻¹ is √(gr), the minimum speed at the top, not at the bottom.", "2": "4.2 m s⁻¹ is √(2gr), enough only to reach the level of the centre.", "3": "9.0 m s⁻¹ is √(9gr). It would complete the circle, but it is more than the minimum."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7153' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the bottom, T_b − mg = mv_b²/r. At the top, T_t + mg = mv_t²/r. Energy conservation over the height 2r gives v_b² − v_t² = 4gr. Subtracting: T_b − T_t = 2mg + m(4gr)/r = 6mg, whatever the speed.', wrong = '{"0": "2mg counts only the weight terms and forgets that the stone is faster at the bottom.", "2": "4mg counts only the speed difference and forgets the two weight terms.", "3": "5mg mixes this up with v_b² = 5gr, the minimum speed condition at the bottom."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7154' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Earth turns through 2π rad in 24 h = 86 400 s, so ω = 2π/86 400 ≈ 7.27 × 10⁻⁵ rad s⁻¹. A point on the equator moves in a circle of radius R, so its speed is v = ωR = 7.27 × 10⁻⁵ × 6.4 × 10⁶ ≈ 465 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "233 m s⁻¹ uses π instead of 2π for one full turn.", "1": "930 m s⁻¹ uses 4π instead of 2π, doubling the correct speed.", "3": "74 m s⁻¹ is R/86 400, which forgets the 2π. One turn is 2πR long."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7155' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First convert rpm to rad s⁻¹: ω = 300 × 2π/60 = 10π rad s⁻¹. The point is r = 10 cm = 0.1 m from the axis. Its centripetal acceleration is a = ω²r = (10π)² × 0.1 = 100π² × 0.1 ≈ 98.7 m s⁻², about 10g.', wrong = '{"0": "3.14 is ωr = 10π × 0.1, the linear speed in m s⁻¹, not the acceleration.", "1": "9.87 m s⁻² uses r = 1 cm instead of 10 cm.", "2": "987 m s⁻² uses r = 1 m instead of 0.1 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7156' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a satellite just above the surface, its weight provides the centripetal force: mg = mv²/R. So v = √(gR) = √(10 × 6.4 × 10⁶) = √(6.4 × 10⁷) = 8000 m s⁻¹ = 8.0 km s⁻¹. This is the speed of the closest possible orbit.', wrong = '{"1": "11.3 km s⁻¹ is √(2gR), the escape speed from Earth, not the orbital speed.", "2": "5.7 km s⁻¹ is √(gR/2), which halves gR before taking the root.", "3": "64 km s⁻¹ does not come from √(gR). It is the wrong order of magnitude, as if 6.4 × 10⁷ were rooted incorrectly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7157' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The tip moves at constant speed v = 2πr/T = 2π × 0.02/60 ≈ 2.09 × 10⁻³ m s⁻¹. In 15 s (a quarter turn) the velocity turns through 90°. The two velocity vectors are equal and perpendicular, so |Δv| = v√2 ≈ 2.96 × 10⁻³ m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "The speed is constant, but the direction changes, so the velocity does change.", "2": "2.09 × 10⁻³ m s⁻¹ is the speed of the tip itself, not the change in velocity.", "3": "4.19 × 10⁻³ m s⁻¹ is 2v, the change over half a turn (30 s), when the velocity reverses."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7158' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A quarter of a full circle is 2π/4 = π/2 rad. The runner covers an arc of (π/2) × 100 ≈ 157 m, but displacement is the straight line from start to finish. The two radii are at 90°, so the chord is 100√2 ≈ 141 m.', wrong = '{"0": "π/2 rad is right, but 157 m is the arc length (distance), not the straight-line displacement.", "1": "90 is the angle in degrees, not radians. A quarter turn is π/2 rad.", "3": "π/4 rad is an eighth of a turn, and 100 m is just the radius."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7159' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Friction supplies the centripetal force. The coin slips when mω²r exceeds μmg, so at the limit ω²r = μg and ω ∝ 1/√r. Moving the coin to 4 times the radius halves the limiting ω, so it slips at 60/2 = 30 rpm.', wrong = '{"0": "15 rpm assumes ω ∝ 1/r. The condition ω²r = μg gives ω ∝ 1/√r.", "1": "120 rpm suggests the coin is safer farther out, which is backwards. A larger radius needs more centripetal force.", "2": "42 rpm is 60/√2, which would suit doubling the radius, not making it four times larger."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7160' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By the equation of continuity, A₁v₁ = A₂v₂. The area of a pipe is proportional to the square of its diameter, so halving the diameter makes the area 1/4. The speed then rises 4 times: 4 × 2 = 8 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "4 m s⁻¹ treats the area as proportional to the diameter. Area depends on d², so the factor is 4, not 2.", "2": "1 m s⁻¹ makes the water slow down in the narrow part. The same flow through a smaller area must move faster.", "3": "16 m s⁻¹ squares the area ratio again, as if area depended on d⁴."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7161' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a horizontal pipe Bernoulli''s equation gives P₁ + ½ρv₁² = P₂ + ½ρv₂². So the pressure drop is P₁ − P₂ = ½ρ(v₂² − v₁²) = ½ × 1000 × (6² − 2²) = 500 × 32 = 16 000 Pa.', wrong = '{"0": "32 000 Pa forgets the ½ in the kinetic energy term ½ρv².", "2": "8 000 Pa uses (6 − 2)² = 16 instead of 6² − 2² = 32.", "3": "18 000 Pa uses only v₂², ½ × 1000 × 36, and forgets to subtract v₁²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7162' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Torricelli''s theorem the jet leaves at v = √(2g(H − h)), and it takes t = √(2h/g) to fall to the ground. Its range is x = vt = 2√(h(H − h)). This product is greatest when h = H/2 = 1.0 m, giving x = 2 m.', wrong = '{"0": "At 0.5 m the range is 2√(0.5 × 1.5) ≈ 1.73 m, less than the 2 m from a hole at 1.0 m.", "1": "At 1.5 m the range is 2√(1.5 × 0.5) ≈ 1.73 m, the same as at 0.5 m and less than at 1.0 m.", "3": "A hole at 2.0 m is at the water surface. There is no depth of water above it, so no jet comes out."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7163' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At terminal velocity the drag balances the weight minus upthrust. Weight and upthrust grow with volume, ∝ r³, while Stokes''s drag 6πηrv grows with r. So r³ ∝ rv, giving v ∝ r². Doubling the radius makes the terminal velocity 4 × 2 = 8 cm s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "4 cm s⁻¹ assumes v ∝ r. The weight grows as r³ and the drag as r, so v ∝ r².", "1": "2.8 cm s⁻¹ (2√2) assumes v ∝ √r, which has no basis here.", "2": "16 cm s⁻¹ assumes v ∝ r³, forgetting that the drag also grows with the radius."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7164' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The total volume stays the same: 8 × (4/3)πr³ = (4/3)πR³, so R³ = 8r³ and R = 2r. Terminal velocity is proportional to radius squared (v ∝ r²). So the big drop falls at 2² × v = 4v.', wrong = '{"1": "8v assumes terminal velocity grows with volume. It depends on r², and the radius only doubles.", "2": "2v assumes terminal velocity is proportional to r. In fact v ∝ r².", "3": "A bigger drop has more weight compared with its drag, so it falls faster. The velocity does change."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7165' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Stokes''s law gives the viscous drag on a small sphere moving slowly through a fluid: F = 6πηrv. With r = 1.0 mm = 1.0 × 10⁻³ m: F = 6 × 3.14 × 1.5 × 1.0 × 10⁻³ × 0.10 ≈ 2.8 × 10⁻³ N.', wrong = '{"0": "9.0 × 10⁻⁴ N is 6ηrv, which leaves out the π.", "2": "2.8 × 10⁻⁶ N takes the radius as 1 µm instead of 1 mm.", "3": "1.4 × 10⁻³ N uses 3π instead of 6π, half the correct drag."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7166' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Volume flow rate = cross-sectional area × average speed. The aorta is circular, so A = πr² = π × (1.0 cm)² ≈ 3.14 cm². Flow rate = 3.14 cm² × 30 cm s⁻¹ ≈ 94 cm³ s⁻¹, nearly 0.1 litre every second.', wrong = '{"0": "30 cm³ s⁻¹ is just the speed. It leaves out the cross-sectional area of the aorta.", "1": "9.4 cm³ s⁻¹ is ten times too small, a slip in powers of ten.", "3": "188 cm³ s⁻¹ uses the circumference 2πr instead of the area πr²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7167' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By the equation of continuity, the total volume flow is the same everywhere: A₁v₁ = A₂v₂. So 3 × 30 = 2000 × v, and v = 90/2000 = 0.045 cm s⁻¹. Each capillary is narrow, but together they have a huge total area, so blood moves slowly and has time to exchange materials.', wrong = '{"0": "0.45 cm s⁻¹ is ten times too large. 90 ÷ 2000 = 0.045.", "1": "20 cm s⁻¹ assumes blood speeds up in narrow vessels. What counts is the total area of all the capillaries.", "2": "0.0045 cm s⁻¹ is ten times too small. 90 ÷ 2000 = 0.045."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7168' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By the equation of continuity, the same volume of blood per second must pass through the smaller area of the narrowed segment, so it flows faster. Bernoulli''s principle says faster flow goes with lower pressure. The low pressure on the wall can let the artery be squeezed further, which is called vascular flutter.', wrong = '{"1": "A narrower section makes blood flow faster, not slower, since the same volume must pass each second.", "2": "The speed does rise, but by Bernoulli''s principle higher speed goes with lower pressure, not higher.", "3": "The same volume per second through a smaller area means a higher speed, so the speed cannot stay the same."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7169' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Flow in a pipe becomes turbulent when the Reynolds number, ρvD/η, is large. Here ρ is density, v speed, D pipe diameter and η viscosity. Increasing the speed raises this number, so turbulence becomes more likely.', wrong = '{"0": "Higher viscosity damps out swirls and eddies. It lowers ρvD/η and keeps the flow smooth (laminar).", "2": "A narrower pipe at the same speed lowers D, so ρvD/η falls and flow is more likely to stay laminar.", "3": "A less dense liquid lowers ρ, which lowers ρvD/η and makes turbulence less likely."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7170' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First find the wavelength: λ = v/f = 340/500 = 0.68 m. The phase difference between two points Δx apart is Δφ = 2π × Δx/λ = 2π × 0.17/0.68 = 2π × ¼ = π/2. The points are a quarter of a wavelength apart.', wrong = '{"0": "π would need the points to be half a wavelength apart, 34 cm.", "1": "π/4 would need the points to be one eighth of a wavelength apart, 8.5 cm.", "3": "2π would need a full wavelength, 68 cm, and the points would then be in phase."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7171' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The speed of sound in a gas is proportional to the square root of absolute temperature. Convert to kelvin: 27 °C = 300 K and 127 °C = 400 K. So v = 347 × √(400/300) = 347 × 1.155 ≈ 401 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "463 m s⁻¹ uses v ∝ T (347 × 4/3). The speed depends on √T.", "1": "The speed of sound in air does change with temperature. It rises as the air gets hotter.", "2": "1634 m s⁻¹ uses the Celsius ratio 127/27. Temperatures must be in kelvin, and the square root is needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7172' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Newton assumed sound compressions are isothermal, giving v = √(P/ρ). Laplace pointed out they happen too quickly for heat to flow, so they are adiabatic and v = √(γP/ρ). So the corrected speed is 280 × √1.4 ≈ 280 × 1.18 ≈ 331 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "392 m s⁻¹ multiplies by γ = 1.4 instead of by √γ.", "2": "280 m s⁻¹ is Newton''s uncorrected value, which is too low compared with experiment.", "3": "237 m s⁻¹ divides by √1.4 instead of multiplying, making the error worse."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7173' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The speed of sound is v = √(γP/ρ). At constant temperature, Boyle''s law says doubling the pressure also doubles the density, so P/ρ stays the same. Hence the speed of sound does not change with pressure alone.', wrong = '{"0": "Doubling v would need P/ρ to become four times larger. In fact P/ρ is unchanged.", "2": "A factor of √2 would follow only if the density stayed fixed. At constant temperature it doubles along with P.", "3": "Higher pressure does not slow sound. The rise in density exactly cancels the rise in pressure."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7174' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a string fixed at both ends, each loop of a stationary wave is half a wavelength long. Four loops fill 0.80 m, so 4 × λ/2 = 0.80 m and λ = 0.40 m. The wave speed is v = fλ = 500 × 0.40 = 200 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "400 m s⁻¹ takes λ = 0.80 m, the whole string. Four loops make two full wavelengths.", "1": "100 m s⁻¹ takes each loop as a full wavelength, λ = 0.20 m. Each loop is only λ/2.", "3": "800 m s⁻¹ takes λ = 1.6 m, which would be the fundamental (one loop), not four loops."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7175' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The fundamental frequency of a stretched string is f = (1/2L)√(T/μ), so f ∝ √T. A 44% rise makes the tension 1.44T, and √1.44 = 1.2. So the new frequency is 200 × 1.2 = 240 Hz.', wrong = '{"0": "288 Hz (200 × 1.44) takes f ∝ T. The frequency depends on the square root of tension.", "1": "220 Hz is only a 10% rise. Since √1.44 = 1.2, the rise is 20%.", "2": "167 Hz divides by 1.2, as if a tighter string vibrated more slowly. Tighter strings give higher notes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7176' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For an open pipe of length 0.60 m, the fundamental is v/2L = v/1.2 and the first overtone (2nd harmonic) is twice this, v/0.60. A closed pipe''s fundamental is v/4L_c. Setting v/4L_c = v/0.60 gives 4L_c = 0.60 m, so L_c = 0.15 m = 15 cm.', wrong = '{"1": "30 cm would match the open pipe''s fundamental (v/1.2), not its first overtone.", "2": "7.5 cm comes from using v/8L_c for the closed pipe''s fundamental instead of v/4L_c.", "3": "60 cm makes the two pipes the same length. A closed pipe has a lower fundamental than an open pipe of equal length."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7177' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A pipe closed at one end has a node at the closed end and an antinode at the open end. Only odd harmonics fit this pattern: 100, 300, 500, 700 Hz, and so on. 200 Hz is the 2nd harmonic, an even one, so the closed pipe cannot produce it.', wrong = '{"0": "300 Hz is the 3rd harmonic (3 × 100 Hz), an odd harmonic that a closed pipe can produce.", "2": "500 Hz is the 5th harmonic, which is odd, so the closed pipe can produce it.", "3": "700 Hz is the 7th harmonic, which is odd, so the closed pipe can produce it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7178' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For two sources in phase, the result at a point depends on the path difference in wavelengths. Here 1.70/0.68 = 2.5, an odd number of half wavelengths. The two waves arrive in antiphase, a crest meeting a trough, so they cancel and P is a quiet spot.', wrong = '{"0": "Constructive interference needs a whole number of wavelengths, such as 2λ or 3λ. Here the path difference is 2.5λ.", "1": "2.5λ is exactly an odd number of half wavelengths, which gives cancellation, not half the loudness.", "3": "Sound waves from coherent sources do interfere. This is how loud and quiet spots form in a hall."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7179' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a stationary wave, a node and the next antinode are a quarter of a wavelength apart. So λ/4 = 5 cm and λ = 20 cm = 0.20 m. The speed of the travelling waves is v = fλ = 680 × 0.20 = 136 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "68 m s⁻¹ takes node to antinode as λ/2, giving λ = 0.10 m. Node to node is λ/2.", "1": "34 m s⁻¹ takes the 5 cm as a full wavelength.", "2": "272 m s⁻¹ takes the 5 cm as λ/8, giving λ = 0.40 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7180' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a stationary wave, all particles between two neighbouring nodes move up and down together, so they are in phase. Their amplitudes differ: zero at the nodes and largest at the antinode in the middle. Particles in the next segment move in the opposite direction, in antiphase.', wrong = '{"1": "Same amplitude with a steadily changing phase describes a travelling wave, not a stationary one.", "2": "Only the nodes are at rest all the time. The particles between them vibrate.", "3": "They are in phase, but the amplitude is not the same. It rises from zero at a node to a maximum at the antinode."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7181' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The energy carried is E ∝ A²f². Doubling the amplitude multiplies E by 2² = 4. Halving the frequency multiplies E by (½)² = ¼. Together 4 × ¼ = 1, so the energy carried stays the same.', wrong = '{"0": "Doubling comes from squaring the amplitude factor (4) but not the frequency factor (½). Both must be squared.", "2": "Four times counts only the change in amplitude and ignores the halved frequency.", "3": "Halving uses only the frequency change, and without squaring it. Both changes must be included."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7182' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the source moves away from a stationary observer, the waves are stretched out and the frequency falls. f′ = f × v/(v + v_s) = 600 × 340/(340 + 34) = 600 × 340/374 ≈ 545 Hz.', wrong = '{"0": "540 Hz uses f(v − v₀)/v, which is the formula for an observer moving away from a stationary source.", "1": "660 Hz is the frequency heard by an observer moving towards a stationary source.", "3": "667 Hz is the frequency heard when the source approaches. A receding source gives a lower frequency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7183' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Case 1, moving source: f′ = f × v/(v − v_s) = 600 × 340/306 ≈ 667 Hz. Case 2, moving listener: f′ = f × (v + v₀)/v = 600 × 374/340 = 660 Hz. The results differ because the Doppler shift depends on which one moves through the air, not just their relative speed.', wrong = '{"0": "This swaps the two results. The moving source gives 667 Hz and the moving listener gives 660 Hz.", "1": "660 Hz is right only for the moving listener. A moving source at the same speed gives 667 Hz.", "2": "667 Hz is right only for the moving source. A moving listener at the same speed gives 660 Hz."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7184' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Approaching, the observer hears f × v/(v − v_s) = 500 × 340/320 ≈ 531.3 Hz. Moving away, the observer hears f × v/(v + v_s) = 500 × 340/360 ≈ 472.2 Hz. The drop as the train passes is 531.3 − 472.2 ≈ 59 Hz.', wrong = '{"1": "29 Hz is about half the drop, roughly the change on one side of 500 Hz only. The full drop is from 531 Hz to 472 Hz.", "2": "The pitch clearly falls as the train passes, from above 500 Hz to below it.", "3": "118 Hz is twice the correct drop."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7185' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a source moving towards a stationary listener, f′/f = v/(v − v_s). A frequency 10% higher means f′/f = 1.1. So 330 = 1.1(330 − v_s), giving 330 − v_s = 300 and v_s = 30 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "33 m s⁻¹ is 10% of the speed of sound. That is the speed a moving listener would need, not a moving source.", "2": "36 m s⁻¹ does not come from the Doppler formula. It would give a rise of more than 10%.", "3": "300 m s⁻¹ is v − v_s, an intermediate step. The source speed is 330 − 300 = 30 m s⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7186' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The angular frequency is ω = 2π/T = 2π/2 = π rad s⁻¹. In SHM the speed at displacement x is v = ω√(A² − x²). So v = π√(4² − 2²) = π√12 ≈ 3.14 × 3.46 ≈ 10.9 cm s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "12.6 cm s⁻¹ is ωA, the maximum speed, reached only at the mean position.", "1": "6.3 cm s⁻¹ is ωx, which uses x instead of √(A² − x²).", "3": "21.8 cm s⁻¹ is double the right answer, from taking ω = 2π instead of π."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7187' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In SHM, the maximum speed is v(max) = ωA, at the mean position, and the maximum acceleration is a(max) = ω²A, at the extremes. Dividing, ω = a(max)/v(max) = 1.8/0.6 = 3 rad s⁻¹. Then A = v(max)/ω = 0.6/3 = 0.2 m.', wrong = '{"0": "0.33 is v(max)/a(max) = 1/ω, which has units of time, not length.", "1": "3 is the angular frequency ω in rad s⁻¹, not the amplitude.", "2": "1.08 is v(max) × a(max), which has no physical meaning here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7188' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a lift accelerating upward at a, the effective gravity is g + a = g + g/3 = 4g/3. The period of a pendulum is T = 2π√(L/g), so T ∝ 1/√g. The new period is 2.0 × √(3/4) ≈ 2.0 × 0.866 ≈ 1.73 s.', wrong = '{"1": "2.31 s multiplies by √(4/3) instead of √(3/4). A larger effective g gives a shorter period.", "2": "The period does change, because the effective value of g in the lift has increased.", "3": "1.5 s takes T ∝ 1/g. The period depends on 1/√g."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7189' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cutting a spring in half makes each half twice as stiff, so k becomes 2k. The period of a mass-spring system is T = 2π√(m/k), so T ∝ 1/√k. The new period is 2.0/√2 ≈ 1.41 s.', wrong = '{"0": "1.0 s takes T ∝ 1/k. The period depends on 1/√k.", "2": "2.83 s assumes the half spring is softer. In fact it is stiffer, so the period is shorter.", "3": "4.0 s assumes k halves and T ∝ 1/k. Both steps are wrong."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7190' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The total energy in SHM is E = ½kA², and the potential energy at displacement x is ½kx². KE equals PE when each is half of the total: ½kx² = ¼kA². So x² = A²/2 and x = A/√2 ≈ 0.71A.', wrong = '{"0": "At A/2 the PE is (½)² = ¼ of the total, so the KE is three times the PE.", "1": "At A/4 the PE is only 1/16 of the total energy.", "3": "At √3A/2 the PE is ¾ of the total, so it is three times the KE."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7191' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The projection of uniform circular motion on a diameter is SHM with amplitude A = 0.10 m and angular frequency ω = 2π × 2 = 4π rad s⁻¹. The maximum acceleration is a(max) = ω²A = 16π² × 0.10 ≈ 15.8 m s⁻².', wrong = '{"0": "1.26 is ωA, the maximum speed in m s⁻¹, not the maximum acceleration.", "1": "3.95 m s⁻² uses ω = 2π, which is one revolution per second, not two.", "2": "158 m s⁻² takes the amplitude as 1 m instead of 10 cm."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7192' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Set x = A/2 in x = A sin(2πt/T): sin(2πt/12) = ½, so 2πt/12 = π/6 and t = 1 s, which is T/12. The rest of the way from A/2 to A takes a further 2 s, because the particle slows down near the extreme position.', wrong = '{"1": "2 s is T/6, the time to go from A/2 on to the extreme position A.", "2": "3 s is T/4, the time to go all the way from the mean position to the extreme.", "3": "0.5 s is T/24. The particle reaches A/2 at sin⁻¹(½) = π/6, which is T/12."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7193' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a wave passes into a new medium its frequency stays the same, because it is set by the source; only the speed and wavelength change. In water λ = v/f = 1480/200 = 7.4 m. In air it was 340/200 = 1.7 m.', wrong = '{"0": "1.7 m is the wavelength in air. Sound travels faster in water, so its wavelength there is longer.", "2": "0.74 m is ten times too small. 1480 ÷ 200 = 7.4.", "3": "0.39 m assumes the wavelength shrinks in water. It actually grows, since v rises and f stays fixed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7194' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The work done by the gas is W = PΔV = 2.0 × 10⁵ × (5.0 − 3.0) × 10⁻³ m³ = 400 J. By the first law, ΔU = Q − W = 1000 − 400 = +600 J. The rest of the heat leaves as work of expansion.', wrong = '{"0": "1400 J adds the work instead of subtracting it. Work done by the gas takes energy out.", "1": "400 J is the work done by the gas, not the change in its internal energy.", "3": "1000 J ignores the 400 J of work the gas does while expanding."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7195' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an isothermal process the temperature stays constant, and for an ideal gas internal energy depends only on temperature, so ΔU = 0. The first law Q = ΔU + W then gives W = Q = 500 J. All the heat absorbed is turned into work.', wrong = '{"0": "Zero work would mean the gas did not expand. Here it does expand.", "1": "250 J splits the heat between ΔU and W. In an isothermal process ΔU = 0, so all of it becomes work.", "2": "1000 J is double the heat supplied. The gas cannot do more work than the energy it receives here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7196' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an adiabatic process no heat enters or leaves, Q = 0. Work done on the gas adds energy, so its internal energy rises by exactly 300 J and its temperature goes up. A bicycle pump getting warm is an everyday example.', wrong = '{"1": "Work done on the gas adds energy to it. It would lose energy only if it did work on the surroundings.", "2": "No heat flows, but work is done on the gas, and work also changes internal energy.", "3": "600 J counts the 300 J of work twice. With Q = 0, ΔU equals the work done on the gas."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7197' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For any ideal gas, Cp − Cv = R, because at constant pressure each mole needs an extra R joules per kelvin to do expansion work. So Cv = Cp − R = 29.1 − 8.31 ≈ 20.8 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, which is (5/2)R, the value for a diatomic gas.', wrong = '{"0": "37.4 adds R to Cp instead of subtracting. Cv is always smaller than Cp.", "2": "29.1 is Cp itself. For a gas, Cv is smaller than Cp by R.", "3": "12.5 is (3/2)R, the Cv of a monatomic gas. Here Cp − R gives 20.8."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7198' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Specific heat is per kilogram and molar specific heat is per mole. One mole of nitrogen has mass 0.028 kg, so Cv = c × M = 743 × 0.028 ≈ 20.8 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. This matches (5/2)R for a diatomic gas.', wrong = '{"0": "26 500 divides 743 by 0.028 instead of multiplying. A mole is much less than a kilogram, so the value must be smaller.", "1": "743 is the specific heat per kilogram, not per mole.", "3": "0.74 is the specific heat in J g⁻¹ K⁻¹, per gram. A mole of nitrogen is 28 g."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7199' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a monatomic ideal gas Cv = (3/2)R = 1.5 × 8.31 ≈ 12.5 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. At constant volume no work is done, so all the heat raises the internal energy: Q = nCvΔT = 2 × 12.5 × 10 ≈ 249 J.', wrong = '{"0": "416 J uses Cp = (5/2)R, the constant-pressure value. At constant volume Cv must be used.", "1": "166 J uses R alone, nRΔT = 2 × 8.31 × 10. That is the work done at constant pressure, not the heat at constant volume.", "2": "125 J is the heat for one mole. There are two moles here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7200' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At constant pressure, the work done by a gas is W = PΔV. For an ideal gas PV = nRT, so PΔV = nRΔT. W = 2 × 8.31 × 10 ≈ 166 J. This holds for any ideal gas, monatomic or diatomic.', wrong = '{"1": "249 J is nCvΔT for a monatomic gas, the rise in internal energy, not the work done.", "2": "416 J is nCpΔT for a monatomic gas, the total heat supplied, not the work.", "3": "83 J is the work done by one mole. There are two moles here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7201' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At constant pressure the heat supplied is Q = nCpΔT, while the rise in internal energy is ΔU = nCvΔT. For a diatomic gas Cv = (5/2)R and Cp = Cv + R = (7/2)R. So ΔU/Q = Cv/Cp = 5/7, and the other 2/7 is used to do expansion work.', wrong = '{"0": "2/7 is the fraction of heat that becomes work done by the gas, not the rise in internal energy.", "2": "3/5 is Cv/Cp for a monatomic gas, (3/2)R ÷ (5/2)R. This gas is diatomic.", "3": "2/5 is the fraction that becomes work for a monatomic gas, not the internal energy fraction here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7202' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After one complete cycle the gas is back in its starting state, so its internal energy is unchanged: ΔU = 0. The first law Q = ΔU + W then gives Q = W = 200 J. The net heat absorbed equals the net work done.', wrong = '{"0": "Zero net heat would mean zero net work. The gas does 200 J of work, so it must absorb 200 J.", "1": "400 J counts the 200 J twice. With ΔU = 0, Q equals W.", "3": "−200 J would mean the gas gave out heat while also doing work, which would create energy from nothing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7203' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an insulated container, heat lost by the hot water equals heat gained by the cold water. With the same specific heat on both sides: 200(80 − θ) = 300(θ − 20). So 16 000 − 200θ = 300θ − 6000, giving 500θ = 22 000 and θ = 44 °C.', wrong = '{"0": "50 °C is the simple average of 80 and 20. It ignores that there is more cold water than hot.", "1": "56 °C weights the temperatures the wrong way round, giving more weight to the smaller hot mass.", "2": "40 °C does not satisfy the heat balance. It would need the cold water to gain more heat than the hot water loses."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7204' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The work done by a gas is the area under its path on a P-V diagram. A straight line from A to B gives a trapezium: average pressure × change in volume = ½(1.0 + 3.0) × 10⁵ Pa × 2.0 × 10⁻³ m³ = 2.0 × 10⁵ × 2.0 × 10⁻³ = 400 J.', wrong = '{"1": "200 J uses only the lower pressure, 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa, the rectangle under point A.", "2": "600 J uses only the higher pressure, 3.0 × 10⁵ Pa, as if the pressure were constant at B''s value.", "3": "800 J adds the two pressures and forgets the ½ in the area of a trapezium."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7205' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Use the first law with careful signs. Work done on the gas adds energy (+100 J), and heat given out removes energy (−40 J). So ΔU = −40 + 100 = +60 J, and the internal energy rises.', wrong = '{"0": "−60 J gets both signs reversed. Work done on the gas raises its internal energy.", "2": "+140 J treats the 40 J as heat absorbed. The gas gives this heat out, so it must be subtracted.", "3": "−140 J treats the work as done by the gas and the heat as lost, so both remove energy. Here work is done on the gas."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7206' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With the same heat Q for one mole: Q = CvΔT₁ = CpΔT₂. So ΔT₁/ΔT₂ = Cp/Cv = γ. For a diatomic gas γ = (7/2)R ÷ (5/2)R = 1.4. At constant volume none of the heat does work, so the temperature rises more.', wrong = '{"0": "0.71 is Cv/Cp, the ratio upside down. The constant-volume rise is the larger one.", "1": "The rises are not equal. At constant pressure part of the heat becomes work, so the temperature rises less.", "3": "1.67 is γ for a monatomic gas. For a diatomic gas γ = 1.4."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7207' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Heat needed = mcΔT = 1.5 × 4200 × (100 − 20) = 504 000 J. Only 80% of the input is useful, so the useful power is 0.8 × 2000 = 1600 W. Time = 504 000/1600 = 315 s, a little over 5 minutes.', wrong = '{"0": "252 s uses the full 2000 W and ignores the 80% efficiency.", "1": "394 s divides by 0.8 twice, applying the efficiency two times.", "2": "504 s uses a useful power of 1000 W. The useful power is 0.8 × 2000 = 1600 W."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7208' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Between two like charges their fields point in opposite directions, so the zero lies between them, nearer the smaller charge. At x from +4 µC: k(4)/x² = k(1)/(0.30 − x)². Taking roots, x = 2(0.30 − x), so x = 0.20 m = 20 cm from the +4 µC charge.', wrong = '{"1": "At 10 cm from +4 µC (20 cm from +1 µC), the larger charge''s field is 16 times stronger, so they do not cancel.", "2": "At the midpoint both charges are equally far, so the +4 µC field is four times stronger.", "3": "Beyond the +1 µC charge both fields point the same way, away from the charges, so they cannot cancel."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7209' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Between unlike charges both fields point the same way (from + towards −), so they cannot cancel there. The zero lies outside, nearer the smaller charge. At d beyond the −1 µC charge: 4/(d + 0.30)² = 1/d², so d + 0.30 = 2d and d = 0.30 m.', wrong = '{"0": "Between opposite charges the two fields point the same way and add, so the field cannot be zero there.", "2": "Between opposite charges the two fields add, wherever the point is, so the field cannot be zero there.", "3": "Beyond the +4 µC charge, the larger charge is also the nearer one, so its field always wins."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7210' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Coulomb''s force in a medium is F = kq₁q₂/(εᵣr²). The medium divides the force by εᵣ = 3. Halving the separation multiplies the force by 2² = 4, since F ∝ 1/r². So the new force is 9 × 4/3 = 12 N.', wrong = '{"0": "3 N divides by 3 for the medium but forgets that halving the distance makes the force 4 times larger.", "1": "27 N multiplies by 3 for the medium instead of dividing, and ignores the change in distance.", "3": "6.75 N (9 × 3/4) gets both effects upside down: ×3 for the medium and ÷4 for the distance."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7211' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each of the other two charges pushes on the chosen charge with F = kq²/a². In an equilateral triangle these two forces are 60° apart. Their resultant is 2F cos 30° = √3 F = √3 kq²/a².', wrong = '{"0": "2kq²/a² adds the two forces as if they acted in the same direction. They are 60° apart.", "1": "kq²/a² is the force from only one of the other two charges.", "2": "The two forces are 60° apart, not opposite, so they cannot cancel."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7212' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each corner is a/√2 from the centre, so each charge gives a field E₀ = kq/(a²/2) = 2kq/a². Along each diagonal, a + charge and the − charge opposite it give fields in the same direction, totalling 2E₀. The two diagonal fields are perpendicular, so E = 2E₀√2 = 4√2 kq/a², pointing towards the negative side.', wrong = '{"1": "The field would be zero only if each diagonal had two like charges. Here each diagonal has a + and a −.", "2": "The field points away from the positive charges and towards the negative ones, and its size is twice 2√2 kq/a².", "3": "4kq/a² is the field along one diagonal only. The other diagonal adds an equal perpendicular field."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7213' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electric field is a vector. At the midpoint the two equal fields point in opposite directions, so they cancel and E = 0. Potential is a scalar, so the two positive potentials add: V = 2kq/(d/2) = 4kq/d, which is not zero.', wrong = '{"0": "Zero potential with a non-zero field is the situation midway between equal and opposite charges.", "2": "The field cancels, but potential is a scalar, and two positive potentials add instead of cancelling.", "3": "The fields do cancel at the midpoint, because they are equal and opposite there."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7214' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each sheet produces a uniform field σ/2ε₀ pointing away from it on both sides. Between two positive sheets, the two fields point in opposite directions and cancel, giving zero. Outside the plates they point the same way and add to σ/ε₀.', wrong = '{"0": "σ/ε₀ is the field between plates with opposite charges, or the field outside these two like plates.", "1": "σ/2ε₀ is the field of one sheet alone. Between these plates the second sheet cancels it.", "3": "2σ/ε₀ counts each sheet as giving σ/ε₀. Each gives only σ/2ε₀, and here they cancel."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7215' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The field of a very large uniformly charged sheet is E = σ/2ε₀, which does not depend on the distance from the sheet. This holds as long as the sheet is very large compared with the distance. So the force qE is the same at 2 cm as at 1 cm: F.', wrong = '{"0": "F/2 assumes the field falls as 1/r, which is true for a long line of charge, not a flat sheet.", "1": "F/4 assumes the field falls as 1/r², which is true for a point charge, not a large sheet.", "2": "Moving away from the sheet cannot make the force stronger. For a large sheet it stays the same."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7216' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Bringing the charge in from infinity against the repulsion stores electric potential energy. The work needed is W = kq₁q₂/r = 9 × 10⁹ × 5 × 10⁻⁶ × 2 × 10⁻⁶/0.30 = 0.09/0.30 = 0.30 J.', wrong = '{"1": "1.0 J divides by r² = 0.09 m², which is the form used for force, not for energy.", "2": "0.030 J slips a power of ten. 0.09 ÷ 0.30 = 0.30.", "3": "0.15 J includes a factor ½ that does not belong in kq₁q₂/r."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7217' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Going round the square, opposite corners hold like charges. Each diagonal has two equal like charges at the same distance from the centre, so their fields cancel and E = 0. The potentials of two +q and two −q at equal distances also add to zero, so V = 0 too.', wrong = '{"0": "The field is zero, but the potential is also zero, since there are equal + and − charges at the same distance.", "2": "The potential is zero, but the field is also zero, since each diagonal holds two equal like charges.", "3": "Both the field and the potential cancel here, by symmetry."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7218' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The electron gains kinetic energy equal to the work done by the field: eV = ½mv². So v = √(2eV/m) = √(2 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 100/9.1 × 10⁻³¹) = √(3.5 × 10¹³) ≈ 5.9 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "4.2 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ forgets the 2 in 2eV/m, using √(eV/m).", "1": "3.5 × 10¹³ is v² in m² s⁻². The square root must be taken to get the speed.", "3": "1.9 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹ is the speed for 1000 V, not 100 V."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7219' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Potential falls along the direction of the electric field. In a uniform field the potential difference is ΔV = Ed = 2000 × 0.03 = 60 V. B is further along the field, so B is lower and V_A − V_B = +60 V.', wrong = '{"0": "−60 V has the sign reversed. Moving along the field lowers the potential, so A is higher than B.", "1": "Points separated along a field line are at different potentials. Only points across the field share a potential.", "2": "+6000 V uses d = 3 m instead of 3 cm = 0.03 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7220' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a point charge, V = kq/r and E = kq/r². Dividing one by the other gives r = V/E = 600/200 = 3 m. Then from V = kq/r, q = Vr/k = 600 × 3/(9 × 10⁹) = 2 × 10⁻⁷ C = 0.2 µC.', wrong = '{"1": "3 m is right, but 2 µC is ten times too large. 1800 ÷ (9 × 10⁹) = 2 × 10⁻⁷ C.", "2": "0.33 m is E/V, the ratio upside down. The distance is V/E = 3 m.", "3": "9 m squares the ratio V/E. The distance is V/E itself, 3 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7221' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor is C = εᵣε₀A/d. Filling the gap with a dielectric of εᵣ = 4 multiplies C by 4, and doubling the separation d divides it by 2. So C = 10 × 4/2 = 20 µF.', wrong = '{"0": "80 µF multiplies by 2 for the larger separation. A larger gap lowers the capacitance.", "2": "5 µF only halves C for the separation and ignores the dielectric.", "3": "40 µF includes the dielectric but ignores the doubled plate separation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7222' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At first U = ½CV² = ½ × 4 × 10⁻⁶ × 200² = 0.08 J. Once disconnected, the charge Q cannot change. Doubling the plate separation halves C, so U = Q²/2C doubles to 0.16 J. The extra energy comes from the work done in pulling the attracting plates apart.', wrong = '{"0": "0.04 J is the result if the capacitor stays connected, so V stays fixed and U = ½CV² halves.", "1": "0.08 J is the energy before the plates were moved apart.", "3": "0.32 J is four times the original. With Q fixed, halving C only doubles the energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7223' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Connected to the supply, the voltage stays 200 V. Doubling the separation halves C to 2 µF. So U = ½CV² = ½ × 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 200² = 0.04 J, half the original 0.08 J. Some charge flows back into the battery.', wrong = '{"0": "0.16 J is the result for an isolated capacitor, where the charge stays fixed instead of the voltage.", "1": "0.08 J is the original energy, before the separation was changed.", "2": "0.02 J is a quarter of the original. With V fixed, halving C only halves the energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7224' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two equal capacitors in series give C = (6 × 6)/(6 + 6) = 3 µF. This pair is then in parallel with the third 6 µF capacitor, and parallel capacitances simply add: 3 + 6 = 9 µF.', wrong = '{"1": "4 µF puts two capacitors in parallel (12 µF) and then that pair in series with the third. The arrangement is the other way round.", "2": "18 µF puts all three capacitors in parallel.", "3": "2 µF puts all three capacitors in series."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7225' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Charge is conserved: Q = 2 µF × 100 V = 200 µC, now shared by 2 + 3 = 5 µF, so V = 200/5 = 40 V. Energy before = ½ × 2 µF × 100² = 10 mJ; after = ½ × 5 µF × 40² = 4 mJ. So 6 mJ is lost as heat and radiation in the connecting wires.', wrong = '{"0": "40 V is right, but energy is not conserved when capacitors share charge. Some is always lost.", "2": "60 V shares the voltage in proportion to capacitance, which is wrong. Also, 4 mJ is the energy left, not the energy lost.", "3": "50 V would be the common voltage only if the two capacitances were equal."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7226' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The time constant is τ = RC = 2 × 10⁶ Ω × 5 × 10⁻⁶ F = 10 s. During discharge the voltage falls as V = V₀e^(−t/τ). It reaches half when e^(−t/τ) = ½, so t = τ ln 2 = 10 × 0.693 ≈ 6.9 s.', wrong = '{"0": "10 s is one time constant. After it, V has fallen to about 37% of V₀, not 50%.", "1": "5 s is τ/2. Exponential decay to half takes τ ln 2, not τ/2.", "3": "14 s is τ/ln 2. The half-time is τ multiplied by ln 2, not divided."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7227' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'While a capacitor charges, the current dies away exponentially: I = I₀e^(−t/RC). At t = 2RC, I/I₀ = e⁻² ≈ 0.135, so the current is about 13.5% of its starting value. At the same moment the charge has reached about 86.5% of its final value.', wrong = '{"0": "86.5% is the charge at t = 2RC, as a fraction of its final value. The current has dropped to 13.5%.", "1": "37% is the current after one time constant, t = RC, not two.", "2": "25% assumes the current halves every RC. It actually falls by a factor of e ≈ 2.72 each time constant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7228' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The field between the plates is E = V/d = 500/0.020 = 2.5 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹. For the drop to stay still, the electric force balances its weight: qE = mg. So q = (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ × 10)/(2.5 × 10⁴) = 6.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, which is 4e.', wrong = '{"1": "3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ C uses a plate gap of 1 cm, which doubles the field.", "2": "1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C uses a plate gap of 0.5 cm, which makes the field four times too large.", "3": "6.4 × 10⁻¹⁷ C uses a gap of 2.0 m instead of 2.0 cm."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7229' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electric potential energy of a group of charges is the sum over every pair. Three charges make three pairs, each a distance a apart with energy kq²/a. So U = 3kq²/a, the work needed to bring the charges together from infinity.', wrong = '{"0": "kq²/a counts only one of the three pairs.", "2": "√3 kq²/a borrows the vector addition used for forces. Energy is a scalar, so the three pair energies simply add.", "3": "Like charges repel, so work must be done to push them together. Their potential energy is positive, not zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7230' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a wire is stretched its volume stays the same, so tripling the length cuts the cross-sectional area to one third. Since R = ρL/A, the new resistance is ρ(3L)/(A/3) = 9ρL/A = 9R.', wrong = '{"0": "3R counts the longer length but forgets that the wire also becomes thinner.", "1": "R/3 has the length effect upside down. A longer wire has more resistance, not less.", "3": "6R adds the two factors of 3. They must be multiplied, giving 9."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7231' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two diametrically opposite points split the ring into two equal halves, each of 16/2 = 8 Ω. Between those two points the halves provide two separate paths, so they are in parallel. R = (8 × 8)/(8 + 8) = 4 Ω.', wrong = '{"0": "8 Ω is the resistance of one half on its own. The other half is in parallel with it.", "1": "16 Ω is the whole wire in series. The two halves are in parallel between the points.", "2": "2 Ω divides 8 Ω by 4. Two equal resistors in parallel give half of one, which is 4 Ω."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7232' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The resistance of a metal rises with temperature as R = R₀(1 + αΔT). Here the temperature rise is ΔT = 70 − 20 = 50 K. So R = 20(1 + 0.004 × 50) = 20 × 1.2 = 24 Ω.', wrong = '{"1": "21.6 Ω uses ΔT = 20 K, the starting temperature, instead of the rise of 50 K.", "2": "28 Ω uses ΔT = 100 K, which is twice the actual rise.", "3": "25.6 Ω uses ΔT = 70 K, the final temperature, instead of the change of 50 K."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7233' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The resistance rises by 13.90 − 10.00 = 3.90 Ω for 100 °C. In the furnace it has risen by 12.34 − 10.00 = 2.34 Ω. With a linear change, θ = (2.34/3.90) × 100 = 60 °C.', wrong = '{"0": "50 °C assumes the reading is halfway between 10.00 and 13.90 Ω. The halfway reading would be 11.95 Ω.", "2": "23.4 °C comes from 2.34 × 10, which does not use the 3.90 Ω per 100 °C calibration.", "3": "88.8 °C divides 12.34 by 13.90. The rise above the 0 °C reading must be used."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7234' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The current is I = E/(R + r) = 12/(5.5 + 0.5) = 2 A. Some voltage is lost inside the cell: Ir = 2 × 0.5 = 1 V. So the terminal p.d. is V = E − Ir = 12 − 1 = 11 V, which also equals IR = 2 × 5.5.', wrong = '{"0": "12 V is the emf. The terminal p.d. equals the emf only when no current flows.", "1": "1 V is the voltage lost across the internal resistance, not the terminal p.d.", "3": "10 V subtracts the lost volts twice. Ir is only 1 V."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7235' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The terminal p.d. is V = E − Ir. Write it for both readings: 1.4 = E − 0.2r and 1.2 = E − 0.6r. Subtracting gives 0.2 = 0.4r, so r = 0.5 Ω. Then E = 1.4 + 0.2 × 0.5 = 1.5 V.', wrong = '{"0": "1.0 Ω comes from 0.2 V ÷ 0.2 A. The change in current is 0.4 A, so r = 0.2/0.4.", "1": "1.5 V is right, but with r = 1.0 Ω the first reading would be 1.3 V, not 1.4 V.", "2": "1.4 V is the terminal p.d. while current flows. The emf must be higher."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7236' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A source gives maximum power to its load when the load resistance equals the internal resistance, R = r = 2 Ω. Then I = 6/(2 + 2) = 1.5 A and P = I²R = 1.5² × 2 = 4.5 W. This equals E²/4r, and the efficiency is then only 50%.', wrong = '{"1": "9 W is E²/2r, double the true maximum of E²/4r.", "2": "18 W is E²/r, the power wasted inside the battery when it is short-circuited.", "3": "4 W is the power in a 4 Ω load. It is less than the maximum, which occurs at 2 Ω."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7237' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The current is I = 6/(4 + 2) = 1 A. The load receives P = I²R = 1² × 4 = 4 W, while the battery supplies EI = 6 × 1 = 6 W in total. Efficiency = 4/6 ≈ 67%. A load larger than r gets less than the maximum power but uses it more efficiently.', wrong = '{"0": "4.5 W and 50% are the values for a 2 Ω load, which gives maximum power.", "2": "4 W is right, but 33% is the fraction wasted inside the battery, not the efficiency.", "3": "6 W is the total power from the battery. Only 4 W of it reaches the load."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7238' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two 6 Ω resistors in parallel give 3 Ω. Adding the 6 Ω in series gives 9 Ω in total, so the supply current is 9/9 = 1 A. This current splits equally between the two equal parallel resistors, 0.5 A each.', wrong = '{"0": "1.0 A is the total current from the supply. It divides between the two parallel resistors.", "1": "1.5 A is 9 V ÷ 6 Ω, as if one resistor were connected to the supply alone.", "3": "0.75 A halves the 1.5 A that a single 6 Ω resistor would draw. It ignores the series resistor."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7239' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From R = V²/P, the 25 W bulb has R = 220²/25 = 1936 Ω and the 100 W bulb has 484 Ω. In series both carry the same current, so the power I²R is larger in the larger resistance. The 25 W bulb therefore glows brighter.', wrong = '{"0": "The ratings apply only when each bulb has the full 220 V. In series the same current flows, and I²R decides the brightness.", "1": "The current is the same, but the resistances differ, so the powers I²R differ too.", "2": "Bulbs in series still carry current. Each just gets less than its full rated voltage."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7240' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Find each resistance from R = V²/P: 220²/100 = 484 Ω and 220²/25 = 1936 Ω. In series the total is 2420 Ω. The total power is P = V²/R = 220²/2420 = 48 400/2420 = 20 W, less than either rating.', wrong = '{"1": "125 W adds the two ratings, which is true only when both bulbs are in parallel on 220 V.", "2": "75 W subtracts the ratings, which has no physical meaning.", "3": "62.5 W averages the ratings. In series the total resistance rises and the power falls below both."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7241' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Current is the charge passing per second, and each electron carries e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C. So the number per second is n = I/e = 4.8/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 3.0 × 10¹⁹ electrons.', wrong = '{"0": "4.8 × 10¹⁹ uses e = 10⁻¹⁹ C instead of 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.", "2": "7.7 × 10⁻¹⁹ multiplies the current by e instead of dividing.", "3": "3.0 × 10¹⁸ slips a power of ten."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7242' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The voltmeter (8 kΩ) in parallel with the 8 kΩ resistor gives 4 kΩ. The circuit is now 4 kΩ + 4 kΩ, so the 12 V divides equally and the meter reads 6 V. Without the meter the p.d. would be 8 V, so a meter of modest resistance changes the circuit it measures.', wrong = '{"0": "8 V is the p.d. before the meter is connected. The meter draws current and lowers the resistance of that part.", "1": "4 V is the p.d. across the 4 kΩ resistor before the meter is added.", "3": "12 V is the whole supply voltage, which divides between the two parts of the circuit."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7243' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Let V be the p.d. across the load. By Kirchhoff''s current rule at the top junction: (6 − V)/1 + (3 − V)/2 = V/2. Multiplying by 2: 12 − 2V + 3 − V = V, so 15 = 4V and V = 3.75 V. The 3 V branch current is negative, so that cell is being charged.', wrong = '{"0": "4.5 V is the simple average of the two emfs. It ignores the different branch resistances and the load.", "1": "9 V adds the emfs as if the cells were in series. They are in parallel.", "2": "3 V is the emf of the smaller cell alone. The 6 V cell pushes the load p.d. higher."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7244' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A Wheatstone bridge with arms P, Q, R and S is balanced when P/Q = R/S, so no current flows through the galvanometer. Swapping the battery and galvanometer changes the condition to P/R = Q/S. This is the same equation rearranged, so the bridge stays balanced.', wrong = '{"1": "The balance condition is unchanged by the swap, so the galvanometer still shows no current.", "2": "Balance needs only the ratio P/Q = R/S. The four resistors do not have to be equal.", "3": "At balance the galvanometer current is zero before and after the swap. Twice zero is still zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7245' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'As the voltage rises, more current flows and the tungsten filament gets hotter. The resistance of a metal increases with temperature, so each extra volt drives less extra current. The I-V graph therefore curves towards the voltage axis, and the lamp is non-ohmic.', wrong = '{"0": "A falling resistance would make the graph bend towards the current axis, as for a thermistor. Metal resistance rises when hot.", "2": "An ohmic conductor gives a straight line through the origin. A curved graph means Ohm''s law is not obeyed.", "3": "The graph plots the lamp''s own V and I. The internal resistance of the source does not change its shape."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7246' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Resistance is R = ρL/A, and A ∝ d², so R ∝ L/d². Halving the length halves R, and doubling the diameter quarters it. So the new resistance is R × ½ × ¼ = R/8, and with the same V the current is 8I.', wrong = '{"0": "2I accounts only for the halved length and ignores the doubled diameter.", "1": "4I accounts only for the doubled diameter and ignores the halved length.", "3": "I/8 has the effect upside down. The second wire has the smaller resistance, so it carries more current."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7247' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The electrical input power is P = VI = 220 × 5 = 1100 W. The motor is 75% efficient, so its mechanical output is 0.75 × 1100 = 825 W. The remaining 275 W is wasted, mostly as heat.', wrong = '{"0": "1100 W is the electrical input power. Only 75% of it becomes mechanical output.", "1": "275 W is the 25% that is wasted, not the useful output.", "2": "1467 W divides by 0.75 instead of multiplying. The output must be less than the input."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7248' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The magnetic force provides the centripetal force, qvB = mv²/r, so r = mv/(qB). r = (1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ × 2.0 × 10⁶)/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 0.50) = (3.34 × 10⁻²¹)/(8.0 × 10⁻²⁰) ≈ 0.042 m = 4.2 cm.', wrong = '{"1": "2.1 cm is the radius for B = 1.0 T. A stronger field bends the proton more tightly.", "2": "8.4 cm is the radius for B = 0.25 T, half the given field.", "3": "0.42 cm slips a power of ten. The radius works out to 0.042 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7249' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With kinetic energy K, momentum is mv = √(2mK), so r = mv/(qB) = √(2mK)/(qB) ∝ √m/q. An alpha particle has 4 times the mass and 2 times the charge of a proton, so √4/2 = 1. The two radii are equal, r_p : r_α = 1 : 1.', wrong = '{"0": "1 : 2 is the ratio for equal speeds, where r ∝ m/q. Here the kinetic energies are equal.", "2": "2 : 1 is the equal-speed ratio upside down. With equal kinetic energies the radii are the same.", "3": "1 : √2 is the ratio for particles accelerated through the same potential difference, where r ∝ √(m/q)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7250' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From qvB = mv²/r, the radius is r = mv/(qB). For equal speeds in the same field, r ∝ m/q. A proton has m/q = 1 unit, while an alpha particle has 4/2 = 2 units. So r_p : r_α = 1 : 2.', wrong = '{"0": "1 : 1 is the ratio for equal kinetic energies, not equal speeds.", "1": "2 : 1 is the ratio upside down. The alpha particle has the larger m/q, so its circle is larger.", "3": "1 : 4 uses the mass ratio but forgets that the alpha particle has twice the proton''s charge."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7251' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The time for one revolution is T = 2πr/v. Since r = mv/(qB), T = 2πm/(qB), which does not contain v. Doubling the speed doubles the radius too, so the period stays the same. This is why a cyclotron can use a fixed frequency.', wrong = '{"0": "The period would halve only if the radius stayed fixed. In fact the radius doubles as well.", "1": "A faster electron does not take longer. Its larger circle is exactly balanced by its higher speed.", "2": "The period does not depend on v at all, so it cannot fall to a quarter."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7252' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Magnetic flux is Φ = BA cos θ, where θ is the angle between B and the normal to the coil. A field at 60° to the plane is at 30° to the normal. So Φ = 0.50 × 0.020 × cos 30° = 0.010 × 0.866 ≈ 8.7 × 10⁻³ Wb.', wrong = '{"1": "5.0 × 10⁻³ Wb uses cos 60°, treating the angle to the plane as if it were the angle to the normal.", "2": "1.0 × 10⁻² Wb is BA, which assumes the field is along the normal, at 90° to the plane.", "3": "0.87 Wb is a hundred times too large, a slip in powers of ten."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7253' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The force on a current-carrying wire in a magnetic field is F = BIL sin θ, where θ is the angle between the wire and the field. F = 0.20 × 4.0 × 0.50 × sin 30° = 0.40 × 0.5 = 0.20 N.', wrong = '{"0": "0.35 N uses cos 30° instead of sin 30°. The force depends on the sine of the angle.", "2": "0.40 N is BIL, which assumes the wire is at right angles to the field.", "3": "0.10 N is half the correct value, applying the factor ½ twice."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7254' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity, so it does no work. The speed, and so the kinetic energy ½mv², stay constant. The direction of motion keeps changing, so velocity, momentum and the force vector all change direction.', wrong = '{"0": "Velocity is a vector. Its size stays the same, but its direction changes all the time.", "1": "Momentum is a vector in the same direction as velocity, so it also keeps changing direction.", "3": "The force keeps the same size, but it always points towards the centre, so its direction changes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7255' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The electric force qE and the magnetic force qvB act in opposite directions. A particle passes straight through when they balance: qE = qvB, so v = E/B = 3.0 × 10⁴/0.20 = 1.5 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹. The charge and mass cancel, so this works as a velocity selector.', wrong = '{"0": "6.0 × 10³ m s⁻¹ multiplies E by B instead of dividing.", "1": "6.0 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹ is four times too large. E/B = 3.0 × 10⁴ ÷ 0.20 = 1.5 × 10⁵.", "2": "1.5 × 10³ m s⁻¹ slips two powers of ten."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7256' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First find the speed: eV = ½mv², so v = √(2(e/m)V) = √(2 × 1.76 × 10¹¹ × 200) ≈ 8.4 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹. In the field, r = mv/(eB), which is v divided by (e/m) × B = 1.76 × 10⁸ s⁻¹. So r = 8.4 × 10⁶/1.76 × 10⁸ ≈ 0.048 m = 4.8 cm.', wrong = '{"1": "2.4 cm is the radius for a field of 2.0 mT, double the given field.", "2": "9.5 cm is twice the radius, which would need twice the speed, from 800 V instead of 200 V.", "3": "0.48 cm slips a power of ten. The radius works out to 0.048 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7257' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Split the velocity into two parts. The part along B feels no force, so it carries the proton steadily along the field lines. The part perpendicular to B makes it go round in a circle. Together these give a helix (spiral) around the field lines.', wrong = '{"0": "A circle happens only when the velocity is exactly perpendicular to the field. Here it has a component along B.", "2": "A straight line happens only when the velocity is parallel to the field, with no perpendicular component.", "3": "A parabola is the path in a uniform electric field, where the force has a fixed direction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7258' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From F = BIL, B = F/(IL), so 1 T = 1 N A⁻¹ m⁻¹. Writing the newton in base units, 1 N = 1 kg m s⁻², gives 1 T = (kg m s⁻²)/(A m) = kg s⁻² A⁻¹. The tesla is also equal to 1 Wb m⁻².', wrong = '{"0": "kg s⁻¹ C has the coulomb on top. Since A = C s⁻¹, the correct form would be kg s⁻¹ C⁻¹.", "1": "N m A⁻¹ multiplies by length instead of dividing. B = F/(IL), so the unit is N A⁻¹ m⁻¹.", "3": "Flux density is flux per unit area, Wb m⁻², not Wb multiplied by metres."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7259' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At first the field passes straight through one face, so Φ = +BA. After a 180° turn the same field enters through the other face, so Φ = −BA. The change has magnitude BA − (−BA) = 2BA.', wrong = '{"0": "The flux reverses sign, from +BA to −BA. It does not return to the same value.", "1": "BA is the change for a 90° turn, when the flux falls from BA to zero.", "2": "BA/2 is the change for a 60° turn, when the flux falls to BA cos 60°."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7260' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Faraday''s law the average emf is ε = N ΔΦ/Δt, where ΔΦ = A ΔB for a coil at right angles to the field. ε = 100 × 0.050 × (0.80 − 0.20)/0.10 = 100 × 0.050 × 6 = 30 V.', wrong = '{"1": "0.30 V is the emf for a single turn. The coil has 100 turns.", "2": "50 V adds the two fields (ΔB = 1.0 T). The change is 0.80 − 0.20 = 0.60 T.", "3": "40 V takes the field as falling all the way to zero. It falls only to 0.20 T."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7261' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The motional emf is ε = BLv = 0.30 × 0.50 × 4.0 = 0.60 V, giving I = 0.60/2.0 = 0.30 A. By Lenz''s law the field pushes back on this current with F = BIL = 0.30 × 0.30 × 0.50 = 0.045 N. An equal pull keeps the rod at steady speed.', wrong = '{"0": "0.60 is the induced emf in volts, not a force.", "2": "0.30 is the current in amperes, not a force.", "3": "0.090 N is twice the correct force."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7262' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The pull needed is 0.045 N, so the mechanical power is P = Fv = 0.045 × 4.0 = 0.18 W. The heat produced in the resistor is I²R = 0.30² × 2.0 = 0.18 W. The two match: the work done against the magnetic opposing force becomes electrical energy and then heat.', wrong = '{"0": "0.045 is the force in newtons. The power is force × speed, 0.18 W, equal to the heat produced.", "1": "The power supplied equals the heat produced, 0.18 W each. It is not twice as much.", "3": "Constant speed means zero net force, but the pull still moves through a distance against the magnetic force, so it does work."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7263' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Lenz''s law the induced current opposes the change causing it, so the coil repels the approaching north pole. The near face must become a north pole. A current that looks anticlockwise when viewed from a face makes that face a north pole.', wrong = '{"0": "A face is north, but a clockwise current seen from that face makes it a south pole, not a north pole.", "1": "An anticlockwise current, seen from the face, makes a north pole, not a south pole.", "2": "A south pole would attract the magnet and help it move, creating energy from nothing. Lenz''s law forbids this."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7264' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'As the magnet falls, the magnetic flux through each ring of the copper pipe changes, inducing circulating eddy currents. By Lenz''s law these currents produce a magnetic field that opposes the magnet''s motion, so it falls slowly. Plastic does not conduct, so no currents flow there.', wrong = '{"1": "Copper is not a magnetic material. A magnet held still in a copper pipe is not attracted to it.", "2": "Air resistance is about the same in both pipes. It cannot explain the large slowing in copper.", "3": "The mass of the pipe does not affect how fast the magnet falls inside it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7265' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'As the magnet approaches, the current induced in the ring repels it. As it leaves, the induced current reverses and attracts it back. In both stages the magnetic force on the magnet points upward, against its motion, so its acceleration is less than g throughout.', wrong = '{"0": "While leaving, the ring pulls the magnet back, which also slows it. The acceleration stays below g.", "2": "The induced force always opposes the motion. It never adds to gravity at any stage.", "3": "The ring does not need to be magnetic. Any closed conductor carries induced currents that oppose the motion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7266' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The peak emf of an AC generator is ε₀ = NBAω. First convert the rotation rate to angular speed: ω = 2πf = 2π × 50 ≈ 314 rad s⁻¹. Then ε₀ = 200 × 0.20 × 0.010 × 314 ≈ 126 V.', wrong = '{"0": "63 V uses ω = π × 50, which misses the factor 2 in ω = 2πf.", "1": "20 V uses ω = 50 rad s⁻¹, forgetting to multiply the revolutions per second by 2π.", "3": "251 V uses ω = 4π × 50, twice the correct angular speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7267' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The induced emf depends on how fast the flux through the coil changes. When the coil''s plane is perpendicular to B, the flux through it is at its maximum and for that instant it is not changing, so the emf is zero. A quarter turn later the flux is zero but changing fastest, so the emf is greatest.', wrong = '{"0": "With the plane parallel to B, the flux is zero but changing at the fastest rate, so the emf is at its maximum.", "1": "Zero flux through the coil is exactly the moment of maximum emf, not zero emf.", "2": "The coil turns at a steady rate. The emf depends on its orientation, not on any change in its speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7268' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For an ideal transformer Nₛ/Nₚ = Vₛ/Vₚ = 220/2200 = 1/10, so Nₛ = 5000/10 = 500 turns. An ideal transformer loses no power, so VₚIₚ = VₛIₛ: 2200 × Iₚ = 220 × 10, giving Iₚ = 1.0 A.', wrong = '{"1": "A step-down transformer steps current up on the secondary side. So the primary current is smaller, 1.0 A, not 100 A.", "2": "50 000 turns would make the secondary larger than the primary and step the voltage up.", "3": "The currents are not equal. They differ by the turns ratio, so the primary current is 10 times smaller."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7269' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The current in the cables is I = P/V = 100 000/10 000 = 10 A. The power lost as heat is I²R = 10² × 5 = 500 W. At 1 kV the current would be 100 A and the loss 50 kW, which is why power is sent at high voltage.', wrong = '{"0": "50 kW is the loss if the power were sent at 1 kV, where the current would be 100 A.", "2": "50 W would need a current of only √10 ≈ 3.2 A. At 10 kV the current is 10 A.", "3": "1 kW would need a resistance of 10 Ω. The total cable resistance is 5 Ω."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7270' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The input power is VₚIₚ = 220 × 2.0 = 440 W. At 90% efficiency the output power is 0.90 × 440 = 396 W, and the other 44 W is lost as heat. The secondary current is Iₛ = 396/22 = 18 A.', wrong = '{"0": "20 A assumes the transformer is 100% efficient, so all 440 W reaches the secondary.", "1": "22 A divides by 0.9 instead of multiplying. The output power must be less than the input.", "3": "1.8 A slips a power of ten. 396 ÷ 22 = 18."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7271' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A transformer works by electromagnetic induction, which needs a changing magnetic flux in the core. A steady DC current from a battery gives a constant flux once the switch has been closed. So no emf is induced in the secondary, and the output is zero apart from a brief pulse at switching on or off.', wrong = '{"0": "12 V would need the secondary to be wired straight to the battery. The coils are linked only by magnetic flux.", "1": "Stepping up requires a changing flux, which a steady battery current cannot give.", "2": "With DC there is no changing flux, so the turns ratio has nothing to act on and the output is zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7272' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'AC mains voltages are quoted as rms values. The rms current is I(rms) = V(rms)/R = 220/55 = 4.0 A. For a sinusoidal current the peak is √2 times the rms value: I₀ = 1.414 × 4.0 ≈ 5.7 A.', wrong = '{"1": "4.0 A is the rms current. The peak is √2 times larger.", "2": "2.8 A divides the rms current by √2 instead of multiplying.", "3": "8.0 A doubles the rms current. The peak is √2 times the rms value, not 2 times."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7273' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Inductive reactance is X_L = 2πfL and capacitive reactance is X_C = 1/(2πfC). Setting them equal gives f = 1/(2π√(LC)). Here LC = 0.10 × 100 × 10⁻⁶ = 10⁻⁵ s², so √(LC) ≈ 3.16 × 10⁻³ s and f ≈ 1/(2π × 3.16 × 10⁻³) ≈ 50 Hz.', wrong = '{"0": "316 is 1/√(LC), the angular frequency in rad s⁻¹. Dividing by 2π gives the frequency in Hz.", "2": "16 Hz uses LC = 10⁻⁴ s², a slip in converting 100 µF.", "3": "100 Hz does not satisfy 2πfL = 1/(2πfC). At 100 Hz X_L is four times X_C."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7274' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An inductor opposes any change in current, so the current builds up a quarter cycle after the voltage. In a pure inductor the current lags the voltage by 90°. In a pure capacitor the current leads by 90°, and in a resistor they are in phase.', wrong = '{"0": "In a pure capacitor the current leads the voltage by 90°. It does not lag.", "1": "In a pure resistor the current and voltage are in phase. There is no 90° difference.", "3": "In an inductor the current lags the voltage. Leading by 90° is the behaviour of a capacitor."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7275' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a pure capacitor the current and voltage are 90° out of phase. For one quarter cycle the capacitor takes energy from the supply as it charges, and in the next quarter cycle it gives that energy back. So the average power over a full cycle is zero.', wrong = '{"0": "V(rms) × I(rms) is the average power only when voltage and current are in phase, as in a resistor.", "1": "V₀I₀/2 equals V(rms) × I(rms), the average power for a resistor, not a capacitor.", "2": "V₀I₀ is the peak instantaneous power in a resistor, not an average."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7276' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The reactance of a capacitor is X_C = 1/(2πfC), so it falls as the frequency rises. A lower reactance lets more current flow through the series circuit at the same voltage. So the lamp glows more brightly.', wrong = '{"1": "Capacitive reactance falls as frequency rises. It is the reactance of an inductor that rises.", "2": "The current depends on the reactance as well as the voltage, and the reactance changes with frequency.", "3": "A capacitor blocks only steady DC (zero frequency). It lets AC pass, which is why the lamp glows."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7277' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Compare V = 311 sin(100πt) with V = V₀ sin(2πft). So 2πf = 100π and f = 50 Hz, and the peak value is V₀ = 311 V. The rms value is V₀/√2 = 311/1.414 ≈ 220 V, the usual mains voltage.', wrong = '{"0": "100 Hz forgets the 2 in ω = 2πf, and 311 V is the peak value, not the rms value.", "2": "50 Hz is right, but 311 V is the peak value. The rms value is 311/√2 ≈ 220 V.", "3": "100π is the angular frequency ω in rad s⁻¹, not the frequency in Hz."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7278' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The voltage is V = V₀ sin(2πft). It reaches V₀/2 when sin(2πft) = ½, which first happens at 2πft = π/6. So t = 1/(12f) = 1/600 s ≈ 1.67 ms, one twelfth of the 20 ms period.', wrong = '{"0": "5.0 ms is a quarter of the period, the time to reach the full peak, not half of it.", "1": "3.33 ms is T/6. At that moment sin(2πft) = √3/2 ≈ 0.87, not ½.", "3": "10 ms is half a cycle, when the voltage has come back to zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7279' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Across the electromagnetic spectrum, wavelength increases as frequency and photon energy decrease. Gamma rays have the shortest wavelength and radio waves the longest. In between come X-rays, ultraviolet, visible, infrared and microwaves, with ultraviolet just shorter than visible and infrared just longer.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the order of decreasing wavelength (increasing frequency), the reverse of what is asked.", "1": "Gamma rays are shorter than X-rays, and microwaves are shorter than radio waves, so two pairs are swapped.", "2": "Ultraviolet has a shorter wavelength than visible light, so it must come before visible."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7280' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A full-wave bridge rectifier turns both the positive and the negative half of each AC cycle into a pulse in the same direction. So each input cycle gives two output pulses. With a 50 Hz supply the output pulses come at 2 × 50 = 100 Hz.', wrong = '{"1": "50 Hz is the output pulse rate of a half-wave rectifier, which uses only one half of each cycle.", "2": "25 Hz would mean one pulse every two cycles, which no rectifier gives.", "3": "200 Hz would need four pulses per cycle. Each cycle has only two halves."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7281' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a bridge rectifier the diodes work in pairs. During each half cycle, one pair of opposite diodes is forward biased and carries the current through the load in the same direction. The other pair is reverse biased and does not conduct. So two diodes conduct at a time.', wrong = '{"0": "With only one diode conducting, the current would have no complete return path to the supply.", "2": "In a bridge, diodes conduct in pairs. Three cannot be forward biased at once.", "3": "If all four diodes conducted together, they would short-circuit the supply."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7282' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a bridge rectifier the current passes through two diodes in series in each half cycle. Each silicon diode drops about 0.7 V, so the total loss is 2 × 0.7 = 1.4 V. The peak output is 20 − 1.4 = 18.6 V.', wrong = '{"0": "19.3 V subtracts only one diode drop, as in a half-wave rectifier. A bridge has two diodes in each path.", "1": "20 V ignores the forward voltage drops across the conducting diodes.", "3": "17.9 V subtracts three diode drops. Only two diodes conduct in each half cycle."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7283' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In reverse bias the p-side is connected to the negative terminal and the n-side to the positive. This pulls holes and free electrons away from the junction, so the depletion region widens and the potential barrier rises. Only a tiny leakage current of minority carriers flows.', wrong = '{"0": "A narrower depletion region and a large current describe forward bias, not reverse bias.", "1": "The depletion region grows under reverse bias. It does not disappear.", "2": "The higher barrier blocks the majority carriers. Only a tiny minority-carrier current flows."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7284' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A forward-biased silicon diode keeps about 0.7 V across itself while conducting. The rest of the supply, 5.0 − 0.7 = 4.3 V, appears across the resistor. So the current is I = 4.3/1000 = 4.3 × 10⁻³ A = 4.3 mA.', wrong = '{"1": "5.0 mA ignores the 0.7 V forward drop of the diode and puts all 5.0 V across the resistor.", "2": "5.7 mA adds the diode''s 0.7 V instead of subtracting it.", "3": "The diode is forward biased and the supply is well above 0.7 V, so it conducts and current flows."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7285' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A single diode in series lets current through only during one half of each cycle (half-wave rectification). In that half the lamp gets the same power as before, and in the other half it gets none. So the average power falls to half.', wrong = '{"0": "A quarter would mean the voltage itself had been halved all the time. Here the full voltage acts for half of each cycle.", "2": "The diode blocks every other half cycle, so the lamp receives power only half the time.", "3": "The diode conducts on alternate half cycles, so current does flow and the lamp still lights."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7286' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The energy of a photon is E = hf = hc/λ. Using hc = 1240 eV nm, E = 1240/620 = 2.0 eV, which is 2.0 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Light of 620 nm is orange-red, in the visible range.', wrong = '{"0": "1.2 eV is the energy of a photon of about 1000 nm, in the infrared, not 620 nm.", "1": "3.1 eV is the energy of a 400 nm violet photon. Longer wavelengths have less energy.", "3": "0.5 comes from λ/hc = 620/1240, the ratio upside down."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7287' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each photon carries E = hc/λ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.0 × 10⁸)/(660 × 10⁻⁹) ≈ 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. The laser emits 2.0 × 10⁻³ J each second. So the number of photons per second is (2.0 × 10⁻³)/(3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹) ≈ 6.6 × 10¹⁵.', wrong = '{"0": "6.6 × 10¹⁸ uses a power of 2.0 W instead of 2.0 mW.", "1": "3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ is the energy of one photon in joules, not the number of photons.", "2": "3.3 × 10¹⁵ uses a power of 1.0 mW, half the given value."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7288' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Einstein''s photoelectric equation, K(max) = hf − φ, where φ is the work function, the least energy needed to free an electron. The photon energy is hc/λ = 1240/400 = 3.1 eV. So K(max) = 3.1 − 2.0 = 1.1 eV.', wrong = '{"1": "3.1 eV is the photon energy. Part of it, 2.0 eV, is used to free the electron from the metal.", "2": "5.1 eV adds the work function instead of subtracting it.", "3": "0.55 eV is half the correct value. There is no factor ½ in K(max) = hf − φ."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7289' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Light arrives as photons, each with energy hf. Doubling the intensity at the same frequency doubles the number of photons per second, so twice as many electrons are emitted. Each photon still has the same energy, so K(max) = hf − φ does not change.', wrong = '{"0": "K(max) depends only on the frequency of the light and the metal''s work function, not on the intensity.", "2": "The number of electrons doubles, but each photon has the same energy, so K(max) stays the same.", "3": "The threshold frequency, φ/h, is a property of the metal. Changing the light cannot alter it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7290' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The photon carries away the energy difference between the levels: ΔE = 13.6(1/2² − 1/3²) = 13.6(1/4 − 1/9) ≈ 1.89 eV. Its wavelength is λ = hc/ΔE = 1240/1.89 ≈ 656 nm. This is the red Hα line of the Balmer series.', wrong = '{"0": "486 nm is the blue-green Balmer line from n = 4 to n = 2.", "1": "122 nm is the Lyman line from n = 2 to n = 1, in the ultraviolet.", "3": "1875 nm is the Paschen line from n = 4 to n = 3, in the infrared."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7291' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A line is produced by a jump between any two of the levels n = 1, 2, 3 and 4. The possible jumps are 4 → 3, 4 → 2, 4 → 1, 3 → 2, 3 → 1 and 2 → 1. That gives n(n − 1)/2 = 4 × 3/2 = 6 different lines.', wrong = '{"0": "3 counts only the one-step jumps 4 → 3 → 2 → 1. Atoms can also jump down several levels at once.", "1": "4 is the number of energy levels involved, not the number of possible jumps between them.", "2": "10 is n(n − 1)/2 for n = 5, the number of lines from atoms excited to the fifth level."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7292' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All Balmer lines end on the n = 2 level. The shortest wavelength needs the largest energy, which comes from a fall from n = ∞ to n = 2: ΔE = 13.6/2² = 3.4 eV. So λ = 1240/3.4 ≈ 365 nm, the series limit.', wrong = '{"1": "656 nm is the longest Balmer line, from n = 3 to n = 2, the smallest energy change.", "2": "91 nm is the shortest Lyman line, from n = ∞ to n = 1, not a Balmer line.", "3": "122 nm is the longest Lyman line, from n = 2 to n = 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7293' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the n = 3 state the energy is E₃ = −13.6/3² = −13.6/9 ≈ −1.51 eV. Ionization means removing the electron completely, to the level E = 0. So the energy needed is 0 − (−1.51) = 1.51 eV.', wrong = '{"0": "12.09 eV is the energy needed to raise the atom from n = 1 to n = 3, not to ionize it from n = 3.", "2": "13.6 eV is the ionization energy from the ground state, n = 1. From n = 3 much less is needed.", "3": "4.53 eV divides 13.6 by 3 instead of by 3² = 9."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7294' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each half-life halves the number of undecayed nuclei: 1 → ½ → ¼ → ⅛. So one-eighth remaining means three half-lives, (½)³ = ⅛. Three half-lives take 24 days, so T½ = 24/3 = 8 days.', wrong = '{"0": "3 days divides 24 by 8, using the fraction as if it were the number of half-lives. 8 is (½)⁻³, so three half-lives have passed.", "1": "With a 12-day half-life, 24 days is two half-lives, leaving ¼, not ⅛.", "3": "With a 6-day half-life, 24 days is four half-lives, leaving (½)⁴ = 1/16."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7295' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The decay constant is λ = 0.693/T½. Convert the half-life to seconds: 6.93 h = 6.93 × 3600 s, so λ = 0.693/(6.93 × 3600) ≈ 2.78 × 10⁻⁵ s⁻¹. The activity is A = λN = 2.78 × 10⁻⁵ × 2.0 × 10¹⁸ ≈ 5.6 × 10¹³ Bq.', wrong = '{"0": "2.0 × 10¹⁷ uses λ = 0.1 per hour as if it were per second. The half-life must be converted to seconds.", "1": "1.4 × 10¹³ Bq uses only a quarter of the correct decay constant.", "2": "2.8 × 10⁻⁵ is the decay constant in s⁻¹. It must be multiplied by N to give the activity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7296' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After two half-lives ¼ of the original nuclei remain, and after three half-lives ⅛ remain. So during the third half-life the number that decays is ¼ − ⅛ = ⅛ of the original nuclei.', wrong = '{"1": "Half of the nuclei present at the start of the third half-life decay, but that is only ½ × ¼ = ⅛ of the original.", "2": "7/8 is the total that has decayed after all three half-lives, not just during the third.", "3": "1/4 is the fraction of the original that decays during the second half-life (½ − ¼)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7297' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 8 h, sample A (T½ = 2 h) goes through 4 half-lives, leaving (½)⁴ = 1/16 of its nuclei. Sample B (T½ = 4 h) goes through 2 half-lives, leaving (½)² = ¼. So N_A : N_B = 1/16 : 1/4 = 1 : 4.', wrong = '{"0": "1 : 2 compares the half-lives, not the numbers of nuclei left.", "2": "4 : 1 is the ratio upside down. A decays faster, so it has fewer nuclei left.", "3": "1/16 is the fraction of A remaining. It must be compared with B''s ¼."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7298' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Alpha emission lowers the mass number by 4 and the atomic number by 2: ²³⁸₉₂U becomes ²³⁴₉₀Th. Each beta-minus emission leaves A unchanged and raises Z by 1, so two of them give ²³⁴₉₂. The final nucleus is an isotope of uranium.', wrong = '{"0": "Z = 90 is the nucleus after the alpha decay alone. The two beta decays then raise Z back to 92.", "1": "Z = 94 adds the two beta decays to 92 but forgets that the alpha decay first lowered Z by 2.", "3": "Mass number 230 removes 8 units, as if two alpha particles were emitted. Beta decay does not change A."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7299' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Only alpha decay changes the mass number, by 4 each time: (226 − 206)/4 = 5 alpha particles. These lower Z by 5 × 2 = 10, from 88 to 78. Lead has Z = 82, so four beta-minus decays must raise Z by 4.', wrong = '{"0": "5 alphas is right, but 6 beta decays would end at Z = 84 (polonium), not lead.", "1": "4 alpha decays would lower the mass number by only 16, ending at 210, not 206.", "2": "10 alpha decays would remove 40 mass units, ending at 186, far below 206."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-7300' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Wave speed is the distance a wave travels per unit time, so in one second it covers a distance equal to its speed. It is linked to frequency and wavelength by v = fλ: in one second f waves pass, each λ long.', wrong = '{"0": "Wavelength is the distance between two neighbouring crests (or troughs), not the distance travelled in a second.", "1": "Amplitude is the greatest displacement of a particle from its mean position.", "2": "Displacement is how far a particle is from its mean position at a given instant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9001' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The wave equation is v = fλ, so the frequency is f = v/λ. Substituting, f = 4 ÷ 0.5 = 8 Hz. A quick check: 8 waves, each 0.5 m long, pass a point every second, covering 8 × 0.5 = 4 m, which matches the speed.', wrong = '{"0": "2 Hz is v × λ = 4 × 0.5. The frequency is v divided by λ.", "1": "4 Hz just repeats the speed value. Dividing by λ = 0.5 doubles it to 8 Hz.", "2": "6 Hz does not come from v = fλ. With f = 6 Hz the speed would be 3 m/s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9002' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A wave in which the particles of the medium vibrate at right angles to the direction the wave travels is a transverse wave. Here the coils move perpendicular to the wave''s direction, so the wave is transverse.', wrong = '{"0": "Matter waves are the de Broglie waves linked with moving particles such as electrons, not vibrations of a spring.", "1": "Electromagnetic waves are oscillating electric and magnetic fields that need no medium. A slinky wave is a mechanical wave.", "3": "In a longitudinal wave the coils vibrate along the direction of travel, forming compressions and rarefactions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9003' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Molar specific heat is the heat needed to raise the temperature of one mole of a substance by one kelvin. Heat divided by moles and by temperature change gives the unit J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "Specific heat capacity is per kilogram, with unit J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.", "1": "Thermal conductivity has the unit W m⁻¹ K⁻¹. It measures how well a material conducts heat.", "3": "Latent heat is the heat per kilogram to change state at constant temperature, J kg⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9004' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With constant acceleration, velocity changes by the same amount every second: v = vᵢ + at. This is a linear equation in t, so the velocity-time graph is a straight line. Its slope is the acceleration and its intercept is the initial velocity.', wrong = '{"0": "A parabola is the displacement-time graph for constant acceleration, since s = vᵢt + ½at².", "1": "A v-t graph that curves upward means the acceleration is increasing, not constant.", "3": "A v-t graph that curves downward means the acceleration is decreasing, not constant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9005' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Newton''s second law in its general form says the net force on a body equals the rate of change of its momentum: F = Δp/Δt. When the mass is constant this becomes F = ma.', wrong = '{"0": "Impulse is the change in momentum itself (F × t), not the rate at which it changes.", "2": "Torque is the rate of change of angular momentum, the turning version of this law.", "3": "Velocity is momentum divided by mass, not the rate of change of momentum."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9006' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The slope of a displacement-time graph is Δd/Δt, which is velocity. At a single point the slope is found from the tangent drawn there. So the tangent''s slope gives the instantaneous velocity at that moment.', wrong = '{"0": "Average velocity over the whole motion is the slope of the straight chord joining the start and end points.", "2": "The tangent gives the velocity at the chosen point only. The starting velocity needs a tangent at the start.", "3": "The tangent gives the velocity at the chosen point only. The final velocity needs a tangent at the end."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9007' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Lenz''s law gives the direction of the induced emf and current: the induced current always flows so as to oppose the change in magnetic flux that produces it. This is a consequence of the conservation of energy.', wrong = '{"0": "The production of an induced emf by a changing flux is described by Faraday''s law of electromagnetic induction.", "1": "The size of the induced emf comes from Faraday''s law, ε = −N ΔΦ/Δt. Lenz''s law supplies the minus sign.", "3": "Lenz''s law says nothing about how strong the magnetic field is. It deals only with direction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9008' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a simple pendulum swinging through small angles, the motion is SHM with angular frequency ω given by ω² = g/l. Since ω = 2πf, ω² = 4π²f². So 4π²f² = g/l, which leads to the familiar period T = 2π√(l/g).', wrong = '{"0": "gl has units of m² s⁻², not s⁻², so it cannot equal 4π²f².", "2": "l/g equals 1/ω², which is T²/4π², not 4π²f².", "3": "√(g/l) is ω itself, which equals 2πf. The quantity asked for is ω²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9009' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each half-life halves what is left. From 50% to 25% is one half-life, and from 25% to 12.5% is another. So 10 s contains two half-lives, and one half-life is 10/2 = 5 s.', wrong = '{"0": "2.5 s would mean four half-lives in 10 s, reducing 50% to about 3.1%, not 12.5%.", "1": "3.3 s would mean three half-lives in 10 s, reducing 50% to 6.25%.", "3": "10 s is one half-life, which would reduce 50% only to 25%."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9010' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An emf is induced only when the magnetic flux through a coil changes. The field is uniform and the coil stays entirely inside it with its plane fixed, so the flux BA through it stays constant. No change in flux means the induced emf is zero.', wrong = '{"1": "A steady non-zero emf needs flux that changes at a steady rate. Here the flux does not change at all.", "2": "An increasing emf would need the flux to change faster and faster. The flux through the coil is constant.", "3": "A decreasing emf still needs some change in flux. In a uniform field the flux stays constant, so the emf is zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9011' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Potential difference between two points is the work done per unit positive charge in moving it from one point to the other against the field: ΔV = W/q. Its unit is the volt, 1 V = 1 J C⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "Electric power is the rate of doing work, energy per unit time, measured in watts.", "2": "Electric flux measures the field passing through an area, E × A, not work per charge.", "3": "Electric field intensity is force per unit positive charge, F/q, in N C⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9012' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electrons in an atom can have only certain fixed energies. When an electron drops from a higher level to a lower one, it emits a photon whose energy equals the gap, E = hf. Each bright line in neon''s spectrum is light of one wavelength, so each line is made of photons of one particular energy.', wrong = '{"0": "All neon atoms are alike and give the same set of lines. Different lines come from different jumps, not different atoms.", "1": "Every line in this spectrum comes from neon. Other elements would show their own different sets of lines.", "3": "Atoms do not split apart to give out light. The light comes from electrons moving between energy levels."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9013' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Bernoulli''s principle, where a fluid moves faster its pressure is lower. Air rushing across the top of the tube lowers the pressure there. The higher pressure on the liquid surface inside the bottle then pushes the liquid up the tube, and it is sprayed out with the air.', wrong = '{"0": "Fast-moving air has low pressure, not high pressure. It is the higher pressure inside the bottle that pushes the liquid up.", "2": "The pressure at the tube''s opening falls when air moves quickly across it. It does not rise.", "3": "Surface tension does not lift the liquid here. The pressure difference created by the moving air does."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9014' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Newton''s second law gives F = ma, so the acceleration is a = F/m. Substituting, a = 10 N ÷ 2 kg = 5 m s⁻². A quick check: a 2 kg mass accelerating at 5 m s⁻² needs 2 × 5 = 10 N, the given force.', wrong = '{"1": "10 m s⁻² just repeats the force value and ignores the 2 kg mass.", "2": "20 m s⁻² multiplies the force by the mass instead of dividing.", "3": "0.2 m s⁻² divides the mass by the force, the ratio upside down."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9015' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Gravitational potential energy near the Earth is P.E. = mgh. For identical masses, m and g are the same, so P.E. is directly proportional to height. The ratio is mgh : mg(2h) = 1 : 2.', wrong = '{"1": "2 : 1 is the ratio upside down. The mass at height 2h has the larger potential energy.", "2": "Potential energy depends on height, so masses at different heights cannot have equal P.E.", "3": "The masses are identical, so mass cancels from the ratio. Only the heights matter."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9016' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The scalar product is A · B = AB cos θ, where θ is the angle between the vectors. For parallel vectors θ = 0° and cos 0° = 1. So A · B = AB, the product of their magnitudes.', wrong = '{"0": "Zero is the scalar product of perpendicular vectors, where cos 90° = 0.", "1": "The square of one magnitude, A², is the result only when a vector is multiplied by itself or by an equal vector.", "3": "A scalar product multiplies the magnitudes. Adding them gives the size of the resultant of parallel vectors, not their dot product."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9017' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In one second, fluid moving at speed v through a pipe of cross-sectional area A fills a length v of the pipe. So the volume passing per second is A × v, with unit m² × m s⁻¹ = m³ s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "mv is momentum, measured in kg m s⁻¹, not a volume per second.", "2": "Aρ has units kg m⁻¹, which is not a flow rate.", "3": "vρ has units kg m⁻² s⁻¹. Multiplying ρAv would give the mass flow rate, not the volume flow rate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9018' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an adiabatic process no heat enters or leaves the gas, so Q = 0. The first law Q = ΔU + W then gives W = −ΔU. All the work done by the gas comes from its own internal energy, so the gas cools as it expands.', wrong = '{"0": "In an isochoric (constant-volume) process the gas does no work at all, since its volume does not change.", "1": "In an isobaric process heat is supplied, so the work comes partly from that heat, not only from internal energy.", "2": "In an isothermal process ΔU = 0 for an ideal gas, so the work comes entirely from heat taken in."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9019' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on its temperature. In an isothermal process the temperature stays constant, so the internal energy does not change: ΔU = 0. Any heat taken in is used entirely to do work.', wrong = '{"0": "Internal energy falls only if the temperature falls. In an isothermal process the temperature stays fixed.", "1": "Internal energy rises only if the temperature rises. Here the temperature is constant.", "3": "The internal energy keeps its existing value. It does not drop to zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9020' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an adiabatic process no heat is exchanged, Q = 0. By the first law, ΔU = Q − W = 0 − 10 = −10 J. The gas pays for the work from its internal energy, so its temperature falls.', wrong = '{"0": "+10 J would mean the gas gained energy. In fact it loses energy by doing work, with no heat coming in.", "2": "+20 J has no basis. No heat is supplied in an adiabatic process, so ΔU = −W.", "3": "ΔU = 0 applies to an isothermal process of an ideal gas, not an adiabatic one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9021' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the equation of continuity, A × v has units m² × m s⁻¹ = m³ s⁻¹, a volume per second. For an incompressible fluid the same volume must pass every section of the pipe each second. So Av is the volume flow rate, the same everywhere.', wrong = '{"0": "Av is a volume per second, not a volume. Its unit is m³ s⁻¹, not m³.", "1": "Av is not a mass. Multiplying by density, ρAv, would give the mass flow rate.", "2": "Density is mass per volume. It is not given by area times speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9022' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An ideal transformer has no energy losses, so all the power fed into the primary comes out of the secondary: VₚIₚ = VₛIₛ. If the voltage is stepped up, the current is stepped down by the same factor, and vice versa. So power is the same on both sides.', wrong = '{"0": "The voltage changes in the ratio of the turns, Vₛ/Vₚ = Nₛ/Nₚ. It is not the same unless the turns are equal.", "1": "The current changes in the inverse ratio of the turns. It is stepped down when the voltage is stepped up.", "3": "An ideal transformer has no losses, so it produces no heat in either coil."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9023' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first law of thermodynamics is energy conservation for heat and work. Heat supplied to a system goes partly into raising its internal energy and partly into work done by the system. So ΔQ = ΔU + ΔW, where ΔW is the work done by the system.', wrong = '{"1": "With ΔW as the work done by the system, it must be added to ΔU, not subtracted.", "2": "With ΔW as the work done by the system, ΔU = ΔQ − ΔW. Adding ΔW is correct only if ΔW means work done on the system.", "3": "ΔW = ΔU + ΔQ is not a form of the first law. It adds heat and internal energy to get work."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9024' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A bullet fired from a gun moves very fast, so it has a large kinetic energy, ½mv². When it strikes the target, this kinetic energy does work against the target material, pushing it apart. So it is the bullet''s kinetic energy that lets it pierce the target.', wrong = '{"0": "Mechanical energy covers both kinetic and potential energy. The more specific answer is kinetic energy, which does the work here.", "1": "Heat is produced as the bullet stops, but heat is a result of the impact, not what pierces the target.", "3": "The bullet''s potential energy is not involved. It moves roughly horizontally, and its height hardly matters."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9025' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ten waves passing a point each second means the frequency is f = 10 Hz. From the wave equation v = fλ, the wavelength is λ = v/f = 10 ÷ 10 = 1 m. A quick check: 10 waves each 1 m long cover 10 m in a second.', wrong = '{"0": "0.1 m comes from dividing f by v. The wavelength is v divided by f.", "2": "10 m just repeats the speed value without dividing by the frequency.", "3": "100 m multiplies the speed by the frequency instead of dividing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9026' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A stationary wave forms when two identical waves travel in opposite directions and overlap. At certain points they always cancel, so the displacement there is permanently zero. These points are called nodes. Halfway between nodes are antinodes, where the displacement is greatest.', wrong = '{"0": "Antinodes are points of maximum displacement, where the two waves always reinforce each other.", "2": "Crests are the highest points of a travelling transverse wave, not points of zero displacement.", "3": "Compressions are regions of high pressure in a longitudinal wave, such as sound."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9027' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Charges flow round a circuit only while a potential difference pushes them. A source of constant emf, such as a cell or battery, keeps a constant potential difference across the circuit. This drives a steady current through it.', wrong = '{"0": "A large resistance only reduces the current. It cannot make charges flow on its own.", "2": "A switch only completes or breaks the circuit. It does not supply the energy to drive the current.", "3": "An ammeter only measures the current. It does not drive it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9028' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cobalt-60 emits highly penetrating gamma rays. In radiotherapy these rays are aimed carefully at a tumour, where they damage the DNA of cancer cells and kill them. Cancer cells divide rapidly, which makes them especially sensitive to radiation.', wrong = '{"0": "X-ray images are made with X-rays from an X-ray tube, not with gamma rays from cobalt-60.", "2": "Healthy tissue is not the target. The beam is aimed to spare it as much as possible.", "3": "Blood pressure is measured with a sphygmomanometer, not with radioisotopes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9029' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Every unstable nucleus of a given kind has the same fixed chance of decaying in each second. It is impossible to say which particular nucleus will decay next, or exactly when. That unpredictability is what is meant by calling decay random.', wrong = '{"0": "The decay rate cannot be controlled, but that describes decay as spontaneous. Randomness is about not knowing which nucleus decays next.", "2": "It is impossible to predict which nucleus will decay next. That is exactly what randomness means.", "3": "The decay rate cannot be speeded up by heat, pressure or chemical change."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9030' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'On level ground with no air resistance, the range is R = v² sin 2θ/g. For a given speed, R is greatest when sin 2θ has its largest value, 1. That happens when 2θ = 90°, so θ = 45°.', wrong = '{"0": "At 22.5°, sin 2θ = sin 45° ≈ 0.71, so the range is only about 71% of the maximum.", "1": "At 30°, sin 2θ = sin 60° ≈ 0.87. The range equals that at 60°, but is less than the maximum.", "3": "At 60°, sin 2θ = sin 120° ≈ 0.87. The range equals that at 30°, but is less than the maximum."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9031' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The speed of sound in a gas is v = √(γP/ρ), so at the same pressure a less dense gas carries sound faster. Water vapour (M = 18 g/mol) is lighter than nitrogen (28) and oxygen (32). Humid air therefore has a lower density, and sound travels a little faster in it.', wrong = '{"0": "Moist air is lighter, not heavier, because water molecules are lighter than N₂ and O₂ molecules.", "2": "Saying the temperature stays the same does not explain why the speed changes.", "3": "The medium does change: humid air contains water vapour, which lowers its density."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9032' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Potential is a scalar, so the potentials of the two charges simply add. At the midpoint each charge is s/2 away: V = kQ/(s/2) + k(−Q)/(s/2) = 2kQ/s − 2kQ/s = 0. The electric field there is not zero, but the potential is.', wrong = '{"0": "Q is a charge, measured in coulombs. Potential is measured in volts and needs k and a distance.", "1": "−kQ/s leaves out the positive charge''s contribution. The two contributions are equal and opposite and cancel.", "2": "The potential becomes infinite only right at a point charge. The midpoint is a finite distance from both."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9033' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The SI unit of angular velocity is the radian per second. One complete revolution is 2π radians, so 2 revolutions per second is ω = 2 × 2π = 4π rad s⁻¹, which is about 12.6 rad s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "rev/s is not the SI unit, and 2π rev/s is also wrong in value. The fan turns at 2 rev/s.", "1": "rev/min is not the SI unit. 2 rev/s equals 120 rev/min, not 4π rev/min.", "2": "π/2 rad/s is only a quarter of a revolution each second, far slower than 2 rev/s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9034' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Rectification is the process of converting alternating current into direct current. It is done with a rectifier made of diodes, which let current flow in one direction only. The output is then a one-way (DC) current.', wrong = '{"0": "Modulation means putting a signal onto a carrier wave, as in radio broadcasting.", "1": "Polarisation means restricting the vibrations of a transverse wave, such as light, to a single plane.", "3": "Quantisation means a quantity can take only certain separate values, like the energy levels of an atom."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9035' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The wave equation v = fλ holds for the travelling waves that form a stationary wave. So f = v/λ = 2 ÷ 0.25 = 8.0 Hz. A quick check: 8 waves each 0.25 m long pass every second, covering 2 m.', wrong = '{"0": "0.125 Hz is λ ÷ v, the ratio upside down.", "1": "2.0 Hz just repeats the speed value without dividing by the wavelength.", "2": "4.0 Hz is half the correct value. 2 ÷ 0.25 = 8."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-9036' AND answer = 3;
